    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-design",
        title: "Design",
        subtitle: "A design is a usable representation of a solution, focused on how value might be realised.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.2",
        explanation: [
            "A design is a usable representation of a solution. It shows how a solution might work, in enough detail for others to understand, judge, build, and test it. A design may be a diagram, a prototype, a specification, or a worked description. Its focus is the solution: how value might be realised if the solution is built. Designs are representations, not the solution itself, and several designs may represent the same solution at different levels of detail.",
            "Designs serve understanding before they serve building. A good design lets stakeholders see the solution early, challenge it cheaply, and agree it before money is spent. Designs also drive discovery: working through a design often reveals new requirements, which in turn lead to more detailed designs. That cycle, requirements leading to designs and designs leading to requirements, is how understanding deepens, and the analyst manages it deliberately rather than letting it happen by accident."
        ],
        outputName: "Design description",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Solution", guidance: "The solution this design represents, and the needs it serves."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Representation", guidance: "The form of the design: diagram, prototype, specification, or description."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "How it works", guidance: "How the solution works, step by step, as the user experiences it."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Open questions", guidance: "What the design does not yet answer, and how each gap will be closed.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Name the solution and the needs it serves before describing anything, so the reader can judge fit. Choose the simplest representation that answers the question in hand: a sketch for an early conversation, a prototype for a user flow, a specification for a build. Describe how the solution works as the user experiences it, step by step, and list the open questions honestly. A design that hides its gaps will have them discovered during build, when they cost the most."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: design description for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Solution: the live status screen. Representation: a prototype built from the current screen, showing the status and its time in the existing layout. How it works: the officer enters the reference, the screen shows the status and its time, and a plain message appears when no status has been received. Open questions: how the screen behaves when the feed is late, and whether the history view is in scope.",
            "Showing the prototype to two officers revealed a new requirement nobody had stated: officers needed to see when the status was last refreshed, not only the status itself. The design had driven discovery, exactly as the cycle predicts. The requirement was added, the design was updated, and the build started with a clearer picture."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "A design is a usable representation of a solution, not the solution itself.",
            "Choose the simplest representation that answers the question in hand.",
            "Designs reveal new requirements; manage that cycle deliberately."
        ],
