    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-enterprise",
        title: "Enterprise",
        subtitle: "An enterprise is a system of one or more organizations and the solutions they use to pursue shared goals.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.2",
        explanation: [
            "An enterprise is a system of one or more organizations and the solutions they use to pursue a shared set of common goals. The word is deliberately broad: an enterprise may be a whole company, a group of companies, or a single business unit, depending on the change. What matters is the system view: the enterprise is the whole within which the change must work, with its own goals, rules, and connections.",
            "Thinking at enterprise level stops local fixes from creating wider harm. A change that helps one team but breaks a shared process, a shared data set, or a shared control has not improved the enterprise. The analyst asks how the change affects the whole: which other parts depend on the part being changed, which goals the change serves, and what the enterprise must still be able to do while the change lands. That view is what separates analysis from local problem solving."
        ],
        outputName: "Enterprise sketch",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Boundary", guidance: "What counts as the enterprise for this change, and what sits outside it."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Goals", guidance: "The shared goals the enterprise pursues, and which ones this change serves."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Parts", guidance: "The organizations and solutions inside the boundary, and how they connect."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Effects", guidance: "How the change affects each part, including parts it does not directly touch.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Draw the boundary first, and state what sits outside it, because an enterprise with no boundary is everything and therefore nothing. Name the shared goals and mark which ones the change serves, then sketch the parts and their connections at a level the reader can follow. For each part, write the effect of the change, including indirect effects. A sketch that shows only the changed part has missed the point of the enterprise view."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: enterprise sketch for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Boundary: the bank's payments business, including operations, technology, and control functions; the wider retail bank sits outside. Goals: safe, timely payments and trusted customer answers. Parts: the payment hub, the status feed, the operations screen, and the back office queue, connected by the status and reference data. Effects: the screen change touches operations directly, the feed team through monitoring, and the back office through fewer telephone calls.",
            "The sketch showed an indirect effect the team had missed: fewer telephone calls would reduce the back office's early warning of feed problems, because officers currently report silent feeds by phone. The change added a feed monitoring alert to replace that warning. The enterprise view had caught a harm that a local view would have created."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "An enterprise is the whole system within which the change must work.",
            "Draw the boundary, name the shared goals, and sketch the parts and their connections.",
            "Check indirect effects: a local fix can create wider harm."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-organization",
        title: "Organization",
        subtitle: "An organization is an autonomous group of people under one management, working towards common goals.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.2",
        explanation: [
            "An organization is an autonomous group of people under the management of a single individual or board, working towards common goals and objectives. Organizations usually have a clear boundary and operate continuously, unlike a project team, which disbands when its objectives are met. A company, a division, a department, and a regulator can each be an organization, depending on the change being analysed.",
            "The organization is where change lands. Its structure decides who owns each step, its culture decides how change is received, and its habits decide what actually happens after the formal change is complete. The analyst must understand the organizations affected by a change: their goals, their structure, their decision makers, and their capacity to absorb change. A solution designed without that understanding may be correct on paper and unworkable in practice."
        ],
        outputName: "Organization note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Identity", guidance: "The organization, its boundary, and its management."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Goals", guidance: "The goals it pursues, and how the change relates to them."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Structure", guidance: "The teams and roles inside it, and who owns each affected step."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Capacity", guidance: "Its ability to absorb this change now: time, skills, and competing demands.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Name the organization and its boundary, then describe its goals in its own terms, not in the language of the change. Map the teams and roles the change touches, and name the owner of each affected step. End with capacity: the time, skills, and competing demands that decide whether the organization can absorb the change now. A note that ignores capacity will propose a change the organization cannot receive."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: organization note for payments operations",
        workedExample: [
            "Organization: the payments operations department, led by the operations head, covering three teams. Goals: accurate, timely processing and trusted customer answers. Structure: the exceptions team owns repair cases, the status team owns customer queries, and the team leads own daily allocation. Capacity: the status team is short staffed this quarter and is also absorbing a queue change, so training for the new screen must be short and scheduled outside peak hours.",
            "The capacity finding changed the plan. Training was cut to a single short session with a one page guide, and the go live date was moved to avoid the month end peak. The change landed well because the plan respected what the organization could absorb, rather than what the calendar preferred."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "An organization has a boundary, a management, and common goals, and operates continuously.",
            "Map the teams and roles the change touches, and name the owner of each step.",
            "Check capacity: time, skills, and competing demands decide whether change can land."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
