    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-plan",
        title: "Plan",
        subtitle: "A plan is a proposal for doing or achieving something.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.2",
        explanation: [
            "A plan is a proposal for doing or achieving something. It describes a set of events, the dependencies among them, the expected sequence, the schedule, the results or outcomes, the materials and resources needed, and the stakeholders involved. A plan is a proposal, not a promise: it states what is intended, on the evidence available, and it is revised as evidence improves. Plans give a change its shape in time.",
            "A good plan names dependencies honestly. Most plans fail not because the work was hard, but because one piece waited on another and nobody said so. The analyst writes plans for analysis work itself: what will be explored, in what order, with whom, and when decisions are needed. A plan that lists only dates, without dependencies, resources, and outcomes, is a calendar, not a plan, and it will not survive first contact with reality."
        ],
        outputName: "Plan outline",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Events", guidance: "The events or pieces of work, in their expected sequence."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Dependencies", guidance: "What each event waits on, and what waits on it."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Schedule", guidance: "When each event happens, and the dates decisions are needed."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Resources and outcomes", guidance: "The people, materials, and resources needed, and the result each event produces.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "List the events in sequence, then add the dependencies before adding any dates: a date without a dependency is a guess. For each event, name the resources it needs and the outcome it produces, so progress can be judged by results rather than by activity. Share the plan with the people named in it, and revise it when facts change, recording what changed and why. A plan revised openly keeps trust; a plan revised silently loses it."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: plan outline for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Events: confirm feed coverage, agree wording rules, describe the screen change, and review with operations. Dependencies: the wording review waits on the compliance reviewer, who is free two hours a week; the screen description waits on the feed coverage answer. Schedule: coverage confirmed in week one, wording agreed in week two, description reviewed in week three. Resources: two officer interviews, one compliance review, and the feed log. Outcomes: a coverage note, agreed wording, and a reviewed description.",
            "In week one, the feed coverage answer slipped by three days. Because the dependencies were written down, the effect was clear at once: the screen description slipped too, but the wording review did not, because it waited on nothing. The plan was revised openly, the sponsor was told the same day, and no one was surprised."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "A plan covers events, dependencies, sequence, schedule, resources, outcomes, and stakeholders.",
            "Add dependencies before dates; a date without a dependency is a guess.",
            "Revise the plan openly when facts change, and record what changed and why."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-requirement",
        title: "Requirement",
        subtitle: "A requirement is a usable representation of a need.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.2",
        explanation: [
            "A requirement is a usable representation of a need. It takes a need, which may be vague and spoken, and turns it into a form others can use: to design, to build, to test, and to judge. Requirements focus on understanding what kind of value could be delivered if the requirement is fulfilled. A requirement that cannot be used by the next person in the chain, whether designer, builder, or tester, has not done its job, however well it is written.",
            "Requirements are written to be judged. Each one should be clear enough that two readers reach the same meaning, testable enough that someone can check whether it is met, and traced to the need it serves. Requirements come in kinds, from business goals down to transition needs, and the classification schema in this Studio teaches each kind. The discipline is the same at every level: represent the need faithfully, in a form the next person can use, and keep the representation honest when the need changes."
        ],
        outputName: "Requirement statement",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Need", guidance: "The need this requirement serves, traced to the need statement."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Statement", guidance: "The requirement itself, in one clear sentence."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Test", guidance: "How it will be checked that the requirement is met."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Source", guidance: "Who stated it, and when, so it can be confirmed or challenged.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write each requirement as one sentence, naming what is needed without naming how it will be built. Check it with two readers: if they reach different meanings, rewrite it. Add the test at the same time as the statement, because a requirement written without its test is a wish. Record the source and the date, so the requirement can be confirmed, challenged, or traced when the need changes."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: requirement statement for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Need: officers cannot answer status queries without manual checks. Statement: the operations screen must show the latest payment status and the time it was received, for every payment with a status report. Test: view a payment with a received status report and confirm the screen shows the status and its time. Source: operations lead, confirmed in the interview record of the first week.",
            "An early draft said the screen must show status information quickly. Two readers disagreed about what quickly meant, and no test could be written. The rewrite named the exact content and the exact condition, and the test wrote itself. The discipline of writing the test exposed the vagueness before build, not during testing."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "A requirement is a usable representation of a need, written for the next person in the chain.",
            "Each requirement must be clear, testable, and traced to its need.",
            "Write the test with the statement; a requirement without a test is a wish."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-risk",
        title: "Risk",
        subtitle: "Risk is the effect of uncertainty on the value of a change, a solution, or the enterprise.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.2",
        explanation: [
            "Risk is the effect of uncertainty on the value of a change, a solution, or the enterprise. Uncertainty is everywhere in analysis: needs may shift, data may be incomplete, systems may behave unexpectedly, and people may respond in ways nobody predicted. Risk is what that uncertainty does to value. A risk may reduce value, when a change fails or harms, or it may hide an opportunity, when uncertainty keeps a better option unexplored.",
            "Risks must be named, understood, and managed, not merely worried about. Naming a risk means stating the uncertain event, its cause, and its effect on value, in plain words. Understanding it means judging how likely it is and how much value it could affect. Managing it means choosing a response: avoid it, reduce it, transfer it, accept it, or, for an opportunity, pursue it. A risk register that nobody reads is not management; it is paperwork."
        ],
        outputName: "Risk note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Risk", guidance: "The uncertain event, its cause, and its effect on value."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Likelihood and effect", guidance: "How likely the event is, and how much value it could affect."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Response", guidance: "The chosen response, and what triggers a change of response."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Owner", guidance: "The person who watches the risk and acts when it moves.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "State each risk as cause, event, and effect: because of this cause, this event may happen, and it would affect value in this way. Judge likelihood and effect with evidence where possible, and mark judgements as judgements where evidence is thin. Choose a response for each risk, name an owner, and set a trigger for revisiting it. Review the notes regularly, closing risks that have passed and adding risks that have appeared."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: risk note for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Risk: because one payment type produces no status report, the screen may show no status for a large share of queries, and officers may lose trust in the screen. Likelihood and effect: likely for that payment type, and the effect is reduced value for the whole change if trust falls. Response: reduce, by showing a plain no status message with the manual check route, and by narrowing the value claim to covered payment types. Owner: the operations lead, with a review at go live plus two weeks.",
            "At the review, the risk had moved: the missing report was now scheduled for delivery, so the response changed from reduce to accept for a short period, with a clear end date. The risk note recorded the change and the reason, so the decision could be explained later. Managing the risk meant revisiting it, not filing it."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Risk is the effect of uncertainty on value, for a change, a solution, or the enterprise.",
            "State each risk as cause, event, and effect, and judge likelihood and effect honestly.",
            "Choose a response, name an owner, and revisit the risk as facts change."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-requirements-classification-schema",
        title: "Requirements Classification Schema",
        subtitle: "The schema sorts requirements into kinds, so each kind is written, judged, and managed at the right level.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.3",
        explanation: [
            "The requirements classification schema sorts requirements into kinds: business requirements, stakeholder requirements, solution requirements, and transition requirements, with solution requirements split into functional and non-functional. The schema helps the analyst and other stakeholders categorise requirements, so each kind is written at the right level of detail, judged by the right people, and managed in the right way.",
            "Classification is a practical tool, not a filing exercise. Business requirements state why the change exists, stakeholder requirements state what stakeholders need, solution requirements state what the solution must do and how well, and transition requirements state what is needed only to move from the current state to the future state. When a requirement is written at the wrong level, it misleads: a business goal written as a system feature invites argument about the feature, while a system feature written as a goal cannot be built or tested.",
            "The kinds form a chain. Business requirements are met by stakeholder requirements, which are met by solution requirements, and transition requirements carry the change from the current state to the future state. The analyst traces the chain, so every solution requirement can be followed back to a stakeholder need and a business goal, and every business goal can be followed forward to the work that serves it. The five lessons that follow teach each kind in turn."
        ],
        outputName: "Classified requirements list",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Business", guidance: "The business requirements: goals, objectives, and outcomes."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Stakeholder", guidance: "The stakeholder requirements: the needs that must be met to achieve the business requirements."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Solution", guidance: "The solution requirements: functional and non-functional."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Transition", guidance: "The transition requirements: temporary capabilities for the move."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Trace", guidance: "The chain linking each requirement to the level above it.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Sort each requirement into its kind by asking who judges it and at what level it is written: goals are business, needs are stakeholder, capabilities and qualities are solution, and temporary move needs are transition. Write each kind at its own level, and trace the chain in both directions, from goal to solution and back. Challenge any requirement that fits no kind, or two kinds at once: it is usually two requirements written as one, or a design disguised as a requirement.",
            "Review the classified list with the stakeholders who judge each kind: sponsors for business requirements, stakeholder groups for their own needs, and the delivery team for solution requirements. A list classified alone is a private filing system; a list classified with its judges is a shared agreement."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: classified requirements for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Business requirement: answer customer status queries accurately and without delay. Stakeholder requirement: operations officers need the latest status and its time on the screen they already use. Functional requirement: the screen must show the latest status and its time when a status report has been received. Non-functional requirement: the status must refresh within a stated time of the report arriving. Transition requirement: officers must be trained on the new screen before go live, and the manual check route must stay available for one month.",
            "Classifying the list exposed two problems. One item mixed a goal with a feature, and was split into a business requirement and a functional requirement. One item described a training need that would end after go live, and was moved to transition requirements, so it would not be mistaken for a lasting capability. The classified list was shorter, clearer, and easier to judge."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "The schema sorts requirements into business, stakeholder, solution, and transition kinds.",
            "Write each kind at its own level, and judge it by the right people.",
            "Trace the chain from business goal to solution, and back again."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-business-requirements",
        title: "Business Requirements",
        subtitle: "Business requirements are statements of goals, objectives, and outcomes that describe why a change was initiated.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.3",
        explanation: [
            "Business requirements are statements of goals, objectives, and outcomes that describe why a change was initiated. They apply to the whole enterprise, a business area, or a specific initiative. They answer the why question: what outcome does the organisation seek, and why does it matter now? Business requirements stay free of solution language, because their job is to state the destination, not the route.",
            "Business requirements are judged by sponsors and senior stakeholders, who own the goals. They must be measurable enough to judge afterwards: an outcome nobody can recognise has not been stated. The analyst tests each business requirement by asking how the organisation will know the outcome arrived, and what evidence will show it. If no answer exists, the requirement is a hope, and hopes do not survive contact with delivery."
        ],
        outputName: "Business requirements statement",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Goal", guidance: "The goal, objective, or outcome sought, in plain words."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Scope", guidance: "Whether the requirement applies to the enterprise, a business area, or one initiative."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Measure", guidance: "How the outcome will be recognised, with a baseline."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Reason", guidance: "Why this change, and why now.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write each business requirement as an outcome, not an activity: what will be true when the change succeeds. Name the scope honestly, because a goal claimed for the whole enterprise but funded for one team will fail. Agree the measure and the baseline with the sponsor before delivery starts, and record the reason for acting now. Read each statement and remove any word that names a system, a screen, or a feature; those belong to solution requirements."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: business requirements for payment status answers",
        workedExample: [
            "Goal: corporate customers receive accurate answers to payment status queries without waiting for a manual check. Scope: the payments operations area. Measure: time from query to answer, and the share of answers needing a manual check, both baselined from the query log. Reason: customer complaints about slow and inconsistent answers have grown, and manual checking consumes officer time every day.",
            "An early draft said the business required a new status screen. The sponsor agreed the rewrite: the business required accurate, timely answers, and the screen was one way to reach them. Keeping the goal free of the solution left the team able to compare options, and the sponsor able to judge the outcome rather than the tool."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Business requirements state goals, objectives, and outcomes: why the change exists.",
            "Keep them free of solution language; state the destination, not the route.",
            "Agree the measure and the baseline with the sponsor before delivery."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-stakeholder-requirements",
        title: "Stakeholder Requirements",
        subtitle: "Stakeholder requirements describe the needs of stakeholders that must be met to achieve the business requirements.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.3",
        explanation: [
            "Stakeholder requirements describe the needs of stakeholders that must be met in order to achieve the business requirements. They bridge the gap between business goals and solution requirements: they state what each stakeholder group needs, in terms that group would recognise, without yet describing the solution. A stakeholder requirement is written from the stakeholder's point of view, in the stakeholder's language.",
            "Stakeholder requirements are confirmed with the stakeholders themselves. The analyst elicits them through interviews, observation, workshops, and document review, then confirms the record with the people who gave it. Conflicting stakeholder requirements are normal: two groups may need different things, and the analyst surfaces the conflict, explores the trade off, and records the resolution. Hiding a conflict does not resolve it; it moves it to testing, where it costs more."
        ],
        outputName: "Stakeholder requirements list",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Group", guidance: "The stakeholder group, and its relationship to the change."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Need", guidance: "What the group needs, in its own language."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Business link", guidance: "The business requirement this need serves."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Confirmation", guidance: "How and when the group confirmed the requirement.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write each requirement from the group's point of view, using its words, and trace it to the business requirement it serves. Confirm every requirement with the group that stated it, and record the confirmation: an unconfirmed requirement is a draft. When two groups conflict, write both requirements down, explore the trade off with both groups together, and record the resolution and its reason. Never resolve a conflict alone, in the analyst's own favour."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: stakeholder requirements for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Group: operations officers. Need: see the latest payment status and its time on the screen already in use, without telephoning the back office. Business link: accurate answers to status queries without delay. Confirmation: agreed in the workshop record, confirmed by the operations lead. Group: compliance. Need: no status message may overstate what is known. Business link: the same goal, through trust. Confirmation: agreed in a one to one review.",
            "The two requirements pulled in different directions: officers wanted the fullest status shown, while compliance wanted nothing shown beyond the evidence. The trade off was explored with both groups: the screen would show the status and its time, plus a plain message when no status had been received. Both groups confirmed the resolution, and the record showed the reasoning."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Stakeholder requirements bridge business goals and solution requirements.",
            "Write them in the stakeholder's language, and confirm them with the stakeholder.",
            "Surface conflicts, explore trade offs together, and record resolutions."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-functional-requirements",
        title: "Functional Requirements",
        subtitle: "Functional requirements are the solution requirements that describe the behaviour and information a solution must provide.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.3",
        explanation: [
            "Functional requirements are solution requirements that describe the behaviour of a solution and the information it will manage. They state what the solution must do: the actions it performs, the data it holds, the rules it applies, and the responses it gives. They are written at a level a builder can build from and a tester can test from, and each one traces back to a stakeholder requirement it serves.",
            "Functional requirements must be precise. A vague functional requirement will be built one way and tested another, and the argument that follows costs more than the precision would have. The analyst writes each requirement as one behaviour or one rule, names the condition under which it applies, and states the expected result. Exceptions and error cases are functional requirements too: a solution that handles only the happy path is half a solution."
        ],
        outputName: "Functional requirements list",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Behaviour", guidance: "What the solution must do, stated as one behaviour per requirement."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Condition", guidance: "When the behaviour applies, including exceptions and error cases."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Information", guidance: "The information the solution manages, and the rules it applies."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Test", guidance: "How the behaviour will be checked.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write one behaviour per requirement, in plain words, with the condition and the expected result. Cover the exception and error cases with the same care as the happy path: what happens when data is missing, late, or wrong. Trace each requirement to the stakeholder requirement it serves, and write the test beside the statement. If two readers reach different meanings, rewrite the requirement before anyone builds from it."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: functional requirements for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Behaviour: the screen shows the latest payment status and the time it was received. Condition: when a status report has been received for the payment. Information: the status value and the received time, taken from the status feed. Test: view a payment with a received report and confirm the screen shows the status and its time. Exception: when no report has been received, the screen shows a plain message saying so, and shows no invented status.",
            "An early list covered only the happy path. Reviewing it against the exception log added three requirements: the no status message, the behaviour when the feed is late, and the behaviour when a report arrives for a payment already shown. The added requirements were the ones that prevented the most testing arguments, because they answered the questions testers ask first."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Functional requirements state what the solution must do and what information it manages.",
            "Write one behaviour per requirement, with its condition and expected result.",
            "Cover exceptions and errors with the same care as the happy path."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
