    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-non-functional-requirements",
        title: "Non-Functional Requirements",
        subtitle: "Non-functional requirements are the solution requirements that describe the qualities a solution must have and the conditions under which it must remain effective.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.3",
        explanation: [
            "Non-functional requirements are solution requirements that describe the qualities a solution must have, or the conditions under which it must remain effective. They cover performance, security, usability, reliability, and similar qualities. While functional requirements state what the solution does, non-functional requirements state how well it must do it, and under what conditions it must keep doing it.",
            "Non-functional requirements are often discovered late, because stakeholders describe what they want before they describe how well they want it. The analyst must elicit them deliberately, asking about volumes, timing, access, failure, and ease of use. Each one needs a measure: a quality stated without a measure cannot be tested, and a quality that cannot be tested will be argued about after delivery. A fast screen, a secure screen, and a usable screen each need their own stated standard."
        ],
        outputName: "Non-functional requirements list",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Quality", guidance: "The quality required: performance, security, usability, reliability, or another."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Standard", guidance: "The measurable standard the solution must meet."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Condition", guidance: "The conditions under which the standard must hold."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Test", guidance: "How the standard will be checked.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Name the quality, then the measurable standard, then the conditions: a standard without conditions invites argument about when it applies. Elicit non-functional requirements by asking about volumes, timing, access, failure, and ease of use, and record the answers as requirements, not as background notes. Write the test beside each standard, and agree who judges the result. A quality nobody measures is a wish, however firmly it is stated."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: non-functional requirements for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Quality: performance. Standard: the status screen shows the latest status within a stated time of the report arriving, during peak query hours. Condition: peak hours, with the normal number of officers signed in. Test: measure the refresh time across a sample of reports during a peak period. Quality: access. Standard: only officers with the status role may view payment status. Test: attempt access with and without the role.",
            "The performance standard was first written as fast refresh. That wording was replaced with a measured standard and a stated condition, agreed with the technology team. When the first test showed the standard missed during peaks, the gap was visible and fixable before go live, instead of becoming a complaint after it."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Non-functional requirements state qualities and conditions: how well the solution must perform.",
            "Every quality needs a measurable standard and stated conditions.",
            "Elicit them deliberately; stakeholders rarely volunteer them first."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-transition-requirements",
        title: "Transition Requirements",
        subtitle: "Transition requirements describe the capabilities a solution must have, and the conditions it must meet, to move from the current state to the future state.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.3",
        explanation: [
            "Transition requirements describe the capabilities that the solution must have, and the conditions the solution must meet, to facilitate transition from the current state to the future state. They are temporary: they are needed only for the move, and not once the change is complete. Training, data conversion, parallel running, and temporary support all belong here.",
            "Transition requirements are easy to forget, because they describe work that will disappear. Forgetting them is expensive: a solution may be ready while the people, data, and support needed to adopt it are not. The analyst plans the transition as carefully as the solution, asking what must be true on day one, what must stay available during the move, and when each temporary capability can end. Each transition requirement needs an end point, or temporary work becomes permanent cost."
        ],
        outputName: "Transition requirements list",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Capability", guidance: "The temporary capability or condition needed for the move."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Period", guidance: "When it is needed, and when it ends."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Owner", guidance: "Who provides it, and who decides it can end."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "End test", guidance: "How it will be known that the transition need has ended.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "List every temporary need separately from lasting requirements, so temporary work is never mistaken for a permanent capability. For each need, state the period, the owner, and the end test: the evidence that shows the need has ended. Plan the move in steps, with the fallback available at each step, and agree the end of each temporary capability in advance. A transition need with no end test will linger, costing effort long after its purpose has passed."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: transition requirements for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Capability: officers trained on the new screen and the no status message, before go live. Period: the two weeks before go live. Owner: the operations lead, with training delivered by the analyst. End test: every officer has completed the short session and the one page guide is in use. Capability: the manual telephone check route stays available for one month after go live. End test: telephone volumes fall below the agreed level for two consecutive weeks.",
            "The telephone route was due to end after one month, but volumes stayed high for one payment type: the type with no status report. The end test made the decision easy: the route stayed for that payment type only, with a new end date tied to the missing report. The transition requirement had done its job, by making the temporary need visible and its end testable."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Transition requirements are temporary: needed for the move, not after it.",
            "Every transition need needs a period, an owner, and an end test.",
            "Plan the fallback at each step, and agree the end in advance."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-stakeholders",
        title: "Stakeholders",
        subtitle: "Stakeholders are the individuals and groups who participate in, or are affected by, business analysis activities.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.4",
        explanation: [
            "Stakeholders are the individuals and groups who participate in, or are affected by, the execution of business analysis tasks within a change. Each task in this Studio names the stakeholders likely to be involved, because analysis is done with people, not to them. A stakeholder is anyone the analyst is likely to interact with directly or indirectly, from the sponsor who funds the change to the end user who lives with it daily.",
            "The generic list of stakeholder roles covers the business analyst, the customer, the domain subject matter expert, the end user, the implementation subject matter expert, operational support, the project manager, the regulator, the sponsor, the supplier, and the tester. The list is generic: a real change may use different titles, combine roles, or add roles of its own. What matters is not the title but the relationship: who needs what, who decides what, and who is affected by what.",
            "The analyst works with stakeholders throughout the change, not only at the start. Stakeholders provide information, make decisions, confirm understanding, and judge value. The eleven lessons that follow take each generic role in turn, teaching what the role contributes, what it needs, and how the analyst works with it. Together they give a complete picture of the people side of analysis."
        ],
        outputName: "Stakeholder role map",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Role", guidance: "Each generic role, and the person or group filling it in this change."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Contribution", guidance: "What the role contributes: information, decisions, approval, or judgement."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Need", guidance: "What the role needs from the analysis work."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Engagement", guidance: "How and when the analyst engages the role.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Map each generic role to the real person or group filling it, and leave no role blank without a reason: a blank role is a missing voice. For each role, write its contribution and its need separately, because a role that gives information but receives nothing will stop engaging. Agree the engagement rhythm early, and revisit the map when people change roles or new groups appear. A role map that is never revisited soon describes a team that no longer exists.",
            "Share the map with the team, and invite corrections: the map is most useful when the people named in it recognise themselves in it. A map corrected by its own subjects is a map the team will use."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: stakeholder role map for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Roles for the status screen change: the sponsor is the head of payments operations, the customer is represented by the corporate banking team, the domain experts are two senior officers, the end users are the status team, the implementation experts are the feed developers, operational support is the service desk, the project manager coordinates the plan, the regulator's rules are represented by compliance, the supplier is the feed provider, and the testers are the quality team. The analyst is the author of this map.",
            "Mapping the roles exposed a gap: operational support had not been told about the change, and the service desk would have faced the first queries unprepared. The analyst briefed the desk before go live, with the one page guide and the known limits. The role map had turned an overlooked group into a prepared one."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Stakeholders participate in, or are affected by, business analysis work.",
            "Map every generic role to a real person or group, and leave no role blank without a reason.",
            "Record each role's contribution, need, and engagement rhythm, and revisit the map."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-role-business-analyst",
        title: "Business Analyst",
        subtitle: "The business analyst is inherently a stakeholder in all business analysis activities.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.4",
        explanation: [
            "The business analyst is inherently a stakeholder in all business analysis activities. The analyst plans the analysis, elicits information, analyses it, communicates it, and supports the decisions others make. The analyst is the person who keeps the need, the solution, and the value in view at the same time, and who notices when one of them drifts from the others.",
            "The analyst's stake is the quality of the analysis itself. When information is unclear, the analyst clarifies it. When stakeholders disagree, the analyst surfaces the disagreement with evidence. When a decision is made, the analyst records it with its reasoning. The analyst does not own the business decision, but owns the clarity of the material the decision rests on. That ownership is the analyst's professional duty, and it is what stakeholders trust."
        ],
        outputName: "Business analyst responsibility note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Analysis scope", guidance: "The analysis work owned, and its boundaries."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Outputs", guidance: "The information the analyst will produce, and when."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Decisions supported", guidance: "The decisions the analysis supports, and who makes them."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Standards", guidance: "The standards the analysis will meet: clarity, evidence, and traceability.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write the scope of the analysis work plainly, including its boundaries, so others know what the analyst owns and what sits elsewhere. List the outputs with dates, and name the decisions each output supports and the person who makes each decision. State the standards the work will meet, and hold to them when pressure rises: clarity, evidence, and traceability are the analyst's promise, and breaking them quietly breaks trust loudly."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: responsibility note for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Scope: analysis for the status screen change, from need to reviewed solution description. Outputs: the need statement, the elicitation record, the wording decisions, and the solution description, each dated. Decisions supported: the scope decision, made by the sponsor, and the wording decision, made by compliance. Standards: every requirement traced to a need, every decision recorded with its reasoning, and every assumption marked as an assumption.",
            "Midway through, the sponsor asked the analyst to decide the scope alone, to save time. The analyst declined politely, presenting the options with their trade offs instead, and the sponsor decided with the evidence in front of them. The responsibility note had already said who decides, and the analyst held to it."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "The analyst is a stakeholder in all business analysis activities.",
            "The analyst owns the clarity of the material decisions rest on, not the decisions.",
            "State scope, outputs, decisions supported, and standards, and hold to them."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-role-customer",
        title: "Customer",
        subtitle: "The customer is the stakeholder who uses or is affected by the products or services the change delivers.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.4",
        explanation: [
            "The customer is the stakeholder who uses, or is affected by, the products or services that the change delivers. In payments work, the customer may be a corporate client awaiting a payment, a retail customer checking a balance, or an internal team consuming a service. Customers judge value from the outside: they feel the outcome, whether or not they understand the machinery behind it.",
            "Customers rarely speak in requirements. They speak in outcomes, frustrations, and expectations, and the analyst must translate those into needs without losing their meaning. Customers also vary: different segments want different things, and a change that pleases one segment may frustrate another. The analyst learns what each segment values, tests ideas against real customer experience, and keeps the customer's voice present in decisions made far from the customer."
        ],
        outputName: "Customer needs note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Segments", guidance: "The customer segments affected, and how they differ."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Outcomes wanted", guidance: "The outcomes each segment wants, in the customer's words."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Pain today", guidance: "What frustrates each segment today, with evidence."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Test", guidance: "How ideas will be tested against real customer experience.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Segment the customers before writing any need: a single customer view hides the differences that decide whether a change succeeds. Record outcomes in the customer's words, and support the pain with evidence from complaints, queries, and observation. Test every significant idea against real customer experience, through a prototype, a walkthrough, or a small trial, and record what was learned. A customer need that was never tested with a customer is a guess."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: customer needs note for payment status answers",
        workedExample: [
            "Segments: corporate treasurers, who need accurate status for cash decisions, and small businesses, who need a simple answer without jargon. Outcomes wanted: treasurers want the status and its time; small businesses want to know whether the payment has arrived. Pain today: both segments wait for manual answers, and the answers sometimes differ between calls. Test: the prototype screen was walked through with two treasurers and two small business owners, and the wording was changed from their feedback.",
            "The walkthrough changed one decision: small business owners found the status label confusing, and the label was rewritten in plain words. The change cost nothing at prototype stage and would have cost a release to fix later. Testing with customers early had paid for itself."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Customers judge value from the outside, by the outcome they feel.",
            "Segment customers before writing needs; different segments want different things.",
            "Test ideas against real customer experience, and record what was learned."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-role-domain-sme",
        title: "Domain Subject Matter Expert",
        subtitle: "The domain subject matter expert holds deep knowledge of the business area the change affects.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.4",
        explanation: [
            "The domain subject matter expert holds deep knowledge of the business area the change affects. In payments work, this may be a senior operations officer who knows every exception type, a product specialist who knows the scheme rules, or a control specialist who knows the risk framework. Domain experts hold the detail that decides whether a design will work in practice, including the cases nobody wrote down.",
            "Working with experts needs care. Experts are busy, their knowledge is often unwritten, and they may describe the work as it should happen rather than as it does. The analyst earns their time by preparing well, asking precise questions, testing understanding against real cases, and confirming the record afterwards. The analyst also checks expert views against other evidence: one expert's view, however deep, is one view, and the analysis must hold when several views are combined."
        ],
        outputName: "Expert input record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Expert", guidance: "The expert, and the area of knowledge they hold."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Questions", guidance: "The precise questions asked, and why each mattered."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Input", guidance: "What the expert contributed, as facts and views, kept separate."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Confirmation", guidance: "The record confirmed by the expert, with corrections.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Prepare precise questions before meeting the expert, and share them in advance, so the expert's time is spent on answers, not on context. Ask for real cases and examples, and test the answers against the logs and the live work. Record facts and views separately, and confirm the record with the expert afterwards, noting every correction. Thank the expert by showing how the input was used; experts who see their input used give their time again."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: expert input record for payment exceptions",
        workedExample: [
            "Expert: a senior exceptions officer with long experience of repair cases. Questions: which exception types arrive most often, what causes each, and what the officer does when the status is unclear. Input: three exception types dominate, one cause is a late reference, and the officer telephones the back office when the screen is silent. Confirmation: the record was confirmed with one correction, that the telephone check goes to the back office, not to the sending bank.",
            "The expert's view was checked against the exception log, which confirmed the three dominant types and added a fourth the expert had not mentioned. The analyst took the fourth type back to the expert, who confirmed it was handled by a different team. Combining the expert view with the log gave a fuller picture than either alone."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Domain experts hold the unwritten detail that decides whether a design works.",
            "Prepare precise questions, ask for real cases, and confirm the record.",
            "Check expert views against other evidence; one view is one view."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-role-end-user",
        title: "End User",
        subtitle: "The end user is the stakeholder who directly uses the solution in daily work.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.4",
        explanation: [
            "The end user is the stakeholder who directly uses the solution in daily work. End users are the people whose screens, queues, and routines the change alters. In payments work, the end user may be the operations officer working the queue, the analyst reading the report, or the customer using the portal. End users know the work as it is lived, including the workarounds that keep it moving.",
            "End users are often consulted late, when the design is already fixed, and their objections then read as resistance. Consulted early, the same people are the best source of practical detail and the strongest test of a design. The analyst involves end users from the start, observes their work, tests designs with them, and plans the training and support they will need. A solution end users cannot or will not use is not a solution, whatever the specification says."
        ],
        outputName: "End user needs note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Users", guidance: "The end user groups, and the work each group does daily."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Daily work", guidance: "How the work happens today, including workarounds."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Needs", guidance: "What each group needs from the solution, in its own words."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Support", guidance: "The training, guidance, and help each group will need.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Describe the daily work from observation, not only from interviews: watch the work, note the workarounds, and ask why each exists. Write the needs in the users' words, and test every design with real users before it is fixed, using prototypes and walkthroughs. Plan the training and support with the users, at a length and time they can absorb. A solution tested only by its designers will surprise its users, and the surprise will arrive after go live."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: end user needs note for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Users: the status team, who answer customer queries all day. Daily work: officers read the query, check the screen, and telephone the back office when the screen is silent, then call the customer back. Needs: the status and its time on the screen, a plain message when no status exists, and no extra steps for the common case. Support: one short training session and a one page guide, scheduled outside peak hours.",
            "Testing the prototype with two officers revealed a need nobody had stated: officers wanted the last refresh time visible, so they could tell a customer how fresh the status was. The need was added before build. The officers, consulted early, became advocates for the change among their colleagues."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "End users know the work as it is lived, including the workarounds.",
            "Observe the work, test designs with real users, and plan support with them.",
            "Consult end users early; consulted late, their objections read as resistance."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-role-implementation-sme",
        title: "Implementation Subject Matter Expert",
        subtitle: "The implementation subject matter expert holds deep knowledge of how the solution will be built and supported.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.4",
        explanation: [
            "The implementation subject matter expert holds deep knowledge of how the solution will be built, configured, integrated, and supported. This may be a developer, an architect, a configuration specialist, or an infrastructure expert. Implementation experts know what the systems can and cannot do, what the data really looks like, and what a change will cost in effort and risk.",
            "The analyst works with implementation experts early, before the solution is described in detail, so that feasibility shapes the description rather than contradicting it afterwards. The analyst brings the need and the business rules; the expert brings the constraints of the systems and the data. Together they find the design that serves the need within what is possible. An analyst who describes a solution without implementation input risks describing a solution that cannot be built."
        ],
        outputName: "Implementation input record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Expert", guidance: "The expert, and the system or technology area they know."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Constraints", guidance: "What the systems and data can and cannot do, with evidence."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Options", guidance: "The implementation options, with their effort and risk."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Agreement", guidance: "The agreed approach, and what it rules out.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Bring the need and the business rules to the expert, not a finished design, and ask what is possible, what is hard, and what is impossible. Record the constraints with evidence: a constraint stated without evidence is a preference. Compare the implementation options on effort, risk, and fit with the need, and agree the approach together, recording what it rules out. Revisit the agreement when the need changes, because a changed need may reopen a ruled out option."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: implementation input record for a status feed",
        workedExample: [
            "Expert: the developer who owns the status feed. Constraints: the feed supplies status for three payment types, and one type has no report; the feed updates on a fixed cycle, not continuously. Options: show the latest status with its time, or hold the screen until the next cycle. Agreement: show the latest status with its time and the last refresh time, and record that continuous updating is ruled out for this change.",
            "The constraint about the missing report changed the scope of the whole change, and it was learned in the first week because the analyst involved the expert early. Had the solution been described first, the missing report would have been discovered during build, when the redesign would have cost far more."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Implementation experts know what systems and data can and cannot do.",
            "Involve them early, before the solution is described in detail.",
            "Record constraints with evidence, and agree the approach together."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-role-operational-support",
        title: "Operational Support",
        subtitle: "Operational support is the stakeholder who keeps the solution running and helps its users after go live.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.4",
        explanation: [
            "Operational support is the stakeholder who keeps the solution running and helps its users after go live. This may be a service desk, a support team, or the operations team that owns the daily running of a process. Support teams see the solution as users experience it: the failures, the confusing messages, and the questions that arrive when something does not work as expected.",
            "Support is often forgotten until go live, when the first queries arrive and nobody has briefed the desk. The analyst involves support early, learning what queries the current solution generates, what information the desk needs to answer them, and what the new solution will change. Support needs guides, known limits, escalation routes, and a named contact for the first weeks. A change that launches without support ready launches into confusion."
        ],
        outputName: "Operational support needs note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Support team", guidance: "The team, and the hours and channels it covers."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Current queries", guidance: "The queries the current solution generates, and their causes."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "New needs", guidance: "What the new solution changes for support: guides, limits, and escalation."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Readiness", guidance: "What must be ready before go live, and who owns each item.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Learn the current queries before describing the new needs: the query log shows what users actually struggle with. Write the guides and known limits with the support team, in the words the team will use with users, and agree the escalation route for cases the desk cannot answer. Check readiness before go live, item by item, with a named owner for each. Support that is briefed late will improvise, and improvised answers become the official story."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: support needs note for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Support team: the service desk, covering working hours by phone and message. Current queries: status questions dominate, mostly about payments with no status report. New needs: a one page guide to the new screen, the known limit for the payment type with no report, and an escalation route to the feed team. Readiness: the guide written, the desk briefed, and the escalation route tested, all before go live.",
            "In the first week after go live, the desk received fewer status queries than before, and the queries it did receive were answered from the guide. One new query type appeared, about the refresh time, and the guide was updated within a day. Support had been ready, so the new query type was handled as a routine update, not a crisis."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Operational support sees the solution as users experience it.",
            "Learn current queries from the log before describing new needs.",
            "Brief support before go live, with guides, limits, and escalation routes."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-role-project-manager",
        title: "Project Manager",
        subtitle: "The project manager is the stakeholder who plans, coordinates, and controls the delivery of the change.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.4",
        explanation: [
            "The project manager is the stakeholder who plans, coordinates, and controls the delivery of the change. The project manager owns the schedule, the resources, the risks to delivery, and the reporting to sponsors. While the analyst focuses on the need and the solution, the project manager focuses on the delivery: what happens when, by whom, and at what cost.",
            "The analyst and the project manager work as partners with different lenses. The analyst tells the project manager what analysis is needed, how long it will take, and when decisions are required. The project manager tells the analyst what the plan allows, what depends on what, and where the pressures fall. When the two disagree, the disagreement is usually about the same facts seen from different sides, and it is resolved by sharing those facts openly and early."
        ],
        outputName: "Delivery coordination note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Plan", guidance: "The delivery plan, and where analysis sits within it."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Dependencies", guidance: "What analysis waits on, and what waits on analysis."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Decisions", guidance: "The decisions needed, by when, and from whom."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Pressures", guidance: "The pressures on the plan, and how they affect analysis.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write the coordination note together with the project manager, not alone, so both lenses shape it. Name the dependencies in both directions: what analysis waits on, and what waits on analysis, because a dependency named in one direction only is half a plan. List the decisions needed with dates and owners, and state the pressures on the plan honestly. Update the note whenever the plan or the analysis changes, and tell the other party the same day."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: coordination note for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Plan: the status screen change delivers in three cycles, with analysis running one cycle ahead of build. Dependencies: build waits on the reviewed solution description, and analysis waits on the feed coverage answer. Decisions: the scope decision, needed from the sponsor before the second cycle, and the wording decision, needed from compliance before build. Pressures: the compliance reviewer is free two hours a week, so the wording decision is booked early.",
            "When the feed coverage answer slipped, the coordination note showed the effect at once: the solution description slipped, but the wording decision did not. The project manager replanned one cycle, the analyst replanned the analysis, and the sponsor was told the same day. The partnership had turned a slip into a replan, not a surprise."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "The project manager owns schedule, resources, delivery risks, and reporting.",
            "Share facts openly: the analyst states analysis needs, the manager states plan pressures.",
            "Name dependencies in both directions, and update the note together."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-role-regulator",
        title: "Regulator",
        subtitle: "The regulator is the stakeholder who sets the rules the change must follow.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.4",
        explanation: [
            "The regulator is the stakeholder who sets the rules the change must follow. In payments work, regulators set requirements for safety, transparency, reporting, and customer protection, and their rules are constraints the solution must respect. The analyst rarely deals with the regulator directly; the rules usually arrive through compliance teams, legal teams, or published guidance. The regulator's stake is the integrity of the system and the protection of its users.",
            "Regulatory constraints are fixed points, not preferences. The analyst must learn which rules apply, what they require in practice, and what evidence shows compliance. Rules change, and the analyst must know who watches for change and how the team learns of it. A solution that meets the need but breaks a rule is not a solution, and discovering the breach after build is the most expensive way to learn the rule."
        ],
        outputName: "Regulatory constraint note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Rules", guidance: "The rules that apply to this change, and where each is stated."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Requirements", guidance: "What each rule requires in practice, for this solution."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Evidence", guidance: "The evidence that shows the rule is met."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Watcher", guidance: "Who watches for rule changes, and how the team learns of them.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "List the rules that apply, with their source, and translate each into what it requires in practice for this solution: a rule quoted without its practical meaning helps nobody. For each requirement, state the evidence that will show it is met, and agree that evidence with compliance before build. Name the watcher who tracks rule changes, and the route by which changes reach the team. Review the note when rules change, and record the effect on the solution."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: regulatory constraint note for status wording",
        workedExample: [
            "Rules: the status wording rules, stated in the compliance guidance, require that no customer facing message overstate what is known. Requirements: the screen must show the status only when a report has been received, and must say plainly when no status exists. Evidence: the wording review record, signed by compliance, and the test cases covering the no status message. Watcher: the compliance team, which issues a monthly update.",
            "A guidance update midway through the change tightened the wording for one status value. Because the watcher route existed, the team learned of it within days, and the wording was updated before build. The regulatory note recorded the change and its effect, so the decision history stayed clear."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Regulatory rules are fixed constraints, not preferences.",
            "Translate each rule into its practical requirement, and agree the evidence with compliance.",
            "Name a watcher for rule changes, and review the note when rules move."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-role-sponsor",
        title: "Sponsor",
        subtitle: "The sponsor is the stakeholder who champions the change, provides its resources, and owns its business outcome.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.4",
        explanation: [
            "The sponsor is the stakeholder who champions the change, provides its resources, and owns its business outcome. The sponsor authorises the work, removes obstacles, and is accountable for whether the change delivers its promised value. In payments work, the sponsor may be a head of operations, a product owner with budget authority, or a senior executive. The sponsor's stake is the outcome, not the detail.",
            "Sponsors need clear, honest material in short form: the need, the options, the costs, the risks, and the recommendation, with the reasoning attached. They do not need every detail, but they need the truth, including bad news early. The analyst serves the sponsor by presenting decisions in a form the sponsor can judge, by raising problems as soon as they are known, and by reporting value honestly after the change is live. A sponsor surprised by bad news late will not trust the next proposal."
        ],
        outputName: "Sponsor decision record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Decision", guidance: "The decision needed, stated as one sentence."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Options", guidance: "The realistic options, with their benefits, costs, and risks."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Recommendation", guidance: "The recommended option, with its reasoning and conditions."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Outcome", guidance: "The decision made, by whom, and when.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "State the decision in one sentence, then present the options on a comparable basis, with benefits, costs, and risks for each. Recommend one option, with the reasoning and the conditions that would change the recommendation. Keep the record short: a sponsor who must read ten pages to find the decision will decide without reading them. Record the outcome, the decider, and the date, and share the record with everyone affected by the decision."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: sponsor decision record for payment status scope",
        workedExample: [
            "Decision: approve the scope of the status screen change. Options: cover all payment types, accepting the missing report gap, or cover only the types with a report, with a separate investigation for the gap. Recommendation: cover only the types with a report, because claiming full coverage would mislead users and damage trust. Outcome: the sponsor approved the narrower scope, with the investigation funded separately, and the decision was recorded the same day.",
            "Six weeks later, a stakeholder asked why one payment type was excluded. The decision record answered the question in one page: the options, the reasoning, and the sponsor's decision. No meeting was needed, and no one's memory was tested. The record had done its job."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "The sponsor champions the change, provides resources, and owns the outcome.",
            "Present decisions in short, honest form: options, costs, risks, and a recommendation.",
            "Record the decision, the decider, and the date, and share it with those affected."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-role-supplier",
        title: "Supplier",
        subtitle: "The supplier is a stakeholder outside the boundary of the organization, who provides a product or service the change needs.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.4",
        explanation: [
            "The supplier is a stakeholder outside the boundary of a given organization or organizational unit, who provides a product or service the change needs. In payments work, suppliers may provide a status feed, a payment service, a software product, or specialist expertise. Suppliers hold knowledge the organisation does not: how their product behaves, what its limits are, and what changes are planned.",
            "Working with suppliers needs clarity about boundaries. The analyst must learn what the supplier provides, what it does not, what it guarantees, and how changes and failures are handled. Supplier promises must be tested against evidence: demonstrations, trials, references, and contract terms. A supplier relationship managed only through sales conversations will surprise the team at the worst moment, when a limit is discovered during live operation."
        ],
        outputName: "Supplier input record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Supplier", guidance: "The supplier, and the product or service provided."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Provision", guidance: "What is provided, what is not, and what is guaranteed."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Limits", guidance: "The known limits, and how they affect this change."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Handling", guidance: "How changes, failures, and queries are handled, and through whom.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Record what the supplier provides and what it does not, in the same document, because the boundary is where misunderstandings live. Test every significant promise against evidence: a trial, a reference, or a written term, not a presentation. State the known limits and their effect on this change, and agree how changes, failures, and queries are handled, with named contacts on both sides. Review the record when the supplier changes its offering, and record the effect."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: supplier input record for a status feed",
        workedExample: [
            "Supplier: the provider of the status feed. Provision: status reports for three payment types, delivered on a fixed cycle, with an agreed availability level. Not provided: reports for the fourth payment type, and continuous updating. Limits: the fixed cycle means status is never fresher than the last cycle, which the screen must show honestly. Handling: feed failures are reported to the named supplier contact, with an agreed response time, and the service desk holds the escalation route.",
            "Testing the feed against a trial showed the cycle was longer than the sales material suggested. The screen design was changed to show the last refresh time, so users could judge freshness. The trial had cost a week and saved a release of complaints."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Suppliers sit outside the organization boundary and hold knowledge it does not.",
            "Record what is provided and what is not; the boundary is where misunderstandings live.",
            "Test promises against evidence: trials, references, and written terms."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-role-tester",
        title: "Tester",
        subtitle: "The tester is the stakeholder who checks that the solution meets its requirements and serves its need.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.4",
        explanation: [
            "The tester is the stakeholder who checks that the solution meets its requirements and serves its need. Testers design checks, run them, and report what they find, including the failures nobody expected. In payments work, testing covers the happy path, the exception paths, the error cases, and the qualities: performance, security, and usability. Testers are the last line of defence before users meet the solution.",
            "Testers need requirements they can test, and they need them early. A requirement that cannot be tested will be built on one reading and tested on another, and the argument that follows is the analyst's to prevent. The analyst involves testers while requirements are written, asking how each requirement will be checked, and shares the exception cases and edge cases testers must cover. Testing is not a phase after analysis; it is a conversation that starts with the first requirement."
        ],
        outputName: "Test input record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Requirements", guidance: "The requirements to be checked, each with its test."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Cases", guidance: "The cases to cover: happy path, exceptions, errors, and qualities."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Data", guidance: "The data needed for testing, and where it comes from."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Findings", guidance: "What testing found, and what was done about it.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write the test beside each requirement as the requirement is written, and ask the tester whether the test is possible with the data available. Cover the exception and error cases with the same care as the happy path, and include the qualities: performance, security, and usability. Agree the test data early, because testing without realistic data proves little. Record the findings and the actions taken, so the same failure is not reported twice and fixed never."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: test input record for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Requirements: the screen shows the latest status and its time, and shows a plain message when no report exists. Cases: a payment with a report, a payment without a report, a payment whose report arrives late, and the screen during peak hours. Data: a sample of real payment references with known statuses, agreed with operations. Findings: the no status message failed on one payment type, the cause was a missing condition, and the fix was verified the same week.",
            "The tester asked, while requirements were still being written, how the refresh time would be checked. That question exposed a vague performance standard, which was rewritten with a measure before build. The early conversation had prevented a testing argument and a late fix."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Testers check that the solution meets its requirements and serves its need.",
            "Write the test beside each requirement, and involve testers while requirements are written.",
            "Cover exceptions, errors, and qualities, not only the happy path."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-concept-requirements-and-designs",
        title: "Requirements and Designs",
        subtitle: "Requirements are focused on the need; designs are focused on the solution, and the two develop together in a cycle.",
        kind: .concept,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "2.5",
        explanation: [
            "Requirements and designs are related but different. Requirements are focused on the need: they represent what is needed, and what value could be delivered if the need is met. Designs are focused on the solution: they represent how value might be realised if a solution is built. The same techniques are used to elicit, model, and analyse both, but the analyst must keep clear which one is being discussed at any moment, because a design debated as if it were a requirement closes off options, and a requirement debated as if it were a design cannot be built from.",
            "Requirements and designs develop together in a cycle. A requirement may be used to define a design, and that design may then be used to elicit further requirements, which lead to more detailed designs. The cycle repeats as understanding deepens. The analyst manages the cycle deliberately: each round makes the need clearer and the solution more detailed, and each round is checked with stakeholders before the next begins. Left unmanaged, the cycle spins: designs multiply, requirements drift, and nobody knows which version is current."
        ],
        outputName: "Requirements and designs trace",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Requirement", guidance: "Each requirement, and the need it serves."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Design", guidance: "The design that represents the solution for that requirement."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "New requirements", guidance: "The requirements the design revealed, each traced to the design."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Current version", guidance: "Which version of each item is current, and who confirmed it.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Keep requirements and designs in separate lists, linked by the trace, so the two are never confused. When a design reveals a new requirement, add the requirement to its list and trace it to the design that revealed it. Mark the current version of each item, and who confirmed it, and retire old versions visibly rather than deleting them. Review the trace at each round of the cycle, and stop the cycle when the design is detailed enough to build from and the requirements are stable enough to test against."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: requirements and designs trace for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Requirement: the screen must show the latest status and its time. Design: a prototype built from the current screen, showing the status in the existing layout. New requirement revealed by the design: officers need the last refresh time visible, so they can judge freshness. Current version: requirement version two and prototype version two, both confirmed by the operations lead. The trace shows the cycle in one view: requirement, design, new requirement, updated design.",
            "A later design round added a history view the prototype had not shown. The trace showed the history view had no requirement behind it, and the analyst asked the operations lead whether it was needed. It was not, for this change, and it was removed before build. The trace had caught scope growth that a design alone would have hidden."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Requirements focus on the need; designs focus on the solution.",
            "The two develop in a cycle: requirements define designs, designs reveal requirements.",
            "Keep separate lists, linked by a trace, with current versions marked."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    ]
}

extension StudioLesson {
    /// All 30 BABOK tasks, in knowledge area order.
    static let tasks: [StudioLesson] = [
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-approach",
        title: "Plan Business Analysis Approach",
        subtitle: "Choosing how the analysis work will run",
        kind: .task,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: .planning,
        babokReference: "3.1",
        explanation: [
            "Planning the approach means deciding how business analysis work will be done before the detailed work starts. The business analyst looks at the size of the change, the level of uncertainty, how stable the needs are, and how the wider team prefers to work. A small, well understood change needs a light approach. A large payments change with many connected systems needs more structure, more checks, and clearer handover points.",
            "The approach also covers timing, order, and depth. The analyst decides what will be explored first, how much detail is needed before build or configuration starts, and when stakeholders will be asked to confirm understanding. In a payments project, for example, the analyst might explore exception handling and reconciliation early, because those areas often drive design choices. Leaving them until late creates rework.",
            "Estimation sits inside this planning. The analyst estimates the analysis effort, the time needed for workshops and reviews, and the effort to keep information current. Good estimates come from breaking work into smaller parts and checking assumptions with people who have done similar work. Poor estimates usually come from guessing one large number with no breakdown.",
            "Without a planned approach, analysis drifts. Different people expect different documents, reviews happen too late, and the team discovers gaps only when testing starts. A clear approach, agreed early and revisited when conditions change, keeps the work focused and gives everyone the same picture of what analysis will produce and when."
        ],
        outputName: "Business analysis approach note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Change summary", guidance: "One short paragraph: what is changing, and why now."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Approach choice", guidance: "How analysis will run: light or structured, and the reason for the choice."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Sequence and depth", guidance: "What is explored first, how much detail is needed before build, and when understanding is confirmed."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Outputs and reviews", guidance: "What analysis will produce, who reviews each output, and when decisions are needed."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Estimates and assumptions", guidance: "The effort breakdown, the assumptions behind it, and what would change the estimate.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Start from the change, not from a template. Judge size, uncertainty, risk, and how stable the needs are, then choose the lightest approach that still gives the team enough clarity to proceed safely. A small report change and a multi-system migration need different amounts of structure, and the note should say why the chosen amount fits this change.",
            "Write the sequence before the detail. Name what is explored first, especially the areas that drive design choices such as exception handling and reconciliation, and name the points where stakeholders confirm understanding. End with the estimate as a breakdown: workshops, drafting, reviews, and rework counted separately, with assumptions stated so others can challenge them early."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: approach note for a reconciliation report change",
        workedExample: [
            "Change summary: the daily reconciliation report must show breaks by age band so the operations team can work the oldest breaks first. The change affects one report and one team, and the underlying data already exists. The need is stable and the risk is low.",
            "Approach choice: a light approach. One short session with report users and the operations lead, a draft of the new report layout, and one review round. No long discovery phase: the report is well understood and the change is contained.",
            "Sequence and depth: first confirm the age bands and the definition of a break, then draft the layout, then review with the operations lead. Estimates: half a day for the session and draft, half a day for review and updates. Assumption: the data team confirms the age band fields in the same week. If the fields are not available, the approach moves to a structured one with a data investigation first."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Agree the analysis approach early, and revisit it when scope, risk, or timing changes.",
            "Match the depth of analysis to uncertainty and risk, not to habit.",
            "Break analysis work into smaller parts before estimating effort and timing.",
            "Make clear what analysis will produce, who will review it, and when decisions are needed."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-01", title: "The approach that fitted the last project", takeaway: "Choose the analysis approach for this change, based on size, risk, and uncertainty.", story: [
            "A payments reporting change was starting.\nThe BA copied the approach from a large migration.\nNo one checked if it fitted this smaller change.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ A long discovery phase was planned for a simple report change\n→ Workshops were booked for six weeks before any build\n→ The operations team waited with no early view of the output\n→ Sponsors asked why a small change needed so much upfront work\n→ The team lost confidence before analysis had produced value",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will complete full discovery before any design work starts.\"",
            "So the team followed a heavy approach for a light change, and the work felt slow and out of proportion. Stakeholders stopped attending workshops, and the BA had to restart with a shorter, more focused plan.",
            "Why?",
            "An approach is a choice, not a template. What worked for a complex migration can slow down a simple change, and what works for a simple change can leave a complex change exposed. The BA needs to weigh size, uncertainty, and risk, then choose the lightest approach that still gives the team enough clarity to proceed safely.",
            "The right approach fits this change, not the last one.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Start by asking how big, how risky, and how uncertain the change is\n→ Choose depth and timing to match the answers, not past habit\n→ Agree the approach with the team and revisit it as you learn",
            "Have you ever seen a small change slowed down by a plan built for a large one?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-02", title: "An estimate with no breakdown behind it", takeaway: "Estimate from a breakdown, and show your assumptions.", story: [
            "The BA was asked how long analysis would take.\nThe answer was given in the same meeting.\nNo breakdown was prepared.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA estimated four weeks from experience alone\n→ Workshop time, review time, and rework were not counted\n→ Key stakeholders were only free for two hours a week\n→ Review rounds took longer than expected\n→ Analysis finished in seven weeks, not four",
            "The plan said:\n\"Analysis will take four weeks.\"",
            "So the plan slipped, and trust in later estimates fell. The team had booked build work to start in week five, and that work had to wait while analysis caught up.",
            "Why?",
            "Estimation works best when work is broken into smaller parts that can be judged separately. Workshops, drafting, reviews, and updates each take time, and each depends on other people being available. When the BA shows the breakdown and the assumptions behind it, others can challenge it early and help fix it. A single number with no workings hides every risk.",
            "An estimate without a breakdown is a guess with a number attached.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Break analysis work into parts before estimating any total\n→ Count workshops, drafting, reviews, and rework separately\n→ State assumptions about availability and decision speed\n→ Share the basis of the estimate so others can test it",
            "What would you do if you were asked for an estimate before you had seen the scope?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-03", title: "A detailed pack for an iterative build", takeaway: "Match analysis timing to delivery rhythm, and analyse just ahead of build.", story: [
            "The delivery team worked in short cycles.\nThe BA produced one large requirements pack.\nThe pack was finished before the first cycle started.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The pack described every feature in full detail\n→ The team built the first slice and learned new facts\n→ The pack no longer matched what was learned\n→ The BA spent cycles updating pages no one had read yet\n→ Decisions in the pack were revisited during testing",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will agree all requirements before build starts.\"",
            "So the upfront pack gave false comfort while the real understanding grew during build. The team built from conversations, the pack fell behind, and testing exposed choices that had never been rechecked.",
            "Why?",
            "The analysis approach must match how the team delivers. When build happens in short cycles, analysis works best just ahead of build, in the same small slices. That keeps detail fresh and lets learning feed the next slice. Writing everything upfront for an iterative team wastes effort and creates a document that ages before it is used.",
            "Analysis that runs too far ahead of build ages before it is used.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Ask how the team builds before deciding how you will analyse\n→ Detail only what the next slice needs, in full\n→ Keep later slices lighter until their turn comes\n→ Update analysis as build feedback arrives",
            "Have you ever written detail that was out of date before anyone used it?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-04", title: "The approach nobody revisited", takeaway: "Revisit the analysis approach whenever scope, risk, or stakeholders change.", story: [
            "The approach was agreed in week one.\nScope doubled in week five.\nThe approach stayed exactly the same.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ A second payments channel was added to scope\n→ New stakeholders joined with new needs\n→ Workshop time stayed at the original level\n→ Analysis depth stayed light while risk grew\n→ Gaps appeared late, during end to end testing",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will keep the original analysis plan.\"",
            "So the team carried on with a light approach while the change grew heavier. New needs were noted but never explored in depth, and testing became the first real check of whether the analysis held together.",
            "Why?",
            "Planning the approach is not a one off event. Scope, risk, stakeholders, and timing change during a project, and the approach must change with them. A good BA watches for those shifts and raises the need to adjust depth, time, or review effort early. Waiting until testing to discover the gap is the most expensive way to learn.",
            "A plan agreed once is a starting point, not a promise that conditions will stay still.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Review the approach whenever scope or risk changes\n→ Add depth, time, or checks when the change grows\n→ Tell stakeholders what you are changing and why\n→ Treat the approach as a living agreement, not a fixed rule",
            "What would you do if scope doubled but your analysis time stayed the same?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-approach-q1", question: "You are the BA and you are asked to plan the analysis approach for a small change to a payments reconciliation report. How do you decide how much analysis is needed?", answer: [
            "I start by understanding the size and risk of the change before I decide anything. I ask what is changing, who is affected, and what happens if we get it wrong. A small report change that affects one team is very different from a change that affects settlement or customer balances. I also check how stable the needs are and how much is already known. If the need is clear and the risk is low, I keep the approach light. I would hold a short session with the report users and the operations team, confirm the fields and rules, and agree the output with one quick review. I would not write a long pack for a small change. I also agree what I will produce and when, so the team knows what to expect. I set out the analysis approach, the stakeholders I need, and the review step in plain terms. If I learn something that raises the risk, I add more depth then. My aim is to give the team enough clarity to build with confidence, without slowing a small change down with work it does not need."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-approach-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you had to estimate business analysis work. How did you build the estimate, and what did you do when it started to slip?", answer: [
            "I break the work into parts I can judge separately, rather than guessing one large number. I list the workshops, the drafting, the reviews, and the time to update information after feedback. I use Estimation by checking each part against similar work I have done, and I ask colleagues who know the stakeholders how long reviews usually take. I also state my assumptions in the open. I note how much stakeholder time I need, who must attend, and how fast decisions are usually made. That way, if an assumption is wrong, we can see it early. On one payments change, review rounds took twice as long as planned because approvers were only free for short windows. I raised it as soon as I saw the pattern, I showed the breakdown, and I agreed a new plan with fewer, better prepared reviews. I kept the team informed and I did not hide the slip. I learned to build review time into every estimate, and to check stakeholder availability before I commit to dates. A clear breakdown makes a slip easier to explain and easier to fix."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-approach-q3", question: "A stakeholder says your analysis plan is too heavy for the change, and the delivery lead says it is too light. How do you resolve the disagreement?", answer: [
            "I bring both views back to the facts of the change, rather than arguing about the plan in the abstract. I ask what risk each person is worried about. The stakeholder may be worried about delay and cost. The delivery lead may be worried about building on unclear needs. Both worries can be valid. I walk through the analysis approach with them, showing the size of the change, the uncertainty, and the cost of getting it wrong. In payments work, a missed exception rule or a wrong reconciliation check can cause real harm, so some depth is not optional. But depth does not have to mean slow. I look for a middle path. I might agree to analyse the risky areas in full detail first, and keep the stable areas lighter. I might agree shorter review cycles so feedback comes faster. I write down what we agree, including what we will produce and when decisions are needed. I also agree when we will revisit the approach. My goal is a plan both sides can support, based on risk, not on habit or pressure."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-approach-q4", question: "You are the BA on a programme that is moving from a predictive approach to an iterative approach midstream. How do you replan the analysis work without losing control?", answer: [
            "I start by being honest that the old approach will not fit the new way of working, and that we need a new agreement, not just a new label. I meet the delivery lead and the key stakeholders and I agree what iterative working means for analysis in this programme. We agree that analysis will run just ahead of build, in small slices, with the riskiest areas first. I replan the backlog of analysis work so each slice has a clear question to answer and a clear output. I keep traceability light but real, so we can still show how each slice links to the overall need. I agree new review habits, with short checks at the end of each slice rather than one large sign off at the end. I also protect the areas that still need upfront care, such as payments controls, reconciliation, and exception handling. Those are explored early, even in an iterative plan. I keep stakeholders informed about what has been agreed and what is still open. I review the new approach after two cycles and adjust it. Control comes from clear slices, regular checks, and honest reporting, not from a large pack written upfront."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-plan-stakeholder-engagement",
        title: "Plan Stakeholder Engagement",
        subtitle: "Getting the right people involved in the right way",
        kind: .task,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: .planning,
        babokReference: "3.2",
        explanation: [
            "Planning stakeholder engagement means deciding who is affected by the change, what each person or group needs from the analysis work, and how they will be involved. The business analyst does not treat all stakeholders the same. A senior approver, a front line operator, and a technology specialist each need different information, at different times, and in different levels of detail. The plan sets out who will be consulted, who will decide, and who simply needs to be kept informed.",
            "A Stakeholder List, Map or Personas approach helps the analyst keep this clear. A list records who the stakeholders are and what they care about. A map shows how they relate to each other and to the change. Personas describe typical users in plain terms, so the team can test ideas against real working patterns. In payments work, for example, a persona for an exceptions handler will surface very different needs from a persona for a corporate client user, and both matter, since neither can speak for the other.",
            "Engagement also covers timing and method. Some stakeholders need early workshops to shape the direction, others need short reviews of specific outputs, and some prefer written material they can read in their own time. The analyst agrees the rhythm early, confirms how decisions will be reached, and checks that people with limited time are asked for input at the moments when it matters most. Good engagement respects time and makes it easy for people to contribute.",
            "Without an engagement plan, the same problems repeat. Key voices are discovered late, decisions are revisited, and people feel the change was done to them rather than with them. A clear plan, shared openly and updated as new stakeholders appear, builds trust and keeps analysis grounded in how work is really done."
        ],
        outputName: "Stakeholder engagement plan",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Stakeholder groups", guidance: "Each group that touches the change, and what each group cares about."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "What is needed from each group", guidance: "Decisions, information, or approval, named per group."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Engagement approach", guidance: "How each group is involved: workshop, interview, review, or update, and how often."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Communication plan", guidance: "What is shared, with whom, in what form, and when."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Risks and gaps", guidance: "Groups that are hard to reach, conflicting views, and how gaps are handled.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "List groups by their stake in the change, not by job title alone. For each group, write what you need from them and what they need from the analysis: a decision, information, or approval. A group with no named need is a group you have not thought about yet, and it is the group most likely to surprise you late.",
            "Match the engagement to the person and the decision. Sensitive topics suit one to one conversations before any group session. Shared decisions suit workshops where every view is heard in the room. Write the communication plan as a short table: audience, message, form, and timing, so no group learns about a decision second hand."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: engagement plan for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Groups: payment operations (daily users of the screen), the corporate banking team (answers customer questions), compliance (owns status wording rules), and the technology team (builds the screen). Operations cares about speed and accuracy. Compliance cares that no status overstates what is known.",
            "Needed: operations defines the statuses the screen must distinguish. Compliance approves the wording. Technology confirms which status events the systems can supply. Engagement: a workshop with operations and technology to agree the status list, then a one to one review with compliance on wording, then a short update to the corporate banking team before build starts.",
            "Communication: workshop notes shared the same day, wording decisions recorded in the requirements, and a single update when the screen design is agreed. Risk: the compliance reviewer is available two hours a week, so the wording review is booked two weeks ahead and the workshop does not wait for it."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "List stakeholders early, and record what each group needs and worries about.",
            "Use a map or personas to test ideas against real working patterns.",
            "Agree who decides, who is consulted, and who is kept informed.",
            "Match the method and timing of engagement to each stakeholder group."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-05", title: "The operations team found out too late", takeaway: "Include the people who do the daily work from the start.", story: [
            "A new payments workflow was being designed.\nThe BA spoke mainly to managers and technologists.\nThe operations team was not on the stakeholder list.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Workshops covered the happy path in detail\n→ No one asked how exceptions were handled day to day\n→ The design assumed manual checks that did not exist\n→ Operations saw the design only at review stage\n→ They raised twelve practical problems in one meeting",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will engage all affected teams.\"",
            "So the review became a redesign session, and weeks of work had to be rethought. The operations team felt ignored, and trust had to be rebuilt before the work could move forward again.",
            "Why?",
            "A stakeholder list is only useful if it is complete. The people who do the daily work often hold the detail that decides whether a design will work. Managers can describe the goal, but operators can describe the exceptions, the workarounds, and the timing pressures. Leaving them out does not remove their needs. It only delays the moment when those needs appear, when they cost more to meet.",
            "The people closest to the work see the problems first.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Build the stakeholder list with others, not alone\n→ Ask who does the work, who checks it, and who fixes it when it fails\n→ Involve operators early, not only at review\n→ Update the list whenever new groups are affected",
            "Have you ever discovered a key stakeholder only at the review stage?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-06", title: "One message for every stakeholder", takeaway: "Shape engagement material for each stakeholder group.", story: [
            "The BA sent the same long pack to all stakeholders.\nSenior leaders, operators, and technologists got identical material.\nFew people read it.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ A forty page pack went to twenty people\n→ Leaders wanted a short summary and a decision\n→ Operators wanted worked examples from daily tasks\n→ Technologists wanted rules, fields, and edge cases\n→ Feedback arrived late, and much of it missed the point",
            "The request said:\n\"Please review the attached pack and comment.\"",
            "So the review produced confusion instead of confirmation. Each group read the parts that made sense to them and skipped the rest, and the BA could not tell what had actually been agreed.",
            "Why?",
            "Stakeholders need different views of the same change. A persona helps the BA ask a simple question: what does this person need to do their part well? A leader needs the decision and the impact. An operator needs to see the daily task. A technologist needs precise rules. Sending one generic pack to everyone serves no one well, and it wastes the limited time stakeholders can give.",
            "The same change needs different stories for different readers.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Use personas to shape what each group receives\n→ Give leaders decisions and impact, in short form\n→ Give operators examples from real daily work\n→ Give specialists the detail they need to build and test",
            "What would you change if your review pack came back unread?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-07", title: "The approver who stopped attending", takeaway: "Agree a workable decision route when a key stakeholder cannot attend.", story: [
            "A senior approver attended the first two workshops.\nThen attendance stopped without explanation.\nThe BA carried on and noted decisions anyway.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Workshop invites kept going out each week\n→ The approver sent apologies, then stopped replying\n→ Decisions were recorded as agreed in the room\n→ The approver later challenged three key decisions\n→ Two weeks of analysis had to be revisited",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"I was never asked about that decision.\"",
            "So work that looked settled came undone. The team had moved forward on decisions that lacked the one approval that mattered, and confidence in the process dropped.",
            "Why?",
            "Engagement is not the same as invitation. Sending an invite does not mean a stakeholder is engaged, and silence does not mean agreement. When a decision maker stops attending, the BA needs to find out why and agree a workable alternative. That might be a short weekly check in, a written decision note, or a named delegate. The goal is a clear, agreed way for decisions to be made, not a hope that absence means consent.",
            "Silence from a decision maker is a risk, not agreement.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Notice early when a key stakeholder disengages\n→ Ask what rhythm and format would work for them\n→ Agree a delegate or a written decision route if needed\n→ Never record a decision as agreed without its owner",
            "Have you ever carried on without a decision maker and paid for it later?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-08", title: "Two teams, two opposite needs", takeaway: "Bring conflicting needs together early, and agree how the choice will be made.", story: [
            "A payments change affected two teams.\nOne team wanted speed and fewer checks.\nThe other team wanted control and more checks.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Each team met the BA separately\n→ Each team believed its view had been accepted\n→ A joint session exposed the direct conflict\n→ The meeting became a debate about blame\n→ No decision was reached, and work stalled",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"We cannot accept a process that slows us down.\"",
            "So the BA was left with two strong positions and no agreed way forward. The longer the conflict stayed hidden, the harder it became to resolve, because each side had already promised its own outcome.",
            "Why?",
            "Conflicting needs are normal, and the BA role includes bringing them into the open early. Separate conversations can hide a conflict. A joint discussion, framed around the shared goal and the real trade off, gives both sides a chance to weigh speed against control. The BA does not pick a winner. The BA helps both sides see the impact of each choice and agree who will decide when they cannot agree.",
            "A conflict named early can be managed. A conflict hidden grows.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Surface conflicts early, in a joint conversation\n→ Frame the trade off in terms of impact and risk\n→ Keep the focus on the shared goal, not on sides\n→ Agree who decides if the groups cannot agree",
            "What would you do if two teams asked you for opposite outcomes?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-stakeholder-engagement-q1", question: "You are the BA and you join a payments project where no stakeholder list exists. How do you build one, and how do you keep it current?", answer: [
            "I start with the people I know, and I ask each of them who else is affected. I ask who does the work, who checks it, who approves it, and who fixes it when it fails. In payments work, that usually brings in operations, reconciliation, technology, risk, and the client facing teams. I record each group, what they care about, and what they need from analysis. I use a Stakeholder List as a working tool, not a one off document. I share a draft early and I ask people to spot gaps. I also build a simple map to show how groups relate to each other and where interests may clash. I keep the list current by reviewing it whenever scope changes or a new group appears. I ask in workshops if anyone is missing. I also note who decides, who is consulted, and who is kept informed, so expectations are clear. A good list prevents late surprises. It helps me plan workshops, reviews, and decisions around the people who truly matter."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-stakeholder-engagement-q2", question: "Tell me about a time a key stakeholder stopped engaging with your work. What did you do?", answer: [
            "I noticed the pattern early, and I did not assume silence meant agreement. The stakeholder had attended the first sessions and then stopped replying. I spoke to them directly and I asked what was getting in the way. It turned out the workshop rhythm did not fit their diary, and the material was too long for the time they had. I changed the engagement approach for that person. We agreed a short weekly check in, with a one page decision note sent in advance. I also agreed a named delegate for detailed sessions, so work did not stall when the stakeholder was away. I recorded the new agreement and I shared it with the team, so everyone knew how decisions would be made. I kept the notes short and focused on decisions, impact, and timing. Engagement improved quickly because the format fitted the time available. I learned to check engagement as I go, and to adapt the method rather than repeating the same invite and hoping for a different result."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-stakeholder-engagement-q3", question: "A stakeholder says your personas are unrealistic and do not reflect how their team works. How do you respond?", answer: [
            "I treat that challenge as useful information, not as an attack. Personas are only helpful if they reflect real working patterns, so if a stakeholder says they are wrong, I need to understand what is wrong. I ask for examples from daily work. I ask what a typical day looks like, what exceptions arise, and where the persona misses the mark. I listen carefully and I take notes in their own terms. I then update the persona with them, testing each change against real tasks. In payments operations, for example, the difference between a handler who clears routine items and one who works complex exceptions can change the whole design. I would not defend a persona that does not fit. I would use the conversation to build a better one. I share the updated version and I ask the stakeholder to confirm it feels true. I also check the change with other members of the same team, so one view does not dominate. A persona that survives honest challenge is far more valuable than one that was never tested."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-stakeholder-engagement-q4", question: "You are the BA and two senior stakeholders give you directly conflicting instructions about the scope of a payments change. Both outrank you. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I do not pick a side, and I do not try to satisfy both instructions at once. I start by understanding the need behind each instruction. I meet each stakeholder and I ask what outcome they need, why it matters, and what risk they are trying to avoid. I then bring the conflict into the open in a structured way. I prepare a short comparison of the two options, showing scope, impact, risk, cost, and timing in plain terms. I frame it around the shared goal of the change, not around personalities. I ask for a joint discussion, with the person who owns the final decision present. If no single decider exists, I raise that gap and I ask for one to be named. During the discussion, I keep the focus on trade offs and evidence, and I record what is agreed. I also make clear what stays open and what work must pause until the choice is made. I follow up in writing so there is no confusion later. My role is to make the choice clear, fair, and owned, even when the conversation is difficult."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-stakeholder-management"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-governance",
        title: "Plan Business Analysis Governance",
        subtitle: "Agreeing who decides, and how changes are controlled",
        kind: .task,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: .planning,
        babokReference: "3.3",
        explanation: [
            "Planning governance means agreeing how decisions about business analysis work will be made, who can make them, and how changes will be handled. The business analyst helps the team agree who approves requirements, who approves changes to them, and what happens when people disagree. Without those agreements, decisions are made informally, revisited often, and hard to trace. With them, the team knows where a decision stands and who owns it.",
            "Reviews are a central part of governance. A Review is a structured check of analysis work by people who can spot errors, gaps, and risks. The analyst plans who will review what, how much time they will have, and what a review must confirm before work moves forward. A good review is not a formality. It asks whether the work is clear, complete enough for its purpose, and consistent with what was agreed before.",
            "Governance also covers change control. Needs change during a project, and that is expected. The question is how changes are raised, assessed, and agreed. In a payments change, for example, a small change to a validation rule can affect settlement, reporting, and exception handling. The analyst plans a simple route for raising a change, checking its impact, and recording the decision, so small changes do not quietly grow into large ones.",
            "Good governance is light where risk is low and stronger where risk is high. It protects the team from rework, protects stakeholders from surprises, and gives auditors and reviewers a clear trail. The analyst keeps governance practical, agreed in advance, and visible to everyone who needs to follow it."
        ],
        outputName: "Business analysis governance note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Decision rights", guidance: "Who decides what, including who can approve requirements and who can approve changes."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Approval route", guidance: "The steps a requirement follows from draft to approved, and who signs at each step."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Change control", guidance: "How a change request is raised, assessed, and decided, and what record is kept."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Escalation route", guidance: "When a disagreement escalates, to whom, and how quickly."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Review rhythm", guidance: "How often governance is checked, and what triggers a change to the governance itself.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Name decision rights before the first disagreement, not during it. For each kind of decision, write one owner and the people who must be consulted. If two people can both approve the same requirement, no one owns the decision, and the requirement will be reargued at every review.",
            "Keep the approval route as short as the risk allows. A low risk change needs one reviewer and a recorded decision. A change that affects settlement or customer balances needs more than one pair of eyes and a written record. Write the change control steps so that anyone can follow them: raise, assess impact, decide, record, and tell everyone affected."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: governance note for a beneficiary validation change",
        workedExample: [
            "Decision rights: the payments product owner approves functional requirements. The compliance lead approves validation wording and any rule that stops a payment. The technology lead approves interface changes. No requirement is approved by two owners at once.",
            "Approval route: draft, review by the operations lead, approval by the product owner, and a recorded decision in the requirements tool. Change control: any change after approval is raised as a change request, assessed for impact on validation rules and test cases, decided by the product owner, and recorded with the reason.",
            "Escalation: if operations and compliance disagree on wording, the disagreement goes to the product owner within two working days, with both positions written down. Review rhythm: governance is checked at the end of each release, and sooner if a decision takes longer than five working days."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Agree who approves requirements and who approves changes, before work starts.",
            "Plan Reviews with named reviewers, enough time, and a clear purpose.",
            "Use a simple change route: raise, assess impact, decide, record.",
            "Scale governance to risk: lighter for low risk, stronger for high risk."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-09", title: "The scope change agreed in a corridor", takeaway: "Route every scope change through an agreed check, however small it sounds.", story: [
            "A stakeholder asked for an extra payments report.\nThe request was agreed in a short corridor chat.\nNo one recorded the decision or checked the impact.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The extra report was added to the build list\n→ No one assessed effort, timing, or knock on effects\n→ The delivery team built it alongside planned work\n→ The planned work slipped by two weeks\n→ The sponsor asked why dates had moved",
            "The request said:\n\"Can we just add one more report?\"",
            "So a small sounding request changed the plan without any formal check. The team had done the work in good faith, but no one could show who had approved the change or what trade off had been accepted.",
            "Why?",
            "Governance is about making decisions visible and owned. A corridor agreement feels fast, but it skips the impact check and the record. The BA needs a simple, agreed route for changes, even for small ones. Raise the request, check the impact on scope and timing, get agreement from the right person, and record the outcome. That protects both the requester and the team.",
            "A decision no one can trace is a decision no one owns.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Agree a simple change route before changes arrive\n→ Check impact on scope, timing, and other work\n→ Get agreement from the person who owns the decision\n→ Record what was agreed, and what was traded off",
            "Have you ever seen a small request quietly change a whole plan?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-10", title: "The review that approved everything", takeaway: "Plan reviews with time, focus, and real cases to test against.", story: [
            "Requirements for a payment validation rule were sent for review.\nReviewers had one day and no guidance on what to check.\nAll reviewers approved without comment.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The pack arrived late on a Friday\n→ Reviewers scanned it between other meetings\n→ No one checked the rule against real cases\n→ The rule was approved and built\n→ Testing found the rule rejected valid payments",
            "The plan said:\n\"Requirements will be reviewed before build.\"",
            "So the review step existed on paper but added no value in practice. Errors that a focused check would have caught reached testing, where they cost far more time and trust to fix.",
            "Why?",
            "A Review needs time, focus, and a clear question. Reviewers need to know what to check, which cases to test the work against, and how to raise concerns. Sending a large pack with a short deadline and no guidance invites a quick approval, not a real check. The BA plans reviews so they happen early enough, with enough time, and with reviewers who can genuinely challenge the work.",
            "A review without time or focus is approval in name only.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Give reviewers enough time and a clear focus\n→ Ask reviewers to test rules against real cases\n→ Use checklists for common gaps and edge cases\n→ Record review comments and how each was resolved",
            "What would you do if every reviewer approved your work without a single comment?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-11", title: "Two approvers, two different answers", takeaway: "Name a single owner for each decision type, before decisions are needed.", story: [
            "A change to a payments cut off time needed approval.\nTwo senior stakeholders both believed they owned the decision.\nThey gave opposite answers on the same day.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA asked both approvers in parallel\n→ One approved the earlier cut off time\n→ The other rejected it for client impact reasons\n→ The team did not know which answer to follow\n→ Work paused while the ownership question was settled",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"That decision sits with my team.\"",
            "So the project lost a week to a question that should have been settled before the work started. Both approvers acted in good faith, but the lack of agreed decision rights turned a simple choice into a standoff.",
            "Why?",
            "Governance must name a single owner for each type of decision. When ownership is shared but unclear, the team receives mixed signals and cannot move forward with confidence. The BA helps agree decision rights early, including who decides, who must be consulted, and how ties are broken. That agreement is shared openly, so no one has to guess in the middle of a project.",
            "Every decision needs one named owner.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Name one owner for each type of decision\n→ Agree who is consulted and who is informed\n→ Share the decision rights openly with the team\n→ Settle ownership questions before the decision arrives",
            "Have you ever been given two opposite answers to the same question?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-governance-q1", question: "You are the BA and you are asked to set up governance for a small payments project. What do you put in place, and how do you keep it light?", answer: [
            "I keep governance simple and I agree it before the work starts. I start by naming who approves requirements and who approves changes. I make sure each type of decision has one owner, so the team never has to guess. I agree a simple change route. Anyone can raise a change, I check the impact on scope and timing, the owner decides, and I record the outcome. I plan Reviews with named reviewers and enough time, and I give reviewers a clear focus so the check is real. I agree how decisions will be recorded, in a place everyone can see. I scale the effort to risk. A small, low risk change needs a short note and one reviewer. A change to payment rules or settlement needs more care and more eyes. I share the agreements in plain terms and I revisit them if the project grows. Good governance should help the team move faster with confidence, not slow it down with forms."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-governance-q2", question: "Tell me about a time a change request arrived mid-project. How did you assess it and get a decision?", answer: [
            "I treated the request seriously, even though it sounded small. I asked the requester to describe what was needed and why it mattered. I then checked the impact. I looked at scope, timing, other work that would be affected, and any knock on effects for reporting or exception handling. In payments work, a small rule change can have a wide effect, so I checked with the technology team and the operations team before I formed a view. I prepared a short summary showing the request, the impact, and the options. I took it to the named decision owner, not to a large group, so the choice was clear and owned. The owner approved the change with a trade off. We deferred a lower priority item to protect the date. I recorded the decision, the trade off, and the reason, and I shared it with the team. I updated the requirements and the plan in the same day, so no one worked from the old version. A clear route made a difficult choice feel fair and controlled."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-governance-q3", question: "A stakeholder keeps raising changes directly with the delivery team, bypassing the agreed governance. How do you handle it?", answer: [
            "I speak to the stakeholder directly, and I focus on the impact rather than the rule. I explain that direct requests, however well meant, can cause rework and confusion, because the team may build something that has not been checked for impact or approved by the owner. I ask what is driving the direct approach. Often the agreed route feels slow, or the stakeholder does not know who owns the decision. I make the route easier and faster. I agree a short change note, a quick impact check, and a clear turnaround time. I also agree that urgent items can be raised verbally, with the record completed the same day. I speak to the delivery team and I agree they will point new requests to the same route, kindly and consistently. I do not blame anyone. I keep the change control process visible, so the stakeholder can see progress and decisions. If the pattern continues, I raise it with the decision owner, with examples of impact. My aim is a route people want to use, because it is fast, fair, and clear."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-governance-q4", question: "You are the BA and a review has approved requirements that you believe contain a serious flaw in a payments control. The approver will not reopen the review. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I do not let the matter drop, and I do not go around the approver in secret. I gather clear evidence first. I write down the flaw in plain terms, I show a worked example of how the control would fail, and I explain the impact in terms of risk, rework, and customer effect. I share this with the approver directly, in a calm and factual way. I ask what evidence would change their view, and I offer to run a short focused Review with a specialist who understands the control. If the approver still will not reopen the review, I escalate through the agreed governance route. I raise it with the person who owns risk for the change, with the same evidence. I record my concern, the evidence, and the decision, so the trail is clear. I also tell the delivery team what is open, so they do not build on a known flaw without understanding the risk. I stay respectful throughout. My duty is to make the risk visible and owned at the right level, even when that conversation is uncomfortable."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-brd-fsd"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-information-management",
        title: "Plan Business Analysis Information Management",
        subtitle: "Keeping analysis information organised and trustworthy",
        kind: .task,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: .planning,
        babokReference: "3.4",
        explanation: [
            "Planning information management means deciding how business analysis information will be organised, stored, kept current, and shared. The business analyst agrees where information lives, how it is named and versioned, who can change it, and how people find the current version. When this is planned well, the team trusts what it reads. When it is not, people work from old copies, duplicate effort, and lose track of open questions.",
            "Backlog Management is part of this planning. A backlog is an ordered list of work items, from large needs down to small, ready items. The analyst helps keep the backlog ordered by value and risk, refined as understanding grows, and free of items that are no longer needed. In a payments programme, for example, the backlog might hold items for new payment types, exception handling, and reporting. Keeping the order honest helps the team work on what matters most first.",
            "Item Tracking is the discipline of recording and following up open items, issues, questions, and decisions. Each item needs an owner, a due date, and a clear status. The analyst plans how items will be raised, tracked, and closed, so nothing important is lost in email threads or meeting notes. A question about a cut off time, a missing rule, or an unresolved decision all deserve the same care: recorded once, visible to all, and followed until closed.",
            "Information management also covers access and retention. Some payments information is sensitive and must only be shared with people who need it. The analyst agrees who can view, edit, and approve information, and how long it will be kept. A simple, agreed approach, used consistently, keeps information trustworthy without turning the BA into a librarian."
        ],
        outputName: "Business analysis information management plan",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Information types", guidance: "What information analysis produces: requirements, models, decisions, questions, and working notes."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Storage and access", guidance: "Where each type lives, who can read it, who can change it, and where the single shared copy sits."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Naming and versioning", guidance: "How items are named, numbered, and versioned, so two versions are never confused."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Traceability approach", guidance: "How items link to each other, from need through requirement to test."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Retention and archiving", guidance: "How long information is kept, what is archived, and what is deleted.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Start by listing what analysis actually produces, then decide where each type lives. The rule that prevents most confusion is one shared record: the current version lives in one agreed place, and personal copies are working notes, not the record. Write down who can read and who can change each type, because access decided by habit becomes access decided by accident.",
            "Make naming and versioning boring and consistent. Give every requirement a stable identifier that follows it through every stage, and version every shared document so a reviewer can tell whether they read the current draft. State how long information is kept and what happens at the end of a change, so the record survives the people who made it."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: information plan for a direct debit change",
        workedExample: [
            "Information types: mandate rules, process models, requirements, test scenarios, and decision notes. Storage: requirements and models live in the shared requirements tool. Decision notes live in the project record. No requirement lives only in a personal file or an email thread.",
            "Naming and versioning: requirements carry identifiers such as DD-001, DD-002, and every identifier stays with its requirement for life. Shared documents carry a version number and a date, and reviewers are told which version they reviewed. Traceability: each requirement links to the need it serves and to the test scenarios that check it.",
            "Retention: the requirements pack, the decision notes, and the test evidence are kept for audit after release. Working notes are archived at release and deleted only under the agreed retention rule. Access: the whole delivery team can read; only the analyst and the product owner can change approved requirements."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Agree one home for analysis information, with clear naming and versioning.",
            "Keep the backlog ordered by value and risk, and refine it as you learn.",
            "Track every open item with an owner, a due date, and a visible status.",
            "Agree who can view, edit, and approve information, especially sensitive material."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-12", title: "A backlog with no order at all", takeaway: "Keep the backlog ordered, refined, and free of duplicates.", story: [
            "A payments backlog held over a hundred items.\nNo order had been agreed.\nThe team picked items that looked easy.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ High value items sat untouched for months\n→ Easy, low value items were built first\n→ Stakeholders could not see when their need would arrive\n→ Duplicate items appeared under different titles\n→ Planning meetings became arguments about priority",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will work through the backlog in order.\"",
            "So the team stayed busy while the most important work waited. Stakeholders lost faith in the order, and each group lobbied for its own items outside the planning process.",
            "Why?",
            "Backlog Management is about order, not just listing. An ordered backlog shows what matters most, what is ready, and what can wait. The BA helps stakeholders agree the order using value, risk, and dependencies, and keeps refining items so the top of the backlog is always clear. Without that discipline, a backlog becomes a store cupboard, and the team works on what is visible rather than what is valuable.",
            "A backlog without order is a list, not a plan.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Order the backlog by value, risk, and dependencies\n→ Refine the top items so they are clear and ready\n→ Remove duplicates and items that are no longer needed\n→ Review the order regularly with stakeholders",
            "Have you ever worked from a backlog where no one knew what mattered most?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-13", title: "The open question lost in email", takeaway: "Track open questions with an owner and a date until they close.", story: [
            "A question about a payments validation rule was raised in email.\nNo one owned the answer.\nThe thread went quiet after three days.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The question was buried in a long thread\n→ Two people assumed the other would answer\n→ Build started with the rule still unclear\n→ Testing exposed the gap six weeks later\n→ The fix required rework across three areas",
            "The request said:\n\"Can someone confirm the validation rule?\"",
            "So a simple question became an expensive defect. The information existed in the organisation, but no one had tracked the question to an answer, and the team built on an assumption instead.",
            "Why?",
            "Item Tracking turns questions and issues into visible work with an owner and a date. When an item is recorded once, in a shared place, everyone can see it is open and who is chasing it. Email alone is a poor tracker, because threads fork, people drop off, and items sink. The BA plans a simple tracking habit and follows items until they close.",
            "An untracked question will resurface as a defect.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Record every open question in one shared place\n→ Give each item an owner and a due date\n→ Review open items at a regular rhythm\n→ Close items only when the answer is confirmed",
            "What would you do if a key question had no owner and no answer?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-14", title: "Four versions of the same requirements", takeaway: "Keep one current version in one agreed place.", story: [
            "Requirements were shared as email attachments.\nEach reviewer saved a local copy.\nFour versions circulated within a week.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ One version held the latest validation rules\n→ Another held the latest reporting needs\n→ The delivery team built from an old version\n→ A review compared two different documents\n→ No one could say which version was current",
            "The plan said:\n\"Requirements will be shared with the team.\"",
            "So the team argued about documents instead of discussing the change. Time was spent merging versions and rechecking work, and trust in the requirements fell with each new copy.",
            "Why?",
            "Information management needs one home for the current version. When the current version lives in one agreed place, with clear naming and a change history, everyone reads the same material. Email attachments create copies that drift apart. The BA agrees the home, the naming, and who can edit, and makes the current version easy to find.",
            "One current version, in one known place, prevents most version confusion.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Agree one home for current analysis information\n→ Use clear naming and a simple change history\n→ Share links, not attachments, wherever possible\n→ Agree who can edit and who can approve changes",
            "Have you ever built or reviewed from the wrong version of a document?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-15", title: "Sensitive examples shared too widely", takeaway: "Control access to analysis information, and anonymise examples for wide sharing.", story: [
            "The BA used real payment examples in a workshop pack.\nThe pack was shared with a wide distribution list.\nIt contained client details that should have stayed restricted.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Real examples made the workshop easy to follow\n→ The pack was forwarded beyond the workshop group\n→ A reviewer flagged the client details\n→ The pack had to be recalled and reissued\n→ The team lost a week while examples were remade",
            "The request said:\n\"Please share the workshop pack widely.\"",
            "So a helpful choice created a control problem. The examples were realistic, but the sharing was not controlled, and the recall damaged confidence in how information was handled.",
            "Why?",
            "Information management includes access control. The BA must think about who needs to see material, what is sensitive, and how examples are handled. Realistic examples help learning, but they must be anonymised or made up when they leave a restricted group. Agreeing access rules early protects clients, protects the team, and keeps workshops useful without creating risk.",
            "Helpful examples must still respect access and privacy.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Agree who can view, edit, and share analysis material\n→ Use anonymised or fictional examples for wide sharing\n→ Check packs for sensitive detail before distribution\n→ Recall and fix quickly if sensitive material is shared",
            "What would you do if you spotted sensitive detail in a pack you had shared?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-information-management-q1", question: "You are the BA and you find the team is working from three different versions of the requirements. What do you do first, and how do you prevent it happening again?", answer: [
            "I stop the confusion first, and I fix the system second. I gather the versions, I compare them, and I identify which content is latest in each area. I agree one current version with the team and the key stakeholders, and I make it clear which version is now the single source. I tell everyone in plain terms where it lives and that older copies should not be used. I then agree the information management approach. We choose one home for the current version, with clear naming and a short change history. I share links rather than attachments, so people always open the current copy. I agree who can edit and who approves changes, so edits do not happen in private copies. I also agree a simple habit for updates. When a change is agreed, I update the single version the same day and I tell the team what changed. That habit prevents drift. A single current version saves time, prevents rework, and rebuilds trust in the analysis."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-information-management-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you had to bring order to a messy backlog. How did you approach it?", answer: [
            "I started by making the whole backlog visible in one place, so we could see the scale of the problem. I removed duplicates and items that were no longer needed, and I checked with stakeholders before I removed anything that might still matter. I then worked with stakeholders to agree the order. I used Backlog Management principles, ordering by value, risk, and dependencies, rather than by who shouted loudest. In payments work, items that affect settlement, controls, or exception handling often deserve early attention, even if they are harder. I refined the top items so each one was clear, small enough to start, and testable. I left lower items lighter, because they would change before their turn came. I agreed a regular review rhythm, so the order stayed honest as needs changed. I also made the order visible, so stakeholders could see when their item was likely to arrive. The backlog became a plan the team trusted, not a store cupboard of hopes."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-information-management-q3", question: "A stakeholder asks you to share a pack containing real client payment examples with a wide group. How do you respond?", answer: [
            "I explain the risk clearly and I offer a safe alternative. I do not refuse help, and I do not share sensitive material widely. I check what the group actually needs. Usually they need to understand the pattern, the rule, or the exception, not the real client detail. I offer to remake the examples using fictional data that shows the same pattern. That keeps the learning value without the risk. I explain the access rules in plain terms, and I agree who should see the original material. I also check the pack for any other sensitive detail before it is shared. If the stakeholder insists, I raise it with the person who owns the information, with a clear note of the risk. I record the decision and the sharing list. In payments work, trust depends on careful handling of client information. A BA who protects that information, while still helping people learn, earns trust from both the business and the client facing teams."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-plan-business-analysis-information-management-q4", question: "You are the BA on a large programme with many teams producing analysis information in different formats and places. How do you bring consistency without slowing teams down?", answer: [
            "I agree a small set of shared habits, not a heavy standard that every team must follow in full. I start by learning how each team works and what already works well. I then agree the minimum that must be consistent: one home for current information, clear naming, a shared way to track open items, and a common way to show status. I use Item Tracking across teams, so open questions and decisions are visible in one place, even if the detail lives with each team. I agree templates only where they add value, such as for decisions and change notes. I keep them short. I hold a brief regular check in where teams share what changed and what is open. I do not force every team into the same document style, because that slows delivery and breeds resistance. I focus on findability, currency, and traceability. If a reviewer can find the current version, see what changed, and follow an item to its answer, the system works. Consistency should serve delivery, not replace it."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-brd-fsd"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-identify-business-analysis-performance-improvements",
        title: "Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements",
        subtitle: "Learning what works, and getting better at the work",
        kind: .task,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: .planning,
        babokReference: "3.5",
        explanation: [
            "Identifying performance improvements means looking honestly at how business analysis work is going, finding what helps and what hinders, and making small, practical changes. The business analyst does not wait until the end of a project to reflect. Improvement happens during the work, while there is still time to change course. It starts with noticing patterns: reviews that always run late, workshops that miss key voices, or estimates that always slip in the same place.",
            "Metrics and Key Performance Indicators help the analyst see those patterns clearly. A metric is a simple measure, such as how many review rounds were needed, how long open items stayed open, or how often rework followed a decision. The analyst chooses a small number of measures that reflect outcomes, not just activity. Counting pages produced says little. Counting rework avoided, decisions that held, or items closed on time says far more about whether analysis is working.",
            "Lessons Learned sessions turn experience into shared learning. The analyst helps the team record what worked, what did not, and what will change next time. The best lessons are specific and owned. A lesson that says workshops need better preparation is vague, while a lesson that says every workshop needs an agenda, a named decider, and a pre-read sent two days early is something the team can actually do. Reviews of past work feed the same learning, when they are honest and focused on improvement rather than blame.",
            "Improvement only counts if it changes behaviour. The analyst agrees one or two changes, tries them, and checks whether they helped. Small, tested improvements build over time into a stronger practice. In payments work, where errors can be costly, that steady learning protects both the team and the clients it serves."
        ],
        outputName: "Business analysis performance improvement note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "What was measured", guidance: "The measures used: rework, review cycles, defect causes, and stakeholder feedback."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Findings", guidance: "What the measures show, stated as evidence, with examples."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Root causes", guidance: "Why the pattern happens, separated from who happened to be involved."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Improvements", guidance: "The changes to try, each with an owner and a date to check the effect."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Follow up", guidance: "How the effect is judged, and when the improvement is kept, changed, or dropped.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Measure the work, not the worker. Useful measures include how often requirements change after approval, how many review rounds a pack needs, and what causes defects found in testing. Collect the evidence first and write the finding as a pattern with examples, before anyone proposes a fix.",
            "Separate the cause from the person. If reviews keep finding the same gap, the cause is usually a missing check in the approach, not one analyst. Write each improvement as a small change with an owner and a date to judge the effect, and review it honestly: keep what helped, change what did not, and drop what added effort without value."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: improvement note after a payments release",
        workedExample: [
            "What was measured: over one release, eleven requirements changed after approval, and six of the eleven concerned exception handling. Testing found four defects, and three traced back to exception paths that were never specified. Review rounds averaged three per pack.",
            "Findings and causes: exception paths were explored late, after the happy path was already specified. The approach had no explicit step for exception handling, so it depended on individual memory. The improvement is to add an exception review to the approach for every payment change, with a checklist of reject, return, timeout, and duplicate cases.",
            "Follow up: the owner is the lead analyst, and the effect is judged after the next two releases by counting post approval changes to exception requirements and defects traced to exception paths. If the count does not fall, the checklist is revised rather than abandoned without evidence."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Review performance during the work, not only at the end.",
            "Choose a small number of measures that reflect outcomes, not just activity.",
            "Record Lessons Learned that are specific, owned, and actionable.",
            "Try one or two changes at a time, and check whether they helped."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-16", title: "Measuring pages, missing the problems", takeaway: "Measure outcomes like held decisions and rework, not just output volume.", story: [
            "The BA team reported progress by pages produced.\nEach month the page count rose.\nRework and late changes rose too.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Reports celebrated long requirements packs\n→ No one measured review rounds or rework\n→ Decisions were revisited again and again\n→ Stakeholders grew frustrated despite the page counts\n→ The team felt busy but not effective",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will measure analysis output each month.\"",
            "So the team optimised for the wrong thing. Producing more pages looked like progress, while the real problems, unclear decisions and late discovery, went unmeasured and unchanged.",
            "Why?",
            "Metrics and Key Performance Indicators must reflect outcomes, not just activity. Pages, workshops held, and hours spent describe effort. They do not show whether analysis helped the team build the right thing with less rework. Better measures include decisions that held, open items closed on time, and rework caused by unclear analysis. A small set of honest measures helps the team improve. A large set of flattering measures hides the need to change.",
            "Measure what matters, not what is easy to count.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Choose a small number of outcome focused measures\n→ Track decisions that held and rework that followed\n→ Review measures regularly and act on what they show\n→ Drop measures that do not lead to better work",
            "Have you ever hit a target while the real work got worse?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-17", title: "The same workshop mistake, third time", takeaway: "Record specific, owned lessons, and check that the agreed change happens.", story: [
            "A workshop failed because no agenda was sent.\nThe same failure happened on the next project.\nThen it happened again on the third.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Each workshop started with confusion about purpose\n→ Key people arrived unprepared\n→ Decisions were deferred to a follow up\n→ No lesson was recorded after any failure\n→ Each new BA repeated the same approach",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will learn from experience.\"",
            "So experience was gained and then lost. Without a recorded lesson and an agreed change, each project paid the same price for the same avoidable mistake.",
            "Why?",
            "Lessons Learned only help when they are recorded, specific, and acted on. A vague note that preparation matters changes nothing. A specific agreement, that every workshop needs an agenda, a named decider, and a pre-read sent two days early, gives the next BA a clear habit to follow. The BA helps the team capture lessons promptly, assign an owner, and check that the change actually happens.",
            "A lesson not recorded and not acted on is not learned.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Capture lessons promptly, while detail is fresh\n→ Make each lesson specific and owned\n→ Agree the exact change that will happen next time\n→ Check that the change was used, not just agreed",
            "What would you do if the same mistake happened three times in a row?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-PM-18", title: "Feedback the BA asked for, then used", takeaway: "Ask for feedback, act on it visibly, and check that it helped.", story: [
            "A BA asked stakeholders for honest feedback.\nThe feedback was direct and uncomfortable.\nThe BA thanked them and changed the approach.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Stakeholders said review packs arrived too late\n→ They said workshops ran long with no decisions\n→ The BA shortened packs and set decision goals\n→ Reviews arrived two days earlier\n→ Stakeholders said the next cycle felt clearer",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Your packs are thorough, but they arrive too late to read.\"",
            "So a short feedback conversation led to practical change. The BA did not defend the old approach. The BA tested a new rhythm, checked whether it helped, and kept what worked.",
            "Why?",
            "Performance improvement starts with honest feedback and a willingness to act on it. Stakeholders often know what is not working before the BA does. Asking for feedback, listening without defensiveness, and making one or two visible changes builds trust and improves the work faster than any self review alone. Improvement is a habit, not a single event at the end of a project.",
            "Feedback acted on builds more trust than feedback merely collected.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Ask for feedback during the work, not only at the end\n→ Listen for patterns, not just single comments\n→ Make one or two visible changes and test them\n→ Tell stakeholders what changed because of their input",
            "Have you ever changed how you work because of direct feedback?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-identify-business-analysis-performance-improvements-q1", question: "You are the BA and you want to know if your analysis work is effective. What measures do you use, and why?", answer: [
            "I use a small number of measures that show outcomes, not just activity. I do not count pages or hours, because those show effort, not value. I look at how many decisions held without being revisited, how long open items stayed open, and how much rework followed unclear analysis. I also look at review feedback. If reviews find the same gaps again and again, that tells me where my preparation needs to improve. I use Metrics and Key Performance Indicators as a learning tool, not as a scorecard to impress others. I review the measures with the team at a regular rhythm, and I ask what they tell us about the work. If a measure does not lead to a better decision or a better habit, I drop it. In payments work, I pay close attention to late discovery, because a gap found in testing costs far more than a gap found in a workshop. Honest, simple measures help me improve steadily, without turning analysis into a numbers game."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-identify-business-analysis-performance-improvements-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you learned a lesson from a project and applied it on the next one. What changed?", answer: [
            "On one project, my workshops kept running long and ending without decisions. I had sent no agenda and no pre-read, so people arrived unprepared and the discussion wandered. At the end of the project, I recorded this as a Lessons Learned item, but I made it specific. I agreed that every workshop I ran would have an agenda, a named decider, and a short pre-read sent two days early. I also agreed a clear decision goal for each session. On the next project, I applied that habit from the first workshop. I sent a one page pre-read with the decision we needed to make. I kept the session focused on that decision. I recorded the outcome the same day. Stakeholders noticed the difference quickly. Sessions were shorter, decisions held, and fewer follow ups were needed. I checked the change by asking for feedback after the first two workshops. The lesson stuck because it was specific, owned, and tested, not just written down and forgotten."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-identify-business-analysis-performance-improvements-q3", question: "A stakeholder gives you critical feedback about your analysis style in front of the team. How do you respond, and what do you do afterwards?", answer: [
            "I stay calm, I listen fully, and I thank them for raising it. I do not defend myself in the moment, and I do not dismiss the feedback because of how it was delivered. I ask a short clarifying question, so I understand the specific behaviour they mean. I note the point and I say I will reflect on it and follow up. Afterwards, I speak to the stakeholder privately. I ask for examples and I ask what good would look like from their view. I look for patterns by checking whether others have raised the same point. If the feedback is fair, I make a visible change. For example, if my packs arrive too late, I change my review rhythm and I tell stakeholders what will change. I use the Review of my own work as a chance to improve, not as a judgement on my worth. I follow up after a few weeks to check whether the change helped. Handling criticism with grace, and acting on it, builds far more trust than being defensive or ignoring it."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-identify-business-analysis-performance-improvements-q4", question: "You are the BA and the team resists any discussion of performance improvement, saying there is no time to reflect. How do you introduce improvement without adding burden?", answer: [
            "I do not add a heavy process or a long meeting. I start with tiny, useful habits that fit the work already happening. I add five minutes at the end of an existing meeting to ask two questions. What helped this week, and what slowed us down? I record one specific action, with an owner, and I check it next time. I use existing evidence rather than new forms. I look at Item Tracking data, review comments, and rework patterns, and I share one clear insight at a time. I choose improvements that save time, such as earlier pre-reads or clearer decision notes, so the team feels the benefit quickly. I celebrate small wins openly, so improvement feels rewarding rather than critical. I also model the habit myself. I share one thing I will change in my own work, and I report back on whether it helped. Over time, the team sees that reflection takes minutes and saves hours. Improvement becomes part of how we work, not an extra task competing for time."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-prepare-for-elicitation",
        title: "Prepare for Elicitation",
        subtitle: "Get Ready Before You Ask Anything",
        kind: .task,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: .elicitation,
        babokReference: "4.1",
        explanation: [
            "Preparation is the work a business analyst does before any questions are asked. It sets a clear purpose for the session, so everyone knows what decision or understanding is needed by the end. It also identifies who must take part, what material should be read in advance, and how the results will be captured. Without this groundwork, sessions drift, key voices are missing, and the same topics return again and again.",
            "A prepared analyst chooses the right approach for the topic and the people involved. Interviews suit sensitive or detailed topics, while Workshops suit shared decisions that need many views in one room. A short Survey or Questionnaire can gather a broad view before a session, so face to face time is spent on the hard questions. The choice is never about habit, it is about what will produce the clearest and most honest information.",
            "Good preparation also means doing homework before asking others for their time. Document Analysis of existing process notes, payment flow descriptions, and issue logs often answers basic questions in advance. That leaves the session free for gaps, conflicts, and judgement calls that only people can resolve. A short agenda, shared early with background material, helps stakeholders arrive ready to contribute rather than hearing the topic for the first time.",
            "Consider a bank preparing to change how corporate payment instructions are checked before release. If the analyst reads the current checking steps first and invites both the payments team and the control team, the session can focus on where checks add value and where they cause delay. If no preparation happens, the session becomes a general discussion, the control view is missed, and rework follows later. Preparation does not guarantee agreement, but it gives agreement a fair chance."
        ],
        outputName: "Elicitation preparation pack",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Purpose and outcome", guidance: "The decision or understanding needed by the end, written as one sentence."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Participants", guidance: "Who must take part, and what each person brings."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Technique choice", guidance: "Which technique fits the topic and the people, and why."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Pre-reading", guidance: "What participants read in advance, and what question each item answers."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Capture plan", guidance: "How results are recorded during the session, and who owns the record."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Questions", guidance: "The opening questions, written in advance, with follow ups for vague answers.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write the purpose as the decision or understanding needed by the end of the session. If you cannot write that sentence, the session is not ready. Then choose participants by what they know, not by who is available, and choose the technique by the topic and the people: interviews for sensitive or detailed topics, workshops for shared decisions, and a short survey before a session when a broad view would help.",
            "Prepare the questions in advance, starting with open questions about how work happens today. Plan the capture before the session starts: who records, in what form, and how the record is confirmed afterwards. Send pre-reading early enough to be read, and keep it short: one question answered per item, or it will not be read at all."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: preparation pack for an exception handling workshop",
        workedExample: [
            "Purpose: agree the list of exception types the new payment hub must handle, and the owner of each type. Participants: two payment operations officers who work exceptions daily, the operations lead who owns the queue, and one technology representative who can answer feasibility questions on the spot.",
            "Technique: a workshop, because the outcome is a shared decision that needs several views in one room. Pre-reading: the current exception list, with one question attached: which exceptions on this list do you still see, and which are missing? Capture: the analyst records decisions on a visible board during the session, and the operations lead confirms the record before the session closes.",
            "Opening questions: walk me through the last exception you handled from start to finish. What happens when an exception arrives after cut off? Which exception takes the longest to resolve, and why? Follow up for any vague answer: can you show me an example from this week?"
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Define the purpose and the outcome needed before inviting anyone.",
            "Invite the people who own the decision and the people who do the work.",
            "Read existing material first, so sessions focus on gaps and choices.",
            "Share an agenda and background notes early, and plan how results will be recorded."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-01", title: "The Workshop With No Agenda", takeaway: "Preparation turns a meeting into a decision.", story: [
            "A workshop was booked to agree payment exception handling.\nInvites went out with only a title and a time.\nNo agenda or background material was attached.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Attendees arrived with different ideas about the purpose\n→ The first hour was spent explaining the background\n→ Two views on exception handling were debated without facts\n→ The decision owner had not been invited\n→ The group agreed to meet again with no outcome recorded",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will agree the exception process in one workshop.\"",
            "So the team left with notes that no one owned, and the payments team kept using the old workaround while waiting for a second session. The delay pushed the design work back, and confidence in the process fell because the meeting had promised a decision it could not deliver.",
            "Why?",
            "A workshop is a tool for shared decisions, not a first introduction to a topic. When people arrive unprepared and the decision owner is absent, discussion replaces decision making. Preparation would have named the decision, shared the background, and confirmed the right people were present. The failure was not the topic, it was the setup.",
            "A session without a purpose and the right people cannot produce a decision.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ State the decision or outcome in the invite itself\n→ Share a short agenda and background material in advance\n→ Confirm the decision owner will attend before the session starts\n→ Record who owns each follow up action before the session closes",
            "Have you ever sat in a meeting that ended without knowing what had been decided?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-02", title: "The Wrong Room For Reconciliation", takeaway: "The right room matters more than a senior room.", story: [
            "Reconciliation breaks were rising on corporate payments.\nA session was called to understand why breaks happened.\nOnly senior managers were invited to the session.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Managers described the process as documented\n→ The daily matching work was never discussed in detail\n→ The team who cleared breaks each morning was absent\n→ Causes were guessed from summary reports\n→ Actions were assigned to people who did not do the work",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"The process is clear, the team just needs to follow it.\"",
            "So the actions were completed on paper while the breaks kept appearing each morning. The operations team continued to fix the same issues by hand, and the managers were surprised when the next report showed no improvement.",
            "Why?",
            "The people who describe a process and the people who run it often see different realities. Managers see the intended flow, while the operations team sees the exceptions, the workarounds, and the points where the flow breaks. Elicitation must include both views, or the analysis will solve a tidy version of the problem that does not exist.",
            "Invite the people who live the process, not only the people who describe it.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ List who does the work daily, not only who manages it\n→ Invite a mix of managers and frontline staff to the session\n→ Ask how exceptions are handled, not only how the normal flow works\n→ Check proposed actions with the people who must carry them out",
            "What would you do if the only people in the room had never done the task themselves?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-03", title: "Unprepared Questions, Vague Answers", takeaway: "Prepared questions earn specific answers.", story: [
            "An interview was booked with a corporate client contact.\nThe topic was how payment instructions were approved.\nThe analyst arrived with no written questions.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The first questions were broad and open ended\n→ Answers stayed general and polite\n→ The analyst asked follow ups without a clear thread\n→ Approval limits and cut off times were never discussed\n→ The interview ended with warm feelings and thin notes",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Our approval process works well enough, I think.\"",
            "So the analyst wrote up a summary that sounded positive but answered none of the design questions. A second interview had to be booked, and the client contact was less willing to give time a second time.",
            "Why?",
            "Interviews reward preparation more than charm. Without planned questions, the conversation follows whatever comes to mind, and the important details stay hidden. Prepared questions give the session structure, while still leaving room to follow an interesting answer. The analyst must know what must be learned before the first question is asked.",
            "Vague questions produce vague answers, however friendly the conversation feels.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Write your must answer questions before the interview\n→ Start broad, then move to specifics like limits and timings\n→ Prepare prompts for examples, not only for opinions\n→ Leave time at the end to check what you heard",
            "Have you ever left an interview realising you forgot to ask the most important question?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-04", title: "Reading The Files First", takeaway: "Read first, then ask what only people can answer.", story: [
            "A change to payment status messages was being planned.\nThe analyst spent a day reading before booking any session.\nExisting flow notes and issue logs were reviewed in full.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Document Analysis answered most basic questions early\n→ A short list of genuine gaps was drawn up\n→ Only two focused sessions were booked, not five\n→ Stakeholders received the gap list in advance\n→ Both sessions stayed on the open questions only",
            "The request said:\n\"Please confirm how status updates should work.\"",
            "So the sessions were short and useful, and stakeholders praised the analyst for respecting their time. The design team received clear answers quickly, and the change moved forward without repeated rounds of basic questions.",
            "Why?",
            "Reading first is a form of respect for stakeholder time and a shortcut to better questions. When basic facts are already known, the conversation can focus on judgement, trade offs, and exceptions. Preparation does not remove the need to talk to people, it makes those talks count.",
            "Homework before the session makes the session worth attending.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Read existing notes, flows, and logs before inviting anyone\n→ Turn what you read into a short list of open gaps\n→ Share the gap list so stakeholders can prepare too\n→ Book fewer, sharper sessions instead of many general ones",
            "What would you read first before asking busy people for their time?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-prepare-for-elicitation-q1", question: "You are the BA and you must prepare for your first elicitation session on a new corporate payments onboarding process. How do you prepare?", answer: [
            "I start by getting clear on the purpose before I invite anyone. I ask what decision or understanding must come out of the session, and what will happen if we do not get it. Then I work out who must be there, and I mean both the people who own the decision and the people who do the onboarding work every day. I read what already exists first, using Document Analysis on process notes, forms, and issue logs, so I do not waste the session on basic facts. From that reading I build a short list of gaps and questions, and I choose the right approach, such as Interviews for sensitive detail or a Workshop for a shared decision. I share a short agenda and background notes early, so people arrive ready. I also plan how I will capture results, who will take notes, and how we will confirm what was agreed. Good preparation is quiet work, but it is what makes the session itself short, focused, and useful."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-prepare-for-elicitation-q2", question: "A stakeholder says, just send me your questions by email, there is no need for a meeting. How do you respond and prepare?", answer: [
            "I thank them for the offer and I explain what email can and cannot do well. Email is fine for facts and for a short Survey or Questionnaire, but it is poor for trade offs, unclear answers, and follow up questions. I would still prepare properly, so I send a short, focused set of questions with background context, and I ask for examples rather than general opinions. I also ask whether a short call would help for the two or three questions where a written answer is likely to need discussion. I prepare by reading the existing material first, so my questions are specific and I do not ask for information that is already documented. I set a clear deadline and I explain what decision the answers will support, so the stakeholder sees why their input matters. If the written answers leave gaps, I go back with a short summary and a request for a brief discussion on the open points only. My aim is to respect their time while still getting answers I can rely on."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-prepare-for-elicitation-q3", question: "You are the BA and a key approver is unavailable for the elicitation workshop you have planned. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I do not go ahead and pretend the workshop can decide without them. First I check what the workshop must decide and whether that decision truly needs this approver, or whether part of the session can proceed and the decision can be confirmed later. If the approver is essential, I reschedule or I split the work, using the session to gather views and options, and booking a short decision check with the approver afterwards. I prepare a clear briefing pack so the approver can give input in advance, even if they cannot attend, and I make sure their concerns are represented fairly in the room. I also confirm attendance early in future, rather than discovering an absence on the day. I tell the other attendees honestly what can and cannot be decided, so no one leaves thinking an agreement is final when it is not. The key point is that a decision made without its owner is not a decision, it is a hope, and I would rather be honest about that than redo the work later."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-prepare-for-elicitation-q4", question: "You have only one hour with the operations team to understand how payment exceptions are handled. How do you make that hour count?", answer: [
            "I treat that hour as precious, so most of my work happens before it. I use Document Analysis to learn the documented exception flow, and I review recent issue logs to see which exceptions happen most often. I prepare a short agenda focused on the exceptions themselves, not on a general tour of the process. I ask for real examples, including one straightforward case and one difficult case, because examples reveal the true steps, checks, and workarounds. During the hour I keep the group on the gaps I could not answer from documents, and I use simple prompts like, what happens next, and who decides that. I capture results live on a shared screen where possible, so the team can correct me in the moment. I close by reading back the key points and agreeing what I will send for confirmation. One focused hour, well prepared, can teach more than three unfocused hours."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-stakeholder-management"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-conduct-elicitation",
        title: "Conduct Elicitation",
        subtitle: "Listen, Ask, And Observe With Purpose",
        kind: .task,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: .elicitation,
        babokReference: "4.2",
        explanation: [
            "Conducting elicitation is the live work of drawing information out of people and evidence. It is more than asking questions, it is listening closely, noticing what is not said, and following up when an answer is vague or incomplete. The analyst creates a space where people feel safe to share problems as well as successes. What is learned here shapes every requirement, design choice, and test that follows.",
            "Different techniques suit different moments. Brainstorming opens up many ideas quickly, while Focus Groups reveal how a group of users thinks and where their views differ. Observation shows what people actually do, which can differ sharply from what they describe in a meeting. Interviews allow depth on a sensitive topic, and Workshops allow a group to build a shared view together in real time.",
            "Good facilitation keeps the session fair and productive. The analyst manages dominant voices, invites quieter people to speak, and pauses when the group rushes past a disagreement. Collaborative Games and simple Prototyping can help here, because they give people something concrete to react to instead of abstract questions. The goal is honest, usable information, not a polite session that avoids the hard parts.",
            "Consider a bank studying how payment investigations are handled when a transfer goes missing. In a meeting, the process sounds neat and linear. When the analyst spends time observing the investigations team, the real picture appears, with repeated checks, phone calls, and manual searches across several screens. That observed truth leads to better requirements than any tidy description could, because it reflects the work as it truly happens."
        ],
        outputName: "Elicitation session record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Session facts", guidance: "Date, participants, purpose, and the technique used."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "What was said", guidance: "The information gathered, recorded as facts and views, kept separate."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Decisions", guidance: "What was agreed, and by whom."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Open questions", guidance: "What is still unknown, with an owner and a date for each."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Conflicts", guidance: "Where views differed, recorded faithfully, not smoothed over.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Listen more than you talk, and ask open questions about how work happens today. Follow up whenever an answer is vague: ask for an example, a recent case, or the exact step. Record facts and views separately, because a statement of opinion recorded as fact will mislead everyone who reads it later.",
            "Keep the session on its purpose without shutting down useful detours. When a new topic matters, record it as a follow up rather than letting it take over. Before the session closes, read back the decisions and the open questions, so participants leave with the same understanding they agreed in the room."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: record of an interview about payment repairs",
        workedExample: [
            "Session facts: interview with a payment operations officer, purpose to understand how repair cases are worked today. What was said: repairs arrive in a shared queue, the officer checks the payment status first, then contacts the sending bank when the status is unclear. Fact: the queue has no age ordering. View: the officer believes age ordering would cut handling time.",
            "Decisions: the repair queue needs age ordering and a status summary per case. Open questions: who owns repairs that arrive after cut off, owned by the operations lead, due within three working days. Conflicts: none in this session. A second officer had earlier said repairs are worked in arrival order; that difference is recorded as an open check, not resolved by assumption."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Listen for gaps and follow up, do not accept the first vague answer.",
            "Match the technique to the moment, and mix techniques when one is not enough.",
            "Make space for quiet voices and slow down when views conflict.",
            "Capture what you hear as you go, so nothing depends on memory alone."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-05", title: "Watching The Work Told The Truth", takeaway: "Observation reveals the work as it truly happens.", story: [
            "The investigations team described their process as simple.\nThree steps were listed in the meeting, in neat order.\nThe analyst asked to watch the work for one morning.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The analyst sat beside the team during live cases\n→ Each case needed checks across several screens\n→ Phone calls and manual searches filled the gaps\n→ Steps were repeated when information was missing\n→ The neat three steps took far longer in practice",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"It is straightforward, we just follow the three steps.\"",
            "So the requirements were rewritten to reflect the real work, including the repeated checks and the need for information in one place. The design team stopped building for a simple flow that did not exist, and the investigations team felt heard for the first time.",
            "Why?",
            "People often describe work as they believe it should happen, not as it does happen. Memory smooths over the messy parts, and pride can hide workarounds. Observation closes that gap because it shows behaviour, not just belief. It is one of the most honest techniques an analyst has, when used with respect and permission.",
            "What people do teaches more than what people say they do.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Ask permission, then observe real work in its real setting\n→ Note exceptions and workarounds, not only the normal path\n→ Ask gentle questions about what you see, without judging\n→ Check your observations with the team before writing them up",
            "Have you ever discovered that the real process looked nothing like the described one?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-06", title: "A Game That Unlocked Honest Ideas", takeaway: "A better format can unlock ideas discussion cannot.", story: [
            "A team needed fresh ideas for reducing payment delays.\nTwo open discussions had produced only polite agreement.\nThe analyst tried a different approach in the third session.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Brainstorming rules were set, no idea judged at first\n→ A Collaborative Game asked pairs to rank delay causes\n→ Quiet team members wrote ideas on cards first\n→ Cards were grouped together on a shared board\n→ Three causes stood out that no one had voiced before",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"We have discussed this before, nothing new will come up.\"",
            "So the group left with a ranked list of causes and a shared sense of ownership. The most useful idea came from a quiet team member whose card had never been spoken aloud in earlier meetings. The session felt different because the format changed who could contribute and how.",
            "Why?",
            "Open discussion often favours the loudest voices and the safest ideas. People hold back when they fear judgement or when senior staff speak first. Games and structured Brainstorming lower that barrier by giving everyone the same way in, often in writing before speaking. The technique does not create ideas, it creates the conditions for ideas to surface.",
            "Change the format when the conversation keeps producing the same answers.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Set a no judgement rule for the idea stage of the session\n→ Let people write before they speak, so all voices start equal\n→ Group and rank ideas together, in full view of the group\n→ Thank contributors by name in role terms, and follow up on ideas",
            "What would you try if your next discussion produced only polite agreement?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-07", title: "The Survey That Spoke Up", takeaway: "Ask broadly, then explore deeply.", story: [
            "Frontline staff handled payment queries every day.\nTheir views were rarely sought in change projects.\nA short survey was sent before a Focus Group session.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ A Survey or Questionnaire asked about common query types\n→ Staff answered anonymously and in plain language\n→ Results showed one query type caused most rework\n→ The Focus Group explored that query type in depth\n→ Staff shared examples they had never raised before",
            "The request said:\n\"Tell us what causes the most repeat queries.\"",
            "So the project focused on the one query type that mattered most, instead of spreading effort across many small issues. Frontline staff felt their daily experience had shaped the change, and later sessions were easier to fill because trust had grown.",
            "Why?",
            "Surveys and Focus Groups work well together when one broad view is needed first and depth is needed second. The survey gives a safe, anonymous route for honest views, while the group session adds stories and detail. Used alone, a survey can feel distant, and a group can be dominated by a few voices. Together, they balance breadth and depth.",
            "Breadth first, then depth, gives both the pattern and the story behind it.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Use a short survey to find patterns before meeting people\n→ Keep surveys short, clear, and anonymous where honesty matters\n→ Use Focus Groups to explore the top patterns in detail\n→ Share back what you learned, so contributors see the value",
            "Have you ever learned more from an anonymous survey than from a meeting?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-08", title: "A Sketch Ended The Confusion", takeaway: "A rough sketch can end weeks of wordy debate.", story: [
            "A corporate client asked for a clearer payment screen.\nThree meetings had failed to agree what clearer meant.\nThe analyst brought a simple sketch to the fourth meeting.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ A rough Prototyping sketch showed one possible layout\n→ The client pointed at what was wrong within minutes\n→ Missing fields and unclear labels were named directly\n→ A second sketch was drawn live in the meeting\n→ Agreement was reached on the revised layout",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"I will know it when I see it, I cannot describe it.\"",
            "So the team stopped debating words and started reacting to something visible. The sketch was never meant to be the final design, but it gave the conversation a focus. The agreed layout became the basis for detailed requirements, and the project moved forward.",
            "Why?",
            "Some needs are hard to describe in words, especially when they involve screens, flows, or layouts. A prototype, even a rough sketch, makes the abstract concrete and gives stakeholders something to correct. People who struggle to say what they want can often point at what is wrong. That reaction is valuable elicitation, not a shortcut.",
            "When words fail, show something simple and let people react to it.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Use a rough sketch early, and label it as draft only\n→ Invite corrections, and redraw live where you can\n→ Ask what is missing, what is unclear, and what is extra\n→ Turn agreed sketches into written requirements afterwards",
            "What would you sketch if a stakeholder said they could not describe what they wanted?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-conduct-elicitation-q1", question: "Tell me about a time you ran an interview or workshop and the stakeholder gave only vague answers. What did you do?", answer: [
            "I had a session where every answer stayed general, and I realised my questions were too broad. I paused and changed my approach, asking for a recent real example instead of an opinion. I asked them to walk me through the last time the process happened, step by step, including who was involved and what went wrong. That concrete story gave me far more than the general answers had. I used simple follow ups like, what happened next, and what did you do when that failed. I also used Observation afterwards, watching the work for a short time, to check whether the story matched reality. I captured the examples live and read them back, so the stakeholder could correct me. The lesson I took away was that vague answers often mean vague questions, and examples are the fastest route to specific, useful information. I now always prepare example based prompts before any Interviews or Workshops."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-conduct-elicitation-q2", question: "You are facilitating a workshop and one person is dominating the discussion while others stay silent. How do you handle it?", answer: [
            "I handle it in the room, gently but firmly, because silence from others is lost information. I thank the dominant speaker for their input and I redirect the question to the group, asking for other views before we move on. I change the format, using short written input first, so everyone contributes before anyone speaks. That is where Brainstorming with cards or a short Collaborative Games activity helps, because it gives quieter people an equal way in. I also watch for non verbal cues, and I invite specific roles to speak, such as asking what the operations view is, without putting one person on the spot. If the pattern continues, I speak to the dominant person during a break and ask for their help in making space. After the session, I follow up with quieter stakeholders individually, so their views are still captured. Good facilitation is about balance, and the analyst owns that balance."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-conduct-elicitation-q3", question: "Two stakeholders give you conflicting accounts of how a payments process works. How do you elicit the truth?", answer: [
            "I do not choose a side in the moment, and I do not average the two accounts into something neither recognises. I treat the conflict as useful information, because it usually means the process differs by case type, by team, or by time of day. I ask each stakeholder for a recent example, and I compare the steps, the inputs, and the decisions in each example. I use Document Analysis to check what the documented process says, and I use Observation to see what actually happens on live cases. If I can, I bring both stakeholders together with the evidence and I walk through one case end to end, asking where their accounts diverge and why. I record both views honestly, and I note the conflict as an open question until evidence resolves it. My job is to surface the difference clearly and help the group agree on the true process, not to hide the disagreement in tidy notes."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-conduct-elicitation-q4", question: "During observation, you see staff doing something very different from what a senior stakeholder told you. How do you handle the difference?", answer: [
            "I handle it with care, because this is sensitive. I do not confront the senior stakeholder, and I do not blame the staff. First I make sure my Observation is fair, by watching more than one case and checking whether what I saw was normal or a one off exception. I note what happened in neutral terms, focusing on steps and outcomes, not on people. Then I check my notes with the staff I observed, asking whether I understood the reasons correctly. When I raise it with the senior stakeholder, I present evidence, not judgement. I say something like, in the cases I observed, these extra checks happened, and I ask what drives them. Often there is a good reason, such as missing information or a system limit, and the senior view simply did not include it. I frame the finding as a chance to improve the design, so it reflects real work. The analyst must tell the truth kindly, with evidence, and with respect for everyone involved."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-stakeholder-management"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-confirm-elicitation-results",
        title: "Confirm Elicitation Results",
        subtitle: "Check It Back Before You Build On It",
        kind: .task,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: .elicitation,
        babokReference: "4.3",
        explanation: [
            "Confirming elicitation results means checking what was heard with the people who said it, before that understanding becomes a requirement or a design. Elicitation is never finished when the session ends, because notes can miss meaning, and words can mean different things to different people. Confirmation closes that gap by asking stakeholders to review, correct, and agree the record. It is a short step that prevents long and costly rework later.",
            "Confirmation can take many forms, and the analyst chooses the form that fits the material. A written summary works for simple points, while a walkthrough works better for a complex flow. Concept Modelling helps when the team must agree what key terms mean, such as what counts as a settled payment or a failed instruction. Mind Mapping can show how ideas connect, so stakeholders can see the whole picture and spot what is missing or misplaced.",
            "The analyst must make confirmation easy, or it will not happen. Summaries should be short, clear, and sent promptly while the session is still fresh. Questions should invite correction, not just approval, so stakeholders feel free to say when something is wrong. Prototyping can also confirm understanding, because reacting to a sketch or a mock flow often reveals misunderstandings that words alone would hide.",
            "Consider a bank confirming how settlement timing should be described to corporate clients. If the analyst sends a one page summary the next day, the operations team can correct a timing detail before it reaches design. If no confirmation happens, the wrong timing is built into messages, training, and client guidance, and the fix touches every part of the change. Confirmation is not bureaucracy, it is the cheapest quality check in the whole change."
        ],
        outputName: "Confirmed elicitation record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Record reviewed", guidance: "What was reviewed, and which version."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Confirmation", guidance: "Who confirmed what, and when, including any corrections."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Corrections", guidance: "What changed after review, and why."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Outstanding items", guidance: "What is still unconfirmed, with an owner and a date."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Sign off", guidance: "The recorded agreement that the record is accurate.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Send the record back to the people who gave the information, promptly, while the session is fresh. Ask them to confirm, correct, or challenge it, and record every correction rather than quietly rewriting the record. A correction is valuable evidence: it shows where understanding was wrong before it became a requirement.",
            "Separate confirmed facts from open items. What is confirmed can be built on. What is unconfirmed stays marked as unconfirmed, with an owner and a date, and no requirement is written as if it were settled. Confirmation is not a formality: it is the check that what you heard is what they meant."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: confirmed record for a cut off time change",
        workedExample: [
            "Record reviewed: workshop notes version 1, covering cut off times for three payment types. Confirmation: the operations lead confirmed the cut off times for two payment types and corrected the third, which follows the scheme calendar, not the local calendar. The correction is recorded with the reason.",
            "Outstanding items: the cut off for one low volume payment type is unconfirmed, owned by the operations lead, due within five working days. Sign off: the operations lead and the analyst recorded agreement that the corrected record is accurate. No requirement on cut off times is approved until the outstanding item is confirmed."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Send a clear summary promptly, while the session is still fresh.",
            "Invite correction, not just approval, when you ask for confirmation.",
            "Use models and sketches to confirm shared meaning, not only words.",
            "Record what was confirmed, what changed, and what is still open."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-09", title: "Notes Nobody Checked", takeaway: "Check notes early, or pay to fix them late.", story: [
            "A workshop produced detailed notes on payment limits.\nThe notes were filed without being sent for review.\nDesign work started from the notes the following week.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Notes captured what the analyst thought was agreed\n→ One limit was recorded the wrong way round\n→ No stakeholder reviewed or corrected the notes\n→ Design and testing were built on the error\n→ The mistake was found only at final review",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will move fast and check details later.\"",
            "So weeks of design and test work had to be redone, and trust in the analysis fell. The error itself was small, but the cost of finding it late was large, because every later artefact had carried it forward.",
            "Why?",
            "Unconfirmed notes are an analyst guess, however carefully they were taken. Memory fades, shorthand misleads, and a single misunderstood number can change a design. Confirmation turns a personal note into a shared record that the team can rely on. Skipping it to save a day often costs weeks later.",
            "A note no one has checked is not yet a fact the team can build on.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Send notes for review within a day of the session\n→ Highlight decisions and numbers that must be checked\n→ Ask directly, is this right, and what is missing\n→ Do not start design on unconfirmed points that carry risk",
            "Have you ever found a small note error only after much work was built on it?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-10", title: "One Picture, Shared Meaning", takeaway: "Shared meaning starts with a shared picture.", story: [
            "Two teams used the same payment terms differently.\nMeetings kept circling the same misunderstandings.\nThe analyst drew one shared picture for both teams.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Concept Modelling listed key terms and their meanings\n→ A Mind Mapping sketch showed how terms connected\n→ Both teams corrected the picture together\n→ Agreed meanings were written beside each term\n→ The picture was shared as the common reference",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"We thought we meant the same thing, we did not.\"",
            "So later discussions became shorter and clearer, because everyone used the same terms in the same way. The picture stayed on the wall of the project room, and new joiners used it to learn the language of the change quickly.",
            "Why?",
            "Many disagreements are not about facts, they are about language. When the same word means different things to different teams, every discussion starts with hidden confusion. A shared model makes meaning visible, so it can be discussed and agreed. Once meaning is shared, the real work can begin.",
            "Agree what words mean before you debate what to do.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ List key terms early and agree a plain meaning for each\n→ Draw connections between terms so the whole picture is visible\n→ Invite both sides to correct the model together\n→ Keep the agreed model where everyone can see and use it",
            "What would you draw if two teams kept misunderstanding the same words?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-11", title: "The Correction That Saved Launch", takeaway: "Prompt checking turns errors into quick fixes.", story: [
            "A summary described when settlement messages were sent.\nThe analyst sent it for confirmation the next morning.\nAn operations reviewer spotted a timing error at once.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ A one page summary was sent promptly after the session\n→ The reviewer read it with fresh eyes the next day\n→ Settlement timing was shown one step too early\n→ The correction was made and rechecked the same day\n→ Design, training, and client wording used the right timing",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"That timing is wrong, it happens after the check, not before.\"",
            "So the launch went ahead with correct timing in every message and guide. The correction took minutes because it was caught early, and the reviewer felt valued because their check had mattered.",
            "Why?",
            "Confirmation works best when it is quick, short, and specific. A one page summary sent promptly is far more likely to be read than a long document sent weeks later. The reviewer in this story could spot the error because the summary was clear and the timing stood out. Early correction is cheap, late correction is not.",
            "A prompt summary invites a prompt correction.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Send short summaries fast, while memory is fresh\n→ Make timings, numbers, and decisions easy to spot\n→ Thank reviewers when they correct you, and act at once\n→ Recheck corrected points before they move into design",
            "Have you ever caught an important error simply by asking someone to check your summary?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-confirm-elicitation-results-q1", question: "How do you confirm elicitation results with stakeholders after a session?", answer: [
            "I confirm quickly, clearly, and in a way that invites correction. Within a day of the session, I send a short summary covering what was discussed, what was decided, and what is still open. I highlight the points that carry risk, such as numbers, timings, and decisions, so reviewers know where to focus. I ask direct questions like, is this right, and what is missing, rather than just asking for approval. For complex topics, I walk through the summary with the stakeholder, or I use a simple model or sketch, such as Concept Modelling or a Mind Mapping view, so shared meaning can be checked visually. I record what was confirmed, what changed, and who confirmed it, so there is a clear record. If someone does not reply, I follow up once, politely, and I do not treat silence as agreement on risky points. Confirmation is how I turn my notes into a shared record the team can build on."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-confirm-elicitation-results-q2", question: "A stakeholder says your session notes are wrong. How do you respond?", answer: [
            "I welcome the correction, because that is exactly what confirmation is for. I thank them and I ask them to point to the specific parts that are wrong, so I can understand the difference clearly. I listen without defending my notes, and I ask for an example that shows the correct position. I compare what they are saying with what I heard in the session and with any documents I reviewed, using Document Analysis where it helps. If the correction is clear, I update the notes at once and I send the revised version back for a quick recheck. If the correction conflicts with what another stakeholder said, I record both views and I raise it as an open question to resolve with evidence, rather than choosing silently. I also reflect on why I misunderstood, so I can listen better next time. Being corrected early is a gift, and I treat it that way."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-confirm-elicitation-results-q3", question: "Two stakeholders confirm different versions of the same requirement. How do you resolve it?", answer: [
            "I do not pick one version and hope the other stakeholder will accept it. First I lay out the two versions side by side, in plain language, so the difference is visible to everyone. I ask each stakeholder for the reason behind their version, including the business need, the risk, and any example that supports it. I check the evidence, including documents, observed practice, and any prior decisions, to see which version fits the facts. Then I bring both stakeholders together, with the evidence, and I facilitate a discussion focused on the need behind the requirement, not on winning the point. I use a simple model or sketch where it helps, so both sides can react to the same picture. If agreement still cannot be reached, I escalate the decision to the person who owns it, with both options and their impacts set out fairly. I record the agreed version, who agreed it, and why, so the decision does not reopen later."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-confirm-elicitation-results-q4", question: "A senior leader refuses to confirm elicitation results, saying they trust you to get it right. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I appreciate the trust, and I explain kindly why confirmation still matters, even when trust is high. I say that my notes are my understanding, and a short check protects both of us from a costly misunderstanding later. I make confirmation as easy as possible, sending a very short summary focused only on the decisions and risks that need their view, rather than a long document. I offer a five minute walkthrough instead of asking them to read, and I capture their agreement live. If they still will not confirm, I record that the summary was shared, what it contained, and that no correction was received, and I share that record transparently. I do not treat silence as formal agreement on high risk points, and I flag the risk to the project lead so the decision is visible. I keep the tone respectful throughout, because the goal is shared understanding, not a signature for its own sake. Trust and checking can live together, and good analysts protect both."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-acceptance-criteria"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-communicate-business-analysis-information",
        title: "Communicate Business Analysis Information",
        subtitle: "Share The Right Information, In The Right Way",
        kind: .task,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: .elicitation,
        babokReference: "4.4",
        explanation: [
            "Communicating business analysis information means sharing findings, requirements, and decisions in a form each audience can use. The same information cannot be shared in the same way to everyone, because a senior leader, an operations team, and a technology team need different levels of detail. The analyst must think about who needs what, when they need it, and how they will use it. Clear communication turns analysis into action, while poor communication leaves good work unread and unused.",
            "Good communication starts with the audience, not with the document. Before writing, the analyst asks what decision the reader must make, what detail they need, and what format suits them best. A short summary with a clear recommendation suits a busy decision maker, while a detailed flow suits the team who must build or operate the change. Visual aids help many audiences, and a simple model or sketch can carry meaning faster than pages of text when the topic is complex.",
            "The analyst must also manage how information is packaged and shared over time. Version control matters, because working from an old copy can undo careful agreement. Feedback should be invited and acted on, so communication is a two way exchange rather than a broadcast. When information changes, the change must be flagged clearly, so readers know what is new and what it affects.",
            "Consider a bank sharing new payment cut off times with corporate clients and internal teams. Clients need a short, plain notice about what changes for them and from when. Internal teams need the full detail, including exception handling and system impacts. If both groups receive the same long technical pack, clients will miss the key point and internal teams may still lack the detail they need. Tailoring the message is not extra work, it is the work."
        ],
        outputName: "Business analysis communication pack",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Audience", guidance: "Who receives the information, and what each audience needs from it."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Message", guidance: "What is communicated, in plain language, with facts separated from open questions."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Form", guidance: "The form that fits the audience: pack, summary, model, or walkthrough."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Timing", guidance: "When the information is shared, and how often it is updated."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Feedback route", guidance: "How readers respond, ask questions, and record corrections.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write for the reader, not for the author. The same change needs a different message for operations, technology, and a sponsor: operations needs the steps that change, technology needs the behaviour and interfaces, and a sponsor needs the outcome, the cost, and the risk. The meaning must stay the same in every version, even though the words change.",
            "Share information early enough to be useful, and in a form the audience will actually read. A long pack sent the night before a decision is not communication. Name the feedback route: who to contact, how corrections are recorded, and by when responses are needed, so silence is never mistaken for agreement."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: communication pack for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Audiences: operations needs the new status list and the handling steps. Technology needs the status events and the interface changes. The sponsor needs the expected effect on customer queries and the release date. Each audience receives a version written for its needs, from the same agreed requirements.",
            "Form: operations receives a one page status guide and a walkthrough. Technology receives the requirements pack and the interface specification. The sponsor receives a half page summary. Timing: the pack goes out five working days before the decision meeting, and the walkthrough happens two days before, so questions arrive in time to be answered.",
            "Feedback route: comments go to the analyst, corrections are recorded in the requirements tool, and unanswered questions are listed at the decision meeting rather than assumed away. No audience is asked to approve a version it has not seen."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Start with the audience and the decision they must make.",
            "Tailor detail and format, do not send one pack to everyone.",
            "Control versions clearly, so no one works from an old copy.",
            "Flag changes plainly, and invite feedback on what you share."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-12", title: "One Pack Did Not Fit Everyone", takeaway: "One pack for all is a pack for no one.", story: [
            "A long technical pack described a payment change.\nThe same pack was sent to leaders, operations, and clients.\nEach group reacted differently, and none was satisfied.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Leaders found the pack too long to read in full\n→ Operations staff found key steps buried in detail\n→ Clients were confused by technical language\n→ Questions arrived from all sides at once\n→ The analyst had to rewrite the pack three times",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"I read the first page and gave up, it was not written for me.\"",
            "So the launch communication slipped, and the analyst spent more time rewriting than the original tailoring would have taken. Each audience eventually received what it needed, but trust in the communication had already been dented.",
            "Why?",
            "One pack rarely serves many audiences well, because needs differ by role. Leaders need decisions and impacts, operations need steps and exceptions, and clients need plain effects on their work. Sending the same document to all groups feels efficient, but it creates rework and confusion. Tailoring from the start is faster in the end.",
            "Write for the reader in front of you, not for every reader at once.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ List each audience and what decision they must make\n→ Create a short version for leaders and detail for doers\n→ Use plain language for client facing material\n→ Test the pack with one reader from each audience first",
            "Have you ever received a document that clearly was not written for you?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-13", title: "Two Audiences, Two Messages", takeaway: "Tailor the shape, keep the facts the same.", story: [
            "New payment approval steps were ready to share.\nThe analyst prepared two different messages for two groups.\nBoth messages carried the same facts in different forms.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Senior leaders received a one page summary and dates\n→ The operations team received a full step by step guide\n→ Both versions used the same terms and the same dates\n→ Questions were few, because each group had what it needed\n→ Feedback from both groups was collected and acted on",
            "The plan said:\n\"Share the change clearly with each audience.\"",
            "So the change was understood quickly by both groups, and the operations team felt prepared rather than surprised. Leaders could answer questions confidently, because their summary matched the detailed guide in every fact.",
            "Why?",
            "Tailoring does not mean changing the facts, it means changing the shape around the facts. When the core message stays consistent, different formats strengthen each other instead of creating confusion. The analyst earns trust by respecting each audience time and needs.",
            "Same facts, different shapes, for different readers.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Keep facts, terms, and dates identical across versions\n→ Give leaders brevity and doers full detail\n→ Check that versions agree before you send them\n→ Invite feedback from each audience, and close the loop",
            "What would you change if you had to explain the same change to two very different groups?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-14", title: "Two Versions, One Mistake", takeaway: "Clear versions prevent silent divergence.", story: [
            "A requirements document went through several revisions.\nCopies were shared by email with similar file names.\nTwo teams worked from two different versions.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Version two was shared with the design team\n→ Version three was shared with the testing team\n→ No version number appeared on the first page\n→ Design and testing diverged without anyone noticing\n→ The mismatch was found during a joint review",
            "The request said:\n\"Please use the latest version of the requirements.\"",
            "So design and testing had to be reconciled, and several test cases were rewritten. The teams lost confidence in the shared material, and extra checks were added that slowed the remaining work.",
            "Why?",
            "Version confusion is a communication failure, not a filing detail. When people cannot tell which copy is current, they make reasonable choices from the wrong material. Clear version labels, a single shared location, and a short change note prevent this waste. The analyst owns the clarity of what is shared.",
            "If readers cannot tell which version is current, the pack has failed.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Put the version and date on the first page, every time\n→ Share from one location, not from scattered email copies\n→ Add a short note on what changed in each version\n→ Tell readers directly when a new version replaces an old one",
            "Have you ever worked from an old version without realising it?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-15", title: "The Short Summary That Landed", takeaway: "Lead with the decision, back it with detail.", story: [
            "A complex payment flow needed senior approval.\nThe full analysis ran to many pages of detail.\nThe analyst prepared a two page summary for leaders.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The summary stated the decision needed on page one\n→ A simple flow picture showed the proposed change\n→ Impacts, risks, and dates were listed plainly\n→ The full detail was attached as backup only\n→ Leaders approved the change in a single meeting",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"At last, something I can read and decide on.\"",
            "So the decision was made quickly, and the detailed work could proceed with clear backing. The analyst learned that brevity, when backed by solid detail, is a sign of respect for decision makers.",
            "Why?",
            "Senior readers need to grasp the choice, the impact, and the risk without wading through every detail. A short summary with a clear picture does that work, while the full analysis remains available for those who need it. Communication that respects the reader time earns faster and better decisions.",
            "Brevity backed by detail earns decisions, length alone earns delay.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Put the decision needed on the first page\n→ Use one clear picture to show the proposed change\n→ List impacts and dates in plain language\n→ Attach full detail as backup, not as the main message",
            "What would you put on page one if a busy leader had only five minutes to decide?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-communicate-business-analysis-information-q1", question: "How do you decide how to communicate business analysis information to different stakeholders?", answer: [
            "I start with the audience, not with the document. I ask who needs the information, what decision they must make, and how much detail they need to make it well. A senior leader usually needs a short summary with the decision, the impact, and the dates, while the operations team needs full steps, exceptions, and checks. I also think about format, because some people absorb a picture faster than text, and a simple flow or model can help. I keep the facts, terms, and dates identical across versions, so tailored messages never contradict each other. I share from one clear location with a version label, and I flag what has changed. I also check timing, so urgent decisions get a direct message and routine updates wait for the regular pack. Finally, I invite feedback and I act on it, because communication is a conversation, not a broadcast. My aim is that each reader can find what they need quickly and trust that it is current."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-communicate-business-analysis-information-q2", question: "A stakeholder tells you your document is too long and they will not read it. How do you respond?", answer: [
            "I take the feedback seriously, because a document no one reads has failed, however good its content is. I ask what decision they need to make and what information they need for it, so I can reshape the material around their need. I create a short summary, ideally one or two pages, with the decision on the first page, a simple picture of the change, and the key impacts and dates in plain language. I keep the full document as backup, clearly labelled, so detail is still available. I also check whether a short walkthrough would help more than another document, and I offer that. I thank them for being honest, because their feedback helps me communicate better for everyone. I also ask which format suits them best in future, so the next pack starts in a form they will actually open and use. In future, I lead with the summary and attach detail, rather than expecting busy readers to find the key points themselves."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-communicate-business-analysis-information-q3", question: "You must share business analysis information that includes sensitive client details. How do you handle it?", answer: [
            "I handle sensitive information with care from the start. I ask what is truly needed for the decision or task at hand, and I remove or mask any sensitive detail that is not needed. I share only with the people who need it, using the approved sharing route for the organisation, and I avoid sending sensitive material in general emails or chat messages. I label the material clearly so readers know it must be handled with care, and I check that any examples I use do not expose a real client where a generic example would do. If I am unsure whether something is sensitive, I ask before sharing, rather than sharing first and regretting it later. I also keep a record of what was shared, with whom, and why, so there is a clear trail. The analyst must balance openness with discretion, sharing enough to help the work while protecting the people and clients behind the data."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-communicate-business-analysis-information-q4", question: "A key requirement changes halfway through a project. How do you communicate the change so everyone stays aligned?", answer: [
            "I communicate the change quickly, clearly, and in a way that shows exactly what is different. First I confirm the change with its owner and I understand the reason, the impact, and what it affects, including design, testing, training, and client wording. I update the shared requirements in one place, with a clear version label and a short change note that says what changed, why, and what it replaces. I notify affected teams directly, rather than hoping they will notice, and I tailor the message, giving leaders the impact and dates, and giving delivery teams the full detail. I check that old copies are not still in use, and I ask key people to confirm they have seen the change. I also hold a short session where the change is complex, so questions can be answered live. Clear, prompt, and targeted communication is what keeps a mid project change from becoming confusion."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-brd-fsd"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-manage-stakeholder-collaboration",
        title: "Manage Stakeholder Collaboration",
        subtitle: "Keep People Working Well Together",
        kind: .task,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: .elicitation,
        babokReference: "4.5",
        explanation: [
            "Managing stakeholder collaboration means keeping the many people involved in a change working well together over time. Elicitation is not a single event, it is a relationship that must be tended through disagreements, delays, and changing priorities. The analyst helps stakeholders share information, resolve conflicts, and stay engaged, even when the work is difficult. Without active collaboration, stakeholders drift apart, decisions stall, and the change loses momentum.",
            "Good collaboration starts with shared expectations. The analyst helps the group agree how decisions will be made, how often they will meet, and how disagreements will be handled. Workshops and Collaborative Games can build shared understanding, while regular check ins keep small issues from growing into large ones. The analyst also watches for signs of disengagement, such as missed sessions or silence, and acts early rather than waiting for a crisis.",
            "Conflict is a normal part of collaboration, not a sign of failure. Stakeholders bring different goals, pressures, and views of success, and those differences will surface. The analyst helps the group focus on shared outcomes, surface the reasons behind each view, and find options that serve the wider goal. Brainstorming options together can turn a contest between positions into a joint search for a workable path.",
            "Consider a bank where the payments team and the control team disagree on the priority for a release. The payments team wants speed for clients, while the control team wants stronger checks before launch. An analyst who manages collaboration well will bring both sides together, make the trade off visible, and help them agree a release that serves clients without weakening controls. The result is not just a decision, it is a working relationship that survives the next disagreement too."
        ],
        outputName: "Stakeholder collaboration record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Agreements", guidance: "What was agreed with each group, and when."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Commitments", guidance: "Who promised what, by when, including the analyst."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Conflicts and resolutions", guidance: "Disagreements, how they were handled, and what was decided."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Relationship notes", guidance: "What each group needs to stay engaged, and what has worked."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Next steps", guidance: "The next engagement for each group, and its purpose.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Treat collaboration as a record, not a feeling. Write down agreements and commitments as they are made, including your own, and keep small promises reliably: notes shared the same day, questions answered by the date given. Trust is built by a run of small promises kept, and lost by one large promise missed without warning.",
            "Handle conflict early and on the evidence. When groups disagree, write down both positions, the evidence for each, and the decision, including who decided it. A disagreement recorded and decided is progress. A disagreement left vague will return at the worst moment, usually during testing or release."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: collaboration record for a reconciliation change",
        workedExample: [
            "Agreements: operations agreed the break age bands on the agreed date. The data team agreed to confirm field availability within five working days. Commitments: the analyst committed to share the draft layout within two working days, and did so on the same day.",
            "Conflicts: operations wanted four age bands and the data team warned that one band needed a new field. The resolution: three bands at release, with the fourth band added when the field is available, decided by the product owner and recorded with the reason. Next steps: a review of the draft layout with operations, then a confirmation session with the data team."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Agree how decisions, meetings, and disagreements will be handled.",
            "Watch for disengagement early, and re-engage people with care.",
            "Treat conflict as normal, and focus on shared outcomes.",
            "Keep communication regular, so small issues stay small."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-16", title: "Two Priorities, One Release", takeaway: "Make trade offs visible, then decide together.", story: [
            "A release could not include everything both teams wanted.\nThe payments team wanted speed for corporate clients.\nThe control team wanted stronger checks before launch.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Each team stated its priority without hearing the other\n→ The analyst mapped both priorities on one shared board\n→ Trade offs were made visible, with impacts listed\n→ A joint Workshop explored options that served both goals\n→ The group agreed a phased release with clear checks",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"We cannot launch fast and launch safe at the same time.\"",
            "So the release went ahead in phases, with early client benefit and agreed checks at each stage. Both teams felt their concern had shaped the outcome, and the working relationship between them grew stronger rather than weaker.",
            "Why?",
            "Competing priorities often look impossible until the trade off is made visible. When each side only hears its own case, the discussion becomes a contest. When both cases are shown together, with impacts and options, the group can search for a path that serves the wider goal. Collaboration is the work of making that search possible.",
            "Visible trade offs turn a contest into a joint decision.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Show both priorities side by side, with impacts stated plainly\n→ Ask what each side fears losing, not only what it wants\n→ Explore phased or combined options together\n→ Record the agreed path and who owns each part of it",
            "Have you ever seen two good priorities clash, with no easy winner?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-17", title: "Bringing A Quiet Voice Back", takeaway: "Close the loop, and people stay engaged.", story: [
            "A key operations stakeholder stopped attending sessions.\nInvites were accepted, then missed without explanation.\nThe analyst decided to reach out directly and kindly.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The analyst asked for a short one to one conversation\n→ The stakeholder shared feeling unheard in large sessions\n→ Earlier input had been recorded but never acted on\n→ The analyst shared what had changed as a result of input\n→ A smaller session format was agreed for future input",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"I stopped coming because nothing I said seemed to matter.\"",
            "So the stakeholder returned, and their practical knowledge improved the next round of design. The analyst learned that disengagement is often a message, not laziness, and that listening to the message restores collaboration faster than reminders do.",
            "Why?",
            "People disengage when they feel their time is wasted or their views are ignored. Reminders and escalations rarely fix that feeling, while honest conversation often does. The analyst must notice the pattern early, ask with genuine curiosity, and show how input has been used. Closing the loop is what keeps people engaged.",
            "Disengagement is a message, listen to it before you send another invite.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Notice missed sessions and silence early, do not ignore them\n→ Ask privately and kindly what has changed\n→ Show how past input was used, or explain honestly if it was not\n→ Offer a format that suits the person, not only the project",
            "What would you do if a key stakeholder quietly stopped showing up?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-EC-18", title: "The Agreement That Held Firm", takeaway: "Agreed ground rules hold firm under pressure.", story: [
            "A project involved four teams with different goals.\nLate change requests had derailed the previous project.\nThe analyst proposed a working agreement at the start.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The group agreed how decisions would be made\n→ A simple rule for late changes was written down\n→ Meeting rhythms and response times were agreed\n→ The agreement was shared and kept visible to all\n→ When a late change arrived, the rule was followed calmly",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will agree how we work together before we start.\"",
            "So the late change was assessed fairly, its impact was weighed, and a clear decision was made without blame or panic. The teams trusted the process because they had built it together, and the project finished with relationships intact.",
            "Why?",
            "Working agreements prevent many collaboration problems before they start. When the group agrees in calm times how it will handle pressure, it does not need to invent rules in a crisis. The agreement is not about control, it is about shared fairness, so every team knows what to expect. Analysts who facilitate such agreements protect both the project and the relationships within it.",
            "Agree the rules in calm times, so pressure does not decide them for you.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Facilitate a short working agreement at project start\n→ Cover decisions, late changes, meetings, and response times\n→ Keep the agreement visible and revisit it when needed\n→ Apply the agreement fairly, even when it is uncomfortable",
            "Have you ever wished a team had agreed its ground rules before trouble arrived?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-manage-stakeholder-collaboration-q1", question: "Two stakeholder groups disagree on priorities for a payments change. How do you help them collaborate?", answer: [
            "I bring the disagreement into the open, kindly and with evidence. First I meet each group separately to understand not just what they want, but why they want it and what they fear losing. Then I bring both groups together and I show both priorities side by side, with impacts stated plainly, so the trade off is visible to everyone. I facilitate a Workshop where the group explores options together, including phased approaches that might serve both goals. I keep the focus on the shared outcome, such as serving clients well without weakening controls, rather than on winning. I use simple visuals to show options and impacts, because a shared picture helps people move from positions to problem solving. If agreement cannot be reached, I escalate fairly, with both options and impacts set out. I record the agreed path and who owns each part, so the decision holds. Good collaboration is not about avoiding disagreement, it is about working through it well."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-manage-stakeholder-collaboration-q2", question: "A key stakeholder stops attending sessions and stops replying to your updates. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I notice the pattern early and I reach out directly, with curiosity rather than blame. I ask for a short one to one conversation, at a time that suits them, and I ask honestly how they are finding the project and what has changed. Often the reason is simple, such as feeling unheard, being overloaded, or finding the sessions unhelpful. I listen carefully and I show how their past input was used, or I explain honestly if it was not used and why. I ask what format would work better for them, such as smaller sessions, written input, or a shorter check in. I also review my own communication, checking whether my updates were too long or too frequent. I agree a simple way forward together, and I follow through on it, closing the loop by showing the impact of their renewed input. Re-engagement is built on respect and follow through, not on reminders."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-manage-stakeholder-collaboration-q3", question: "You must build collaboration between teams in different locations who have never worked together. How do you approach it?", answer: [
            "I start by building relationships before I need them, because trust makes later collaboration far easier. I arrange short introductory sessions where each team shares its goals, pressures, and ways of working, so people see each other as partners rather than distant names. I agree a simple working agreement covering how decisions are made, how often we meet, how we share information, and how disagreements will be handled. I use shared visual tools, such as a common board or model, so everyone works from the same picture despite the distance. I keep communication regular and predictable, with short summaries after each session and clear actions. I watch for silence from any team, because distance can hide disengagement, and I check in individually when needed. I celebrate shared wins openly, so the group builds a sense of joint ownership. Collaboration across distance needs more structure, not less, and the analyst provides that structure."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-manage-stakeholder-collaboration-q4", question: "Collaboration has broken down between two teams and trust is low. How would you rebuild it as the BA?", answer: [
            "I start by accepting that trust rebuilds slowly, through actions, not through a single meeting. I speak to each team separately first, listening without taking sides, to understand what happened and what each side needs to move forward. I look for one small, shared goal that both teams care about, and I help them achieve it together, because a small joint win does more than any speech about teamwork. I facilitate a session focused on the future, not on blame, where the group agrees how it will work together from now on, including how decisions and disagreements will be handled. I make information sharing transparent, so no team feels surprised or excluded. I follow through reliably on every commitment I make, and I hold others gently to theirs, because consistency rebuilds trust. I keep the senior sponsor informed, without escalating every issue. Rebuilding collaboration takes patience, fairness, and visible follow through, and the analyst must model all three."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-stakeholder-management"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-trace-requirements",
        title: "Trace Requirements",
        subtitle: "Follow every requirement from need to delivery",
        kind: .task,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: .lifecycle,
        babokReference: "5.1",
        explanation: [
            "Tracing a requirement means keeping a visible link from the original need through analysis, design, build and testing. The BA records where each requirement came from and where it is used. That link lets anyone ask why a piece of work exists and get a clear answer. Without a link, a requirement floats on its own and no one can prove what it supports.",
            "Tracing matters because change is constant and memory is unreliable. When a stakeholder asks for a change, the BA can follow the links and see what else will be affected. When a test fails, the team can trace back to the need and decide what still matters. Item Tracking supports this work by giving each requirement a stable identity that follows it through every stage.",
            "Without tracing, teams build features no one asked for and miss needs no one remembered to check. A payment rule can be coded twice in two places because no one saw the shared need. A compliance check can be dropped in testing because no one linked it to the original request. Reviews help here, because a second pair of eyes can spot a missing link before it becomes a defect.",
            "Consider a change to how failed payments are retried. The BA traces the business need to the retry rule, the error message, the operations procedure and the test case. If the retry count changes later, every linked item can be found and updated together. If the link is missing, one screen still shows the old count while the back end uses the new one. Good tracing keeps the whole story together, even when the work is spread across teams."
        ],
        outputName: "Requirements traceability list",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Requirement", guidance: "The requirement, with its stable identifier."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Source", guidance: "The need, stakeholder, or regulation it came from."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Design and build links", guidance: "The design, component, or configuration that implements it."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Test links", guidance: "The test scenarios that check it."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Status", guidance: "The current state: draft, approved, built, tested, or changed.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Give every requirement a stable identifier that follows it through every stage, and record the links as the work happens, not at the end. A trace recorded after the fact is a reconstruction, and reconstructions miss the links that matter. The question every link must answer is simple: why does this piece of work exist, and how do we know it works?",
            "Check the trace in both directions. Forward: every requirement has a design and a test. Backward: every design element and test traces to a requirement, so nothing is built that no one asked for. When a link is missing, record it as a gap with an owner, rather than assuming the link exists."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: traceability list for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Requirement ST-001: the screen must show the status received from the status report, with the time it was received. Source: the operations need to answer customer queries without calling the back office. Design link: status display component. Test links: scenarios for each status value, including a missing status report.",
            "Requirement ST-002: the screen must never show a payment as settled before settlement is confirmed. Source: the compliance rule on status wording. Design link: status wording rules. Test link: scenario where settlement confirmation is absent. Status: both requirements approved, design complete, tests written, build in progress."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Every requirement needs a clear source and a clear place where it is delivered.",
            "Use Item Tracking to give each requirement a stable identity from start to finish.",
            "Use Reviews to check that links are complete before work moves to the next stage.",
            "If a requirement cannot be traced to a need, question whether it should be built."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-01", title: "The orphan rule nobody could explain", takeaway: "A requirement with no traceable source should be paused, not built on trust.", story: [
            "A payment retry rule sat in the build backlog.\nNo one remembered who had asked for it.\nThe BA was asked to confirm it before testing.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA searched the notes and emails for the source\n→ No workshop record mentioned the retry rule\n→ The operations team said they had not asked for it\n→ The build team had already coded the rule\n→ Testing was due to start the next week",
            "The request said:\n\"Add a retry for failed payments.\"",
            "So the BA paused the test preparation and asked the process owner to confirm whether the rule was needed at all, and the owner said the retry idea had been discussed but never agreed, so the coded rule was removed before it reached testing.",
            "Why?",
            "A requirement without a source cannot be checked, challenged, or defended. Tracing gives each requirement a home, so the team knows why it exists and who can change it. When the link is missing, work goes ahead on trust, and trust is a poor substitute for evidence.",
            "If you cannot trace it, you cannot prove it is needed.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Record the source for every requirement when it is first captured\n→ Check the link before build and again before testing\n→ Pause work that has no confirmed source\n→ Use Item Tracking so the link survives every handover",
            "Have you ever found work in progress that no one could explain?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-02", title: "Two teams built the same check twice", takeaway: "Shared tracing exposes duplication early, while one shared rule is still cheap to agree.", story: [
            "Two teams were updating payment checks in parallel.\nBoth teams thought they owned the amount limit check.\nThe BA spotted the overlap in a review meeting.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ One team coded a limit check for online payments\n→ A second team coded a similar check for batch payments\n→ Both checks used different limit values\n→ No shared link showed they served the same need\n→ The difference surfaced only in joint testing",
            "The plan said:\n\"Each team will deliver its own payment checks.\"",
            "So the BA mapped both checks back to the single business need for a consistent limit, and the teams agreed on one shared rule, and one of the duplicate builds was retired before release, which saved rework and avoided two different limits reaching customers.",
            "Why?",
            "Tracing shows when two pieces of work serve the same need. Without that view, teams duplicate effort in good faith and create inconsistency. A shared trace makes the overlap visible early, while it is still cheap to fix. It also gives the teams a common reference point for the decision.",
            "One need should have one visible home, even when many teams deliver it.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Trace related work to the same need, not to separate team lists\n→ Review traces across teams, not only inside one team\n→ Agree one shared rule before both builds go too far\n→ Keep the retired option recorded so it is not rebuilt later",
            "What would you do when you spot two teams solving the same need in different ways?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-03", title: "The test passed but the need was missed", takeaway: "Link needs to tests, or a green report can hide a missing journey step.", story: [
            "Testing finished on time for a new payment screen.\nEvery test case passed on the first run.\nThe operations team still rejected the release.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Tests checked the screen fields and buttons\n→ No test checked the full payment journey end to end\n→ The link from need to test had never been made\n→ A key step for failed payments was missing\n→ Operations found the gap in their own checks",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"The screen works, but the job still cannot be finished.\"",
            "So the team went back and linked each business need to its test coverage, and the missing failed payment step was added and tested, and the release went ahead only after operations confirmed the full journey worked from start to finish.",
            "Why?",
            "Passing tests prove the build matches the test cases. They do not prove the build meets the need unless need and test are linked. Tracing from need to test exposes the gaps that a green test report can hide. It also shows which needs still lack proof, so the team can fix coverage before release.",
            "A passing test suite is not proof that the need is met.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Link every need to at least one test before testing starts\n→ Check coverage by need, not only by screen or function\n→ Ask operations to walk the full journey, not only the new screen\n→ Treat an untested need as unfinished work",
            "Have you ever seen tests pass while the real job still failed?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-04", title: "A small change broke three linked screens", takeaway: "Trace the blast radius first, because one shared message can touch many screens.", story: [
            "A wording change was requested for a payment error.\nIt looked small and safe to the requester.\nThe BA checked the links before agreeing.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The request asked for clearer wording on one screen\n→ The BA traced the error message to its source rule\n→ The same message was used on three screens\n→ Two other teams relied on the exact wording\n→ A quick change would have broken their checks",
            "The request said:\n\"Just change the words on this one screen.\"",
            "So the BA shared the trace with all three teams and they agreed a single new wording together, and the change was made once and tested in all three places, so no team was surprised by a silent change to shared wording.",
            "Why?",
            "Small changes are rarely small when items are shared. Tracing reveals the blast radius before the change is made, so the BA can bring the right people into the decision. Without it, a helpful fix in one place becomes a defect in another. The cost of checking links is small compared with the cost of fixing three surprises.",
            "Check the links first, because shared wording is shared behaviour.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Trace every change request before estimating its size\n→ Bring every affected team into the decision early\n→ Change shared items once, with one agreed version\n→ Test all linked places, not only the place that asked",
            "What would you check before agreeing to a change that looks small?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-trace-requirements-q1", question: "You are the BA and a developer asks why a payment validation rule exists, but you cannot find its source. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I would start by saying I do not have the answer yet, and I would not guess. I would check the traceability links for the rule first. I would look for the source need, the workshop note, and the linked test case. If the link is missing, I would treat that as the problem to fix, not as a small gap. I would ask the stakeholders who own the payment process whether they recognise the rule and why it matters. I would also check the requirements log to see when the rule appeared and who added it. If no one can confirm the need, I would park the rule and raise it for a decision. I would not let the team build or test something with no clear purpose. Once the source is confirmed, I would record the link clearly so the next person does not have to search again. I would also check nearby rules to see if the same gap exists elsewhere. Tracing is not paperwork for me. It is how I prove that work is needed, and how I stop the team from building the wrong thing with confidence."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-trace-requirements-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you found a gap between a requirement and its test coverage. How did you handle it?", answer: [
            "I would describe a case where the tests looked healthy but the need was not covered. I would start by explaining how I checked the traceability from each need to its tests. I found that one need had no linked test at all, so the green report was hiding a gap. I would raise this early with the test lead, using the trace as evidence, not as blame. I would work with the test team to add coverage for the missing journey step, including the failure path. I would also ask the business owner to confirm what good looks like, so the new test checks the real outcome. I would update the links so the coverage is visible to everyone. I would then recheck the full set, because one gap often means there are more. I would keep the language simple and focus on risk, not on fault. My lesson was clear. I now check need to test links before testing starts, not after the report arrives. That habit saves rework and gives the business real confidence in the release."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-trace-requirements-q3", question: "A stakeholder says tracing requirements is slow paperwork that adds no value. How do you respond?", answer: [
            "I would listen first, because the concern is often about heavy process, not about tracing itself. I would then give a short, real example. I would explain how a missing link once left the team unsure why a payment rule existed, and how that doubt delayed testing. I would show how traceability answers three simple questions: why this work exists, what it affects, and how we know it works. I would propose a light approach, not a heavy one. Each requirement gets a source, an owner, and a link to its test. I would use Item Tracking so the links stay visible without extra documents. I would also offer to keep the trace current myself, so the stakeholder sees value without extra work. I would invite the stakeholder to test the approach on one small change first. If the trace helps that change go smoothly, we keep it. If it does not, we simplify it together. My goal is useful links, not perfect paperwork."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-trace-requirements-q4", question: "You are the BA and two teams discover they built overlapping payment checks. How do you resolve it without delaying the release?", answer: [
            "I would first map both checks back to the shared business need, using traceability to show the overlap clearly. I would bring both teams together with that map, so the discussion starts from facts, not from team positions. I would ask the business owner to confirm the single correct rule, including the limit value and when it applies. I would then agree which build stays and which one is retired, and I would record that decision with its reason. I would check the linked tests and screens for both builds, so nothing is left pointing at the retired version. I would use a short Review with both teams to confirm the single rule works in each journey. If time is tight, I would focus on the shared rule and the highest risk paths first, and I would be honest about what still needs checking. I would keep the release date in view, but I would not release two different limits. One consistent rule, tested once, is faster than fixing a live inconsistency later."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-test-scenarios"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-maintain-requirements",
        title: "Maintain Requirements",
        subtitle: "Keep the requirement set true as work moves on",
        kind: .task,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: .lifecycle,
        babokReference: "5.2",
        explanation: [
            "Maintaining requirements means keeping the set accurate, current and usable as work moves forward. The BA updates requirements when understanding improves and retires items that are no longer needed. The set should always reflect the agreed need, not the first draft of it. A maintained set is a working tool, not an archive of old decisions.",
            "Maintenance matters because requirements age quickly once build and testing begin. New questions reveal gaps, and early assumptions turn out to be wrong. If the BA updates the record each time, later readers see the current truth. If updates live only in chat messages and memory, the record and the reality drift apart.",
            "Without maintenance, teams work from stale copies and argue about which version is correct. A retired payment rule can be rebuilt because no one marked it as retired. A changed field meaning can be tested against the old meaning because the note was never updated. The Data Dictionary and the Glossary help here, because they hold one agreed meaning for each term and field.",
            "Consider the word settled in a payments team. Operations may mean funds have moved, while reconciliation may mean the record has matched. The BA maintains one Glossary entry that states the agreed meaning and keeps the Data Dictionary aligned for the status field. When the meaning changes, both places are updated together through Item Tracking. That discipline prevents the same word from meaning two things in two teams."
        ],
        outputName: "Maintained requirements pack",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Current requirements", guidance: "The approved set, with identifiers, status, and version."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Changes applied", guidance: "What changed, when, why, and who approved it."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Retired requirements", guidance: "Requirements removed or replaced, with the reason and the replacement."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Consistency checks", guidance: "Checks that related requirements still agree after each change."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Open questions", guidance: "Questions that affect maintained requirements, with owners and dates.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Treat the requirements pack as a living record, not a document written once. Every change is recorded with its reason and its approver, and retired requirements stay visible as retired, with the reason and the replacement, so no one builds against a requirement that no longer applies.",
            "After each change, check the requirements around it. A change to one rule often affects validation, status wording, or exception handling elsewhere, and a pack that contradicts itself will be interpreted differently by every reader. Keep the pack current as the work proceeds: a pack updated at the end of a release is a history, not a guide."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: maintenance note for a validation rule change",
        workedExample: [
            "Current requirements: validation rules VAL-001 to VAL-008, approved, version 3. Changes applied: VAL-004 was changed to require a warning rather than a block when the payee name check returns no match. Reason: the check warns, it does not decide, and a block would stop legitimate payments. Approved by the product owner after review with compliance.",
            "Consistency checks: the status screen wording and the test scenarios for VAL-004 were updated in the same change, and the exception handling procedure was checked and found unaffected. Retired: none. Open questions: whether the warning wording needs a second compliance review, owned by the analyst, due within three working days."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Keep the requirement set current: update, correct and retire items as understanding changes.",
            "Use the Glossary for one agreed meaning per business term.",
            "Use the Data Dictionary for one agreed meaning per field and value.",
            "Mark retired requirements clearly so they are not rebuilt by mistake."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-05", title: "The glossary that stopped the arguments", takeaway: "A shared Glossary ends arguments about meaning so teams can decide the real work.", story: [
            "Two teams argued about the word pending every week.\nEach team used the word to mean something different.\nThe BA was asked to settle the dispute.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Operations used pending for payments awaiting checks\n→ The support team used pending for payments awaiting a reply\n→ Customers received mixed explanations\n→ The BA gathered both uses in one session\n→ One agreed meaning was written down",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"We keep arguing because we mean different things.\"",
            "So the BA added one Glossary entry for pending, with the agreed meaning and two examples, and both teams started using the same words with customers, and the weekly argument stopped because the agreed meaning was easy to find and easy to quote.",
            "Why?",
            "Words carry work. When a key term means two things, every discussion starts with confusion and every handover risks error. A maintained Glossary gives teams one shared language. It does not end debate about what should happen, but it ends debate about what words mean, which frees time for the real decisions.",
            "One term, one agreed meaning, kept where everyone can find it.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Capture both uses before choosing one agreed meaning\n→ Write the meaning with examples, not only a short label\n→ Keep the Glossary where both teams already look\n→ Update the entry when the agreed meaning changes",
            "Have you ever watched a simple word cause repeated confusion?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-06", title: "Old requirements that refused to retire", takeaway: "Retire stale items visibly, with a reason, so planning uses only live work.", story: [
            "A release list still held twelve old payment items.\nSome items had been replaced months earlier.\nThe BA was asked to clean the list before planning.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA checked each item against current work\n→ Four items had been replaced by newer rules\n→ Two items described work already delivered\n→ Owners confirmed six items were still live\n→ Retired items were marked, not deleted",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will plan from the current list as it stands.\"",
            "So the BA marked the replaced and delivered items as retired with a short reason and a link to the newer item, and planning went ahead from a clean list of six live items, and no retired item was estimated or rebuilt by mistake.",
            "Why?",
            "A list that never retires anything grows until no one trusts it. People stop reading it, and planning includes dead work alongside live work. Retiring items with a reason keeps the history without cluttering the future. It also shows respect for past decisions, because the reason for retirement stays visible.",
            "A clean list is a kindness to the next planner.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Review the list with owners before each planning round\n→ Mark retired items with a reason and a link forward\n→ Never delete history silently, retire it visibly\n→ Check for duplicates when an item is replaced",
            "Have you ever planned from a list full of work that was already done?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-07", title: "Two definitions of settled caused a dispute", takeaway: "When meanings diverge, align the Glossary and Data Dictionary and rename the second meaning.", story: [
            "A reconciliation report and an operations report disagreed.\nBoth reports claimed to show settled payments.\nThe BA was asked to explain the difference.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Operations counted a payment as settled when funds moved\n→ Reconciliation counted it as settled when records matched\n→ The two counts differed every day\n→ No shared definition existed for the status field\n→ Each team defended its own count",
            "The request said:\n\"Tell us which settled count is correct.\"",
            "So the BA compared both uses, agreed one definition for settled in the Glossary and the Data Dictionary, and gave the second meaning its own status name, and both reports were relabelled so each count was correct under its own name and the daily dispute ended.",
            "Why?",
            "Both teams were right under their own definitions, which is why the dispute kept returning. Maintenance is not only about updating text. It is about keeping meanings aligned across teams, fields and reports. When the BA aligns the Glossary and the Data Dictionary together, the same word stops producing two different truths.",
            "Align the word and the field together, or the dispute returns.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Compare definitions in use before declaring one side wrong\n→ Align the Glossary entry and the Data Dictionary entry\n→ Give a second meaning its own name, do not force one word\n→ Relabel reports so each count is honest under its name",
            "What would you do when two correct reports disagree every day?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-maintain-requirements-q1", question: "You are the BA and you find a requirement that no longer matches how the payment process works. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I would not quietly edit it and move on. I would first confirm what has changed by checking the current process with the owner. I would compare the old requirement with the new way of working, and I would note exactly what differs. I would then update the requirement in plain language, and I would record why it changed and who agreed it. I would check the linked items too, including tests, screens, and the Data Dictionary, so no linked item still points at the old meaning. I would use Item Tracking so the change is visible and the history is kept. I would tell the affected teams in a short update, focusing on what is different now. I would also ask whether nearby requirements need the same update, because one change often signals more. My aim is simple. The record should match reality, and anyone reading it later should trust it. Maintenance is how I keep that trust alive."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-maintain-requirements-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you had to clean up an outdated set of requirements. What was your approach?", answer: [
            "I would start with the owners, not with the document. I would ask each owner which items were still live, which had been replaced, and which had already been delivered. I would work through the set in small batches, so the review stays manageable and decisions stay fresh. For each stale item, I would mark it as retired with a short reason and a link to the newer item. I would not delete history, because the team may need to know why an item was dropped. I would update the Glossary and the Data Dictionary at the same time, so terms and fields stay aligned. I would use a short Review with the owners to confirm the clean set before planning uses it. I would then share the clean list with a note on what changed and why. The result was a shorter list that the team trusted. Planning became faster, because no one had to guess which items were still real."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-maintain-requirements-q3", question: "A stakeholder keeps using a business term differently from the rest of the team. How do you handle it?", answer: [
            "I would treat it as a language gap, not as a personal fault. I would first listen carefully to how the stakeholder uses the term, and I would note the examples they give. I would then compare that use with the agreed Glossary entry, if one exists. If the entry is missing or unclear, I would arrange a short session with the key users of the term. I would present both uses side by side, with examples, and I would ask the group to agree one meaning. I would write the agreed meaning in plain words, with one example of what it includes and one of what it excludes. I would update the Glossary and check the Data Dictionary for any linked field. I would then use the agreed term consistently in my own notes and messages, so the habit spreads. If the old use returns, I would gently point back to the agreed entry. Shared language is built by repetition, not by one meeting."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-maintain-requirements-q4", question: "You inherit a large requirement set with no owner and many stale items. How do you bring it under control without stopping delivery?", answer: [
            "I would not try to fix everything at once, because that would block the team. I would start by finding the items that delivery depends on right now, and I would make those accurate first. I would assign a temporary owner for each area, even if the long term owner is still unclear. I would use Item Tracking to give every item a status, so live, unsure, and retired items are easy to tell apart. I would run short Reviews with the delivery team to confirm the live set, and I would park the unsure items for later. I would rebuild the Glossary for the most confused terms first, because shared language reduces the most friction. I would update the set in small, visible batches, and I would share progress often. I would keep delivery moving on the confirmed items while the rest is cleaned. Over time, the set becomes trustworthy again, without a big freeze. Control comes from steady progress, not from one perfect cleanup."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-business-requirements"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-prioritize-requirements",
        title: "Prioritize Requirements",
        subtitle: "Put value first when everything claims to be urgent",
        kind: .task,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: .lifecycle,
        babokReference: "5.3",
        explanation: [
            "Prioritizing requirements means putting them in an order that reflects value, risk and dependency. The BA helps stakeholders decide what matters most, what can wait, and what should not be done at all. Not everything can be first, so ordering is a real decision, not a sorting exercise. A clear order lets the team start the most useful work first and stop guessing.",
            "Prioritization matters because capacity is always limited and demand always exceeds it. Without an order, teams start what is easiest or loudest, and high value work waits behind low value work. A good order also makes trade offs visible, so stakeholders choose with open eyes. Backlog Management keeps that order visible and current as new items arrive and old items change.",
            "Without prioritizing, every request arrives marked urgent and nothing is truly urgent. Teams split effort across too many items and finish little of value. A payment risk fix can wait behind a cosmetic report change because no one compared them side by side. Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria help here, because they state how value and success will be judged before ordering begins.",
            "Consider a payments backlog with a failed payment alert, a new report, and a small wording fix. The alert reduces manual chasing and customer harm, so it ranks first even though the report is more visible. The report ranks second because it helps managers, and the wording fix waits because it changes little. The BA records the reasons for the order, so a later challenge can be answered with evidence, not memory."
        ],
        outputName: "Prioritized requirements list",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Prioritization basis", guidance: "The criteria used: value, risk, dependency, and cost, with their relative weight."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Ordered list", guidance: "Requirements in priority order, with the reason for each position."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Dependencies", guidance: "What must come first, and what is blocked by what."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Trade offs", guidance: "What was deferred, and what the deferral costs."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Agreement", guidance: "Who agreed the order, and when it is revisited.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Agree the criteria before ordering anything. Value, risk, dependency, and cost are the usual criteria, and their relative weight is a decision, not an assumption. A list ordered without agreed criteria is ordered by who argued longest, and it will be reordered at the first challenge.",
            "Write the reason for each position, not just the order. When a requirement is deferred, write what the deferral costs and when it is revisited, so deferral is a decision with an owner, not a quiet disappearance. Revisit the order when conditions change: new risk, new information, or a dependency slipping all justify a fresh look."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: prioritized list for a payment hub change",
        workedExample: [
            "Basis: risk first, then value, then dependency. A requirement that prevents duplicate payments outranks a requirement that improves a report, because the cost of a duplicate is direct and immediate. Dependencies: the duplicate check must come before the new payment type it protects.",
            "Order: first, the duplicate detection requirement. Second, the exception queue ordering requirement. Third, the report improvement. Trade off: the report improvement is deferred to the next release, costing the operations team continued manual sorting for one more release, accepted by the operations lead. Agreement: the product owner and the operations lead agreed the order, to be revisited if the duplicate check slips."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Order by value, risk and dependency, not by who shouted loudest.",
            "Use Prioritization to compare items side by side with agreed criteria.",
            "Use Backlog Management to keep the order visible and current.",
            "Record the reason for the order so later challenges can be answered with evidence."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-08", title: "Everything was urgent until value was ranked", takeaway: "Rank by value, risk and dependency, and record the reasons so the order holds.", story: [
            "A payments backlog held twenty items for one release.\nEvery requester had marked their item as urgent.\nThe BA was asked to produce a workable order.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA listed value, risk and dependency for each item\n→ Items were compared side by side in one session\n→ The risk fix for failed payments ranked first\n→ Three nice to have items moved to a later release\n→ Requesters agreed the order with reasons recorded",
            "The request said:\n\"All of these are urgent and all must ship together.\"",
            "So the team delivered the top ranked items in order and the release went out on time, and the deferred items were planned for the next release with clear reasons, and no requester felt ignored because the ordering logic was visible to all.",
            "Why?",
            "Urgent is a feeling, not an order. Prioritization turns feelings into comparisons by asking what value each item brings, what risk it removes, and what depends on it. When stakeholders see items side by side, most accept that some work must wait. The recorded reasons protect the order when pressure returns later.",
            "An order with reasons survives pressure better than an order with labels.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Compare items side by side, never one at a time in isolation\n→ Use value, risk and dependency as the ordering lenses\n→ Record the reason for each placement, not only the rank\n→ Revisit the order when new items arrive",
            "Have you ever faced a backlog where everything was marked urgent?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-09", title: "The loudest voice lost to the evidence", takeaway: "Same criteria for every item lets evidence, not volume, set the order.", story: [
            "A senior requester pressed for a new dashboard.\nA quieter team needed a reconciliation fix.\nThe BA was asked to recommend the order.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA gathered the case for both items in writing\n→ The dashboard offered visibility, but no risk reduction\n→ The fix removed daily manual work and error risk\n→ Both cases were shown side by side to the group\n→ The group agreed the fix should go first",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"The dashboard is the priority, we need visibility now.\"",
            "So the reconciliation fix was delivered first and the daily manual work fell away, and the dashboard followed in the next cycle with better data behind it, and the senior requester accepted the order because the evidence had been shown openly rather than decided behind closed doors.",
            "Why?",
            "Volume is not value. Prioritization protects quiet but important work by judging every item on the same criteria. When the BA presents evidence calmly and side by side, seniority stops deciding the order and value starts deciding it. The BA does not need to win an argument. The BA needs to make the comparison fair and visible.",
            "Judge every item on the same criteria, whatever the volume behind it.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Gather each case in writing before comparing\n→ Use the same criteria for every item, large or small\n→ Show the comparison openly, do not decide in private\n→ Confirm the deferred item has a real later slot",
            "What would you do when a loud request competes with quiet but vital work?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-10", title: "A small fix outranked a large feature", takeaway: "Order by user effect per effort, not by size or demo appeal.", story: [
            "A large new feature and a small fix competed for one slot.\nThe feature looked impressive in a demo.\nThe fix looked dull on paper.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA checked what each item would change for users\n→ The feature added a new report few people had asked for\n→ The fix stopped a repeated payment failure for many users\n→ Effort was small for the fix and large for the feature\n→ The group ranked the fix first on value per effort",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will build the feature, it shows progress best.\"",
            "So the small fix was delivered quickly and the repeated failures stopped, and the feature was redesigned with better input from users before it was built, and the team learned that visible progress is not the same as useful progress.",
            "Why?",
            "Size and shine can mislead ordering. A small fix that removes a repeated pain can matter more than a large feature that adds a rarely used option. Prioritization asks about effect per effort, not about demo appeal. When the BA keeps the focus on user effect, small high value work gets its fair place at the top.",
            "Value per effort beats demo appeal when ordering work.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Ask what changes for users, not how impressive the demo looks\n→ Weigh effect against effort for every item\n→ Challenge features with weak user demand before ranking them high\n→ Celebrate small fixes that remove repeated pain",
            "Have you ever seen a small fix matter more than a large feature?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-11", title: "The backlog that finally told the truth", takeaway: "Keep the backlog ordered by agreed criteria, and place new items by comparison.", story: [
            "A backlog held many items with no clear order.\nNew items were added at the top by whoever asked last.\nThe BA was asked to make the backlog usable again.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA reviewed every item with its owner\n→ Stale items were retired or rewritten clearly\n→ Remaining items were ordered by agreed criteria\n→ The order was shared where everyone could see it\n→ New items joined the order by comparison, not by arrival",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Just add mine at the top, it is the most important.\"",
            "So the backlog became a trusted order that the team planned from each cycle, and new requests were compared against the order instead of jumping it, and stakeholders stopped lobbying for the top slot because the ordering rules were clear and applied to everyone.",
            "Why?",
            "Backlog Management is ongoing care, not a one time sort. A backlog that reflects arrival order tells a false story about value. A backlog that reflects agreed criteria tells the truth about trade offs. When the rules are visible and consistent, stakeholders trust the order even when their item waits.",
            "A backlog should show the order of value, not the order of arrival.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Review and retire stale items before reordering\n→ Order by agreed criteria, and publish the criteria\n→ Place new items by comparison, not by arrival time\n→ Keep the backlog visible to all requesters",
            "What would you do to rebuild trust in a backlog no one believes?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-prioritize-requirements-q1", question: "You are the BA and two stakeholders both say their payment requirement is the top priority. How do you decide?", answer: [
            "I would not choose based on who asked first or who is more senior. I would bring both items together and compare them on the same criteria. I would ask each stakeholder to state the value, the risk of delay, and any dependency in plain words. I would write both cases side by side, so the comparison is visible and fair. I would then facilitate a short discussion where the group agrees the order, rather than deciding it alone. I would use Prioritization to keep the focus on outcomes, not on personal preference. If one item must wait, I would agree a clear later slot and record the reason. I would also check Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria for both items, so we know what done looks like. I would share the agreed order with both stakeholders in writing. My role is to make the trade off visible, so the decision is owned by the group, not imposed by me."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-prioritize-requirements-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you had to say no, or not yet, to a stakeholder request. How did you handle it?", answer: [
            "I would describe a request that was reasonable but could not fit the current release. I would start by thanking the stakeholder and confirming I understood the need. I would then show the current backlog order and explain what would have to move to make room. I would use Prioritization criteria, so the answer was about value and capacity, not about personal refusal. I would offer a clear alternative, such as a later slot or a smaller first step. I would record the request properly, so not yet did not become forgotten. I would agree the Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria for the future slot, so expectations were clear. I would follow up when the slot approached, to show the request was still valued. The stakeholder accepted the delay because the reasoning was open and the future slot was real. I learned that a clear not yet, with a reason and a date, builds more trust than a vague yes."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-prioritize-requirements-q3", question: "A senior stakeholder demands a low value feature jump the queue ahead of a risk fix. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I would stay calm and focus on evidence, not on rank. I would first confirm I understand why the feature matters to the stakeholder, and I would note their reasoning. I would then present the risk fix and the feature side by side, using the same Prioritization criteria for both. I would make the risk of delaying the fix explicit, in plain terms, including the manual work and the chance of error. I would ask the stakeholder to confirm they accept that risk if the feature goes first, and I would record the decision. I would also offer options, such as a smaller version of the feature or a split delivery. I would involve the accountable owner, so the trade off is decided at the right level. I would use Backlog Management to show the full effect on the order, not only the two items. If the decision still goes to the feature, I would record it clearly and plan the fix next. My duty is to make the trade off visible and owned."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-prioritize-requirements-q4", question: "How do you prioritize when value is clear but dependencies and risk point in different directions?", answer: [
            "I would start by mapping all three lenses separately, so nothing is hidden. I would score value by user effect, risk by harm if delayed, and dependency by what is blocked. I would then look for the item that unlocks the most other work, even if its own value is modest. I would discuss the tension openly with the team and the owners, using a simple visual order. I would use Prioritization to agree weights for value, risk, and dependency before ranking, so the debate is about principles, not about favourite items. I would check Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria to confirm what each item must achieve. I would propose a sequence that reduces risk early while still delivering value in each step. I would record the reasoning, including what was traded away. I would revisit the order as new facts arrive, because dependencies change. A good order is a living decision, not a fixed list."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-user-stories"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-assess-requirements-changes",
        title: "Assess Requirements Changes",
        subtitle: "Understand the impact before agreeing to change",
        kind: .task,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: .lifecycle,
        babokReference: "5.4",
        explanation: [
            "Assessing a requirements change means understanding its impact before anyone agrees to it. The BA asks what will change, what else is affected, what it will cost, and what risk it brings. The assessment is shared in plain language so the decision maker can choose with open eyes. A change without assessment is a guess, and guesses are expensive once build has started.",
            "Assessment matters because every change has a footprint that is larger than the request itself. A new field can affect screens, reports, tests, training and operations procedures. Item Tracking helps the BA follow those links and size the footprint honestly. Reviews add a second view, so an impact the BA missed can still be caught before approval.",
            "Without assessment, changes slip in quietly and scope grows without a decision. Teams absorb small changes until the release date slips and no one can say why. A payment rule changed in one journey can break another journey that shared the rule. Prioritization must be revisited too, because a new change may matter more or less than the work it displaces.",
            "Consider a request to move a payment cut off time by one hour. The BA traces the time to the payment journey, the batch schedule, the customer message and the operations rota. Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria are checked to see whether the new time still meets the agreed service promise. Only then can the owner decide whether the benefit is worth the disruption, with the full impact in view."
        ],
        outputName: "Requirements change assessment",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Change requested", guidance: "What is requested, by whom, and why."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Impact on requirements", guidance: "Which requirements change, which are added, and which are retired."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Impact on design, build, and test", guidance: "What else moves: design, interfaces, test cases, training, and documentation."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Impact on schedule and cost", guidance: "The effect on dates and effort, with the basis for the estimate."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Recommendation", guidance: "Accept, defer, or reject, with the reason and the risks of each option.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Assess the change through the trace, not from memory. Follow the links from the affected requirements to design, build, and test, and list everything that moves. A change that looks small in one requirement often moves several tests and one interface, and the assessment must say so plainly.",
            "State the recommendation with its reason and its risks. Accepting, deferring, and rejecting each carry a cost, and the decision maker needs all three spelled out. Record the decision, the reason, and the date, so the same change is never reassessed from scratch because no one remembers why it was deferred."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: change assessment for a new payment status value",
        workedExample: [
            "Change requested: add a new status value for payments held for review, requested by operations, so held payments are visible in the queue. Impact on requirements: two requirements change, one is added. Impact on design and test: the status display, the queue filter, and six test scenarios change. The interface already carries the status, so no interface change is needed.",
            "Schedule and cost: two days of build and one day of test, based on the last similar status change. Recommendation: accept, because the change is small and the operational benefit is immediate. Risk of deferral: held payments stay invisible for another release, and customer queries continue to need manual checks."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Assess impact on scope, cost, risk and linked items before agreeing any change.",
            "Use Item Tracking to find every item the change touches.",
            "Use Reviews to catch impacts the first assessment missed.",
            "Revisit Prioritization after a change, because the order may need to move."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-12", title: "The cut-off change that touched everything", takeaway: "Trace a change to every linked item before promising a date.", story: [
            "A request arrived to move a payment cut off time.\nIt sounded like a simple settings change.\nThe BA assessed the impact before agreeing.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA traced the cut off time to linked items\n→ The batch schedule and customer message both used it\n→ The operations rota depended on the same time\n→ Testing would need to cover the full day cycle\n→ The true footprint was far larger than the request",
            "The request said:\n\"Just move the cut off time, it is one setting.\"",
            "So the BA shared the full impact with the requester and the owner, and the owner chose a later date that allowed all linked items to change together, and the change landed cleanly without surprising operations or customers.",
            "Why?",
            "A setting is rarely only a setting when it is shared. Assessment reveals the linked screens, schedules and procedures that depend on one value. When the footprint is visible, the owner can choose a date and a plan that fit reality. When it stays hidden, a simple change becomes a chain of surprises.",
            "Size the footprint before promising the date.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Trace the changed item to every linked place first\n→ Include operations, messages and schedules, not only screens\n→ Share the footprint in plain language with the owner\n→ Agree a date that fits the full impact, not only the request",
            "Have you ever seen a simple setting change surprise a whole team?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-13", title: "A late change with a hidden cost", takeaway: "Make late change costs visible so deferral is a real, respected option.", story: [
            "A change request arrived two weeks before release.\nThe requester said it was essential and simple.\nThe BA was asked to assess it quickly.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA checked the linked tests and procedures\n→ The change would require retesting three journeys\n→ Training material would need a rewrite\n→ The release date would move by at least a week\n→ The benefit was real, but not urgent",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"We must have this change in this release.\"",
            "So the BA presented the cost and the benefit side by side, and the owner chose to defer the change to the next release, and the current release shipped on time with the change planned properly instead of rushed.",
            "Why?",
            "Late changes cost more because rework spreads across build, test and training. Assessment makes that cost visible while the choice is still open. The BA does not decide alone, but the BA must ensure the owner decides with the full cost in view. Deferring a good change is often wiser than rushing it and risking the release.",
            "A good change at the wrong time can still be the wrong choice.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Assess build, test, training and operations together\n→ Present cost and benefit side by side, in plain terms\n→ Offer deferral as a real option, with a real date\n→ Record the decision and the reason, whatever is chosen",
            "What would you do when a late change threatens a release date?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-14", title: "The small tweak that was not small", takeaway: "Trace fields downstream before estimating, because one field feeds many outputs.", story: [
            "A stakeholder asked for a small tweak to a payment field.\nThe tweak was described as a five minute job.\nThe BA checked the links before estimating.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The field fed two reports and one downstream file\n→ The downstream file had a fixed format\n→ A format change would need partner agreement\n→ The tweak also affected a validation rule\n→ The five minute job became a multi team change",
            "The request said:\n\"It is only a small tweak to one field.\"",
            "So the BA shared the linked impact and the team agreed a phased approach, and the validation rule was updated first while the file change was agreed separately, and no partner was surprised by a silent format change.",
            "Why?",
            "Fields rarely live alone. A field feeds reports, files, rules and checks, and each link adds cost and risk to a tweak. Assessment protects the team from optimistic estimates that ignore those links. It also protects partners and downstream users from changes they never agreed to. Honest sizing is a service to everyone, even when it disappoints the requester.",
            "Follow the field, because fields feed more than screens.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Trace fields to reports, files and rules before estimating\n→ Flag downstream and partner impacts early\n→ Phase the change when parts move at different speeds\n→ Never estimate from the screen alone",
            "Have you ever watched a five minute job grow into a multi team change?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-requirements-changes-q1", question: "You are the BA and a stakeholder asks for a change late in the build. What do you do first?", answer: [
            "I would first thank the stakeholder and capture the change clearly, in their own words. I would not promise a date or a yes at that point. I would then assess the impact using traceability, checking which screens, rules, tests, and procedures the change touches. I would talk to the build and test teams to size the effort honestly, including retesting. I would check the Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria to see whether the change still meets the agreed outcomes. I would present the impact, the cost, and the benefit side by side to the decision maker. I would offer clear options, such as now, next release, or a smaller first step. I would record the decision and the reason, whatever is chosen. I would also update the requirements and the links, so the record stays true. My first duty is clarity, not speed. A clear assessment helps the owner choose well."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-requirements-changes-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you assessed a change and recommended against it. How did you present your case?", answer: [
            "I would describe a change that sounded helpful but carried hidden cost. I would start by showing I understood the need behind the request, so the stakeholder felt heard. I would then present my impact assessment in plain language, covering scope, effort, risk, and linked items. I would use traceability to show every place the change would touch, including tests and operations steps. I would compare the benefit with the cost, side by side, without exaggerating either side. I would offer an alternative, such as a smaller change or a later slot, so my recommendation was not only a no. I would invite the team to challenge my assessment in a short Review, so the view was shared. I would let the owner decide, and I would support the decision once made. The owner agreed to defer the change, and the release stayed on track. I learned that evidence, presented calmly, earns more trust than strong opinions."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-requirements-changes-q3", question: "A stakeholder insists a change is free because the code is not finished yet. How do you assess it honestly?", answer: [
            "I would acknowledge the point, because unfinished code can sometimes absorb change more cheaply. I would then check the full footprint, not only the code. I would look at the design, the tests, the Data Dictionary, the messages, and the operations steps. I would ask the build team what rework the change creates, even in unfinished code. I would ask the test team what retesting is needed, because new paths need new coverage. I would use Item Tracking to list every linked item, so nothing is missed. I would present the assessment as a range, not as a false precise figure, and I would state my assumptions. I would compare the change with the work it displaces, using Prioritization. If the change is genuinely cheap, I would say so. If it is not, I would show why, with evidence. Honest assessment means checking the whole footprint, not only the part that looks free."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-requirements-changes-q4", question: "How do you assess a change that affects shared payment rules used by several teams and several journeys?", answer: [
            "I would start by mapping the shared rule to every team and journey that uses it, using traceability as the backbone. I would bring the affected teams together early, so no team learns about the change late. I would ask each team to state the impact on its own journey, including screens, tests, files, and procedures. I would consolidate those views into one impact assessment, with conflicts and gaps called out clearly. I would use a Review to test the consolidated view before it goes to the owner. I would check Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria for each journey, because one shared change can pass in one journey and fail in another. I would propose options, such as one joint change, a phased change, or separate rules where sharing no longer fits. I would record the decision, the owner, and the agreed test coverage. Shared rules need shared assessment, or one team pays for another team change."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-impact-assessment"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-approve-requirements",
        title: "Approve Requirements",
        subtitle: "Get a clear yes from the right owners",
        kind: .task,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: .lifecycle,
        babokReference: "5.5",
        explanation: [
            "Approving requirements means confirming that the set is complete, correct and ready to guide the next stage of work. The BA seeks agreement from the people who own the need and who can commit to the scope. Approval is not a formality at the end of analysis. It is the moment when stakeholders accept the requirements as the agreed basis for build and testing.",
            "Approval matters because it creates a baseline that everyone can rely on. Once approved, the set becomes the reference point for change, testing and acceptance. The Roles and Permissions Matrix helps here, because it shows who may approve each area and prevents approval by the wrong person. Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria support approval too, because they state how success will be judged before work begins.",
            "Without proper approval, teams build from drafts that keep shifting. A quiet nod in a meeting is treated as agreement, then challenged weeks later when the cost is high. A person without authority approves a payment limit, and the real owner rejects it at release. Reviews before approval reduce this risk, because they let stakeholders check the set while changes are still cheap.",
            "Consider approval for new payment limits and alert rules. The BA confirms the owner for limits, the owner for alerts, and the owner for operations procedures. Each owner reviews the relevant requirements and the acceptance criteria, then records approval with the date and any conditions. Later changes go through change assessment against that baseline, so scope drift is visible and owned."
        ],
        outputName: "Requirements approval record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Requirements approved", guidance: "The set approved, with identifiers and version."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Review evidence", guidance: "Who reviewed, what was checked, and what was corrected."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Approvers", guidance: "Who approved, their role, and the date."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Conditions", guidance: "Any conditions attached to the approval, with owners and dates."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Change rule", guidance: "What happens after approval: the change control route for any further change.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Approval follows review, and review needs evidence. Record who reviewed the requirements, what they checked, and what was corrected, so approval rests on a checked pack rather than on trust alone. The approver should be able to see the review evidence before deciding, not after.",
            "Write the conditions down. An approval with unwritten conditions will be remembered differently by everyone involved. State the change rule in the same record: after approval, any change follows change control, so the approved set stays the agreed baseline and no change slips in unrecorded."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: approval record for a payment initiation pack",
        workedExample: [
            "Requirements approved: PI-001 to PI-014, version 2, covering payment initiation, validation, and error handling. Review evidence: the operations lead reviewed the handling steps, the technology lead reviewed the interface requirements, and three corrections were made and recorded.",
            "Approvers: the product owner approved on the agreed date. Conditions: one error message wording is subject to a compliance check within five working days, owned by the analyst. Change rule: any change after this approval is raised as a change request and assessed before it is accepted."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Approval must come from the people who own the need and can commit to the scope.",
            "Use the Roles and Permissions Matrix to confirm who may approve each area.",
            "Use Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria so approvers judge success before work begins.",
            "Record approval with date and conditions, and treat the approved set as the baseline."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-15", title: "Approval from the wrong person unravelled", takeaway: "Check authority before seeking approval, because availability is not authority.", story: [
            "A set of payment limit requirements was approved quickly.\nThe approver was helpful and available.\nThe real owner was on leave at the time.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA sought approval to keep the plan on track\n→ A nearby manager approved the limits in good faith\n→ Build started from the approved set\n→ The real owner returned and rejected the limits\n→ Three weeks of build had to be revisited",
            "The plan said:\n\"Get approval from anyone available, do not wait.\"",
            "So the BA went back to the real owner with the full set and the reasons behind each limit, and the limits were re-agreed correctly, and the team rebuilt the affected work from a valid baseline, and the delay was longer than waiting would have been.",
            "Why?",
            "Approval is only valid when it comes from the person who owns the decision. Availability is not authority. The Roles and Permissions Matrix exists to prevent this exact mistake by naming the approver for each area. Waiting for the right approver feels slow, but rebuilding from a false approval is far slower.",
            "Only the owner of the decision can approve it.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Confirm the approver from the Roles and Permissions Matrix\n→ Wait for the right owner, do not substitute availability\n→ Record who approved, what was approved, and when\n→ If the owner is away, agree a delegate in advance",
            "Have you ever seen work unravel because the wrong person approved it?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-16", title: "The sign off that meant nothing", takeaway: "Seek informed approval through review, not a fast signature on unread pages.", story: [
            "A stakeholder signed off a long requirement set.\nThe sign off arrived within an hour of receipt.\nThe BA doubted the set had been read.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The set ran to many pages with detailed rules\n→ Approval arrived with no questions or comments\n→ Build started from the signed set\n→ Gaps appeared in testing that review would have caught\n→ The stakeholder said the set was never properly checked",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Approved, please proceed as planned.\"",
            "So the BA paused and ran a focused review session on the riskiest rules, and the stakeholder raised real issues that had been missed, and the set was corrected and re-approved with genuine understanding, and testing went far more smoothly afterwards.",
            "Why?",
            "A fast signature is not the same as informed approval. Approvers need time and focus to check what they are agreeing to, especially for detailed rules. The BA serves the approver by highlighting risks, walking through key decisions, and inviting challenge. Genuine approval takes longer up front, but it prevents the far longer pain of late discovery.",
            "Informed approval needs time, focus and challenge.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Give approvers time and a clear review path\n→ Walk through the riskiest rules, do not only send pages\n→ Invite questions and record the answers\n→ Treat silent approval as a risk, not as a win",
            "What would you do when approval arrives too fast to be real?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-17", title: "Approval with conditions saved the release", takeaway: "Conditional approval works only when conditions are recorded, owned and tracked to closure.", story: [
            "An approver agreed the requirements with two conditions.\nThe conditions were noted in the meeting.\nThe BA was asked to record them properly.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Approval was given, subject to two open checks\n→ One check concerned a payment limit value\n→ The other concerned an operations procedure\n→ The BA recorded both conditions with owners and dates\n→ Both checks were closed before build finished",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Approved, provided those two checks are closed.\"",
            "So build proceeded on the approved baseline while the two checks were tracked to closure, and no one later claimed the conditions had been forgotten, and the release went ahead with the conditions met and the approval fully honoured.",
            "Why?",
            "Conditional approval is common and useful, but only when conditions are recorded and tracked. An unrecorded condition becomes a dispute later, with each side remembering it differently. Recording the condition, the owner and the due date turns a verbal caveat into managed work. The BA protects both the approver and the team by keeping conditions visible until they close.",
            "Record conditions with owners and dates, or they will be forgotten.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Record every condition at the moment of approval\n→ Give each condition an owner and a due date\n→ Track conditions to closure before release\n→ Confirm closure with the approver in writing",
            "Have you ever seen an approval condition forgotten after the meeting?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RL-18", title: "The baseline that stopped scope drift", takeaway: "Use the approved baseline to separate corrections from new ideas, and decide each visibly.", story: [
            "A payments project had an approved requirement set.\nNew requests arrived every week during build.\nThe BA was asked to protect the release scope.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Each new request was compared with the baseline\n→ Some requests were genuine corrections to the baseline\n→ Others were new ideas that belonged in a later release\n→ Corrections went through change assessment\n→ New ideas were logged for future planning",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will hold the approved scope for this release.\"",
            "So the release shipped the approved scope on time, and the new ideas were planned properly for later releases instead of creeping into the current build, and stakeholders trusted the process because every request received a clear answer and a clear home.",
            "Why?",
            "A baseline gives the BA a fair way to judge new requests. Without it, every request feels equally urgent and scope drifts silently. With it, the BA can separate corrections from new ideas and handle each properly. The baseline does not freeze thinking. It ensures change is decided, not absorbed, and that the release promise stays honest.",
            "A baseline turns scope drift into visible, owned decisions.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Compare every new request with the approved baseline\n→ Treat corrections and new ideas differently\n→ Assess corrections through change control\n→ Log new ideas visibly for future planning",
            "What would you do to protect an approved scope without blocking good ideas?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-approve-requirements-q1", question: "A stakeholder approves requirements without reading them. How do you handle it?", answer: [
            "I would not accept a signature I do not trust, but I would handle it with respect. I would first make review easier, by highlighting the riskiest rules and the key decisions. I would offer a short walkthrough, focused on the areas where the stakeholder view matters most. I would ask direct questions, such as whether the payment limits and the failure paths match their intent. I would record their answers, so approval rests on real discussion. I would use a Review to structure the session, with time for challenge. I would share the Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria early, so the stakeholder knows how success will be judged. If the stakeholder still cannot give time, I would agree a delegate or a staged approval, rather than a blank yes. I would record who approved what, and when. My goal is informed approval, not a fast signature. Genuine approval protects the stakeholder as much as the team."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-approve-requirements-q2", question: "You are the BA and two approvers disagree about a payment requirement. How do you get a clear approval?", answer: [
            "I would first understand the disagreement in plain terms, without taking sides. I would meet each approver briefly to hear their reasoning and their concerns. I would then bring both views together in one short session, with the requirement and its options written clearly. I would use the Roles and Permissions Matrix to confirm who owns the final decision for this area. I would present the impact of each option, including effects on users, operations, and testing. I would use Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria to test each option against agreed outcomes. I would facilitate the discussion toward one agreed wording, and I would record the decision with its reason. If agreement is still not possible, I would escalate with a clear summary of both views and my recommendation. I would keep the tone calm and factual throughout, so the disagreement stays about the work. I would not record approval until one clear position exists. A contested approval is worse than a delayed one."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-approve-requirements-q3", question: "How do you manage approval when requirements must be approved in stages, and later stages reveal issues in earlier approvals?", answer: [
            "I would plan staged approval from the start, with each stage having a clear scope and owner. I would record each stage approval as a baseline, with its date and any conditions. When a later stage reveals an issue in an earlier stage, I would raise it openly and quickly. I would use traceability to show which earlier items are affected, so the impact is clear. I would assess the change through the normal change process, rather than quietly editing the earlier baseline. I would bring the earlier approver back into the discussion, with the new evidence presented plainly. I would use a Review to agree the correction and its effect on later stages. I would update the baseline and record the new approval, keeping the history visible. I would also check whether other stages need the same correction. Staged approval works when each stage is respected, and when corrections are owned, not hidden."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-approve-requirements-q4", question: "A senior leader pressures you to record approval that was never properly given. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I would stay calm, polite, and firm, because approval records must be truthful. I would explain what proper approval means in this case, and who needs to give it. I would offer to get that approval quickly, by arranging a short review with the right owner. I would show the Roles and Permissions Matrix to confirm who holds the authority. I would explain the risk of a false record, including rework, dispute, and loss of trust. I would not record approval that did not happen, even under pressure. I would offer alternatives, such as recording that approval is pending, or recording a conditional position that is honestly stated. I would document the request and my response factually, without emotion. If pressure continues, I would escalate through the proper channel with the facts. My duty is to the integrity of the record. A BA who records false approval loses the trust that the whole role depends on."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-brd-fsd"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-analyze-current-state",
        title: "Analyze Current State",
        subtitle: "Understand Where the Organisation Stands Today",
        kind: .task,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: .strategy,
        babokReference: "6.1",
        explanation: [
            "Analysing the current state means building an honest picture of how work happens today, before anyone proposes a change. The business analyst gathers evidence about processes, systems, data, people, and customer experience, and checks what the organisation can actually do well today. Business Capability Analysis helps here, because it looks at what the organisation must be able to do, rather than starting with systems or job titles. The aim is a shared, evidence-based view that everyone can agree is true, even when the truth is uncomfortable.",
            "This work matters because every future plan rests on it. If the starting point is wrong, the gap looks smaller or larger than it really is, and the change plan will be built on guesswork. SWOT Analysis is useful at this stage, because it forces a balanced look at internal strengths and weaknesses alongside external opportunities and threats. A clear current state also stops teams from fixing a problem that does not exist, or from missing a strength they could build on.",
            "Without this step, teams work from memory and habit. They assume the documented process is the real process, they overlook manual workarounds, and they miss the informal checks that keep payments moving. Benchmarking and Market Analysis can expose these blind spots, by showing how similar organisations handle the same work and where expectations have moved on. The cost shows up later, in rework, in missed dependencies, and in solutions that do not fit the work people actually do.",
            "Consider a bank that wants faster onboarding for corporate clients who make bulk payments. On paper, onboarding takes five steps. In practice, the operations team adds three manual checks, waits for documents by email, and rekeys data into a second system. A business analyst who observes the real work, talks to the people who do it, and reviews recent cases will find the true delays. That evidence turns a vague complaint about slowness into a clear list of causes the team can actually address."
        ],
        outputName: "Current state analysis",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Scope", guidance: "The process, systems, and teams covered, and what is excluded."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "How work happens today", guidance: "The process as it really runs, including waits, workarounds, and exceptions."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Capabilities", guidance: "What the organisation can do well today, and where it struggles."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Evidence", guidance: "The sources: observations, data, documents, and interviews, with dates."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Problems and causes", guidance: "The problems found, with their causes, separated from symptoms."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Strengths to keep", guidance: "What works well and must not be lost in the change.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Build the picture from evidence, not from the org chart or the documented process alone. Observe the work, read real cases, and ask the people who do the work daily. The documented process and the real process often differ, and the difference is where the problems live.",
            "Separate symptoms from causes, and record strengths as carefully as problems. A current state that lists only problems invites a change that breaks what works. End with a picture everyone can agree is true, even when the truth is uncomfortable, because every future plan rests on this starting point."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: current state for payment exception handling",
        workedExample: [
            "Scope: exception handling for cross border payments, from the first failed check to resolution, covering operations and the payment hub. How work happens today: exceptions arrive in a shared queue with no age ordering, officers check status by phone and email, and the same exception is often worked twice by different officers.",
            "Evidence: observation of the queue over five working days, interviews with three officers, and the exception log for the previous month. Problems: no age ordering, no single owner per exception, and no status summary. Cause: the queue was built for a lower volume and never redesigned. Strength to keep: officers resolve complex cases well once they own them, and that expertise must be kept in the new design."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Start from evidence, not from the documented process alone.",
            "Cover processes, systems, data, people, and customer experience together.",
            "Name strengths as clearly as weaknesses, so the future state builds on what works.",
            "Agree the current state with stakeholders before discussing solutions."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-01", title: "The Process Map That Missed the Real Work", takeaway: "The documented process is a starting point, not the truth.", story: [
            "A payments operations team handled exception cases every day.\nThe documented process showed four clear steps.\nThe team said the work felt much harder than the map suggested.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA reviewed the process map and recent case volumes\n→ The BA sat with the team and watched live cases for two days\n→ Three extra manual checks appeared that were not on the map\n→ Staff copied details between systems to work around missing data\n→ The real end to end time was double the documented time",
            "The request said:\n\"Please automate the four step process as documented.\"",
            "So the BA paused the automation request and shared the observed work alongside the map, and the team agreed the map had to be corrected first, which changed what automation would need to cover.",
            "Why?",
            "A process map is a claim about how work happens, not proof. Manual checks and workarounds often grow quietly, because people solve small problems locally and never update the record. When the BA checks the map against live work, the true current state appears, and the change can be sized honestly. Automating a wrong map only makes the wrong work faster.",
            "Check the real work before you improve the documented work.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Observe live work, do not rely only on documents and interviews\n→ Ask what happens when the normal path fails\n→ Record workarounds as facts, not as faults\n→ Agree the corrected current state before proposing a fix",
            "Have you ever found a hidden step that changed the whole picture?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-02", title: "Two Teams Doing the Same Check Twice", takeaway: "Map what must be done, then decide who should do it.", story: [
            "A corporate client complained about slow payment approvals.\nTwo separate teams both checked the same payment details.\nNeither team knew the other was doing the same check.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA mapped capabilities instead of starting with job titles\n→ Both teams listed payment validation as part of their work\n→ The checks used slightly different rules and different data\n→ Clients waited while the same payment queued twice\n→ Errors slipped through when each team assumed the other had checked",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Each team owns its own checks, so there is no overlap.\"",
            "So the BA showed both capability lists side by side, and the managers agreed to one shared check with clear ownership, which removed a full day from the approval time.",
            "Why?",
            "Business Capability Analysis looks at what must be done, not who happens to do it today. When two teams claim the same capability, the overlap stays hidden inside team boundaries. Naming capabilities in plain language makes duplication visible, and it makes ownership a decision rather than an accident. The fix is often simpler than a new system: one check, one owner, one set of rules.",
            "One capability with two owners is often no owner at all.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ List capabilities before listing teams or systems\n→ Ask who else does this work, in different words\n→ Compare rules and data, not just team names\n→ Agree a single owner for each shared capability",
            "What would you do if two teams both insisted the check was theirs?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-03", title: "A Strength That Was Hiding a Weakness", takeaway: "Test strengths as hard as weaknesses before you scale them.", story: [
            "A retail payments team was proud of its speed.\nVolumes were high and complaints were low.\nLeaders wanted to grow volume further on the same model.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA ran a balanced review of strengths and weaknesses\n→ Speed depended on a small group of experienced staff\n→ Knowledge of exception handling lived in memory, not in guides\n→ New joiners took months to reach the same speed\n→ A single absence could slow the whole queue",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will build on our speed and add more volume.\"",
            "So the team slowed the growth plan and first recorded the exception knowledge, cross trained two more staff, and simplified the trickiest checks, which protected speed before adding volume.",
            "Why?",
            "SWOT Analysis only helps when strengths are tested honestly. A strength that depends on a few people is also a weakness, because it does not scale and it does not survive absence or turnover. Naming that risk early is not negative thinking. It is the difference between growth that holds and growth that breaks the very thing that made the team successful.",
            "A strength that depends on memory alone will not scale.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Test every strength: what would break it?\n→ Ask where knowledge lives when experts are away\n→ Separate real capability from heroic effort\n→ Fix fragility before adding volume",
            "Have you ever seen a team strength that quietly depended on one person?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-04", title: "Why Rivals Settled Payments Faster", takeaway: "Judge the current state against the market, not only against habit.", story: [
            "A bank offered business payments with next day settlement.\nClients began asking why settlement took so long.\nThe internal team believed next day was normal.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA compared settlement times with similar providers\n→ Several peers offered same day settlement for similar payments\n→ Client expectations had moved while internal habits had not\n→ The delay came from batch cut off times, not from checks\n→ Front line staff had no clear answer when clients asked why",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Our process has always worked this way, so clients accept it.\"",
            "So the BA shared the comparison with leaders, and the team reviewed cut off times and batching, and gave front line staff a clear explanation to use while a faster option was explored.",
            "Why?",
            "Benchmarking and Market Analysis pulls the current state out of internal habit and into the wider market. A process can be stable, familiar, and still fall behind what clients now expect. The BA does not need to copy others blindly. The point is to see the gap clearly, understand what causes it, and decide deliberately whether to close it.",
            "Normal inside the building is not the same as normal in the market.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Compare with similar providers, not only with last year\n→ Ask clients what they expect now, not what they accepted before\n→ Find the real cause of the gap before proposing change\n→ Give front line staff words they can use honestly",
            "What would you do if the market had moved and your team had not noticed?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-05", title: "The Report Said All Was Well", takeaway: "Check how measures are built before you trust them as a baseline.", story: [
            "A monthly report showed payment processing in good health.\nGreen indicators covered volumes, speed, and errors.\nYet corporate clients kept raising the same complaints.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA reviewed the report definitions behind each indicator\n→ Speed was measured only for payments that completed first time\n→ Failed and retried payments were excluded from the average\n→ Client complaints clustered around retries and unclear status\n→ The report hid the exact experience clients were describing",
            "The request said:\n\"Use the monthly report as the current state baseline.\"",
            "So the BA rebuilt the baseline to include retries, waiting time, and status clarity, and the new picture showed where clients actually struggled, which gave the improvement work a honest starting point.",
            "Why?",
            "Measures shape what people see. A report can be accurate on its own definitions and still mislead, if it leaves out the cases that matter most to clients. The BA must ask how each number is built, what is excluded, and whose experience it represents. A current state built on flattering measures will produce a future state that solves the wrong problem.",
            "A green report is not the same as a good experience.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Ask how each measure is defined and what it excludes\n→ Include failed and retried cases in the baseline\n→ Test reports against real client complaints\n→ Agree measures that reflect the client journey",
            "Have you ever trusted a report that did not match what clients said?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-analyze-current-state-q1", question: "You are the BA and three stakeholders describe the current process in three different ways. How do you establish what is really happening today?", answer: [
            "I would start by treating each description as a clue, not as the truth. I would ask each stakeholder to walk me through a recent real case, step by step, and I would note where their versions agree and where they differ. Then I would observe the work directly where I could, and I would review recent cases, handoffs, and evidence such as queues, logs, and client messages. I would also check the current state against the documented process, and I would record workarounds separately so they are visible rather than hidden. Once I had a draft picture, I would take it back to the people who do the work and to the stakeholders, and I would ask them to correct it together. My aim would be one agreed picture that everyone recognises, even if it shows problems. I would keep the language plain, I would separate facts from opinions, and I would not move on to solutions until the starting point was agreed. That shared baseline makes later decisions faster, because people stop arguing about today and start planning tomorrow."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-analyze-current-state-q2", question: "Tell me about a time when you had to understand a process or area you knew nothing about. How did you get up to speed?", answer: [
            "I would start by being honest about what I did not know, and I would ask for a simple end to end walkthrough before I read detailed documents. I like to follow one real case from start to finish, because that shows me the handoffs, the waits, and the checks in the order they really happen. I would ask short, open questions, such as what happens next, what happens when it fails, and who decides. I would build a simple process view as I learned, and I would check it with the people who do the work, so mistakes get fixed early. I would also read recent examples, look at the data involved, and note the terms people use, so I do not misread the work. I would keep a list of questions I could not yet answer, and I would close them one by one rather than guessing. By the end, I would test my understanding by explaining the process back in plain words. If the team says that sounds right, I know I have a fair starting point to work from."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-analyze-current-state-q3", question: "A stakeholder says the documented process is accurate, but the people doing the work say it is out of date. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I would not take sides, and I would not argue about who is right. I would suggest we test the document against recent real work, together, so the evidence decides. I would pick a small set of recent cases and trace them through the documented steps, noting every extra check, wait, and workaround. I would use observation where possible, because watching the work often reveals steps that interviews miss. Then I would present the findings as a comparison, showing the documented path next to the observed path, with no blame attached. I would invite the stakeholder and the team to agree the corrected current state, and I would record why the document drifted, so the fix lasts. If the stakeholder still doubts the findings, I would ask what evidence would persuade them, and I would gather that next. My job is to make the truth easy to see, not to win an argument. Once everyone agrees on today, we can have a useful conversation about what should change."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-analyze-current-state-q4", question: "A stakeholder says current state work is a waste of time and wants to jump straight to a solution. How do you respond?", answer: [
            "I would acknowledge the urgency, because nobody wants analysis for its own sake. Then I would explain the risk in plain terms: if we do not know the starting point, we can build the wrong fix, miss a dependency, or automate a broken step. I would propose a short, focused current state review, with a clear time limit and a clear output, so it does not feel open ended. I would focus only on the areas the solution would touch, such as the process, the capabilities, the data, and the client impact. I would show how this work saves time later, by preventing rework and by giving the solution a fair test. If the stakeholder still wants to move fast, I would agree the smallest set of facts we must check first, and I would record the assumptions we are accepting. That way the decision is explicit, not accidental. I would also share early findings quickly, so the stakeholder sees value, not delay. My goal is to protect the outcome while respecting the pressure to move."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-analyze-current-state-q5", question: "You discover the current state shows a much deeper capability gap than the sponsor expected. How do you share that news?", answer: [
            "I would prepare the evidence carefully before the conversation, so I am sharing facts, not impressions. I would map the gap using Business Capability Analysis, showing what the organisation can do today, what the goal requires, and where the distance is greatest. I would use simple visuals and real examples, such as a payment journey that needs three capabilities we do not yet have. I would present options, not just problems, including a narrower first step, a longer path, and what each would cost in time and effort. I would be direct about the impact, and I would avoid softening the message so much that the sponsor misses it. I would also name what is strong, so the conversation stays balanced and the team keeps confidence. Then I would ask the sponsor to choose the appetite for change, rather than asking them to accept my view without discussion. If they choose a smaller scope, I would help reshape the goal honestly. My duty is to tell the truth early, when it is still cheap to act on it."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-impact-assessment"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-define-future-state",
        title: "Define Future State",
        subtitle: "Describe a Future Worth Moving Towards",
        kind: .task,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: .strategy,
        babokReference: "6.2",
        explanation: [
            "Defining the future state means describing, in concrete terms, how the organisation should work after the change. The business analyst captures the outcomes to be achieved, the capabilities that must exist, the experience for clients and staff, and the constraints that cannot be ignored. Scope Modelling helps here, because it draws a clear boundary around what is in, what is out, and what connects to the change without being part of it. A good future state is specific enough to guide decisions, but open enough to allow different ways of getting there.",
            "This work matters because a vague vision invites different interpretations. Each stakeholder fills the gaps with their own hopes, and the project only discovers the differences when build work starts. The Business Model Canvas is useful at this stage, because it shows how value is created, delivered, and sustained, on one shared view. When everyone can point to the same picture of the future, trade-offs become discussions about the picture, not arguments about memory.",
            "Without a defined future state, teams drift towards solution shopping. They compare tools, copy what others have done, or rebuild the present with new technology. Financial Analysis belongs in this conversation too, because a future that looks attractive but cannot be afforded, staffed, or sustained is not a real option. The business analyst keeps the future honest by testing it against cost, effort, risk, and the capabilities found in the current state.",
            "Consider a bank that wants to offer faster payments to small businesses. A weak future state says only that payments will be faster and easier. A strong future state says which clients are served, which payment types are included, how quickly funds arrive, what clients see at each step, and which checks still apply. It also names what will stop, such as manual status calls and duplicate entry. That level of clarity lets the team judge whether a proposal truly delivers the future, or only sounds like it does."
        ],
        outputName: "Future state description",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Vision", guidance: "The outcome in one paragraph: what will be true when the change works."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Future process", guidance: "How work will happen, step by step, including exceptions."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Capabilities needed", guidance: "What the organisation must be able to do, and what is new or changed."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Constraints", guidance: "What must stay true: rules, limits, and non negotiable requirements."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Measures of success", guidance: "How the future state is judged, with baselines from the current state."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Out of scope", guidance: "What the future state deliberately does not cover.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write the future state as an outcome first, then as a process. The vision paragraph should be readable by anyone affected: what will be true, for whom, and why it matters. Then describe the future process at the same level of honesty as the current state, including exceptions and waits, or the future state will be a drawing of a happy path that reality will ignore.",
            "Name the constraints and the measures in the same document. Constraints stop the future state from promising what rules forbid. Measures, with baselines taken from the current state, stop the future state from being declared a success on opinion alone. State what is out of scope, so the future state is judged on what it promised, not on what it never covered."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: future state for exception handling",
        workedExample: [
            "Vision: every exception has one owner, an age, and a status summary, and no exception is worked twice. Future process: exceptions arrive in an ordered queue, the system assigns an owner, the officer sees the status summary and the next step, and the resolution is recorded against the exception.",
            "Capabilities needed: automatic assignment, age ordering, and a status summary drawn from the payment hub. Constraints: the handling steps must follow the existing control rules, and no exception is closed without a recorded resolution. Measures: time from arrival to first action, and the count of exceptions worked twice, both baselined from the current state log. Out of scope: changes to the payment hub validation rules."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Describe outcomes and capabilities, not just systems or features.",
            "Draw the boundary early, so in scope and out of scope stay visible.",
            "Test the future against cost, capacity, and the current state.",
            "Agree one shared picture before choosing how to get there."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-06", title: "A Vision Everyone Read Differently", takeaway: "Replace slogans with concrete outcomes everyone can test.", story: [
            "A programme aimed to modernise business payments.\nLeaders described the future in broad, inspiring words.\nEvery team heard a different promise in the same words.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA asked each team to describe the future in plain terms\n→ One team expected instant settlement for all payment types\n→ Another team expected better reporting with no process change\n→ A third team expected a new portal with the same back office work\n→ The differences only surfaced when delivery plans were compared",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will deliver a modern, seamless payments experience.\"",
            "So the BA facilitated a session to write one concrete future state, with outcomes, clients, payment types, and boundaries, and leaders approved the shared version before delivery replanned.",
            "Why?",
            "Broad words feel like agreement because everyone can nod at them. The disagreement hides inside the detail, where one word means different work to different teams. Writing the future state in concrete terms forces those differences into the open while they are still cheap to resolve. Agreement on paper that survives a detailed read is worth far more than applause for a slogan.",
            "A future everyone interprets differently is not a shared future.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Ask each team to describe the future without slogans\n→ Name clients, payment types, outcomes, and boundaries\n→ Compare delivery plans against one shared picture\n→ Get approval for the detailed version, not the headline",
            "Have you ever agreed a vision, only to find everyone meant something else?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-07", title: "Built for the Wrong Customer", takeaway: "Check who the future serves before you design how it looks.", story: [
            "A team designed a polished portal for large corporate clients.\nThe design assumed dedicated finance teams and complex needs.\nGrowth, however, was coming from smaller businesses.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA mapped client segments and what each valued most\n→ Large clients valued control, approvals, and detailed reporting\n→ Smaller businesses valued speed, simplicity, and clear status\n→ The portal design served the first group and confused the second\n→ Early feedback from smaller businesses was polite but unenthusiastic",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Our largest clients asked for this, so it must be the priority.\"",
            "So the BA shared a view of both segments, their needs, and where growth was coming from, and leaders agreed to simplify the core journey first, then add advanced controls as a second layer.",
            "Why?",
            "The Business Model Canvas forces the question of who the value is for. A future state built for one segment can actively harm another, even when the design is excellent on its own terms. The BA must check who benefits, who pays the cost of complexity, and whether the priority matches where value will actually come from. Designing for the loudest voice is not the same as designing for the most important need.",
            "A brilliant design for the wrong client is still the wrong design.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Name each client segment and what it values\n→ Check where growth and pain actually sit\n→ Test designs with the segment they must serve\n→ Sequence advanced features after the core journey works",
            "What would you do if the loudest client was not the future client?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-08", title: "Too Many Futures at Once", takeaway: "A visible boundary protects the future from quiet growth.", story: [
            "A future state for payments kept growing.\nEvery workshop added another area to the picture.\nThe boundary became so wide that no one could deliver it.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA listed every area named across the workshops\n→ Reporting, pricing, onboarding, and support all appeared\n→ Each area had its own owner and its own timeline\n→ The team could not say what the first release would contain\n→ Leaders grew uneasy but no one wanted to cut scope in public",
            "The request said:\n\"While we are changing payments, please include reporting, pricing, and onboarding too.\"",
            "So the BA drew a scope model showing the core change, the connected areas, and the excluded areas, and leaders agreed the core first, with connected areas handled as later phases.",
            "Why?",
            "Scope Modelling makes boundaries visible instead of leaving them to memory and goodwill. When everything is in scope, nothing has priority, and the team spreads thin across too many goals. A clear model lets leaders make the hard choice once, in the open, rather than forcing the team to make it daily under pressure. Saying not yet is not saying no forever.",
            "A future with no boundary is a promise that cannot be kept.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Draw what is in, connected, and out, on one view\n→ Ask what the first release must contain, and only that\n→ Record later phases so nothing feels lost\n→ Revisit the boundary when new requests arrive",
            "Have you ever watched scope grow until the goal disappeared?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-09", title: "A Future the Numbers Could Not Support", takeaway: "Test the future against running costs before you promise it.", story: [
            "A future state promised round the clock support.\nIt also promised instant processing for every payment type.\nThe vision was popular, and no one asked what it would cost.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA estimated running costs, not just build costs\n→ Night cover needed new shifts, training, and backup cover\n→ Instant processing for rare payment types cost far more than it saved\n→ The benefits were real but smaller than the running costs\n→ Leaders had assumed the future would pay for itself quickly",
            "The plan said:\n\"Cost is a detail we will sort out later.\"",
            "So the BA presented a simpler future that covered common payment types instantly and kept rare types on a fast same day path, which kept most of the benefit at a cost the business could sustain.",
            "Why?",
            "Financial Analysis is not about blocking ambition. It is about testing whether the future can survive contact with budgets, staffing, and time. A future state that only works if costs are ignored will be cut later, in a rushed and painful way. Testing early lets the team shape a future that keeps its best value and drops its most expensive extras, while the choice is still calm and deliberate.",
            "An unaffordable future is a wish, not a plan.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Estimate running costs, not only build costs\n→ Compare benefit and cost by payment type or segment\n→ Protect the core benefit, trim the expensive extras\n→ Present a sustainable alternative, not only a warning",
            "What would you do if a popular vision could not pay for itself?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-10", title: "The Small Pilot That Proved the Future", takeaway: "Prove the future in a small slice before you scale it.", story: [
            "Leaders doubted a new onboarding journey for clients.\nThey liked the idea but feared disrupting live work.\nNo one wanted to approve a full change on trust alone.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA proposed a small pilot with one client segment\n→ The pilot covered one payment type and a short time period\n→ Staff and clients gave feedback each week\n→ Two steps were simplified after the first week\n→ Completion time fell and client feedback improved",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"We cannot commit until we know it works.\"",
            "So leaders approved the wider rollout with confidence, because the pilot had proved the future in real work, and the team already knew which steps needed care.",
            "Why?",
            "A future state is a hypothesis until it meets real work. A small pilot tests the riskiest assumptions first, with limited harm if the idea fails. It also builds belief, because people trust what they have seen work more than what they have only heard described. The BA earns credibility by proposing a test that is small, honest, and easy to learn from.",
            "A small true test beats a large confident guess.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Pick one segment and one journey for the pilot\n→ Define what success looks like before starting\n→ Gather feedback weekly and act on it visibly\n→ Use pilot evidence to decide the wider rollout",
            "Have you ever changed a sceptical mind with a small pilot?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-future-state-q1", question: "You are the BA and a stakeholder describes the future state only as a new system they want to buy. How do you help them describe the outcome instead?", answer: [
            "I would thank them for the idea, and then I would ask what outcome the system should deliver. I would ask who it helps, what gets faster or simpler, and what success would look like for clients and for staff. Then I would help them describe the future state in plain terms, without naming a tool, so we can test different ways of getting there. I would capture the capabilities needed, the client experience, and the boundaries, including what will not change. I would also ask what should stop, because a future state that only adds work is rarely realistic. Once we have that picture, I would check it against the current state and ask whether the proposed system truly delivers it. If it does, we have a stronger case. If it does not, we have saved time and money. My aim is to move the conversation from a product to an outcome, while keeping the stakeholder engaged rather than dismissed."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-future-state-q2", question: "Two stakeholders want different future states for the same payments service. How do you bring them to one agreed picture?", answer: [
            "I would start by making both futures visible, side by side, in the same format. I would capture the outcomes, the clients served, the capabilities, and the boundaries for each view, using plain language and no jargon. Then I would look for where they agree, because shared ground is often larger than it first seems. For the differences, I would ask each stakeholder what problem their version solves, and what evidence supports it. I would use Decision Analysis thinking, by comparing options against agreed criteria such as client benefit, cost, risk, and fit with strategy. I would present the comparison to both stakeholders together, and I would ask them to choose or combine, rather than choosing for them. If they cannot agree, I would escalate with the evidence and the trade-offs clearly stated, so the decision maker can decide. My role is to make the choice clear, fair, and based on facts, not on who speaks loudest."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-future-state-q3", question: "How do you define a future state without designing the solution too early?", answer: [
            "I focus on the what and the why, and I leave the how open for as long as I can. I describe the future state in terms of outcomes, capabilities, client experience, and constraints, without naming a specific system or build approach. For example, I would say clients need clear payment status within minutes, rather than saying we need a particular dashboard tool. I use Scope Modelling to draw the boundary, so everyone can see what is included and what is not. I also test the future state against the current state, to show the gap in plain terms. When someone jumps to a solution, I capture it as an option, and I park it until the future state is agreed. Then we can judge each option fairly against the same picture. This keeps the conversation honest, and it stops the team from locking in a solution before the need is clear."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-future-state-q4", question: "You are the BA and the agreed future state looks good, but it ignores a major capability gap. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I would present the future state and the capability gap together, on one page, so the distance is impossible to miss. I would show the capabilities the future needs, the capabilities we have today, and the gap for each one, using simple ratings and real examples. Then I would offer honest paths, such as a narrower first phase, a longer timeline, or investment in people and process before technology. I would use Financial Analysis in simple terms, showing what each path costs to run, not just to build. I would ask the sponsor to choose the appetite for change, and I would record that choice, so later pressure does not quietly restore the full scope. I would also highlight quick wins that build capability while delivering value, so the team keeps momentum. My message would be direct but constructive: the future is possible, but only if we are honest about the steps. Hiding the gap now only makes it more expensive later."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-future-state-q5", question: "A sponsor wants an ambitious future state delivered in a timeframe you believe is unrealistic. How do you handle it?", answer: [
            "I would start by respecting the ambition, because the sponsor is trying to move fast for good reasons. Then I would test the future state against time, capacity, and dependencies, using evidence from the current state and from similar past work. I would build two or three honest options, such as a smaller scope on time, the full scope on a longer timeline, and a phased path that delivers value early. For each option I would show the trade-offs in plain terms: what clients get, what it costs, what risks rise, and what must be true for it to work. I would ask the sponsor to choose explicitly, rather than accepting an impossible plan and hoping it works out. If they insist on the original date, I would record the assumptions and risks in writing, and I would agree the earliest warning signs. I would also protect the team from silent scope growth by holding the agreed boundary. My duty is to be loyal to the outcome, not to the date, and to tell the truth while there is still time to act."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-business-requirements"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-assess-risks",
        title: "Assess Risks",
        subtitle: "Surface Risks Before They Surface Themselves",
        kind: .task,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: .strategy,
        babokReference: "6.3",
        explanation: [
            "Assessing risks means looking ahead for what could prevent the future state from working, harm clients, or damage trust. The business analyst helps stakeholders name risks in plain language, describe what could cause them, and explain what would happen if they occurred. Risk Analysis and Management provides the discipline for this work, by separating the risk itself from its causes and its effects. The aim is not to list fears, but to build a shared view of what needs attention, and what can be accepted.",
            "This work matters because risks do not disappear when they are ignored. They wait for the worst moment, such as a busy settlement window, a client migration, or a period when key staff are away. A good assessment weighs how likely each risk is, how serious the impact would be, and how soon it might appear. It also looks for risks that are connected, where one failure makes another more likely, which is common in payments where systems, data, and manual checks depend on each other.",
            "Without this step, teams confuse optimism with planning. They assume data will be clean, dependencies will arrive on time, and clients will behave as expected. Decision Analysis helps here, because it compares responses on their merits, such as avoiding the risk, reducing it, transferring it, or accepting it with eyes open. The business analyst makes sure each significant risk has an owner, a response, and a warning sign, so action starts early rather than after harm occurs.",
            "Consider a bank moving corporate clients to a new payment file format. The obvious risk is a failed file, but the deeper risks sit around it. Clients may use old templates, support teams may lack answers for new errors, and a single busy day could turn small failures into a queue of urgent cases. A business analyst who maps these risks with operations, technology, and client teams can agree early checks, clear client guidance, and a fallback plan. That preparation turns a risky migration into a managed change."
        ],
        outputName: "Risk assessment",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Risk list", guidance: "Each risk, written as cause, event, and consequence."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Likelihood and impact", guidance: "The judgement for each risk, with the basis for the judgement."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Risk owners", guidance: "One owner per risk, named by role."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Responses", guidance: "What is done about each risk: avoid, reduce, transfer, or accept, with actions."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Residual risks", guidance: "What remains after the responses, and who accepts it.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write each risk as a sentence with three parts: the cause, the event, and the consequence. A risk written as a single vague word, such as delay, cannot be owned or acted on. A risk written with its cause and consequence can be judged, owned, and reduced.",
            "Judge likelihood and impact on evidence, and say what the judgement rests on. A risk judged high with no basis is just worry in a table. Name one owner per risk, write the response as actions with dates, and record what remains after the response, so the residual risk is accepted knowingly, not discovered later."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: risk assessment for a payment hub migration",
        workedExample: [
            "Risk 1: because the new hub changes status handling, there is a risk that status reports are delayed, which would leave operations unable to answer customer queries. Likelihood judged medium, based on the test plan covering status handling late. Impact judged high, because customer queries would need manual checks. Owner: the technology lead.",
            "Response: bring status handling tests forward, and prepare a manual status check procedure as a fallback. Residual risk: a short delay in status reports during the first week, accepted by the product owner. Risk 2: because cut off times differ by payment type, there is a risk that a payment misses its cut off during migration, which would delay settlement by one working day. Response: a cut off checklist per payment type, checked before each migration step."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Name the risk, its cause, and its effect separately.",
            "Weigh likelihood, impact, and timing together, not likelihood alone.",
            "Give every significant risk an owner, a response, and a warning sign.",
            "Review risks often, because new information changes them."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-11", title: "The Risk Everyone Knew but No One Owned", takeaway: "Every significant risk needs a named owner and a response.", story: [
            "Daily payment reconciliation depended on one spreadsheet.\nOne experienced staff member understood how it worked.\nNo backup guide existed, and no one else had run it alone.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA asked who owned the spreadsheet and its checks\n→ Each manager assumed another team owned it\n→ The expert described fixes held only in memory\n→ A short absence had once delayed reconciliation by a day\n→ No warning sign existed for when the process was at risk",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Someone looks after that, it has always been fine.\"",
            "So the BA recorded the risk plainly, named an owner, and arranged for the process to be documented and a second person trained, which removed the single point of failure before it caused harm.",
            "Why?",
            "Risk Analysis and Management starts with ownership. A risk that belongs to everyone in general belongs to no one in particular, and no one acts until harm occurs. Naming an owner, a response, and a warning sign turns a vague worry into a managed item. The fix here was not a new system. It was clarity, documentation, and cover, agreed while there was still time.",
            "A risk with no owner is a risk with no response.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Ask directly: who owns this risk, by role?\n→ Document knowledge that lives in one head\n→ Train cover before absence forces the issue\n→ Agree a warning sign that triggers action early",
            "Have you ever found a critical task that only one person could do?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-12", title: "A Small Change With a Large Blast Radius", takeaway: "Trace dependencies before you call any change minor.", story: [
            "A team planned a small change to a payment reference field.\nThe change looked minor in the main system.\nDownstream teams had not been asked about it.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA traced where the reference field was used\n→ Reporting, matching, and client files all relied on its format\n→ Two downstream checks would fail if the format changed\n→ Client guidance still described the old format\n→ A test with real examples exposed the breaks early",
            "The plan said:\n\"This is a minor field change, no wider impact is expected.\"",
            "So the team paused, updated downstream checks and client guidance together, and retested the full journey, which turned a risky small change into a coordinated one.",
            "Why?",
            "In payments, a field is rarely just a field. It is a promise shared across systems, reports, and clients, and changing it in one place can break trust elsewhere. Tracing dependencies before change is cheaper than fixing failures after release. The BA earns value by asking where else this is used, and by refusing to call a change minor until the evidence supports it.",
            "Small in one system can be large across the journey.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Trace every changed field across systems and reports\n→ Ask downstream teams before assuming no impact\n→ Test with real examples, not only happy path cases\n→ Update client guidance in the same change",
            "What would you do if a small change threatened the whole journey?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-13", title: "The Optimistic Plan With No Fallback", takeaway: "Agree and rehearse a fallback before a big launch.", story: [
            "A cutover was planned for a Friday evening.\nThe new process would go live for all clients at once.\nNo fallback had been agreed if the launch failed.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA asked what would happen if the launch failed at midnight\n→ No one could describe how to return to the old process\n→ Support cover for the weekend was thin\n→ The busiest client window fell on the following Monday\n→ The plan assumed every step would work first time",
            "The request said:\n\"We will fix any issues live, there is no need for a fallback.\"",
            "So the BA helped the team agree a fallback, rehearse the return steps, add weekend cover, and set clear decision points, which gave leaders a real choice if problems appeared.",
            "Why?",
            "Optimism is not a control. A launch without a fallback forces the team to improvise under pressure, when mistakes are most likely and most costly. Agreeing a fallback in advance is not expecting failure. It is protecting clients and staff by deciding calmly what to do if the worst happens. Rehearsal matters too, because a fallback no one has practised may not work when it is needed.",
            "A plan without a fallback is a bet, not a plan.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Ask how to return to the old way, step by step\n→ Rehearse the fallback before launch day\n→ Agree decision points and who makes the call\n→ Ensure cover matches the risk, not the calendar",
            "Have you ever launched without a way back?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-14", title: "The Warning That Was Called Negativity", takeaway: "A warning shared with evidence is care, not negativity.", story: [
            "A BA raised a data quality risk before launch.\nA sample of client records showed repeated errors.\nThe warning was heard as negativity about the launch.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA shared the sample errors with examples and counts\n→ The team agreed to fix data after launch, to protect the date\n→ The risk was noted but no owner or response was agreed\n→ After launch, errors blocked payments for affected clients\n→ Support teams worked long hours to fix cases one by one",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Let us stay positive and not delay the launch.\"",
            "So the team paused new onboarding, fixed the data in batches, and added checks to stop the same errors returning, and the BA recorded the lesson so future launches would test data quality earlier.",
            "Why?",
            "Raising a risk is an act of care, not a lack of team spirit. When warnings are dismissed as negativity, the team loses its early warning system, and clients pay the price. Recording the risk, with an owner and a response, protects everyone. It lets leaders choose with eyes open, and it lets the team learn without blame when a risk becomes real.",
            "A recorded warning protects the team and the client.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Share evidence with examples, not only opinions\n→ Record the risk, owner, and response, even if disputed\n→ Agree what would trigger a pause or rethink\n→ Review lessons after the incident, without blame",
            "Have you ever raised a warning that others did not want to hear?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-risks-q1", question: "You are the BA on a new change. How do you identify the risks that matter most?", answer: [
            "I would start with the future state and the current state side by side, and I would ask what could stop us getting from one to the other. I would talk to the people closest to the work, because they usually know where failures happen first. I would use simple prompts, such as what could go wrong, what do we depend on, and what have we assumed without proof. I would also review past incidents, dependencies, data quality, and busy periods, because risks often repeat in familiar places. For each risk I would record the cause, the event, and the effect in plain words, so everyone understands it the same way. Then I would ask the group to weigh likelihood and impact together, and to name an owner and a warning sign. I would keep the list short and honest, and I would review it often, because new facts change risks quickly."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-risks-q2", question: "A stakeholder dismisses a risk you raised as unlikely and not worth worrying about. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I would not argue about the odds, because that rarely changes minds. I would ask what would happen if the risk did occur, and I would describe the impact in concrete terms, such as delayed payments, manual rework, or loss of client trust. Then I would ask what small, cheap check would reduce the risk, so the response feels proportionate rather than dramatic. I would record the risk, the reasoning, and the decision, using Risk Analysis and Management discipline, so the choice is visible and owned. If the stakeholder still wants to accept it, I would ask them to accept it explicitly as the risk owner, with a warning sign agreed. That keeps the decision honest without making it personal. I would also revisit the risk when new evidence appears, because a risk that was unlikely last month may be likely now. My job is to make the trade-off clear, not to force a particular answer."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-risks-q3", question: "Two significant risks compete for the same limited mitigation budget. How do you help leaders choose?", answer: [
            "I would compare the two risks on the same basis, so the choice is fair. For each one I would describe the cause, the likely impact, the timing, and how much the mitigation would actually reduce the risk. I would use Decision Analysis thinking, weighing benefit, cost, and remaining risk side by side, rather than relying on gut feel. I would also check whether the risks are connected, because fixing one may reduce the other, or ignoring one may make the other worse. Then I would present the comparison to the decision maker with a clear recommendation and the reasoning behind it. I would be honest about what stays exposed if we fund only one response, and I would suggest warning signs and a fallback for the other. If the budget truly cannot cover both, I would look for a cheaper partial response that buys time. The goal is a deliberate choice, with eyes open, not a quiet gamble."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-risks-q4", question: "A sponsor wants to accept a major risk without any mitigation. How do you respond?", answer: [
            "I would first make sure I understand the risk fully, and that the sponsor does too. I would describe the cause, the impact on clients and operations, and the warning signs, in plain language with real examples. Then I would present the options, including a reduced mitigation, a phased approach, and full acceptance, with the trade-offs for each one. I would ask the sponsor to confirm acceptance in writing, as the risk owner, with the impact and the fallback clearly stated. I would also agree what would trigger a rethink, so acceptance does not become permanent by default. If I believed acceptance would cause serious harm to clients, I would say so directly and respectfully, and I would record my concern. I would not hide behind process, and I would not accept the risk on the sponsor's behalf. My duty is to ensure the decision is informed, owned, and reversible where possible, even when I disagree with it."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-risks-q5", question: "Tell me about a time when a risk materialised in the middle of delivery. How did you respond and reshape the plan?", answer: [
            "I would stay calm and focus on the facts in front of me. I would confirm what has happened, who is affected, and what the immediate workaround is, before discussing blame or causes. Then I would work with the owner to put the agreed response into action, and I would check whether the warning signs we agreed had appeared earlier. If the plan needs to change, I would help reshape it quickly, using impact analysis to show what is affected and what can still go ahead. I would communicate the revised plan in plain terms, with clear owners and next steps, and I would keep clients and staff informed honestly. After the immediate pressure passes, I would lead a short review, capturing what we learned and updating the risk register so the lesson is not lost. I would also check connected risks, because one event often reveals others. My aim is to restore trust through clear action, not through reassurance alone."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-exception-handling"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-define-change-strategy",
        title: "Define Change Strategy",
        subtitle: "Choose a Practical Path from Today to Tomorrow",
        kind: .task,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: .strategy,
        babokReference: "6.4",
        explanation: [
            "Defining the change strategy means choosing a practical path from the current state to the future state. The business analyst helps stakeholders compare real options, such as changing all at once, changing in phases, piloting with one group, or improving the present without a large programme. Decision Analysis supports this choice, by testing each option against the same criteria, including benefit, cost, risk, time, and impact on people. The output is not just a preferred option, but the reasoning that lets leaders defend it.",
            "This work matters because the path shapes the outcome as much as the destination does. A good future delivered in the wrong way can still fail, if clients are disrupted, staff are overwhelmed, or dependencies arrive too late. Business Cases bring discipline here, by stating the problem, the options, the costs, the benefits, and what must be true for benefits to appear. A business case that only lists benefits, without costs and risks, does not help leaders choose. It only helps them hope.",
            "Without a change strategy, teams drift into the loudest option or the fastest promise. They start building before sequencing is agreed, and they discover too late that training, data, or client readiness was missing. Financial Analysis and risk insight belong in this choice too, because a phased path may cost more overall but reduce harm, while a single launch may be cheaper but far riskier. The business analyst keeps the comparison honest, by showing trade-offs plainly and by naming what each option demands from the organisation.",
            "Consider a bank replacing how corporate clients submit bulk payments. One option moves all clients on one weekend, which is fast but risky. Another moves clients in groups, starting with those who are ready, which is slower but safer and easier to support. A third pilots the new journey with a small group, learns from it, then rolls out in waves. The right choice depends on client readiness, support capacity, risk appetite, and the cost of failure, and a clear change strategy states the choice, the sequence, and the reasons, so the team can act as one."
        ],
        outputName: "Change strategy",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Gap summary", guidance: "The distance between current state and future state, in capabilities and process."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Options", guidance: "The realistic routes to the future state, each with benefits, costs, and risks."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Recommended route", guidance: "The chosen route, and why it beats the alternatives."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Sequence", guidance: "The order of change, with the reason for the order."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Transition states", guidance: "How work runs during the change, including any temporary arrangements."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Risks and dependencies", guidance: "What could break the sequence, and what the change depends on.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Start from the gap, not from the solution. The gap between current state and future state, stated in capabilities and process, is what the strategy must close. Then write the realistic options, each with benefits, costs, and risks on the same basis, so the comparison is fair and the recommendation can be challenged on evidence.",
            "Sequence the change so that value arrives early and risk is retired early. Name the transition states: how work runs while the change is under way, including any temporary manual steps. A strategy with no transition states assumes the change happens overnight, and overnight changes are where payment operations break."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: change strategy for exception handling",
        workedExample: [
            "Gap: the current queue has no age ordering, no ownership, and no status summary. The future state needs all three, plus automatic assignment. Options: build all three at once, or build age ordering and ownership first, then the status summary. The phased option is recommended: it delivers value in the first release and retires the duplicate working risk early.",
            "Sequence: first, age ordering and ownership, because they need no new data. Second, the status summary, which depends on the hub interface. Transition state: during the first phase, officers use the ordered queue with manual status checks, as today. Dependencies: the hub interface for the status summary, and training for the new queue before each release."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Compare real options on the same criteria, including doing nothing.",
            "State costs, benefits, risks, and assumptions together in the business case.",
            "Sequence the change around readiness, not only around build order.",
            "Record the reasoning, so later pressure does not quietly change the strategy."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-15", title: "Big Bang or Phased: A Choice With Consequences", takeaway: "Compare paths on readiness and risk, not on speed alone.", story: [
            "Leaders debated how to move clients to a new payment journey.\nOne group wanted a single weekend cutover for all clients.\nAnother group wanted a phased move, starting with ready clients.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA compared both options on the same criteria\n→ The single cutover was faster but needed full weekend cover\n→ The phased move was slower but easier to support and learn from\n→ Client readiness varied widely across segments\n→ Support capacity could not safely handle all clients at once",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Let us get it over with in one weekend.\"",
            "So leaders chose a phased move, starting with the most ready clients, with clear waves and review points, which kept support load manageable and let the team fix issues early.",
            "Why?",
            "Decision Analysis earns its keep when options are compared fairly. Speed alone is a poor judge, because it hides support load, learning time, and the cost of failure. A phased path often looks slower on a calendar and faster in reality, because it avoids a large incident and the rework that follows. The BA helps leaders choose the path that delivers value safely, not just quickly.",
            "The fastest path is not always the safest path to value.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Compare options on the same criteria, every time\n→ Test support and readiness, not only build readiness\n→ Sequence waves by readiness and risk\n→ Build review points between waves",
            "What would you do if speed and safety pointed in different directions?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-16", title: "The Business Case That Told the Truth", takeaway: "Test assumptions before you present benefits as fact.", story: [
            "A proposal promised large savings from a payments change.\nThe numbers looked strong in the headline summary.\nNo one had tested the assumptions behind them.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA traced each benefit to its source and timing\n→ Two benefits counted the same saving twice\n→ Training and support costs were missing from the case\n→ Benefits were assumed to arrive in full within three months\n→ A realistic timeline showed benefits building over a year",
            "The plan said:\n\"Benefits will arrive in full within three months.\"",
            "So the BA rebuilt the case with honest costs, timing, and assumptions, and leaders approved a smaller first phase with clear benefit checks, which protected trust when results arrived more slowly.",
            "Why?",
            "Business Cases fail when they become sales documents. Double counted benefits, missing costs, and instant timelines create approval now and disappointment later. An honest case may look less exciting, but it lets leaders plan staffing, cash, and expectations correctly. Trust earned by telling the truth early is worth far more than enthusiasm built on numbers that cannot hold.",
            "A business case that tells the truth earns trust that lasts.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Trace every benefit to a source and a date\n→ Check for double counting before presenting totals\n→ Include training, support, and running costs\n→ Agree how and when benefits will be checked",
            "Have you ever seen a strong headline hide weak assumptions?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-17", title: "When Waiting Was the Right Strategy", takeaway: "Waiting with a plan can be the most honest strategy.", story: [
            "Pressure grew to replace a stable payments system.\nLeaders feared falling behind if they waited.\nYet the future direction for the market was still unclear.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA assessed options including improve, replace, and wait\n→ The current system was stable and met present needs\n→ A replacement now would lock in choices too early\n→ Targeted improvements could fix the worst pain quickly\n→ A review point was set for when direction became clearer",
            "The request said:\n\"We must act now, or we will fall behind.\"",
            "So leaders chose targeted improvements and a dated review, which fixed real pain without committing to a large replacement before the future was clear.",
            "Why?",
            "Doing nothing, or doing less, is a valid strategy when the evidence supports it. Change for its own sake can destroy value, by replacing a working system with an uncertain one at the worst time. A deliberate pause, with improvements and a review date, is not drift. It is a decision to spend learning time wisely and to keep options open until the choice is clearer.",
            "A deliberate pause can be a strategy, not a delay.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Always include improve and wait as real options\n→ Test whether the pain needs a replacement or a fix\n→ Set a review date so a pause stays a decision\n→ Fix the worst pain while direction becomes clear",
            "What would you do if waiting was wiser than acting at once?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SA-18", title: "A Strategy That Forgot the People", takeaway: "Sequence people readiness with the same care as technology.", story: [
            "A technical rollout for a new payment process went live on time.\nSystems worked, data moved, and checks passed.\nStaff, however, had not been prepared for the new work.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Training was scheduled for after go live to save time\n→ Guides described the old process, not the new one\n→ Staff improvised workarounds on the first busy morning\n→ Support queues grew as clients asked questions staff could not answer\n→ Confidence in the change fell within a week",
            "The plan said:\n\"Staff will learn the new process on the job.\"",
            "So the team paused the next wave, delivered short practical training, updated guides with real examples, and named local champions, which restored confidence before rollout continued.",
            "Why?",
            "A change strategy that only sequences technology is incomplete. People need time, training, guides, and support to do new work well, especially under the pressure of live payments. Learning on the job, without preparation, creates errors and workarounds that are hard to undo. Readiness is not a soft extra. It is part of the strategy, sequenced with the same care as build and data.",
            "A change people cannot use is not yet a change.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Sequence training and guides before go live, not after\n→ Use real examples that match live work\n→ Name champions who can help on the floor\n→ Pause the next wave until readiness is real",
            "Have you ever seen a perfect rollout fail because people were not ready?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-change-strategy-q1", question: "You are the BA and leaders ask you to compare three different ways to reach the future state. How do you approach it?", answer: [
            "I would start by making sure the options are real and comparable. I would describe each option in the same format, covering what changes, who is affected, the sequence, the cost, the benefit, and the main risks. I would include doing nothing as an option, so leaders can see the cost of standing still. Then I would agree the criteria for judging them, such as client benefit, risk, time, cost, and readiness, before I score anything. I would use Decision Analysis thinking, comparing options against those criteria with evidence, not gut feel. I would present the comparison on one page, with a clear recommendation and the trade-offs stated plainly. I would also check dependencies and people readiness for each option, because a strong option on paper can fail if the team or clients are not ready. Finally, I would ask leaders to choose and to record why, so the team has a stable basis for planning. My role is to make the choice clear, not to make it for them."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-change-strategy-q2", question: "Stakeholders want the fastest change option, even though you believe it carries the highest risk. How do you handle it?", answer: [
            "I would acknowledge the desire for speed, because delay has real costs too. Then I would show what speed demands, in terms of client readiness, support cover, training, and fallback, so the risk is concrete rather than abstract. I would compare the fastest option with a phased option on the same criteria, showing what each delivers, when, and at what risk. I would use Risk Analysis and Management language, naming the key risks, their owners, and the warning signs for each path. If leaders still prefer speed, I would help them reduce the risk honestly, such as by starting with ready clients, adding cover, or agreeing a pause point. I would record the decision, the assumptions, and the agreed safeguards, so the team is protected if pressure grows. I would also ask what evidence would show the fast path is working, so we can spot trouble early and act before clients are harmed. My aim is to support a fast outcome without hiding the price of haste."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-change-strategy-q3", question: "What belongs in a business case, and how do you keep it honest?", answer: [
            "I would include the problem in plain language, and the evidence that it matters, so the case starts from facts. Then I would describe the options, including doing nothing, with costs, benefits, risks, and assumptions for each one. I would separate build costs from running costs, and I would state when benefits are expected, not just how large they sound. I would name the capabilities and readiness needed, such as training, data, and client communication, because benefits rarely arrive without them. I would also state what must be true for the case to hold, and what would make it fail, so leaders can test it. I would keep the language plain and the numbers traceable, so any leader can follow how each figure was built and challenge it fairly. Finally, I would recommend one option, with the reasoning and the next steps clearly stated. A good business case helps leaders choose with confidence, even when the answer is to start smaller or to wait."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-change-strategy-q4", question: "Your recommended change strategy is rejected and the sponsor chooses a riskier path. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I would first check that I understood the sponsor's reasoning, because they may see constraints I do not. I would ask what mattered most in their choice, such as speed, cost, or visible progress, and I would listen carefully. Then I would work with the chosen path, while making the risks and safeguards explicit, so the team is not exposed silently. I would agree warning signs, decision points, and a fallback, using change strategy discipline, so we can adjust early if problems appear. I would record the decision and the reasoning, including my recommendation and why it differed, without making it a personal dispute. I would also look for ways to keep the best parts of my recommendation inside the chosen path, such as a small pilot or phased start. I would stay constructive in every meeting, because the team needs steady support once the direction is set, not quiet resistance. My duty is to support the decision once made, while keeping the organisation honest about its consequences."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-change-strategy-q5", question: "A new constraint forces the change strategy to change in the middle of delivery. How do you reshape it?", answer: [
            "I would start by understanding the new constraint fully, and by checking which parts of the strategy it truly affects. I would revisit the future state and the options, and I would test whether the goal can still be reached by a different sequence, a narrower scope, or a longer timeline. I would use Financial Analysis and risk thinking together, so the revised options are honest about cost, benefit, and exposure. Then I would present two or three revised paths, with the trade-offs and the impact on clients and staff clearly stated. I would recommend the path that protects the most value with the least harm, and I would explain why plainly. Once leaders choose, I would update the business case, the sequence, and the communications, so no one works from the old plan. I would also capture the lesson, because constraints that appear mid-flight often reveal assumptions worth testing earlier next time."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-impact-assessment"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-specify-and-model-requirements",
        title: "Specify and Model Requirements",
        subtitle: "Turn needs into clear, testable models",
        kind: .task,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: .analysisDesign,
        babokReference: "7.1",
        explanation: [
            "This task turns a business need into requirements that a delivery team can build and test. The BA listens to stakeholders, asks careful questions, and records what the solution must do and how well it must do it. Good requirements state the outcome, the conditions, and the limits, so two readers reach the same understanding. Without this clarity, developers guess, testers guess differently, and the result rarely matches what the business wanted.",
            "Models help because words alone often hide gaps, overlaps, and conflicting ideas about the same process. Process Modelling shows the steps, the handovers, and the decisions in a payment journey from initiation to settlement. Data Modelling shows the information the process needs, how pieces of information relate, and what must stay consistent. Use Cases and Scenarios and User Stories describe the same need from different angles, one focused on interactions and the other on user value.",
            "Consider a corporate client that wants to upload a file of supplier payments through an online portal. A vague request to support file uploads leaves open the file format, the validation rules, the error handling, and the approval steps. The BA models the upload, validation, approval, and release steps, and records what happens when a single payment in the file fails. The BA also checks who can approve, what limits apply, and what confirmation the client sees after release.",
            "Prototyping can help when stakeholders struggle to picture a screen or a flow from text alone. A simple mock-up invites early feedback and reveals missing fields, unclear labels, and awkward steps before build starts. The BA still needs written requirements behind the mock-up, because a picture alone does not state rules, limits, or error cases. When specifying and modelling are done well, the team shares one picture of the work, and change requests arrive earlier and cost less to handle."
        ],
        outputName: "Requirements specification with models",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Requirement statements", guidance: "Each requirement: one outcome, its conditions, and its limits, written so two readers reach the same understanding."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Process models", guidance: "The steps, handovers, and decisions in the journey."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Data models", guidance: "The information the process needs, how pieces relate, and what must stay consistent."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Rules", guidance: "The business rules, each with its conditions and its effect."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Acceptance criteria", guidance: "How each requirement is checked, in testable form.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write each requirement as one outcome with its conditions and limits. State what the solution must do and how well it must do it, and leave the build decisions to the delivery team. A requirement that two readers understand differently is not finished, however polished it looks.",
            "Model what words hide. Process models show steps, handovers, and decisions. Data models show the information the process needs and what must stay consistent. Use cases, scenarios, and user stories show the same need from the user's point of view. Check every model against the requirement statements: a model that contradicts the text will be followed by one team and ignored by another."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: specification for a payment status display",
        workedExample: [
            "Requirement: the screen must show the latest status received for a payment, with the time the status was received, and must distinguish sent, accepted, rejected, and returned. Condition: when no status report has arrived, the screen must say that no status has been received. Limit: the screen shows status only; it does not change the payment.",
            "Process model: the journey from instruction to settlement, with the status report step and the return step shown as separate branches. Data model: the payment, its status history, and the status report, with the rule that a payment has one current status and a full history. Acceptance criteria: one criterion per status value, plus a criterion for a missing status report."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "State the outcome, the conditions, and the limits in every requirement.",
            "Use a model to expose gaps that plain text hides.",
            "Confirm who acts, what triggers the step, and what good looks like.",
            "Keep requirements testable, so the team can show when each one is met."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-01", title: "The file upload nobody defined", takeaway: "Define the rules and error cases before the build starts, not after it.", story: [
            "The operations team asked for bulk payment uploads.\nA corporate client sent files in different formats.\nNo one had written down the validation rules.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The team built an upload screen from a short request note\n→ The first file was rejected with no clear reason shown\n→ The client re-sent the same file three times\n→ Support logged each failure as a different fault\n→ The release date slipped while rules were debated",
            "The requirement said:\n\"We need bulk upload for corporate payments.\"",
            "So the developers built what they assumed bulk upload meant, the testers checked what they assumed it meant, and the client expected something else entirely, which left the team fixing basic questions after build had started.",
            "Why?",
            "Bulk upload is a label, not a requirement. A label hides file format, field rules, duplicate checks, approval steps, and error messages. Process Modelling and Data Modelling force those choices into the open while they are still cheap to change, and a written requirement lets the client confirm the behaviour before anyone builds it.",
            "A label is not a requirement until its rules, limits, and error cases are written down.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Ask what a good file looks like and what a bad file looks like\n→ Model the upload, validation, approval, and release steps\n→ Write the error message the user will see for each failure\n→ Confirm the rules with the client before build starts",
            "Have you ever built from a label and discovered the real requirement too late?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-02", title: "The missing approval step", takeaway: "Walk every actor and alternate path, or control steps will surface late.", story: [
            "A new payment portal was planned for small business clients.\nThe team wrote short stories for each screen.\nApproval was mentioned only in passing.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Stories covered login, payment entry, and confirmation\n→ No story described who approves a payment\n→ Build started with a single user flow\n→ The control team asked about dual approval late in testing\n→ The flow had to be reworked to add a second approver",
            "The request said:\n\"Let clients pay suppliers quickly from the portal.\"",
            "So the first version let one user enter and release a payment alone, which pleased the pilot users but failed the control review, and the team had to add approval queues, notifications, and audit notes after the screens were already built.",
            "Why?",
            "User Stories work well for user value, but they can miss control steps that no single user owns. Use Cases and Scenarios help here because they walk through each actor, each trigger, and each alternate path, including approval, rejection, and expiry. When the BA pairs stories with a fuller walkthrough, control steps appear early instead of arriving as late rework.",
            "Speed without control is not a complete requirement.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Pair each story with the full interaction, including approvals\n→ Ask who approves, who can reject, and what happens on expiry\n→ Walk the alternate paths, not only the happy path\n→ Confirm control steps with the control team early",
            "What would you check when a story sounds simple and fast?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-03", title: "Two records for one payment", takeaway: "Agree the data definition before you connect the systems.", story: [
            "The bank ran two systems that both stored payment details.\nEach system used a different reference for the same payment.\nReconciliation staff matched records by hand each morning.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ A payment was created in the front system\n→ A second record was created downstream with a new reference\n→ Staff searched by amount and date to link the pair\n→ One match was linked to the wrong payment\n→ The error was found only at month end review",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Just make the two systems talk to each other.\"",
            "So the team built a link that copied records across, but without a shared definition of the payment record the link copied confusion as well as data, and reconciliation still needed manual checks to trust the result.",
            "Why?",
            "Systems can exchange data and still misunderstand each other when key terms differ. Data Modelling asks what a payment is, which fields identify it, which fields must match, and what happens when values conflict. A shared model gives both systems one definition to build against, which turns reconciliation from detective work into a check.",
            "Shared data needs a shared definition first.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Define the payment record before building the link\n→ Agree the single field that identifies each payment\n→ State what happens when values disagree\n→ Test with duplicates, corrections, and cancellations",
            "Have you ever linked systems without agreeing what the shared record means?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-specify-and-model-requirements-q1", question: "You are the BA and a stakeholder says, 'We need faster payments.' What do you do first?", answer: [
            "I would start by asking what faster means for this stakeholder, because the phrase can hide several different needs. I would ask which payments feel slow, who waits, and what delay causes the most pain today. I would then map the current flow with Process Modelling so we can see where time is actually lost, whether in entry, approval, validation, or release. I would ask about volumes, peak times, and the cases that break the normal flow, such as failed validations or missing approvals. Next, I would turn the answers into clear requirements that state the outcome, the conditions, and the limits, so the team can test whether faster has been achieved. I would use User Stories to keep the user value visible and Use Cases and Scenarios to cover the full interaction, including error paths. I would check the idea with the operations team and the control team before it goes to build, because speed must not weaken checks. Finally, I would agree how we will measure success, so faster becomes something we can observe rather than something we hope for."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-specify-and-model-requirements-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you had to turn a vague request into clear requirements.", answer: [
            "I worked on a request for a better payment status screen, and at first nobody could say what better meant. I started by watching how the operations team used the current screen and asking what questions they could not answer from it. I learned they needed to know whether a payment was waiting, in progress, failed, or settled, and who had last touched it. I used Process Modelling to draw the payment journey and Data Modelling to list the status values and the fields behind each one. I wrote requirements that stated each status, the event that caused it, and the action available at that point. I built a simple Prototyping mock-up of the screen and walked through it with the team, which exposed two missing filters and a confusing label. I then rewrote the requirements in plain language and asked the team to confirm them in their own words, which caught one misunderstanding about failed payments. The result was a screen the team trusted, because each status had a clear meaning and a clear next step. The lesson I kept was to test understanding early, with a model and a mock-up, rather than assuming a short request was already clear."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-specify-and-model-requirements-q3", question: "A stakeholder says the model you drew is wrong, but cannot say what is wrong. How do you handle it?", answer: [
            "I would not defend the model or ask the stakeholder to fix it alone. I would ask them to walk through a real example with me, step by step, using a recent payment they remember well. As we walk, I would point to each step in the Process Modelling diagram and ask what happens here, who acts, and what comes next. I would listen for the point where their account differs from the drawing, because that gap is where the error lives. I would also check the Data Modelling behind the flow, to see whether a field, a status, or a rule is missing or named differently by their team. I would update the model in front of them when I can, so they see their knowledge appear in the picture and can correct it at once. If two stakeholders describe the flow differently, I would record both versions and arrange a short joint session to agree one version, rather than choosing quietly myself. I would close by asking them to confirm the revised model in plain words and to name any case we have not covered. That way the model becomes a shared record, not my guess about their work."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-functional-spec",
            "studio-user-stories"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-verify-requirements",
        title: "Verify Requirements",
        subtitle: "Check requirements are correct and complete",
        kind: .task,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: .analysisDesign,
        babokReference: "7.2",
        explanation: [
            "Verification asks whether the requirements are written well and say what the stakeholders actually meant. The BA checks each requirement for clarity, consistency, and completeness before the team builds from it. A requirement that can be read two ways will be built one way and tested another, which creates rework and argument. Verification catches those problems while they are still words on a page, when fixing them is quick and cheap.",
            "The BA reads requirements against their sources, checking that nothing important was dropped, added, or changed in translation. Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria help here, because they state how the team will know each requirement has been met. If no fair test can be written for a requirement, the requirement is probably too vague to build from. The BA also checks that related requirements agree with each other, especially where two teams describe the same process differently.",
            "Consider a rule that says high value payments need extra approval. Verification asks what counts as high value, who counts as an approver, and what happens when the approver is away. It also asks whether the rule matches the rule in the operations manual and the rule in the control checklist. If those three sources disagree, the BA raises the conflict now, instead of letting the system enforce the wrong version.",
            "Concept Modelling and a Data Dictionary support verification by giving every key term one agreed meaning. When payment, instruction, and transaction mean different things to different teams, requirements that use them will confuse readers. The BA records the agreed meaning, the related terms, and any term that must not be used as a synonym. Verified requirements still need stakeholder confirmation, but the team can trust that what is written is clear, consistent, and ready to check."
        ],
        outputName: "Requirements verification record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Requirements checked", guidance: "The set checked, with identifiers and version."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Checks applied", guidance: "The checks: completeness, clarity, consistency, testability, and correctness against the source."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Defects found", guidance: "Each defect, with the requirement affected and the correction needed."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Corrections", guidance: "What was corrected, and what was rechecked."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Verification result", guidance: "Whether the set is verified, and what remains open.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Verify the requirements against their own quality standard, before anyone asks whether they are the right requirements. Check completeness: every need is covered. Check clarity: two readers reach the same understanding. Check consistency: no two requirements contradict each other. Check testability: every requirement can be checked with an observable result.",
            "Record every defect with the requirement it affects and the correction needed, and recheck the correction. Verification is a check on the writing, not on the need: a requirement can be perfectly written and still be the wrong requirement. That question belongs to validation, and the verification record should say so rather than blurring the two."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: verification record for a validation pack",
        workedExample: [
            "Requirements checked: VAL-001 to VAL-008, version 2. Checks applied: completeness against the workshop record, clarity, consistency, and testability. Defects found: VAL-003 used the word promptly with no time limit, so it could not be tested. VAL-006 contradicted VAL-002 on the handling of a missing field.",
            "Corrections: VAL-003 now states a time limit agreed with operations. VAL-006 was rewritten to match VAL-002, and both were rechecked. Verification result: the set is verified, with one open item: the time limit in VAL-003 is subject to a compliance check, recorded as an open question with an owner and a date."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Check every requirement against its source before build starts.",
            "A requirement without a fair test is too vague to build from.",
            "Resolve conflicts between sources now, not after release.",
            "Give key terms one agreed meaning and record it."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-04", title: "What urgent really meant", takeaway: "Vague words pass reviews and fail in practice, so test them early.", story: [
            "The support team flagged some payments as urgent.\nEach team used a different cut-off time.\nNo requirement defined the word.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ A client marked a payment as urgent in the morning\n→ Support treated it as same-day handling\n→ Operations treated it as next-day handling\n→ The payment missed the client's deadline\n→ Each team said it had followed the requirement",
            "The requirement said:\n\"Urgent payments must be handled promptly.\"",
            "So the payment waited in a normal queue while each team believed another team owned the urgent handling, and the client learned of the delay only when the supplier asked for the money.",
            "Why?",
            "Promptly and urgent feel clear until two teams act on them. Verification tests each requirement by asking how a reader would know it was met and whether two readers would act the same way. Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria turn vague words into observable checks, such as a time limit, a queue, and an owner. Without that test, the requirement promises care but defines nothing.",
            "If two readers act differently, the requirement is not verified.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Replace vague words with observable checks\n→ Name the owner, the time limit, and the queue\n→ Test the wording with two different teams\n→ Record the agreed meaning in the Data Dictionary",
            "Have you ever seen one word mean two things to two teams?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-05", title: "Two rules that disagreed", takeaway: "Compare related requirements together, or conflicts will surface in testing.", story: [
            "The portal team and the control team each wrote rules.\nBoth rules covered payment limits.\nNo one compared the two sets.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The portal allowed a higher daily limit for trusted clients\n→ The control checklist kept a lower limit for all clients\n→ A trusted client hit the lower limit in testing\n→ Testers logged it as a defect\n→ Developers said the portal was built as specified",
            "The requirement said:\n\"Trusted clients may pay up to the agreed daily limit.\"",
            "So testing stalled while teams argued about which limit was agreed, and the release waited for a decision that should have been made when the requirements were first compared.",
            "Why?",
            "Requirements rarely fail alone. They fail in pairs, when two statements about the same topic disagree and no one notices. Verification means reading related requirements together and asking whether they can all be true at once. When sources disagree, the BA surfaces the conflict, brings the owners together, and records one agreed rule before build or test depends on it.",
            "Related requirements must be true together, not just true alone.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Read related requirements side by side\n→ Compare limits, roles, and rules across sources\n→ Raise conflicts with the owners, not in testing\n→ Record the single agreed rule and its source",
            "What would you do when two trusted sources disagree?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-06", title: "The requirement with no ending", takeaway: "Verify the error paths with the same care as the happy path.", story: [
            "A payment flow covered entry, approval, and release.\nNo one had specified the failure path.\nThe team assumed failures were rare.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ A payment failed validation after approval\n→ The screen showed no status and no next step\n→ The user re-entered the payment from scratch\n→ A duplicate payment was created by mistake\n→ Operations spent a day unwinding the pair",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Just handle the normal flow, we will sort exceptions later.\"",
            "So the normal flow worked in the demo and failed in live use, because real payments include failures, timeouts, and corrections, and a flow with no ending leaves users to invent their own recovery.",
            "Why?",
            "Completeness is part of verification. The BA asks what happens at each step when the expected event does not happen, and whether every path has an ending, an owner, and a message. A requirement that covers only the happy path is unfinished, however clearly the happy path is written. Checking alternate and error paths early prevents duplicates, confusion, and manual recovery work.",
            "A flow without an ending is not a complete requirement.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Ask what happens when each step fails or times out\n→ Give every path a status, an owner, and a message\n→ Check duplicates, retries, and corrections explicitly\n→ Confirm exception handling before the demo, not after it",
            "Have you ever watched a happy path fail on its first real exception?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-verify-requirements-q1", question: "You are the BA and you spot a requirement that could be read two ways. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I would stop and treat the unclear wording as a problem to fix now, not a detail to leave for the team. I would write down the two readings in plain words and note what each reading would mean for the build and the test. I would then go back to the source, whether that is a stakeholder, a workshop note, or an existing manual, and ask which reading they meant. I would use Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria to test the fix, by asking how we would know the requirement had been met under each reading. If only one reading can be tested fairly, that often shows which one is workable. I would rewrite the requirement in simple language, remove the vague word, and add the condition or limit that was missing. I would ask a second person, ideally someone from a different team, to read the new wording and say what they would build from it. If they describe the same behaviour I intend, I know the wording is clearer. Finally, I would record the agreed meaning so the same confusion does not return in a later change."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-verify-requirements-q2", question: "A stakeholder says, 'Just build it, we can fix the wording later.' How do you respond?", answer: [
            "I would acknowledge the pressure to move quickly, because delay has a real cost, and then I would explain the cost of building from unclear wording. I would give a short example from the work in hand, showing how two readings would lead to different builds and different tests. I would suggest a focused check that takes little time, such as reviewing the riskiest requirements together and agreeing the Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria for each one. I would offer to run that check in a short session with the right people, rather than asking for a long review of everything. I would also point out that wording fixed after build often means rework in code, test cases, and training notes, while wording fixed now means a short edit. If the stakeholder still wants to proceed, I would record the risk, the unclear items, and the agreed time to revisit them, so the decision is visible and owned. I would then work with the team to limit the build to the clearest parts first. My aim is to protect pace without letting unclear requirements quietly become expensive rework."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-verify-requirements-q3", question: "Tell me about a time you found a conflict between two requirements. What did you do?", answer: [
            "I found a conflict between a portal rule and an operations checklist during a review of payment limits. The portal requirement allowed a higher limit for one group of clients, while the checklist applied a lower limit to everyone, and neither document mentioned the other. I first confirmed I had the current versions of both sources, so I was not raising a conflict that had already been resolved. I then wrote the two rules side by side in plain language and noted the case where they disagreed. I took that short note to the owners of both documents and asked them to agree one rule, rather than choosing one myself. I used Concept Modelling to check whether a shared term was part of the problem, and I recorded the agreed meaning in the Data Dictionary once the owners confirmed it. I updated both requirements to point to the single agreed rule and asked the test team to confirm the change. The conflict took a short meeting to resolve, and it saved a much longer argument in testing. I now compare related requirements together as a habit, because conflicts hide between documents."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-acceptance-criteria"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-validate-requirements",
        title: "Validate Requirements",
        subtitle: "Confirm requirements deliver real value",
        kind: .task,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: .analysisDesign,
        babokReference: "7.3",
        explanation: [
            "Validation asks whether the requirements, even if written well, will actually meet the business need. A requirement can be clear, consistent, and testable, and still solve the wrong problem. The BA checks requirements with stakeholders against the outcomes they care about, not only against the original request. This check protects the business from building the right solution for the wrong need.",
            "The BA walks stakeholders through the proposed behaviour using examples, models, and walkthroughs they can challenge. Prototyping helps when a screen or flow is hard to judge from text, because people react more honestly to something they can see and try. The BA asks whether each requirement still matters, whether anything is missing, and whether the whole set fits together as a usable whole. Stakeholders often spot missing steps or unwanted side effects only when they see the work as a connected journey.",
            "Consider a plan to speed up payment investigations by adding a new search screen. The requirements are clear, but validation reveals that investigators spend most time waiting for missing information, not searching. A faster search would help a little, while a complete case view with the missing information would help a great deal more. Validation redirects the work toward the outcome that matters, before the team builds a polished answer to a small part of the problem.",
            "Validation also checks that benefits, costs, and risks still make sense as requirements grow and change. A feature that sounded valuable in a workshop may lose value when its full cost, training need, or control impact becomes clear. The BA records what stakeholders confirmed, what they questioned, and what was changed as a result of the check. When validation is done well, stakeholders recognise their need in the requirements and can explain why each part earns its place."
        ],
        outputName: "Requirements validation record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Requirements validated", guidance: "The set validated, with identifiers and version."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Needs checked against", guidance: "The business needs, goals, and constraints the requirements must serve."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Validation method", guidance: "How validation was done: walkthrough, prototype, review, or trial."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Findings", guidance: "Where requirements do not serve a need, or where a need has no requirement."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Validation result", guidance: "Whether the set is valid, and what must change.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Validate the requirements against the need, not against the writing. The question is whether the set, if built, would deliver the value the business asked for. Walk through the requirements with the people who own the need, using a prototype or a worked example where words alone would hide a gap.",
            "Record two kinds of finding: requirements that serve no need, and needs with no requirement. Both are valuable. A requirement with no need is work that should not be built. A need with no requirement is value that will not arrive. Validation ends with a decision: the set is valid, or it changes, with the changes recorded."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: validation record for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Requirements validated: ST-001 to ST-006, version 1. Needs checked against: the operations need to answer customer queries without manual checks, and the compliance rule on status wording. Method: a walkthrough with operations using a prototype screen and three real query cases.",
            "Findings: the requirements covered the status values but not the case where a status report arrives late, which operations meets weekly. One requirement described a filter no one asked for. Validation result: the set is not yet valid. The late status case is added as a requirement, and the unneeded filter is removed, with both changes recorded."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Ask whether the need is met, not only whether the text is clear.",
            "Use examples and walkthroughs stakeholders can challenge.",
            "Drop or reshape requirements that no longer earn their place.",
            "Record what was confirmed and what changed."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-07", title: "A faster search for the wrong wait", takeaway: "Validate the outcome, not only the requested feature.", story: [
            "Investigators complained that cases took too long.\nThe team proposed a faster search screen.\nNo one had timed where the delay sat.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Requirements for the search screen were written and approved\n→ Build started on filters, sorting, and saved searches\n→ A walkthrough with investigators showed the real delay\n→ Most time went to chasing missing information\n→ The search screen alone would save little time",
            "The request said:\n\"Give us faster search so cases close sooner.\"",
            "So the team paused the search build and reshaped the work around a complete case view, which gave investigators the missing information in one place and addressed the wait that actually slowed cases down.",
            "Why?",
            "Validation separates the requested feature from the needed outcome. The request named a search screen, but the outcome was faster case closure, and the two are not the same. Walking the real work with the people who do it reveals where time goes and whether a feature touches that wait. A BA who validates early can redirect effort while the cost of change is still low.",
            "A clear requirement for the wrong problem is still the wrong requirement.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Ask what outcome the request is meant to improve\n→ Walk the real work before approving the feature\n→ Check where time, effort, or errors actually sit\n→ Reshape the work when the evidence points elsewhere",
            "Have you ever built a neat feature that missed the real delay?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-08", title: "The sign-off that meant nothing", takeaway: "Make validation a conversation, not an email reply.", story: [
            "A set of requirements was sent for approval by email.\nStakeholders replied with approval within an hour.\nNo one had walked through the detail.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Approval was recorded and build started\n→ A prototype was shown three weeks later\n→ Stakeholders saw steps they did not recognise\n→ Two approval steps were missing from the flow\n→ The team had to pause and re-agree the design",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Looks fine, please go ahead.\"",
            "So approval was given quickly and withdrawn slowly, because email approval without a walkthrough is not validation, and the missing steps only became visible when stakeholders could see and challenge the flow.",
            "Why?",
            "Validation needs engagement, not just a reply. People approve documents they have not tested against their real work, especially when the document is long and the deadline is near. A walkthrough or prototype turns approval into a check, because stakeholders react to behaviour they can see. The BA should make validation easy to do well, with short sessions, real examples, and clear questions, rather than long documents sent without support.",
            "Approval without understanding is not validation.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Validate in a walkthrough, not by email alone\n→ Use real examples stakeholders recognise\n→ Ask what is missing, not only what is wrong\n→ Record concerns raised and how each was handled",
            "What would you do when approval arrives too quickly to be real?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-09", title: "A good idea at too high a cost", takeaway: "Check value at real volume, not only in a workshop.", story: [
            "A workshop agreed a new alert for every payment event.\nEveryone liked the idea in the room.\nNo one had counted the alerts.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Requirements listed alerts for each status change\n→ Validation counted alerts for a busy day\n→ Staff would face a constant stream of messages\n→ Important alerts would be lost in the noise\n→ The team agreed alerts only for exceptions",
            "The requirement said:\n\"Notify the team at every stage of the payment.\"",
            "So the team kept alerts for failures, delays, and approvals, and dropped routine notifications, which left staff with signals they could act on instead of noise they would learn to ignore.",
            "Why?",
            "Validation includes value, cost, and side effects, not only correctness. A feature can be desirable in isolation and harmful at scale, when volume, effort, or attention cost outweighs the benefit. The BA helps stakeholders see the whole effect by counting, timing, or walking through a busy day. Saying no to a liked idea, or narrowing it to the cases that matter, is part of protecting value.",
            "Value must survive contact with a busy day.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Test ideas against busy-day volumes and effort\n→ Ask what staff will stop noticing if volume is high\n→ Narrow features to the cases that need action\n→ Record why a liked idea was narrowed or dropped",
            "Have you ever liked an idea until you saw its full cost?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-validate-requirements-q1", question: "You are the BA and stakeholders have approved requirements you think solve the wrong problem. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I would raise my concern early and with evidence, rather than letting build start on work I believe misses the need. I would first check my understanding by walking through a real example with the people who do the work, so I am testing a fact and not just sharing a doubt. I would use a simple model or Prototyping mock-up to show the proposed behaviour and ask whether it improves the outcome they care about. I would ask open questions about where time, effort, or errors actually sit, and I would listen for gaps between the requested feature and the real problem. If the evidence supports my concern, I would present the finding plainly, with the example and the likely effect, and suggest a reshaped option to discuss. I would invite stakeholders to challenge my reading, because validation is a joint check, not a correction from me. I would record what was agreed and update the requirements to reflect the outcome we now share. My goal is to protect value without slowing the team, by surfacing the issue while change is still cheap."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-validate-requirements-q2", question: "A stakeholder says, 'We already signed this off, why are you asking again?' How do you respond?", answer: [
            "I would respect the sign-off and explain that I am checking understanding, not reopening the decision for no reason. I would say that sign-off on a document and confidence in the behaviour are different things, and a short walkthrough helps us catch gaps before build makes them expensive. I would offer a focused session using real examples, rather than a full re-read of the document, so the time cost stays low. I would use Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria to frame the check, by asking how we will know each part works in practice. If the walkthrough confirms the requirements, I will have stronger evidence that we are building the right thing, and the stakeholder will have heard the same confirmation. If it reveals a gap, we can fix a sentence now instead of reworking a built feature later. I would keep the tone collaborative and thank them for their time, because validation depends on their knowledge. I would record the outcome so the sign-off and the walkthrough both stand as part of the record."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-validate-requirements-q3", question: "Tell me about a time validation changed the direction of a piece of work.", answer: [
            "I worked on a request for a new reporting screen for payment operations, and the requirements were clear and well written. During validation, I walked through a busy morning with the operations team and asked them to show me how they used the current reports. I learned they rarely opened the report at all, because they worked from a queue and needed better case information, not another report. I used Process Modelling to show the queue, the handover, and the missing information, and I shared the finding with the sponsor in plain language. We agreed to reshape the work from a report into a richer case view, with the fields the team actually used to resolve cases. I updated the requirements, removed the reporting items that no longer earned their place, and recorded why the direction had changed. The team welcomed the change because it matched their real work, and the sponsor welcomed it because the outcome was clearer. The lesson I kept was to validate against observed work, not only against the original request, because requests name solutions and validation must test outcomes."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-validate-requirements-q4", question: "How do you validate requirements when stakeholders disagree about what good looks like?", answer: [
            "I would start by making the disagreement visible, because hidden disagreement becomes rework later. I would ask each stakeholder to describe a good outcome using a real example, and I would record both descriptions in plain words. I would then use a walkthrough or a Prototyping mock-up so both sides can react to the same behaviour, rather than arguing about abstract wording. I would ask what each side values most, whether speed, control, clarity, or effort, and I would look for a design that serves the shared outcome. If a trade-off is unavoidable, I would present the options with their effects, so the decision is made with open eyes and a named owner. I would use Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria to agree how we will judge success, which often reveals common ground that the debate had hidden. I would record the agreed outcome, the trade-off, and the reason, so later changes do not reopen the same argument. My role is to keep the discussion focused on the business need and the evidence, not on who argued longest."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-acceptance-criteria"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-define-requirements-architecture",
        title: "Define Requirements Architecture",
        subtitle: "Structure requirements so they fit together",
        kind: .task,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: .analysisDesign,
        babokReference: "7.4",
        explanation: [
            "Requirements architecture is the structure that shows how requirements relate to each other and to the wider solution. The BA groups requirements into a coherent whole, so the team can see what belongs together and what depends on what. Without structure, requirements become a long list where gaps hide, overlaps grow, and change in one place breaks another. A good structure makes the work easier to build, test, change, and explain to stakeholders.",
            "Functional Decomposition helps by breaking a broad capability into smaller parts that can be understood and delivered separately. The BA checks that each part has a clear purpose, that parts do not overlap without reason, and that together they cover the whole need. Interface Analysis then checks the boundaries between parts, including the systems, teams, and data that must connect. Data Flow Diagrams show how information moves across those boundaries, which reveals missing links and duplicate handling.",
            "Consider a payment service that covers initiation, validation, approval, release, and notification. The architecture groups requirements by those stages, shows the data each stage needs, and names the handover between stages. If approval rules change, the team can see which requirements, interfaces, and tests the change will touch. If notification is delayed to a later release, the team can see what still works and what must wait with it.",
            "Concept Modelling and a Data Dictionary keep the structure honest by giving shared terms and data one agreed meaning across all groups. The BA also checks that non-functional needs, such as performance, availability, and control, are placed where they apply and not left floating. Structure is not paperwork for its own sake, and the BA keeps it as simple as the work allows while still showing the real dependencies. When the architecture is clear, stakeholders can see the whole, teams can work in parallel with less collision, and change becomes a guided decision."
        ],
        outputName: "Requirements architecture",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Levels", guidance: "The levels of requirements: business, stakeholder, solution, and transition, and what sits at each level."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Relationships", guidance: "How requirements at one level relate to requirements at another."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Views", guidance: "The views provided for different audiences: process view, data view, and interface view."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Boundaries", guidance: "Where one set of requirements ends and another begins, including shared requirements."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Consistency rules", guidance: "The rules that keep the levels consistent when one level changes.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Organise requirements so that a reader can find the level they need without reading everything. Business requirements state the need and the value. Stakeholder requirements state what each group needs. Solution requirements state the behaviour and qualities of the solution. Transition requirements state what is needed only during the change. Each level should be readable on its own and traceable to the levels around it.",
            "Draw the boundaries explicitly. Shared requirements, used by more than one set, are named once and referenced elsewhere, never copied. When a requirement at one level changes, the architecture says which other levels must be checked. Without that rule, a change at one level silently breaks the levels around it."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: architecture for a payment hub change",
        workedExample: [
            "Levels: business requirements state the need to cut duplicate payments and the value of doing so. Stakeholder requirements state what operations, compliance, and technology each need. Solution requirements state the duplicate check behaviour, the queue changes, and the interface changes. Transition requirements state the temporary manual check used during migration.",
            "Relationships: each solution requirement traces to a stakeholder requirement, and each stakeholder requirement traces to a business requirement. Views: operations receives the process view, technology receives the interface view, and the sponsor receives the business view. Boundaries: the duplicate check is one shared requirement set, referenced by the payment initiation and the exception handling packs, never copied into both."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Group requirements by purpose and show how groups connect.",
            "Name every interface and the data that crosses it.",
            "Check that the parts together cover the whole need.",
            "Keep shared terms consistent across every group."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-10", title: "The long list with hidden links", takeaway: "Structure requirements so change can be traced, not hunted.", story: [
            "A payment project held requirements in one long list.\nItems were added in the order they were raised.\nNo grouping or dependency view existed.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ A change was made to the approval rule\n→ The team updated the one item that mentioned approval\n→ Three related items kept the old rule\n→ Testing found inconsistent behaviour across screens\n→ The release was delayed while links were traced",
            "The requirement said:\n\"Approval follows the standard rule.\"",
            "So a small change became a hunt through the list, because the structure that would have shown related items did not exist, and each fix risked missing another linked requirement.",
            "Why?",
            "A list records requirements, but it does not show how they relate. Functional Decomposition groups related work, and a simple architecture view shows which items share rules, data, and interfaces. When a rule changes, the team can follow the links instead of searching by memory. Structure turns change from a hunt into a guided check, and it reveals overlap before build locks it in.",
            "Requirements need structure, not just storage.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Group requirements by stage, capability, or purpose\n→ Show shared rules and shared data explicitly\n→ Trace a change through linked items before approving it\n→ Keep the structure simple enough to maintain",
            "Have you ever changed one requirement and broken three others?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-11", title: "The interface nobody owned", takeaway: "Name and agree every interface before both sides build.", story: [
            "A new portal needed payment status from a back office system.\nBoth teams assumed the other owned the link.\nNo interface requirement was written.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The portal team built screens that expected live status\n→ The back office team planned a nightly file\n→ The mismatch surfaced in joint testing\n→ Status values differed between the two designs\n→ The link had to be redesigned under time pressure",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"The systems will share status, that is straightforward.\"",
            "So the portal showed stale or missing status in testing, and the teams had to agree timing, format, and status meanings late, when both sides had already built to different assumptions.",
            "Why?",
            "Interfaces fail quietly when they have no owner and no written agreement. Interface Analysis asks what crosses the boundary, in what format, how often, and what happens when the link fails. Data Flow Diagrams make those crossings visible, so missing links and mismatched meanings appear before build. An interface agreed early is a contract both teams can build against with confidence.",
            "Every boundary needs an owner and an agreement.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ List every system and team boundary in the solution\n→ Agree format, timing, and status meanings in writing\n→ State what happens when the link fails or is late\n→ Assign an owner to each interface",
            "What would you check first when two systems must share data?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-12", title: "One word, three meanings", takeaway: "Agree shared terms once, then use them everywhere.", story: [
            "Three workstreams used the word settlement.\nEach team meant a different stage.\nThe requirements never defined it.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ One team used settlement for release to the network\n→ A second team used it for confirmation back\n→ A third team used it for end of day reporting\n→ Status screens mixed the three meanings\n→ Users could not tell whether a payment had settled",
            "The requirement said:\n\"Show settlement status on the dashboard.\"",
            "So the dashboard showed a status that satisfied each team in isolation and confused every user, because a shared word with three meanings cannot support a shared screen.",
            "Why?",
            "Architecture is not only grouping, it is also consistency. Concept Modelling asks what each key concept means, how it relates to other concepts, and which stages it covers. A Data Dictionary records the agreed meaning so every workstream uses the word the same way. When terms are consistent, screens, reports, and interfaces can be trusted to mean the same thing.",
            "A shared word needs a shared meaning.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ List key terms used across workstreams\n→ Agree one meaning and one owner per term\n→ Record meanings in the Data Dictionary\n→ Check new requirements against agreed terms",
            "Have you ever found one word hiding three different meanings?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-requirements-architecture-q1", question: "You inherit a long, unstructured list of requirements. How do you bring order to it?", answer: [
            "I would start by reading the list without rewriting it, to understand what is there and what is missing. I would then group items by the capability or stage they serve, using Functional Decomposition to break broad areas into smaller, clearer parts. I would check each group for overlap, gaps, and items that belong elsewhere, and I would note where the same rule or term appears in more than one place. Next, I would map the links between groups, using Interface Analysis and Data Flow Diagrams to show what data crosses each boundary and who owns it. I would agree shared terms with stakeholders and record them in the Data Dictionary, so grouping does not create new confusion. I would share the structure with the team and stakeholders as a simple view, not a heavy document, and ask them to challenge the grouping. Finally, I would use the structure to plan the work, showing what can proceed in parallel and what depends on another group. The aim is a structure the team actually uses, not a diagram that is admired once and then ignored."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-requirements-architecture-q2", question: "A change to one requirement affects several other areas. How do you assess the impact?", answer: [
            "I would start from the structure, not from memory, by tracing the requirement to its group, its shared rules, and its interfaces. I would list the requirements that use the same rule, the same data, or the same interface, and I would check each one for a direct effect. I would use Data Flow Diagrams to follow the data across boundaries and Interface Analysis to see which teams or systems must change with it. I would then talk to the owners of the affected areas, because a diagram shows links but people know the practical effects, such as training, manual work, or timing. I would separate certain effects from possible effects, and I would state what I do not yet know, rather than guessing. I would present the impact as options, with the scope, the effort, and the risk of each, so the decision owner can choose with open eyes. Once a decision is made, I would update the affected requirements and the structure together, so the record stays true. That discipline keeps a small change from becoming a surprise in testing."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-requirements-architecture-q3", question: "Two teams build to different assumptions about the same interface. How do you bring them together?", answer: [
            "I would bring both teams into one short session with a single picture of the interface in front of us. I would ask each team to describe what it sends, what it expects to receive, when the exchange happens, and what happens when it fails. I would record both accounts side by side, using Interface Analysis to structure the discussion around format, timing, values, and error handling. I would then use a Data Flow Diagram to show the agreed flow, so both teams can see the same boundary and challenge it together. Where values differ, such as status meanings, I would agree one shared definition and record it in the Data Dictionary. I would name an owner for the interface and agree how future changes will be proposed and approved, so the agreement does not drift. I would close by asking each team to confirm the agreement in its own words and to name the tests that will prove it works. The goal is a written contract both teams trust, built from evidence rather than from hope that the other side understood."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-brd-fsd"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-define-design-options",
        title: "Define Design Options",
        subtitle: "Compare options before choosing a design",
        kind: .task,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: .analysisDesign,
        babokReference: "7.5",
        explanation: [
            "Defining design options means setting out more than one workable way to meet the requirements, so the choice is made with open eyes. The BA describes each option in enough detail for stakeholders to judge its benefits, costs, risks, and trade-offs. Jumping to the first idea feels fast, but it often locks in a design that is hard to change and hard to defend. Options give decision makers a real choice and give the team a record of why the chosen path was preferred.",
            "Decision Modelling helps by showing the rules and outcomes that each option must handle, especially where choices branch. State Modelling shows how a payment or a case moves through its states, which reveals whether an option handles every state fairly. Sequence Diagrams show the order of interactions between people and systems, which exposes timing, handovers, and waiting in each option. Together, these views let stakeholders compare options on behaviour, not only on cost or familiarity.",
            "Consider a choice between manual review of flagged payments and automated checks with manual review only for exceptions. Manual review is familiar and flexible, but it scales poorly on busy days and depends heavily on staff availability. Automated checks handle volume well, but they need clear rules, careful testing, and a plan for cases the rules cannot judge. The BA sets out both options with their effects on effort, control, speed, and risk, so the business can choose the balance it wants.",
            "Prototyping can test an option cheaply, by letting stakeholders try a flow or a screen before the team commits to building it. The BA also checks each option against the requirements, the architecture, and the constraints that cannot be moved. An option that fails a hard constraint is not a real option, however attractive it looks in other respects. When options are compared well, the chosen design has a clear rationale, known trade-offs, and stakeholders who understand what was not chosen and why."
        ],
        outputName: "Design options paper",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Problem and constraints", guidance: "The problem the design must solve, and the constraints it must respect."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Options", guidance: "Each option: what it is, how it works, and what it changes."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Comparison", guidance: "The options compared on the same criteria: value, cost, risk, and fit with constraints."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Trade offs", guidance: "What each option gains and loses, stated plainly."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Recommendation", guidance: "The recommended option, or the decision still needed, with the reason.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Define options that are genuinely different, not one option written three ways. Each option should be described well enough to be compared fairly: what it is, how it works, and what it changes. Include the option of doing nothing, or doing the minimum, so the comparison has a baseline.",
            "Compare the options on the same criteria, with the same evidence standard for each. Value, cost, risk, and fit with constraints are the usual criteria. State the trade offs plainly: what each option gains and what it loses. The paper recommends an option or names the decision still needed, with the reason, so the reader can challenge the reasoning rather than guess at it."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: design options for duplicate detection",
        workedExample: [
            "Problem: duplicate payments reach settlement because the current check runs too late. Constraints: the check must run before settlement, and it must not delay legitimate payments beyond the agreed limit. Option 1: a check at initiation, comparing reference and amount. Option 2: a check at initiation plus a check before settlement. Option 3: do nothing and rely on manual review.",
            "Comparison: Option 1 catches most duplicates early at low cost. Option 2 catches more, including duplicates created after initiation, at higher cost. Option 3 costs nothing to build and leaves the risk unchanged. Trade off: Option 2 is safer but slower to build. Recommendation: Option 1 now, with Option 2 planned for the next release, because the immediate risk is the duplicate created at initiation."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Always set out more than one workable option.",
            "Compare options on behaviour, cost, risk, and trade-offs.",
            "Test options with models and prototypes before committing.",
            "Record why the chosen option was preferred."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-13", title: "The first idea became the design", takeaway: "Compare options before committing, or the first idea wins by default.", story: [
            "A senior stakeholder suggested a new queue for failed payments.\nThe room agreed quickly without exploring alternatives.\nNo other option was written down.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The queue was specified and built as suggested\n→ Staff found the queue mixed urgent and routine failures\n→ Urgent cases waited behind routine work\n→ A simple split by urgency would have helped\n→ Rework was needed to add prioritisation",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Just add a queue, that will fix it.\"",
            "So the team delivered exactly what was suggested and still missed the need, because the first idea solved visibility but not prioritisation, and no alternative had been considered to expose that gap.",
            "Why?",
            "The first idea is rarely the only idea, and it is rarely fully formed. Defining design options forces comparison, which reveals what each idea handles well and what it misses. Even a short comparison of two or three options can expose a missing feature, such as prioritisation, before build locks the design. The BA protects the decision by making alternatives visible and by asking what each option would mean on a busy day.",
            "A single option is not a choice.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Write down at least two workable options\n→ Compare each option on a busy day, not a quiet one\n→ Ask what each option handles poorly\n→ Record why the chosen option was preferred",
            "Have you ever watched a quick agreement become expensive rework?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-14", title: "The payment with no state for waiting", takeaway: "Model the states, or gaps will hide behind tidy flows.", story: [
            "A design covered created, approved, and settled states.\nNo state covered waiting for information.\nCases in that gap showed as approved.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ A payment waited for missing details\n→ The screen still showed it as approved\n→ Staff assumed it was progressing\n→ The client asked why nothing had happened\n→ The team added a waiting state after release",
            "The requirement said:\n\"Payments move from approved to settled.\"",
            "So waiting payments looked active while nothing was happening, and the gap only became clear when a client complaint exposed the missing state that the design had never named.",
            "Why?",
            "State Modelling asks what states a payment or case can be in, what moves it from one state to another, and whether every real situation has a state. Options that look complete on a happy path often miss waiting, failed, or expired states. Comparing options with a state view reveals those gaps early, when adding a state is a modelling change rather than a release fix.",
            "Every real situation needs a state.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ List all states, including waiting and failed\n→ Name the event that moves each state forward\n→ Check each option against the full state list\n→ Confirm what users see in each state",
            "What would you check when a flow looks too tidy to be true?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-15", title: "Two ways to handle flagged payments", takeaway: "Map options equally, or preference will decide for you.", story: [
            "Flagged payments needed review before release.\nThe team debated manual review and automated checks.\nBoth options sounded reasonable in discussion.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Manual review was mapped step by step\n→ Automated checks were mapped the same way\n→ The sequence showed manual review waiting on staff\n→ Automated checks waited only on exceptions\n→ The business chose automation with manual exceptions",
            "The request said:\n\"Review every flagged payment before release.\"",
            "So the team built automated checks for clear cases and a manual queue for exceptions, which kept control where judgement mattered and removed waiting where rules were clear.",
            "Why?",
            "Discussion alone rarely settles an option, because each option sounds good in isolation. Sequence Diagrams make timing, handovers, and waiting visible, so stakeholders can compare how each option actually behaves. Decision Modelling adds the rules that drive each branch, which shows whether an option can be automated fairly. Comparison on behaviour, not on preference, leads to a choice the team can defend and operate.",
            "Compare how options behave, not how they sound.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Map each option in the same level of detail\n→ Show waiting, handovers, and decision points\n→ Check which cases need human judgement\n→ Agree the balance of automation and review",
            "Have you ever compared options side by side and changed your mind?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-design-options-q1", question: "You are the BA and only one design option has been suggested. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I would thank the team for the suggestion and then ask whether we have explored enough to choose well. I would describe the suggested option in plain language, including what it handles well and what it might handle poorly. I would then ask what other approaches could meet the same requirements, even if they seem less familiar at first. I would set out at least one more workable option, using a simple model or Prototyping mock-up so stakeholders can compare behaviour rather than abstract ideas. I would compare the options on effort, speed, control, and risk, and I would be honest about what I do not yet know. I would use Decision Modelling to check whether the rules behind each option are clear enough to build from. I would invite stakeholders to challenge both options with real examples, especially busy-day cases and exceptions. Finally, I would record the comparison and the reason for the chosen option, so the decision stands up to later questions. My aim is to give decision makers a real choice, not to delay a good idea."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-design-options-q2", question: "How do you compare two design options fairly?", answer: [
            "I would compare both options at the same level of detail, because an option described in detail always looks stronger than one described vaguely. I would set out what each option does, step by step, using Sequence Diagrams to show order, handovers, and waiting. I would check each option against the full set of requirements, including states, rules, and exceptions, using State Modelling where the lifecycle matters. I would then compare effort, speed, control, and risk, and I would name the trade-offs in plain language. I would test each option against a busy day and a failure case, because options often differ most under pressure. I would involve the people who will operate the design, since they spot practical problems that a diagram can miss. I would present the comparison as a short, balanced summary, with the evidence behind each judgement, and I would avoid favouring an option before the evidence is heard. The decision owner then chooses with open eyes, and the record shows why the other option was not chosen."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-design-options-q3", question: "A senior stakeholder has already chosen a design before options were compared. How do you handle it?", answer: [
            "I would respect their experience and avoid turning the discussion into a contest. I would ask what outcome they value most in the chosen design, so I understand the reasoning behind the preference. I would then offer a short, focused comparison, framed as a check that the chosen design is the strongest one, rather than as a challenge to their judgement. I would set out one or two alternatives at the same level of detail, using models that show behaviour, waiting, and risk. I would use Decision Modelling and State Modelling to test whether the preferred design handles exceptions and waiting states fairly. If the comparison supports their choice, I will have strengthened the decision with evidence. If it reveals a gap, I can present the finding as a risk to address, with options for fixing it. I would record the decision, the evidence, and the trade-offs, so the choice is defensible. Throughout, I would keep the focus on the business outcome and the evidence, which protects both the relationship and the quality of the decision."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-define-design-options-q4", question: "Tell me about a time you recommended against the most popular design option. What happened?", answer: [
            "I worked on a proposal to add a manual review step for every payment above a certain value, and the idea was popular because it felt safe and familiar. I mapped the option with Sequence Diagrams and found that on busy days, the review queue would create long waits, while the risk it addressed was concentrated in a small set of unusual cases. I also used Decision Modelling to show that most cases could be judged by clear rules, with manual review reserved for exceptions. I presented the evidence plainly, including the waiting time, the staffing effect, and the cases where manual review added real value. I recommended automated checks with a manual exception queue, and I was honest that the popular option was not wrong, only less balanced for the volumes involved. The sponsor asked hard questions, which I welcomed, and we agreed to test the recommended option with a Prototyping walkthrough using real cases. The walkthrough confirmed the design, and the team built it with confidence. The lesson I kept was to respect popularity but decide on evidence, and to present a better option rather than only criticising the favoured one."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-api-requirements"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-analyze-potential-value-and-recommend-solution",
        title: "Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution",
        subtitle: "Recommend the solution with the strongest value",
        kind: .task,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: .analysisDesign,
        babokReference: "7.6",
        explanation: [
            "This task brings the analysis together by judging which solution offers the strongest value for the business. The BA compares the expected benefits, costs, risks, and effort of each option, and recommends the one that best meets the need. Value is not only financial, and the BA also considers effort saved, errors avoided, control strengthened, and experience improved. A recommendation without evidence is an opinion, while a recommendation with evidence is a decision the business can own.",
            "Business Rules Analysis supports the judgement by checking that each option respects the rules that cannot be bent or ignored. Non-Functional Requirements Analysis checks how each option performs under real conditions, including volume, timing, and resilience. An option that works in a quiet test but fails on a busy day, or breaks a control rule, does not offer real value however cheap it looks. The BA states these checks plainly, so decision makers see the full picture and not only the headline benefit.",
            "Consider a choice between improving the current payment process and replacing part of it with a new tool. Improving the current process costs less and disrupts less, but it may leave manual steps that limit future growth. Replacing part of the process costs more and needs training, but it may remove manual work and support higher volumes. The BA sets out both paths with their benefits, costs, risks, and effects on staff, so the business can choose the value it wants to buy.",
            "Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria help the business judge value after delivery, by stating how success will be recognised and measured. The BA recommends one option, explains the trade-offs, names the risks that remain, and states what must happen for value to be realised. The recommendation also covers what should not be built, or what should wait, because focus is part of value. When this task is done well, the business decides with confidence, the team builds with purpose, and success can be recognised when it arrives."
        ],
        outputName: "Value analysis and solution recommendation",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Options assessed", guidance: "The options, with their benefits, costs, and risks on the same basis."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Value measures", guidance: "How value is judged: the measures, the baselines, and the time horizon."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Analysis", guidance: "The comparison, including what is uncertain and how sensitive the result is to the uncertainty."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Recommendation", guidance: "The recommended solution, with the reason and the conditions."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Risks and next steps", guidance: "What could change the recommendation, and what happens next.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Judge value on measures agreed before the analysis, with baselines from the current state. Benefits, costs, and risks are compared on the same basis and over the same time horizon, or the comparison will favour whichever option was measured most generously. State what is uncertain, and how sensitive the result is to that uncertainty: a recommendation that flips when one assumption moves is a recommendation that needs a warning.",
            "Recommend one solution, with the reason and the conditions. The recommendation should be challengeable: a reader who disagrees should be able to point to the measure, the assumption, or the trade off they dispute. End with what could change the recommendation and what happens next, so the decision is a starting point, not an ending."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: value analysis for exception queue automation",
        workedExample: [
            "Options: manual queue ordering, semi automatic ordering with manual assignment, and full automation including assignment. Value measures: time from arrival to first action, and the count of exceptions worked twice, baselined from the current state log, judged over two releases.",
            "Analysis: semi automatic ordering delivers most of the benefit at a third of the cost of full automation, because assignment is the expensive part and the benefit of automatic assignment is uncertain until the queue is ordered. Recommendation: semi automatic ordering now, with full automation revisited after two releases of evidence. Risk: if volumes rise sharply, the recommendation is revisited sooner."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Judge value on benefits, costs, risks, and effort together.",
            "Check rules and real-world performance, not only headline benefits.",
            "Recommend one option and explain the trade-offs honestly.",
            "State how success will be recognised after delivery."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-16", title: "The cheap option that failed busy days", takeaway: "Judge value on busy-day reality, not quiet-day tests.", story: [
            "Two options were compared for payment validation.\nOne was cheaper to build.\nThe other handled higher volumes.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The cheaper option passed quiet-day testing\n→ Validation counted volumes on the busiest days\n→ The cheaper option would queue work for hours\n→ Staff would need overtime to clear the backlog\n→ The business chose the option that handled volume",
            "The request said:\n\"Pick the cheapest option that meets the requirements.\"",
            "So the business paid more to build and less to operate, because value counted busy-day performance and staff effort as well as build cost, and the cheaper option would have cost more in delay and overtime.",
            "Why?",
            "Value analysis looks beyond build cost to the full effect of an option. Non-Functional Requirements Analysis asks how each option behaves at real volumes, at peak times, and when parts fail. An option that queues work for hours does not meet the need, even if it meets the written requirements on a quiet day. The BA makes that effect visible so the business chooses value, not only price.",
            "Cheap to build can be expensive to run.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Test options against busy-day volumes\n→ Count effort, delay, and overtime, not only build cost\n→ State performance needs in observable terms\n→ Recommend on total value, not price alone",
            "Have you ever seen a cheap option cost more in daily work?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-17", title: "The fast option that broke a rule", takeaway: "Protect hard rules, and find value where control stays intact.", story: [
            "A proposed design removed a second approval step.\nIt would have cut processing time sharply.\nThe control team had not been consulted.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The design was presented as a time saving\n→ Business Rules Analysis checked the approval rule\n→ The rule required two approvers for the payment type\n→ Removing the step would breach the control\n→ The team redesigned to keep control and save time elsewhere",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Remove the second check, it only slows us down.\"",
            "So the team kept the second approval and saved time by improving the queue and the information approvers saw, which protected control while still improving speed where it was safe to do so.",
            "Why?",
            "Rules that protect control, accuracy, or trust are not optional extras to trade for speed. Business Rules Analysis identifies which rules must hold, why they exist, and what happens if they are broken. A recommendation that ignores a hard rule is not a value judgement, it is a risk the business has not agreed to take. The BA surfaces the rule early and looks for speed in places that do not weaken control.",
            "Speed gained by breaking a control is not value.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Check each option against the rules that must hold\n→ Ask why each rule exists before challenging it\n→ Find time savings that keep control intact\n→ Record rules that shaped the recommendation",
            "What would you do when speed and control pull in opposite directions?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-RD-18", title: "A recommendation the business could own", takeaway: "Recommend with open reasoning, and agree how success will be judged.", story: [
            "Three options had been compared for case handling.\nEach had different costs and benefits.\nThe sponsor needed a clear recommendation.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA set out benefits, costs, and risks for each option\n→ One option offered the strongest balance of value\n→ The BA recommended it with trade-offs stated plainly\n→ Success measures were agreed before build started\n→ The sponsor approved with confidence in the choice",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Tell us what you recommend, and why.\"",
            "So the sponsor approved a phased option that delivered the core case view first and added reporting later, because the recommendation showed what value would arrive first, what would wait, and how success would be recognised.",
            "Why?",
            "A good recommendation does more than name a winner. It explains the value, the cost, the risk, and the trade-offs, and it states how success will be judged after delivery. Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria turn vague hopes into observable checks, so the business can tell whether value arrived. When the reasoning is open, the decision is owned by the business, not borrowed from the BA.",
            "A recommendation earns trust when its reasoning is open.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Recommend one option, with reasons and trade-offs\n→ State what will wait and what will arrive first\n→ Agree how success will be recognised\n→ Name the risks that remain after the choice",
            "Have you ever owned a decision because the reasoning was clear?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-analyze-potential-value-and-recommend-solution-q1", question: "You must recommend between improving the current process and replacing part of it. How do you decide?", answer: [
            "I would compare both paths on the same evidence, so the choice is fair. I would set out the benefits, costs, risks, and effort of improving the current process, including the manual steps that would remain. I would do the same for replacement, including build cost, training, disruption, and the manual work it would remove. I would use Business Rules Analysis to check that both paths respect the rules that must hold, and Non-Functional Requirements Analysis to test how each path behaves at busy-day volumes. I would talk to the people who operate the process, because they know where effort and errors actually sit. I would then recommend one path, explain the trade-offs honestly, and state what would wait under each option. I would agree Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria for the recommended path, so success can be recognised after delivery. My recommendation would be clear, but I would leave the decision with the business, with the evidence in front of them. The aim is a choice the business owns, not a choice it later regrets."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-analyze-potential-value-and-recommend-solution-q2", question: "A sponsor asks you to recommend the cheapest option, even though you believe another option offers better value. What do you do?", answer: [
            "I would respect the sponsor's concern about cost, because budget pressure is real, and I would present the full value picture rather than arguing about price alone. I would set out the cheapest option with its build cost, its operating effort, its busy-day performance, and its risks. I would set out the stronger option the same way, so the comparison is honest and complete. I would use Non-Functional Requirements Analysis to show how each option behaves under real volumes, and I would count effort, delay, and rework, not only the initial spend. If the stronger option costs more to build but less to run, I would say so plainly, with the evidence behind the claim. I would recommend the option I believe offers the best total value, while making clear that the final decision belongs to the sponsor. I would record the recommendation, the trade-offs, and the risks that remain under each option. My duty is to give honest advice with evidence, even when it is uncomfortable, and to support the sponsor's decision once it is made."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-analyze-potential-value-and-recommend-solution-q3", question: "Tell me about a time your recommendation was challenged. How did you defend it?", answer: [
            "I recommended a phased option for a case handling improvement, with the core case view first and reporting later, and the proposal was challenged because reporting had strong supporters. I did not defend the recommendation by repeating it louder, I returned to the evidence. I showed the Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria we had agreed, the effort each phase required, and the value each phase would deliver. I used Business Rules Analysis to show that the core view addressed the rules and controls that mattered most, while reporting could wait without harming daily work. I invited the challengers to test my reasoning with real cases, and I listened carefully to their concerns about visibility for managers. We agreed a simple interim view for managers within the first phase, which addressed their need without delaying the core work. The sponsor approved the revised phased plan, and the team delivered the core view on time. The lesson I kept was that a recommendation is stronger when it survives challenge, and that listening during a challenge often improves the plan rather than weakening it."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-impact-assessment"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-measure-solution-performance",
        title: "Measure Solution Performance",
        subtitle: "Agree Measures, Then Judge Success",
        kind: .task,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: .solutionEvaluation,
        babokReference: "8.1",
        explanation: [
            "Measure Solution Performance is about checking whether a live solution delivers the value it was meant to deliver. The BA agrees clear measures before judging success, and ties each measure to a business need. A measure without a baseline is only a number, so the BA records how things worked before the change. This work turns opinion into evidence that stakeholders can trust.",
            "The BA uses Metrics and Key Performance Indicators to track outcomes that matter, such as time saved, errors avoided, or work completed without manual help. The BA also uses Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria agreed earlier to judge whether the solution meets its promise. Good measures cover value, quality, and use, not just activity. A busy system is not always a useful system.",
            "Without this task, teams celebrate launches and miss slow failures. A payments screen can look modern while staff still chase missing payments by phone and email. A new process can run on time while costs rise or customers struggle. Problems stay hidden until they become complaints, rework, or lost trust.",
            "In a payments example, a bank launches a new tracker for cross-border payments. The BA agrees measures for tracker use, query volume, and time to resolve a missing payment. The team checks the measures weekly and compares them with the old way of working. When queries fall and resolution time shortens, the team has real proof of value, not just a good feeling."
        ],
        outputName: "Solution performance measurement plan",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Measures", guidance: "Each measure: what it is, how it is calculated, and what good looks like."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Baselines", guidance: "The position before the change, for each measure, with the source and date."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Targets", guidance: "The target for each measure, and who agreed it."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Collection", guidance: "How each measure is collected, how often, and by whom."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Reporting", guidance: "Who sees the results, in what form, and how often.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Agree the measures before judging success, and tie each measure to a business need. A measure without a baseline is only a number, so record how things worked before the change, with the source and the date. Cover value, quality, and use, not just activity: a busy system is not always a useful system.",
            "Write the collection method for each measure: how it is gathered, how often, and by whom. A measure no one collects is a promise, not a measure. Report the results to the people who can act on them, in a form they will read, and keep the reporting rhythm steady, so a slow failure is seen early rather than at the annual review."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: measurement plan for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Measures: the share of customer queries answered from the screen without a manual check, the time from query to answer, and the count of status wording complaints. Baselines: taken from the query log for the month before release, with the source and date recorded. Targets: agreed with operations and the sponsor before release.",
            "Collection: the query log supplies the first two measures weekly. Complaints are counted from the customer feedback record monthly. Reporting: operations sees a weekly summary, the sponsor sees a monthly summary, and any measure moving the wrong way for two consecutive weeks is raised at the weekly review."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Agree measures and a baseline before judging success.",
            "Track value, quality, and use, not activity alone.",
            "Use Metrics and Key Performance Indicators tied to business needs.",
            "Check measures regularly and compare with the old way of working."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SE-01", title: "The Dashboard That Looked Healthy", takeaway: "Agree what good looks like before you call a solution a success.", story: [
            "A new payment tracker went live on Monday.\nThe dashboard showed green for every check.\nThe operations team still felt busy and stressed.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The tracker showed payments as delivered on time\n→ The team still received many calls about missing payments\n→ Staff checked the old system to answer each call\n→ No one had agreed what a good result would look like\n→ Leaders praised the launch while queries kept rising",
            "The report said:\n\"All checks passed. The tracker is working as planned.\"",
            "So the BA paused the praise and agreed measures for query volume, time to answer, and tracker use, then compared the first weeks with the old way of working, and the gap became clear to everyone.",
            "Why?",
            "A green dashboard only proves that checks ran. It does not prove that work got easier or customers got better answers. The BA lesson is to agree measures of value before go live, and to track them after. Without that link, a team can celebrate a launch while the real pain stays untouched.",
            "A healthy dashboard is not proof of a healthy outcome.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Agree value measures before launch, not after\n→ Track query volume, time, and use together\n→ Compare new results with the old baseline\n→ Share the gap plainly, even when the launch looks good",
            "Have you ever seen a green report hide a struggling team?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SE-02", title: "No Baseline, No Proof of Gain", takeaway: "Rebuild a fair baseline so improvement can be shown, not just felt.", story: [
            "A corporate onboarding portal replaced paper forms.\nLeaders asked whether onboarding was now faster.\nNo one could answer with evidence.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The old process had never been timed end to end\n→ Staff recalled it as slow, but recalls differed\n→ The new portal logged starts, but not all waits\n→ Teams argued from memory in every review\n→ The BA rebuilt a baseline from old files and logs",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"It feels faster, but I cannot prove it to the board.\"",
            "So the BA built a fair baseline from old case files, agreed a matching measure for the new portal, and showed the change over the same steps, which ended the argument from memory.",
            "Why?",
            "Improvement needs a starting point. Without a baseline, even a good solution cannot prove its worth, and a weak solution can hide behind good feelings. The BA rebuilds the past as fairly as possible, states the limits of that rebuild, and then measures the same work in the same way.",
            "Without a baseline, improvement is only a feeling.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Rebuild the baseline from files, logs, and case notes\n→ Measure the same steps before and after\n→ State what the baseline cannot prove\n→ Agree the comparison method with stakeholders early",
            "What would you do when no one measured the old way of working?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SE-03", title: "Counting Alerts Missed the Real Risk", takeaway: "Balance volume measures with quality and risk measures.", story: [
            "A new fraud alert tool went live for card payments.\nThe team counted alerts each day with pride.\nMissed cases and false alerts stayed out of sight.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Alert counts rose, so leaders called the tool a win\n→ Analysts spent hours clearing alerts that led nowhere\n→ A few real cases slipped past without an alert\n→ No measure tracked false alerts or missed cases\n→ The BA added balanced measures for quality and risk",
