        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-measure-solution-performance-q1", question: "You are the BA and a new payments dashboard has just gone live. How do you check whether it is actually working well?", answer: [
            "I would start by agreeing what good looks like before I judge the dashboard. I would sit with the business owner and the operations team and agree a small set of Metrics and Key Performance Indicators that link to real value. I would look at query volume, time to resolve a missing payment, and how often staff actually use the tracker. I would also record a baseline from the old way of working, so we can compare like with like. I would keep the measure set small, so the team can act on it. Then I would check the measures on a fixed rhythm, weekly at first, and share the results plainly. I would not rely on the dashboard colour alone, because green checks do not prove easier work. I would talk to users and listen to support calls for early signs of pain. If the numbers and the user stories point the same way, I would trust the picture. If they differ, I would dig deeper before I report success. My goal is honest evidence, not a good news story."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-measure-solution-performance-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you had to agree how success would be measured before a solution went live.", answer: [
            "In one rollout, the team was ready to launch a new case tracker, but no one had agreed how we would know it worked. I called a short session with the business owner, the team leads, and support, and we agreed Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria in plain words. We chose three measures: cases resolved without a follow-up call, time from case open to close, and share of work done inside the tracker. I pushed for a baseline, so we timed a sample of old cases and noted the limits of that sample honestly. We agreed who would own each measure, how often we would check it, and what good would look like in the first month. After launch, we reviewed the measures together each week, and we did not change the definitions mid-month. When one measure dipped, we had a shared language to discuss it, not a debate about feelings. That early agreement saved us from arguing about success later, and it kept the focus on value for users."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-measure-solution-performance-q3", question: "A stakeholder says the new solution is a success because everyone likes it. How do you respond?", answer: [
            "I would thank the stakeholder for the feedback, because user feeling matters, and then I would ask what liking the solution has changed in the work. I would say that feeling is one signal, but we also need evidence of value, quality, and use. I would suggest we check the Metrics and Key Performance Indicators we agreed before launch, such as time saved, errors avoided, and tasks completed without help. I would also check the baseline, so we can show change, not just a pleasant launch. If the measures support the feeling, I would celebrate with confidence. If they do not, I would share the gap plainly and ask what we should fix first. I would avoid sounding negative, and I would keep the focus on users and outcomes. I would propose a short review with users, support, and the owner, so we hear both stories and data together. My aim is to turn a good feeling into proven value, or to find the gap early while it is still cheap to fix."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-measure-solution-performance-q4", question: "You are the BA and the team has no baseline for the old process. How do you measure improvement?", answer: [
            "I would be honest that we cannot invent a perfect past, and then I would rebuild the fairest baseline we can. I would look at old case files, logs, emails, and reports, and I would time a small sample of old work where records still exist. I would agree a matching measure for the new process, so we compare the same steps, the same scope, and the same time window. I would write down what the rebuilt baseline can and cannot prove, and I would get the owner to agree those limits. Then I would track the new measures on a fixed rhythm and share the comparison in plain words. I would add user stories and support data, because numbers alone can mislead when the past is patchy. I would use Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria to judge whether the new way meets its promise, even with an imperfect past. If the gap is unclear, I would say so, and I would keep measuring until the picture is firm. Honest limits build more trust than false precision."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-reconciliation"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-analyze-performance-measures",
        title: "Analyze Performance Measures",
        subtitle: "Read Past the Headline Number",
        kind: .task,
        level: .advanced,
