        babokReference: "8.2",
        explanation: [
            "Analyze Performance Measures is about making sense of what the numbers are really saying. A single figure rarely tells the full story, so the BA looks for patterns, causes, and context before drawing a conclusion. The BA checks definitions, time periods, and groups behind each measure. This careful reading stops hasty fixes for the wrong problem.",
            "The BA may use Data Mining to look through larger sets of records for patterns that a summary report hides. Root Cause Analysis helps the BA move from a symptom, such as failed payments, to the reason behind it. Benchmarking and Market Analysis can add context, but only when the comparison is fair and the settings are alike. A fair comparison explains differences instead of shaming a team.",
            "Without analysis, teams react to noise and miss signals. A weekly dip can look like failure when it is only a calendar effect. A rising average can hide one group that is struggling badly. Leaders may fund the wrong fix while the real cause keeps growing.",
            "In a payments example, failed payments rise on one weekday only. The BA splits the data by channel, time, and payment type, and finds a batch timing clash. The fix is a timetable change, not a system rebuild. Good analysis saves money and keeps trust, because the action matches the evidence."
        ],
        outputName: "Performance measures analysis",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Results", guidance: "The measured results, against baselines and targets, with the period covered."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Patterns", guidance: "What the results show over time: trends, gaps, and surprises."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Causes", guidance: "The likely causes of each pattern, with the evidence for each."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Limits of the data", guidance: "What the measures do not show, and what data is missing or weak."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Conclusions", guidance: "What the analysis concludes, and what is still uncertain.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Present the results against baselines and targets before interpreting them. A result without its baseline invites an argument about whether it is good or bad, when the evidence should settle that question. Look for patterns over time rather than judging one period alone: trends, gaps, and surprises all carry information.",
            "Separate what the data shows from what it suggests. A cause is a conclusion that needs evidence, and the analysis should say how strong the evidence is. State the limits of the data plainly: what the measures do not show, and what is missing. A conclusion that ignores weak data will be believed until it fails, and then no conclusion will be believed."
