    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-assess-solution-limitations",
        title: "Assess Solution Limitations",
        subtitle: "Name What the Solution Cannot Do",
        kind: .task,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: .solutionEvaluation,
        babokReference: "8.3",
        explanation: [
            "Assess Solution Limitations is about finding what the solution cannot do, or cannot do well enough. Every solution has edges, and the BA makes those edges visible instead of letting users discover them by failure. The BA checks function, capacity, reliability, and fit with related work. A clear limit is easier to manage than a hidden one.",
            "The BA gathers evidence from use, defects, workarounds, and Reviews with users and support teams. Root Cause Analysis helps separate a true limit from a training gap or a single fault. The BA records the impact of each limit, who it affects, and how often it bites. A small limit that hits daily work can matter more than a large limit that rarely appears.",
            "Without this assessment, workarounds grow quietly and risks build up. Staff build side spreadsheets, copy data by hand, or skip steps to get the job done. The solution looks live while the real work happens outside it. Later, leaders are surprised by errors, delays, or audit findings.",
            "In a payments example, a reconciliation tool handles most payment types but fails on one complex type. The operations team reconciles that type by hand each morning, which adds delay and error risk. The BA documents the gap, its volume, and its cost in staff time. Leaders can then choose a fix with open eyes, instead of assuming the tool covers all work."
        ],
        outputName: "Solution limitations assessment",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Limitations found", guidance: "Each limitation: what the solution cannot do, or does poorly, with evidence."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Impact", guidance: "Who is affected, how often, and how seriously."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Causes", guidance: "Why the limitation exists: design, data, integration, or scope."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Workarounds in use", guidance: "What people do today to cope, and what the workaround costs."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Options", guidance: "The options for addressing each limitation, with their costs and benefits.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Describe each limitation as a fact with evidence, not as a complaint. What the solution cannot do, or does poorly, who is affected, and how often: those three facts let a reader judge seriousness without having to trust the analyst's tone. Separate the limitation from its cause, because the same limitation can have a design cause, a data cause, or a scope cause, and each cause points to a different fix.",
            "Record the workarounds people use today, and what they cost. Workarounds are evidence of the limitation's real impact, and they often hide the limitation from the measures. End with options for each limitation, costed on the same basis, so the decision is between real alternatives rather than between a fix and a shrug."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: limitations assessment for a payment hub",
        workedExample: [
