            "Separate true limits from training gaps and single faults.",
            "Watch for workarounds, they signal hidden limits.",
            "Use Reviews with users and support teams to gather evidence."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SE-09", title: "The Spreadsheet Beside the New Tool", takeaway: "Treat workarounds as clues to solution limits.", story: [
            "A reconciliation tool went live for daily work.\nStaff still kept a side spreadsheet every morning.\nLeaders assumed the tool covered all cases.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The tool handled most payment types well\n→ One complex type failed matching each day\n→ Staff reconciled that type by hand in a spreadsheet\n→ The workaround was never logged or reviewed\n→ The BA documented the gap, volume, and risk",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"The tool is live, so manual work should have stopped.\"",
            "So the BA assessed the limit openly, showed the daily hand work and error risk, and leaders chose a targeted fix for that payment type.",
            "Why?",
            "Workarounds are signals, not disobedience. When staff build side tools, they are often covering a real limit in the solution. The BA treats the workaround as evidence, measures its cost and risk, and brings the limit into the open. Hidden limits grow, named limits can be managed.",
            "A workaround is evidence of a limit, not proof of failure by users.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Ask where side tools and hand work still live\n→ Record the gap, volume, and risk in plain terms\n→ Share the limit with owners, not just users\n→ Choose a fix that matches the size of the gap",
            "Have you ever found important work hiding in a side spreadsheet?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SE-10", title: "Fine Daily, Slow at Month End", takeaway: "Peak load reveals limits that daily use hides.", story: [
            "A reporting solution ran smoothly most days.\nAt month end, reports slowed to a crawl.\nTeams waited, reran reports, and missed deadlines.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Daily volumes stayed well within capacity\n→ Month end volumes spiked sharply for two days\n→ Queries queued, timed out, and had to rerun\n→ The BA tested the solution at peak volume\n→ A scheduling and query fix eased the peak",
            "The report said:\n\"The solution meets performance needs in normal use.\"",
            "So the BA proved the limit only appeared at peak, and the team fixed scheduling and queries instead of blaming users for running reports at the wrong time.",
            "Why?",
