        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-solution-limitations-q1", question: "You are the BA and users keep using spreadsheets alongside a new tool. What do you investigate?", answer: [
            "I would start by treating the spreadsheets as clues, not as bad behaviour. I would sit with users and ask what the spreadsheet does that the tool does not. I would watch the daily work, and I would note which payment types, steps, or reports still live outside the tool. I would check how often the workaround is used, who uses it, and what happens if it fails. I would ask about training, confidence, and support, because a workaround can also signal a readiness gap. Then I would test whether the gap is a true solution limitation, a missing feature, or a habit that never changed. I would document the limit in plain words, with its volume, cost, and risk, and I would share it with the owner. I would use Reviews with users and support to confirm I have the full picture. My goal is to bring the hidden work into the open, so leaders can fix the real gap instead of blaming users."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-solution-limitations-q2", question: "A stakeholder says the solution is faulty. How do you check whether it is a solution limit or a use problem?", answer: [
            "I would listen first, and I would ask for examples of where the solution failed, and for whom. I would then check the facts in three places: the solution, the data, and the way people use it. I would test the reported steps myself where I can, and I would check logs, defects, and support notes for a pattern. I would also check training, guides, and recent changes in process, because a use problem can look like a fault. I would use Root Cause Analysis to trace the chain from the symptom to the cause, step by step. If the cause sits in the solution, I would document the solution limitation with impact and frequency. If it sits in use, data, or process, I would say so plainly, and I would propose coaching, clearer guides, or a data fix. I would share the evidence with the stakeholder, not just my view. My aim is a fair verdict that points to the right fix, not a defence of the tool."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-solution-limitations-q3", question: "Tell me about a time you found a limitation that only appeared under peak load or a rare case.", answer: [
            "In one reporting project, the solution ran well on normal days, and everyone trusted it. At month end, reports slowed badly, timed out, and teams missed their deadlines. I asked for a peak test, because average load was not the right test for this service. We replayed month end volumes in a safe test, and we watched what slowed first and what failed first. The issue was a mix of scheduling and heavy queries that queued at the same time. I documented the limit as a peak solution limitation, with the volume that triggered it and the teams affected. We fixed the schedule, eased the heaviest queries, and we agreed to test at peak after every major change. I also set up a simple peak watch, so the next spike would be seen early. I learned to judge a solution at the load it must survive, not at the load it enjoys. Peaks reveal the truth that daily averages hide."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-solution-limitations-q4", question: "A stakeholder asks whether the current solution can support a new payment format. How do you assess that?", answer: [
            "I would start by mapping the new format in plain terms: the fields, the checks, the volumes, and the dates it must support. I would then test the current solution against that map, field by field, and I would note what it can store, validate, and report today. I would look for stretch points, such as fields the solution cannot hold, or checks it cannot run without major work. I would speak to the technical team about effort, risk, and the effect on existing flows, and I would ask what would break if we forced a workaround. I would use Decision Analysis to compare a phased upgrade, a rebuild, and a short-term workaround on value, cost, risk, and timing. I would also check Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria for the new need, so we judge fit against the future, not the past. I would present the options with honest effort ranges, and I would warn early if a small tweak is really a rebuild. My goal is a clear fit verdict, with options leaders can own."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-exception-handling"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-assess-enterprise-limitations",
        title: "Assess Enterprise Limitations",
        subtitle: "Check Readiness Around the Solution",
        kind: .task,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: .solutionEvaluation,
        babokReference: "8.4",
        explanation: [
            "Assess Enterprise Limitations is about finding what holds the wider organisation back from gaining full value. The solution may work, but the business around it may not be ready. The BA looks at skills, processes, data, roles, and ways of working. Value depends on the whole setting, not only the tool.",
            "The BA checks whether people know the new way, whether data is fit for use, and whether owners are clear. Lessons Learned from earlier rollouts can reveal patterns, such as training that ends too early or support that fades too fast. The BA also checks local differences, because one team may be ready while another is not. Naming these limits early lets leaders fix readiness, not just software.",
            "Without this task, teams blame the solution for business gaps. A good tool is called a failure when staff were never trained or data was never cleaned. Repeated fixes to the tool do not help, because the blocker sits elsewhere. Trust falls, and the next change faces even more doubt.",
            "In a payments example, a new case tool works well in one office but struggles in another. The second office has older customer records, fewer trained staff, and a different approval habit. The BA maps these gaps team by team and recommends targeted help. The same tool then gains value without a rebuild, because the setting around it improves."
        ],
        outputName: "Enterprise limitations assessment",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Limitations found", guidance: "Each limitation in the wider organisation: skills, processes, data, culture, or capacity."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Impact on the solution", guidance: "How each limitation stops the solution delivering its value."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Evidence", guidance: "The sources for each limitation, with dates."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Readiness", guidance: "Whether the organisation is ready to use the solution as intended."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Actions needed", guidance: "The actions that sit outside the solution itself: training, process change, or resourcing.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Look beyond the solution at the organisation around it. A solution can work perfectly and still fail to deliver value because the skills, the process, the data, or the capacity around it are not ready. Name each limitation, its evidence, and its impact on the solution's value, so the conversation moves from blaming the system to fixing the setting it works in.",
            "Judge readiness honestly. If the organisation is not ready to use the solution as intended, say so, and name the actions that sit outside the solution: training, process change, or resourcing. Those actions need owners and dates just as build actions do, or they will be agreed in the meeting and forgotten by the following week."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: enterprise limitations for a new payment queue",
        workedExample: [
            "Limitation: only half the operations team has been trained on the new queue, and the training covered the happy path only. Impact: untrained officers avoid the queue and work from the old list, so the benefits of ordering and ownership do not arrive. Evidence: training records and observation of the queue in use.",
            "Readiness: not ready. Actions needed: training for the remaining officers, including exception cases, owned by the operations lead, within two weeks. A second limitation: the queue owner role has no backup, so absence leaves the queue unmanaged. Action: name and train a backup owner before the next release."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Check skills, processes, data, roles, and ways of working.",
            "Readiness can differ by team, office, or region.",
            "Use Lessons Learned to spot repeated readiness gaps.",
            "Fix the setting around the tool when the tool itself works."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SE-12", title: "The Tool Was Ready, the Team Was Not", takeaway: "Check readiness before you blame the tool.", story: [
            "A new case tool launched with strong features.\nUse stayed low, and old habits stayed strong.\nLeaders began to call the tool a failure.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Training was a single short session before launch\n→ No practice time was given in live work\n→ Team leads kept asking for the old reports\n→ The BA reviewed skills, habits, and support\n→ Coaching and practice time lifted use steadily",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"We bought a good tool, why is no one using it well?\"",
            "So the BA showed the limit sat in readiness, not in the tool, and the team added coaching, practice, and lead support instead of buying another tool.",
            "Why?",
            "Adoption is a business outcome, not a software feature. People need time, practice, and encouragement to change habits, especially under daily pressure. The BA checks readiness with the same care as function, and recommends help where help is missing. Blaming the tool wastes the investment already made.",
            "Readiness decides value as much as features do.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Check training, practice, and daily support\n→ Watch what team leads ask for, they set habits\n→ Add coaching before adding features\n→ Track use alongside confidence, not just logins",
            "Have you ever seen training end before habits had time to change?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SE-13", title: "One Hub, Two Very Different Offices", takeaway: "Local readiness explains different results from one solution.", story: [
            "A payments hub worked well in the first office.\nThe second office struggled from the first week.\nThe same software produced different results.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The first office had clean records and trained staff\n→ The second office had older records and gaps in cover\n→ Approval habits differed between the offices\n→ The BA mapped readiness office by office\n→ Targeted help closed the gap without a rebuild",
            "The report said:\n\"The hub is live in both offices, with mixed results.\"",
            "So the BA proved the difference came from local readiness, data, and habits, and leaders funded targeted fixes for the second office instead of changing the hub for everyone.",
            "Why?",
            "Enterprise limits are rarely uniform. Data quality, skills, cover, and local habits vary, so the same solution lands differently in each setting. The BA compares settings fairly, names the local gaps, and tailors the help. One blanket fix rarely fits all offices.",
            "The same solution can succeed in one setting and struggle in another.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Map readiness team by team, not just overall\n→ Compare data, skills, cover, and habits fairly\n→ Tailor help to the local gap\n→ Share what the stronger office does differently",
            "What would you compare first when one office thrives and another struggles?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SE-14", title: "Good Tool, Poor Data, No Owner", takeaway: "Name the owner, or the data will decay.", story: [
            "A customer view tool promised a single clear record.\nRecords stayed messy, duplicated, and out of date.\nStaff stopped trusting the tool within weeks.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ No team owned cleaning or updating the records\n→ Each team fixed its own copy in its own way\n→ Duplicates grew, and matching grew harder\n→ The BA traced the limit to ownership and process\n→ Named owners and simple rules restored trust",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"The tool shows the wrong record, so the tool must be wrong.\"",
            "So the BA showed the tool faithfully showed poor data, and the fix was ownership, rules, and regular cleaning, not a new tool.",
            "Why?",
            "A solution can only be as good as the data and ownership around it. Without a named owner, clear rules, and regular care, data decays and trust follows. The BA names the ownership gap plainly, because tools cannot fix a missing owner. Lessons Learned from past launches often point to the same missing role.",
            "No owner means no trust, however good the tool looks.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Name a data owner for each key record\n→ Agree simple rules for updates and duplicates\n→ Schedule regular cleaning, not single fixes\n→ Track trust and use, not just data counts",
            "Have you ever watched good software lose trust over poor data?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-enterprise-limitations-q1", question: "You are the BA and a good tool is not being used well. How do you check for business-side causes?", answer: [
            "I would check the setting around the tool before I blame the tool itself. I would look at training, practice time, guides, and the help available when users get stuck. I would watch daily work, and I would ask team leads what they still ask for in the old way, because leads set habits. I would check data quality, roles, and handoffs, since a good tool cannot fix messy inputs or unclear ownership. I would talk to users in their own words, and I would ask what would help them use the tool with confidence. I would map readiness team by team, because one team may be ready while another is not. I would use Lessons Learned from earlier rollouts to spot patterns, such as support that fades too fast. I would then recommend targeted help, such as coaching or clearer guides, and I would track use alongside confidence. My goal is to fix readiness where readiness is the blocker, not to add features to a tool people do not yet trust."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-enterprise-limitations-q2", question: "Tell me about a time poor data or unclear ownership limited a solution. What did you do?", answer: [
            "In one project, a customer view tool showed the wrong record too often, and staff stopped trusting it. At first, the tool was blamed, but I checked the data behind it. I found duplicates, out-of-date records, and no named owner for cleaning or updates. Each team fixed its own copy in its own way, so the copies drifted apart. I traced the limit to ownership and process, not to the software. I proposed a named owner for each key record, simple rules for updates and duplicates, and a regular cleaning rhythm. I used Reviews with the teams to agree the rules in plain words, and we tracked trust and use, not just data counts. Over a few weeks, records improved, and staff began to trust the tool again. I learned that a tool can only show what the business cares for. Without an owner, data decays, and trust follows. Naming the owner was the smallest fix with the largest effect."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-enterprise-limitations-q3", question: "You are the BA and a solution works in one team but not another. How do you compare the two settings?", answer: [
            "I would compare the settings fairly, team by team, instead of judging the solution as a whole. I would map data quality, skills, cover, approval habits, and support for each team on the same template. I would spend time with both teams, watching the work and listening to their own words. I would look for local differences that explain the gap, such as older records, fewer trained staff, or a different handoff. I would use Benchmarking and Market Analysis carefully, but the most useful benchmark is often the stronger team in the same business. I would share what the stronger team does differently, in practical steps others can copy. Then I would recommend targeted help for the struggling team, rather than changing the solution for everyone. I would track the gap over time, to show whether the help is working. I would keep the comparison kind, because teams share more when they do not feel judged. My goal is to respect local reality, and to close the readiness gap without breaking what already works."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-assess-enterprise-limitations-q4", question: "A senior leader blames the system for slow adoption. How do you show the wider business limits without sounding defensive?", answer: [
            "I would start by agreeing with the concern, because slow adoption is a real problem whatever its cause. I would then share evidence, not argument, showing what I checked in the system, the data, the training, and the daily habits. I would use a simple picture: what the tool does well, where readiness is thin, and where the two meet. I would quote user words, with care, to show the lived experience behind the numbers. I would name the enterprise limitations plainly, such as training that ended too early, data with no owner, or leads still asking for old reports. I would pair each limit with a practical fix, an owner, and a way to track progress, so the conversation stays forward-looking. I would use Lessons Learned to show this pattern is common, not a personal failure. I would invite the leader to sponsor the readiness fixes, so the help has weight. My aim is to move from blame to ownership, with respect for the leader and for the teams doing the work."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-stakeholder-management"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-task-recommend-actions-to-increase-solution-value",
        title: "Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value",
        subtitle: "Choose the Move That Unlocks Value",
        kind: .task,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: .solutionEvaluation,
        babokReference: "8.5",
        explanation: [
            "Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value is about choosing the next best move with evidence. The BA turns findings into clear options, such as fix, improve, replace, retire, or do nothing for now. Each option needs its benefit, cost, risk, and effect on users. A recommendation without options is only an opinion.",
            "Decision Analysis helps the BA compare options against agreed criteria, instead of choosing the loudest request. Financial Analysis adds a check on cost, saving, and payback in plain terms. Lessons Learned keep the BA honest about what worked before and what did not. The best choice is often small and focused, not large and dramatic.",
            "Without this task, teams add features while value leaks elsewhere. Leaders fund new work while adoption, data, or support stays weak. Money goes to visible change instead of the change that unlocks value. The solution grows, but the benefit does not.",
            "In a payments example, leaders want new tracker features while many users still avoid the current tracker. The BA shows that training and query handling would unlock more value than new features. The recommendation is to fix adoption first, then add one feature that users actually asked for. Value rises because the team fixes the blocker, not just the wishlist."
        ],
        outputName: "Value improvement recommendation",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Findings summary", guidance: "What the performance measures and limitation assessments found, in brief."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Options", guidance: "The actions available: improve, replace, retire, or keep, each with benefits, costs, and risks."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Recommendation", guidance: "The recommended action, with the reason and the conditions."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Sequence", guidance: "The order of actions, with the reason for the order."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Measures", guidance: "How the effect of the action is judged, with baselines.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Start from the findings, not from a preference. The performance measures and the limitation assessments say what is working and what is not, and the recommendation must follow from them. Consider the full range of actions: improve the solution, replace part of it, retire it, or keep it as it is. Keeping a solution that works is a valid recommendation, and it should be written with the same care as a recommendation to change.",
            "Recommend one action, or one ordered sequence, with the reason and the conditions. State how the effect is judged, with baselines taken before the action, so the next assessment can tell whether the action helped. A recommendation without a measure is an opinion that cannot be checked."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: recommendation for the payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Findings: the screen works where status data exists. One payment type lacks status data, and half the team is untrained on exception cases. Options: receive the missing status report, train the remaining team, or both. Both is recommended, because each option fixes a different gap and neither alone delivers the full value.",
            "Sequence: training first, because it needs no build and delivers value immediately, then the status report, which needs technology effort. Measures: the share of queries answered from the screen, and the time from query to answer, baselined before training, judged after four weeks. Condition: if the status report cost exceeds the agreed limit, the recommendation is revisited."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Present clear options with benefit, cost, risk, and user effect.",
            "Use Decision Analysis to compare options against agreed criteria.",
            "Use Financial Analysis to check cost and saving in plain terms.",
            "Fix the value blocker first, even when a new feature looks tempting."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SE-15", title: "Fix, Replace, or Live With It?", takeaway: "Compare options on agreed criteria, not on loudest voice.", story: [
            "A payment matching solution caused daily pain.\nLeaders were split on what to do next.\nEach option had fans, costs, and risks.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ Fixing the current tool would be fast but partial\n→ Replacing it would be slow but more complete\n→ Living with it would keep the workaround cost\n→ The BA scored each option on agreed criteria\n→ Leaders chose a fix now, with a replace review later",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"We need a decision, not another debate about options.\"",
            "So the BA used Decision Analysis to compare fix, replace, and live with it on value, cost, risk, and timing, and leaders made a calm, shared choice.",
            "Why?",
            "Hard choices need a shared method, not louder voices. When criteria are agreed first, options can be compared fairly, and compromises become visible. The BA keeps the comparison honest, shows what each option gives up, and lets leaders own the final call.",
            "Agree the criteria first, then compare the options.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ List real options, including do nothing for now\n→ Agree criteria before scoring any option\n→ Show benefit, cost, risk, and timing side by side\n→ Record the choice and the reason, for later review",
            "What would you do when leaders are split and every option has a cost?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SE-16", title: "The Costly Feature Few People Used", takeaway: "Check value against cost, and retire what no longer earns its keep.", story: [
            "A reporting feature cost much to keep running.\nOnly a small group used it, and rarely.\nNo one had checked its value for a long time.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA checked use, cost, and manual alternatives\n→ Use was low, cost was high, and a simple export sufficed\n→ The small group feared losing a familiar report\n→ The BA offered a simpler export plus short help\n→ Retiring the feature freed funds for higher value work",
            "The report said:\n\"The feature is stable and available to all users.\"",
            "So the BA used Financial Analysis in plain terms, showed cost against value, and leaders retired the feature and reinvested the saving in work users actually needed.",
            "Why?",
            "Value means benefit minus cost, not availability alone. A stable feature can still destroy value when it costs more to run than it returns. The BA checks use and cost together, listens to the small group affected, and offers a humane path away from the old way. Retiring well is also creating value.",
            "Retiring low value work can create more value than adding features.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Check use and cost together, in plain terms\n→ Ask what simpler path would serve the small group\n→ Offer help with the change, not just a switch off\n→ Reinvest the saving visibly, so trust grows",
            "Have you ever defended retiring something that still technically worked?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SE-17", title: "Small Fixes Beat Another Big Launch", takeaway: "Phase small wins to rebuild trust after a troubled launch.", story: [
            "A troubled rollout left users wary of change.\nLeaders wanted a bold relaunch to restore faith.\nThe BA feared a big launch would repeat the harm.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA ran a Lessons Learned review with users\n→ Users asked for small, visible fixes, not new features\n→ Support gaps and unclear guides topped the list\n→ The BA proposed phased fixes with quick wins first\n→ Trust returned as each small fix landed well",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"We need a big relaunch to show we listened.\"",
            "So the BA recommended phased fixes grounded in Lessons Learned, leaders agreed, and steady small wins rebuilt trust faster than a dramatic relaunch would have done.",
            "Why?",
            "After a poor rollout, trust is rebuilt by kept promises, not by grand gestures. Small fixes that land well prove that leaders listened, and each win makes the next change easier. The BA resists the urge to fix a credibility gap with scale, and chooses a rhythm the team can sustain.",
            "Trust returns through small kept promises, not grand relaunches.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Start with Lessons Learned from users and support\n→ Rank fixes by visible relief, not by novelty\n→ Deliver in phases, and prove each phase landed\n→ Tell users what changed, and what comes next",
            "What would you recommend when leaders want a big relaunch after a poor rollout?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-SE-18", title: "New Features, or Better Adoption First?", takeaway: "Unlock adoption first, then add features users want.", story: [
            "Leaders asked for new tracker features.\nMany users still avoided the current tracker.\nThe BA saw adoption, not features, as the blocker.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The BA checked tracker use across teams\n→ Use was low where training and help were thin\n→ Users asked for clearer guidance, not more screens\n→ The BA proposed adoption fixes before new features\n→ Leaders agreed, and added one requested feature later",
            "The request said:\n\"Add three new features to the tracker this quarter.\"",
            "So the BA showed that adoption fixes would unlock more value than new features, leaders sequenced the work, and the later feature landed on a base of confident users.",
            "Why?",
            "New features on top of low adoption add complexity without value. When users do not trust or understand the current tool, more screens only deepen the gap. The BA sequences work by blocker, not by wishlist, and proves the case with use data and user words. Patience here creates faster value later.",
            "Fix adoption before adding features to an unused tool.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Check use before agreeing to new features\n→ Ask users what would help them use the tool well\n→ Sequence adoption fixes first, features second\n→ Add the one feature users actually asked for, with care",
            "Have you ever had to say no to new features so adoption could catch up?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-recommend-actions-to-increase-solution-value-q1", question: "You are the BA and you must recommend fix, replace, or retire for a struggling solution. How do you approach it?", answer: [
            "I would start by listing the real options, including fix, replace, retire, and do nothing for now. I would not let one option disappear because it is unpopular. I would agree criteria with leaders before I score anything, covering value, cost, risk, timing, and effect on users. I would gather evidence for each option, using use data, support notes, and Financial Analysis in plain terms. I would use Decision Analysis to score each option against the agreed criteria, and I would show the compromises clearly. I would talk to users, because a neat score can hide a painful change for the people who live with the tool. I would present the options side by side, with a clear recommendation and the reason for it. I would also name what we give up with the chosen path, so leaders own the choice with open eyes. I would record the decision, the criteria, and the review date, so we can learn from the outcome later."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-recommend-actions-to-increase-solution-value-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you recommended stopping or retiring something to protect value.", answer: [
            "In one project, a reporting feature cost much to run, but only a small group used it, and rarely. No one had checked its value for a long time, because it was stable and familiar. I checked use, running cost, and the simpler paths that could meet the real need. I found a simple export would serve the small group well, with short help to ease the change. I used Financial Analysis to show cost against value in plain terms, without jargon. I spoke to the affected group first, listened to their fears, and offered the export plus help, not just a switch off. I recommended retirement, with the saving reinvested in work users actually needed. Leaders agreed, and the saving was shown openly, which built trust. I learned that stopping well is part of a BA role, not a failure. A stable feature can still destroy value when it costs more than it returns, and retiring it can free the team to do better work."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-recommend-actions-to-increase-solution-value-q3", question: "A stakeholder wants new features, but you think adoption is the real blocker. How do you make your case?", answer: [
            "I would respect the request, because it usually comes from a real pain, and I would ask what the new features are meant to fix. I would then check current use across teams, to see whether the existing tool is trusted and used well. In my case, use was low where training and help were thin, and users asked for clearer guidance, not more screens. I would show the use data alongside user words, so the blocker is visible, not just my opinion. I would propose a sequence: fix adoption first, with coaching, guides, and support, and then add the one feature users actually asked for. I would use Metrics and Key Performance Indicators to track whether adoption is rising, and I would agree a review date for the feature. I would keep the tone helpful, not blocking, and I would show how the sequence leads to faster value. New features on an unused tool add complexity, while adoption fixes unlock the value already paid for. Patience here creates faster value later."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-task-recommend-actions-to-increase-solution-value-q4", question: "You are the BA and leaders disagree on the next step. How do you help them choose with evidence?", answer: [
            "I would slow the debate down, and I would move it from positions to evidence. I would ask each leader what outcome matters most, and I would write those outcomes as shared criteria. I would list the real options, with benefit, cost, risk, and timing for each, using Decision Analysis to keep the comparison fair. I would add Financial Analysis in plain terms, so cost and saving are visible without jargon. I would bring user evidence, use data, and Lessons Learned from similar choices, so the past informs the present. I would show where leaders agree, because shared ground is often larger than it feels in a heated room. I would present the options side by side, with a recommendation and the compromises it involves. I would ask leaders to own the choice, and I would record the criteria, the decision, and the review date. My role is not to pick for them, but to make the choice clear, fair, and owned. Evidence calms debate, and shared criteria turn disagreement into a decision."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-business-requirements"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    ]
}

extension StudioLesson {
    /// All 50 BABOK techniques, in chapter order.
    static let techniques: [StudioLesson] = [
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-acceptance-and-evaluation-criteria",
        title: "Acceptance and Evaluation Criteria",
        subtitle: "Acceptance and evaluation criteria state, in advance, how a requirement or a solution is judged.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.1",
        explanation: [
            "Acceptance and evaluation criteria state, in advance, how a requirement or a solution is judged. Acceptance criteria say what must be true for a piece of work to be accepted. Evaluation criteria say how options are compared when a choice must be made. Both are agreed before the work is judged, so the judgement rests on a standard, not on memory.",
            "This technique matters because it removes argument from acceptance. Without agreed criteria, every delivery becomes a negotiation about what good looks like, conducted after the work is done. With criteria agreed early, builders know what to aim for, testers know what to check, and stakeholders know what they are accepting."
        ],
        outputName: "Acceptance and evaluation criteria list",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Criterion", guidance: "One testable statement: the condition and the expected result."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Source", guidance: "The requirement or need the criterion checks."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "How it is checked", guidance: "The evidence that proves the criterion is met.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write each criterion as one condition and one expected result, in words a tester can check without asking you what you meant. Avoid vague words: a criterion that cannot be observed cannot be passed or failed, it can only be argued about. Agree the criteria with the people who will accept the work, before build starts, and record the agreement."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: acceptance criteria for a payment status display",
        workedExample: [
            "AC1: when a status report has been received, the screen shows the status and the time it was received. AC2: when no status report has been received, the screen says that no status has been received, and shows no invented status. AC3: the screen never shows a payment as settled before settlement is confirmed.",
            "Each criterion names its source requirement and its check: AC1 is checked by viewing a payment with a received status report, AC2 by viewing a payment with no report, and AC3 by viewing a payment that is accepted but not yet settled. The criteria were agreed with operations and compliance before build."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-acceptance-criteria"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-backlog-management",
        title: "Backlog Management",
        subtitle: "Backlog management is the ongoing work of keeping a product backlog ordered, clear, and ready.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.2",
        explanation: [
            "Backlog management is the ongoing work of keeping a product backlog ordered, clear, and ready. It covers adding items, ordering them by value and risk, splitting large items into smaller ones, and refining the items near the top so the team can build them without guessing. The backlog is a living list, not a document written once.",
            "This technique matters because the backlog is where priorities become real. A backlog that is unordered, stale, or full of large vague items will stall a team even when everyone is working hard. A backlog whose top items are small, clear, and ordered by value lets the team start the most valuable work first and lets stakeholders see what is coming and why."
        ],
        outputName: "Ordered product backlog",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Item", guidance: "The user story or work item, written from the user's point of view."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Order", guidance: "The position, with the reason: value, risk, or dependency."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Readiness", guidance: "Whether the item is clear enough to build, and what is still open.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Order the backlog by value and risk, not by who asked loudest, and keep the top items small and clear while leaving later items light. Refine little and often: a short regular session beats a long occasional one. Remove or rewrite stale items rather than letting them rot, and record why an item moved, so the order can be explained to anyone who asks."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: backlog for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Top item: as a payment operations officer, I want to see the latest status and its time, so that I can answer customer queries without a manual check. It is small, it has acceptance criteria, and it is ready. Second: the status history view, which depends on the first item. Third: the status filter, deferred because no one asked for it and it adds no value yet.",
            "The order is explained by value and dependency: the status display unblocks the history view and answers the most common query. The filter stays at the bottom with a note recording why it was deferred, so it is not silently lost and not silently built."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-user-stories"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-balanced-scorecard",
        title: "Balanced Scorecard",
        subtitle: "A balanced scorecard judges performance from several angles at once, rather than from cost or activity alone.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.3",
        explanation: [
            "A balanced scorecard judges performance from several angles at once, rather than from cost or activity alone. The usual angles cover financial results, customer outcomes, internal process quality, and learning and growth. Each angle carries measures, targets, and the actions that move them, so a gain in one angle is not bought by a hidden loss in another.",
            "This technique matters because single measure judgements mislead. A payments change that cuts cost while complaints rise has not necessarily succeeded. A scorecard forces the conversation to cover all four angles together, and it makes trade offs visible before they are made, rather than discovered afterwards."
        ],
        outputName: "Balanced scorecard",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Angle", guidance: "One of the four angles: financial, customer, process, or learning."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Measure and target", guidance: "The measure, its baseline, and its agreed target."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Action", guidance: "The action that moves the measure, with an owner.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Choose a small number of measures per angle, each tied to a real outcome, and agree targets with baselines before judging anything. Keep the scorecard to one page: if it needs a second page, it has too many measures and no one will read it. Review it as a set, asking what each angle says about the others, and record the trade offs discussed."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: scorecard for a payment operations change",
        workedExample: [
            "Financial angle: handling cost per exception, baselined from the current log. Customer angle: time from query to answer. Process angle: share of exceptions resolved without rework. Learning angle: share of the team trained on exception cases. Each measure has a target agreed with operations and the sponsor.",
            "After one release, handling cost fell and query time fell, but rework rose slightly. The scorecard made the trade off visible: speed had been bought with less checking. The action was to add a checking step to the two most reworked exception types, owned by the operations lead."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-benchmarking-and-market-analysis",
        title: "Benchmarking and Market Analysis",
        subtitle: "Benchmarking and market analysis compare an organisation's performance, practices, or offering with others, inside or outside the industry.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.4",
        explanation: [
            "Benchmarking and market analysis compare an organisation's performance, practices, or offering with others, inside or outside the industry. Benchmarking compares measures: how long, how much, how often. Market analysis studies the wider market: what customers expect, what competitors offer, and where the market is heading. Both turn inward looking assumptions into outward looking evidence.",
            "This technique matters because internal targets set without external comparison drift. A handling time that looks fast inside one team may be slow beside the market. Used honestly, with comparable measures and stated limits, benchmarking and market analysis show where improvement matters most and where the organisation already leads."
        ],
        outputName: "Benchmarking and market analysis note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Measures compared", guidance: "The measures, defined the same way on both sides of the comparison."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Comparators", guidance: "Who or what is compared, and why those comparators were chosen."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Findings", guidance: "The gaps found, with the evidence and the limits of the comparison."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Implications", guidance: "What the findings mean for the change, and what is not concluded.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Define each measure the same way on both sides before comparing anything: a comparison of differently defined measures is not evidence, it is confusion. Choose comparators that are genuinely comparable, state the limits of the data, and present gaps as findings with implications, not as verdicts. The note should say what is not concluded as clearly as what is."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: benchmarking exception handling time",
        workedExample: [
            "Measure: time from exception arrival to first action, defined the same way for the internal log and the external comparison. Comparator: published industry guidance on exception handling practice, chosen because it defines the measure in the same terms. Limit: the external data covers a different payment mix, so the comparison is indicative, not exact.",
            "Finding: first action time is slower than the comparator, and the gap is largest for exceptions arriving after cut off. Implication: the after cut off handling process is the priority for improvement. Not concluded: that the team is slow in general, because the gap is concentrated in one pattern."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-brainstorming",
        title: "Brainstorming",
        subtitle: "Brainstorming generates a broad set of ideas quickly, by separating idea generation from idea judgement.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.5",
        explanation: [
            "Brainstorming generates a broad set of ideas quickly, by separating idea generation from idea judgement. Participants offer ideas freely, without criticism, while a facilitator records every idea visibly. Only when generation stops does the group sort, combine, and judge the ideas. The separation is the technique: judgement during generation kills the very ideas that would have led somewhere.",
            "This technique matters at the start of a problem, when the range of options is still narrow. It suits questions with many possible answers, such as what could cause a failure, or what options exist for a change. It suits groups that need to hear every voice, including the quiet ones, before the loudest view settles the discussion."
        ],
        outputName: "Brainstorming record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Question", guidance: "The single question the session answers."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Ideas", guidance: "Every idea offered, recorded visibly, without judgement."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Grouped ideas", guidance: "The ideas sorted into themes after generation stops."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Shortlist", guidance: "The ideas taken forward, with the reason for each.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "State one clear question, set a time limit, and record every idea visibly as it is offered. Ban criticism during generation, including facial criticism: the facilitator's job is to protect the quietest voice in the room. When time ends, group the ideas into themes, combine duplicates, and shortlist against agreed criteria, recording why each shortlisted idea was chosen."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: brainstorming causes of failed payments",
        workedExample: [
            "Question: what could cause a payment to fail after it leaves the sending bank? In fifteen minutes the group offered twenty one ideas, recorded on a visible board, including missing status reports, wrong cut off handling, and duplicate references. No idea was criticised during generation.",
            "After generation, the ideas grouped into data, timing, and handling themes. The shortlist: three causes to investigate first, chosen because they matched recent exception log entries. The record shows the question, all the ideas, the grouping, and the shortlist with reasons."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-business-capability-analysis",
        title: "Business Capability Analysis",
        subtitle: "Business capability analysis looks at what an organisation must be able to do, rather than starting with systems or job titles.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.6",
        explanation: [
            "Business capability analysis looks at what an organisation must be able to do, rather than starting with systems or job titles. A capability is an ability, such as the ability to validate a payment, to reconcile accounts, or to handle exceptions. Capabilities are mapped, assessed for strength, and used to show where a change lands and what must improve.",
            "This technique matters because it keeps strategy honest. A change described only as a system replacement hides the real question: which abilities must improve, and by how much? A capability map shows the gap between the abilities the organisation has and the abilities the future state needs, and it survives reorganisations that would make a system map obsolete."
        ],
        outputName: "Business capability map and assessment",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Capability", guidance: "The ability, named as a verb phrase, independent of any system or team."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Assessment", guidance: "The current strength, with the evidence for the judgement."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Gap", guidance: "The distance to the future state need, and what closes it.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Name capabilities as abilities, not as systems or teams: the ability to validate a payment, not the validation system. Keep the map to a readable number of capabilities, grouped by area, and assess each one on evidence: performance data, error rates, and the views of the people who use the capability daily. State the gap for each capability the change touches, and what closes it."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: capability assessment for payment processing",
        workedExample: [
            "Capabilities: validate a payment, route a payment, confirm status, handle exceptions, and reconcile accounts. Assessment: validation is strong, evidenced by low rejection rates. Exception handling is weak, evidenced by the exception log and handling times. Status confirmation is partial, because one payment type lacks a status report.",
            "Gap: the future state needs strong exception handling and full status confirmation. What closes the gap: the queue change for exception handling, and receiving the missing status report for confirmation. The map shows both gaps on one page, with the evidence behind each judgement."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-business-cases",
        title: "Business Cases",
        subtitle: "A business case sets out why a change is worth doing, in terms a decision maker can judge.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.7",
        explanation: [
            "A business case sets out why a change is worth doing, in terms a decision maker can judge. It states the problem, the options, the benefits, the costs, and the risks, on a comparable basis, and it recommends a course of action. It is a decision document, not a sales document: its job is to make the decision easier to make well, including the decision to do nothing.",
            "This technique matters because it forces the hard questions before money is spent. What problem, exactly? What happens if nothing changes? What does each option cost, what does it return, and what could go wrong? A business case that cannot answer those questions is not ready, however urgent the change feels."
        ],
        outputName: "Business case",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Problem", guidance: "The problem, with evidence, and the cost of doing nothing."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Options", guidance: "The realistic options, including doing nothing, on a comparable basis."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Benefits and costs", guidance: "The benefits and costs of each option, with the basis and the time horizon."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Risks", guidance: "The main risks of each option, and how they are handled."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Recommendation", guidance: "The recommended option, with the reason and the conditions.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write the problem first, with evidence, and cost the option of doing nothing: without that baseline, every option looks expensive and none looks necessary. Compare options on the same basis and over the same time horizon, state the assumptions behind every number, and separate quantified benefits from benefits that are real but not quantified. Recommend one option, with the conditions that would change the recommendation."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: business case for exception queue automation",
        workedExample: [
            "Problem: exceptions are worked twice and slowly, evidenced by the exception log and handling times. Doing nothing costs continued manual effort and continued duplicate working. Options: manual improvement, semi automatic ordering, and full automation. Benefits and costs are compared over two releases, on the same basis, with assumptions stated.",
            "Recommendation: semi automatic ordering, because it delivers most of the benefit at a third of the cost of full automation, and the benefit of automatic assignment is uncertain until the queue is ordered. Condition: if volumes rise sharply, the full automation option is revisited with fresh evidence."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-business-model-canvas",
        title: "Business Model Canvas",
        subtitle: "A business model canvas describes how an organisation creates, delivers, and captures value, on a single page.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.8",
        explanation: [
            "A business model canvas describes how an organisation creates, delivers, and captures value, on a single page. It covers customers, the value offered, the channels and relationships through which value reaches them, the activities and resources that make it possible, the partners involved, and the costs and revenues. The single page is the point: the whole model is visible at once, so a change in one part can be traced to the parts it affects.",
            "This technique matters when a change touches the model itself, not just a process inside it. A new payment product, a new customer segment, or a new channel all change more than one part of the model, and the canvas shows those connections before the change is designed in detail."
        ],
        outputName: "Business model canvas",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Customers and value", guidance: "Who is served, and what value is offered to each group."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Channels and relationships", guidance: "How value reaches customers, and how the relationship is maintained."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Activities, resources, and partners", guidance: "What must happen, what it needs, and who helps."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Costs and revenues", guidance: "What the model costs, and how it earns.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Fill the canvas as a group, starting with customers and value, and keep every entry short: a phrase, not a paragraph. Test the canvas by tracing a change through it: if the value changes, which activities, resources, and costs change with it? A canvas where nothing connects to anything else is a list, not a model, and it needs another pass."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: canvas for a new corporate payment service",
        workedExample: [
            "Customers: mid sized corporate customers who pay suppliers across borders. Value: payment status they can see, without calling the bank. Channels: the corporate banking portal and status notifications. Relationships: a named service contact for large customers, self service for the rest.",
            "Activities: payment processing, status reporting, and exception handling. Resources: the payment hub and the operations team. Partners: the scheme and the correspondent banks. Costs: hub running costs and operations time. Revenues: transaction fees. The canvas shows that status visibility depends on the hub and the scheme, so a change to either is traced before it is approved."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-business-rules-analysis",
        title: "Business Rules Analysis",
        subtitle: "Business rules analysis identifies, defines, and organises the rules that govern business behaviour.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.9",
        explanation: [
            "Business rules analysis identifies, defines, and organises the rules that govern business behaviour. A business rule states a constraint or an obligation: what must be true, what must happen, or what must not happen, under stated conditions. Rules are separated from the processes that use them, so a rule can change without the whole process being rewritten.",
            "This technique matters because rules hide inside processes, screens, and people's heads, and a rule that lives only in one person's head will be applied differently by everyone else. Written rules, each with its conditions and its effect, can be checked, tested, and changed deliberately, which is exactly what payments need, where a rule applied wrongly moves real money."
        ],
        outputName: "Business rules catalogue",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Rule", guidance: "The rule, written as: under these conditions, this must be true or must happen."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Source", guidance: "Where the rule came from: regulation, scheme, policy, or business decision."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Effect", guidance: "What happens when the rule is met, and what happens when it is broken.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write each rule as one statement with its conditions and its effect, and name its source. Keep rules atomic: one rule, one decision. When two rules conflict, record the conflict and resolve it with the rule owner, rather than letting each reader resolve it differently. Review the catalogue whenever a related process or regulation changes."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: rules for payment validation",
        workedExample: [
            "Rule 1: when a payment instruction is received, the account must exist and be open, or the instruction is rejected with a message naming the failed check. Source: internal control policy. Rule 2: when the payee name check returns no match, the payer sees a warning and may correct or continue. Source: product decision, agreed with compliance.",
            "Each rule states its conditions, its effect, and its source. Rule 2 was checked against the control policy to confirm that a warning, rather than a block, is permitted. The catalogue is reviewed whenever the validation process changes."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-field-validation"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-collaborative-games",
        title: "Collaborative Games",
        subtitle: "Collaborative games use structured, game like activities to help a group understand a problem or reach a decision.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.10",
        explanation: [
            "Collaborative games use structured, game like activities to help a group understand a problem or reach a decision. The structure matters more than the play: a clear question, a visible shared record, rules that give every participant an equal move, and a facilitator who keeps the game on its question. The game format lowers the cost of disagreeing, which is why it works where open discussion stalls.",
            "This technique matters when a group is stuck, when engagement is low, or when traditional discussion keeps returning to the same two voices. It suits prioritisation, where the group must make trade offs visible, and exploration, where the group must see a problem from several angles before deciding."
        ],
        outputName: "Collaborative game record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Question and rules", guidance: "The question, the rules of the game, and the time allowed."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Shared record", guidance: "The visible record built during the game: cards, rankings, or groupings."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Outcome", guidance: "The decision or understanding reached, and what was left open.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Choose a game that fits the question: a ranking game for priorities, a grouping game for themes, a role play game for seeing a process from another side. State the rules and the time limit before starting, keep the record visible throughout, and close by reading back the outcome and the open items. The game is a means to a decision, not a break from one."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: ranking game for backlog priorities",
        workedExample: [
            "Question: which three backlog items deliver the most value in the next release? Rules: each participant has a fixed number of votes, votes are placed on visible cards, and no item receives more than two votes from one person. Time: twenty minutes, then discussion.",
            "The shared record showed the duplicate check and the queue ordering clearly ahead, with the report improvement far behind. The outcome: the top two items are ordered first, with reasons recorded. The open item: whether the report improvement belongs in the following release, to be decided after the duplicate check is estimated."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-concept-modelling",
        title: "Concept Modelling",
        subtitle: "Concept modelling defines the important concepts in a business area and how they relate, in business language, before any system design.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.11",
        explanation: [
            "Concept modelling defines the important concepts in a business area and how they relate, in business language, before any system design. It names the concepts, such as payment, account, and status, defines each one, and shows the relationships between them. The model is a shared vocabulary with structure: it stops two teams using the same word for different things.",
            "This technique matters whenever a change crosses team boundaries, because each team brings its own vocabulary. In payments, the word status alone can mean five different things to five teams. A concept model agreed early prevents the misunderstandings that only surface during testing, when they are expensive to fix."
        ],
        outputName: "Concept model",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Concepts", guidance: "Each concept, with a plain definition in business language."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Relationships", guidance: "How concepts relate: one payment has one current status and a history of statuses."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Rules", guidance: "The rules that constrain the relationships.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Start from the words people actually use, and define each concept in one or two sentences of business language, with no system terms. Show the relationships as simple statements before drawing anything, and check the model with the people who use the concepts daily. When two teams define the same word differently, record both definitions and agree one, with the decision recorded."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: concept model for payment status",
        workedExample: [
            "Concepts: a payment is an instruction to move value from one account to another. A status is the latest known position of a payment. A status report is a message that carries a status. Definitions are written in business language, with no system names.",
            "Relationships: one payment has one current status and a history of statuses. One status report carries one status for one payment. Rule: a payment is shown as settled only when a settlement confirmation has been received. The model was checked with operations and technology, and one conflicting definition of status was resolved and recorded."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-data-dictionary",
        title: "Data Dictionary",
        subtitle: "A data dictionary defines each data item used by a change: its name, its meaning, its format, its allowed values, and its source.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.12",
        explanation: [
            "A data dictionary defines each data item used by a change: its name, its meaning, its format, its allowed values, and its source. It is the reference that stops two teams defining the same field differently. Where a glossary defines business terms, a data dictionary defines the data itself, at the precision a builder and a tester need.",
            "This technique matters because data misunderstandings are silent. Two systems can both carry a field called reference and mean different things by it, and the difference only appears when a payment fails to match. A dictionary agreed before build, and checked against real data, prevents that class of failure."
        ],
        outputName: "Data dictionary",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Field", guidance: "The field name, as used by the systems involved."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Definition", guidance: "What the field means, in plain language."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Format and values", guidance: "The format, the allowed values, and what is mandatory or optional."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Source", guidance: "Where the value comes from, and who owns it.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Define every field the change touches, including fields that seem obvious, and check each definition against real examples. State the format, the allowed values, and whether the field is mandatory, and name the source and the owner. Review the dictionary with the owning team for each field, and version it, so a builder never works from an outdated definition."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: dictionary entries for a payment instruction",
        workedExample: [
            "Field: payment reference. Definition: the reference supplied by the payer that identifies the payment. Format: text, up to the agreed length, mandatory. Source: the payer, via the initiation channel. Owner: the payments product team.",
            "Field: value date. Definition: the date the value moves, as agreed with the payer. Format: calendar date, mandatory. Source: the payer, defaulting to the next working day when not supplied. Owner: the payments product team. Both entries were checked against real instructions before build."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
