    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-data-flow-diagrams",
        title: "Data Flow Diagrams",
        subtitle: "Data flow diagrams show how data moves through a system or a process: where data comes from, where it goes, what transforms it, and where it is stored.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.13",
        explanation: [
            "Data flow diagrams show how data moves through a system or a process: where data comes from, where it goes, what transforms it, and where it is stored. They show movement, not sequence: a data flow diagram answers where data goes and what happens to it, not the order of steps in time. That distinction is what makes them useful alongside process models.",
            "This technique matters when a change moves data between systems, because the movement is where data is lost, changed, or duplicated. A diagram that shows every flow, every store, and every transformation lets a reviewer ask the right question at each point: what happens to this data here, and who owns it?"
        ],
        outputName: "Data flow diagram",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Sources and destinations", guidance: "The external parties and systems that send or receive data."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Flows", guidance: "Each flow: what data moves, and in which direction."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Processes", guidance: "What transforms the data at each step."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Stores", guidance: "Where data rests, and for how long.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Start with the boundary: what is inside the change and what is outside it. Show the external sources and destinations first, then the flows, then the processes and stores. Label every flow with the data it carries, not just an arrow, and check the diagram with the owning team for each system. A flow with no label is a guess, and guesses about data movement become defects."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: data flow for a payment status update",
        workedExample: [
            "Sources: the scheme sends status reports. Destinations: the payment hub, the status store, and the status screen. Flows: the status report flows from the scheme to the hub, the status flows from the hub to the store, and the screen reads the status from the store. Each flow is labelled with the data it carries.",
            "Processes: the hub validates the report and updates the status. Stores: the status store holds the current status and the history. The diagram was checked with the hub team, who confirmed that no other flow writes to the status store, which removed a suspected duplicate update path."
