    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-data-modelling",
        title: "Data Modelling",
        subtitle: "Data modelling defines the structure of the information a solution needs: the entities, their attributes, and the relationships between them.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.15",
        explanation: [
            "Data modelling defines the structure of the information a solution needs: the entities, their attributes, and the relationships between them. It answers what information exists, how pieces of information relate, and what must stay consistent. It is written at the level the change needs: a business level model uses business language, and a detailed model adds the precision builders need.",
            "This technique matters because data structure decisions made late are expensive. A payment that can have one current status and a history of statuses needs a different structure from a payment with a single status field, and the difference affects every screen, report, and reconciliation that touches the payment. Modelling the data early makes the structure a decision, not an accident."
        ],
        outputName: "Data model",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Entities", guidance: "The things about which information is held, with definitions."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Attributes", guidance: "The information held about each entity, with its definition."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Relationships", guidance: "How entities relate, including how many of each relate to the other."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Rules", guidance: "The consistency rules the data must satisfy.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Start from the business concepts, define each entity in business language, and add attributes only where the change needs them. State the relationships plainly, including how many of each entity relate to the other, and write the consistency rules as statements that can be checked. Review the model with the data owner and with the teams that read and write the data."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: data model for payment status",
        workedExample: [
            "Entities: payment, status, and status report. Attributes: a payment has a reference, an amount, and a currency. A status has a value and a time. A status report has a sender and a time received. Relationships: one payment has one current status and many statuses over time. One status report carries one status.",
            "Rules: a payment has exactly one current status at any time. A status is never deleted, only superseded. The model was reviewed with the data owner, who confirmed the rules match the reconciliation needs."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
