    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-decision-analysis",
        title: "Decision Analysis",
        subtitle: "Decision analysis compares options against agreed criteria, with the trade offs made visible.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.16",
        explanation: [
            "Decision analysis compares options against agreed criteria, with the trade offs made visible. It structures a decision: the options, the criteria, the evidence for each option against each criterion, and the weight of each criterion. The structure does not make the decision for anyone. It makes the reasoning checkable, so a decision can be challenged on its evidence rather than on its outcome.",
            "This technique matters when options compete and the trade offs are real. A cheaper option that carries more risk, a faster option that delivers less value: these are the decisions that go wrong when they are made on the strongest voice in the room. Decision analysis puts the evidence on the table and lets the decision be owned."
        ],
        outputName: "Decision analysis",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Options", guidance: "The realistic options, described on the same basis."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Criteria and weights", guidance: "The criteria, with their relative weight, agreed before scoring."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Evidence", guidance: "The evidence for each option against each criterion."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Result", guidance: "The comparison, the recommendation, and the sensitivity to the weights.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Agree the criteria and their weights before scoring any option, or the weights will quietly follow the preferred option. Score each option on evidence, not on feeling, and record the evidence beside the score. Show how sensitive the result is to the weights: if a small change in weights flips the answer, say so, and let the decision maker own that judgement."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: decision analysis for duplicate detection",
        workedExample: [
            "Options: check at initiation, check at initiation and before settlement, or no change. Criteria: risk reduction, cost, and delivery time, weighted risk first. Evidence: the exception log for the risk of each option, estimates from the technology team for cost and time.",
            "Result: the two stage check scores highest on risk reduction but lowest on cost and time. The single check scores highest overall under the agreed weights, but the result flips if risk is weighted higher. The analysis records this sensitivity, and the decision maker owns the weight judgement."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-impact-assessment"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-decision-modelling",
        title: "Decision Modelling",
        subtitle: "Decision modelling shows how a decision is made: the inputs, the rules, and the outcomes.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.17",
        explanation: [
            "Decision modelling shows how a decision is made: the inputs, the rules, and the outcomes. It separates the decision logic from the process around it, so the logic can be checked, tested, and changed without rewriting the process. Common forms include decision tables, which list every combination of conditions, and decision trees, which show the path from inputs to outcome.",
            "This technique matters wherever a decision has several conditions that combine. Payment validation, routing, and exception handling all depend on combinations of conditions, and a combination that was never written down will be handled differently by every implementation. A decision model makes every combination visible, including the ones no one thought of."
        ],
        outputName: "Decision model",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Decision", guidance: "The decision, stated as one question."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Inputs", guidance: "The conditions the decision depends on, with their possible values."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Rules", guidance: "The rule for each combination, including the default when no rule matches."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Outcomes", guidance: "The outcome for each rule, and what happens next.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "State the decision as one question, list the inputs and their possible values, and write a rule for every combination, including the default for combinations no rule covers. Check the model for gaps and contradictions: a combination with no rule, or two rules giving different outcomes, is a defect in the model, not a detail for build to resolve. Review the model with the rule owner."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: decision table for payment validation",
        workedExample: [
            "Decision: what happens when a payment instruction is validated? Inputs: account exists, account open, and amount within limit, each yes or no. Rules: when all three are yes, the instruction proceeds. When the account does not exist, the instruction is rejected. When the account exists but is closed, the instruction is rejected with a different message.",
            "Default: when the amount exceeds the limit, the instruction is held for review, whatever the other inputs. The table lists all eight combinations, and the review with the rule owner found one combination that had no rule, which was resolved before build."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-document-analysis",
        title: "Document Analysis",
        subtitle: "Document analysis examines existing documents to elicit information: current procedures, previous specifications, regulations, reports, and correspondence.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.18",
        explanation: [
            "Document analysis examines existing documents to elicit information: current procedures, previous specifications, regulations, reports, and correspondence. It is the fastest way to learn what is already known, and it prepares the analyst to ask better questions in interviews and workshops. It is a starting point, not a substitute for talking to people: documents describe the intended process, and people describe the real one.",
            "This technique matters because it prevents the analyst from asking stakeholders to repeat what is already written down, which wastes their time and their goodwill. It also reveals gaps and contradictions between documents, which become precise questions for elicitation rather than vague topics."
        ],
        outputName: "Document analysis note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Documents reviewed", guidance: "Each document, its version, and its date."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Findings", guidance: "What each document says that matters for the change."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Gaps and contradictions", guidance: "What is missing, and where documents disagree."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Questions raised", guidance: "The questions for interviews or workshops, with their source document.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "List the documents, their versions, and their dates before reading, so findings can be traced to a specific version. Record findings as facts with their source, and separate gaps and contradictions into their own section, because those are the most valuable output: each gap or contradiction becomes a precise question. Check the currency of every document: a procedure that no longer matches practice is a finding, not a source of truth."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: document analysis for exception handling",
        workedExample: [
            "Documents: the exception handling procedure version 4, the payment hub user guide, and the scheme rule summary. Findings: the procedure lists six exception types, and the user guide describes the queue screens. Gaps: the procedure does not cover exceptions arriving after cut off, and the user guide describes a screen that no longer exists.",
            "Contradictions: the procedure says exceptions are worked in arrival order, while the user guide says the queue is ordered by age. Questions raised: which order is actually used, and who owns after cut off exceptions? Both questions went to the workshop, with their source documents named."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
