    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-estimation",
        title: "Estimation",
        subtitle: "Estimation judges the effort, time, or cost of work, with the basis and the uncertainty stated.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.19",
        explanation: [
            "Estimation judges the effort, time, or cost of work, with the basis and the uncertainty stated. Good estimation breaks work into smaller parts, judges each part, and states the assumptions behind the judgement. It is a forecast with workings, not a promise: the value of an estimate lies as much in its breakdown and assumptions as in its total.",
            "This technique matters because a single number with no workings hides every risk. When the work slips, no one can tell whether the estimate was wrong, the scope changed, or the assumptions failed. An estimate with a breakdown and stated assumptions can be challenged early, revised as knowledge grows, and learned from afterwards."
        ],
        outputName: "Estimate with breakdown",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Breakdown", guidance: "The work split into parts, each estimated separately."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Assumptions", guidance: "The assumptions behind each part, stated plainly."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Range", guidance: "The range or confidence, not just a single number."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Basis", guidance: "What the estimate rests on: past work, expert judgement, or comparison.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Break the work into parts small enough to judge, estimate each part separately, and state the assumptions for each. Give a range rather than a single number when uncertainty is high, and name the basis: past similar work, expert judgement, or comparison. Revisit the estimate as knowledge grows, and record what changed and why, so the next estimate is better."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: estimate for analysis of a payment change",
        workedExample: [
            "Breakdown: workshops two days, drafting three days, reviews two days, updates one day. Assumptions: stakeholders are available for two hours a week each, and the data team confirms field availability in the first week. Basis: the last similar change, adjusted for the larger number of payment types.",
            "Range: eight to ten days, with the range driven by review availability. If review availability falls, the estimate is revised and the revision is recorded with its reason. The estimate was shared with the breakdown visible, so the team could challenge the review assumption early."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-test-scenarios"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-financial-analysis",
        title: "Financial Analysis",
        subtitle: "Financial analysis judges a change in money terms: what it costs, what it returns, and when.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.20",
        explanation: [
            "Financial analysis judges a change in money terms: what it costs, what it returns, and when. It covers the costs of building and running the change, the benefits in money terms where they can be stated honestly, and the timing of both. It is written so a reader who is not a finance specialist can follow the reasoning and challenge the assumptions.",
            "This technique matters because it tests whether a change is worth doing in the terms the organisation uses to judge investment. A change with strong operational benefits and weak financial returns may still be worth doing, but the decision should be made with the financial picture visible, not discovered afterwards."
        ],
        outputName: "Financial analysis",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Costs", guidance: "The costs: build, run, and change, with the basis for each."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Benefits", guidance: "The benefits in money terms, separated from benefits that are real but not quantified."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Timing", guidance: "When costs and benefits fall, over the agreed horizon."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Assumptions", guidance: "The assumptions behind every figure, stated plainly.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "List costs and benefits on the same basis and over the same horizon, and state the assumption behind every figure. Separate quantified benefits from benefits that are real but not quantified, rather than inventing a number for the latter. Show the timing: a benefit that arrives in year three is not the same as a benefit that arrives next month. Let the finance team check the analysis before it is used in a decision."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: financial analysis for queue automation",
        workedExample: [
            "Costs: build effort, estimated by the technology team, and running costs for the queue change, estimated from current running costs. Benefits in money terms: handling time saved, based on the exception log and the measured handling time, over two releases. Benefits not quantified: fewer duplicate payments, real but not given a money value, because the value depends on cases that have not happened.",
            "Timing: costs fall in the first release, benefits from the second release onwards. Assumptions: volumes stay near current levels, and handling time falls by the amount measured in the trial. The finance team reviewed the analysis and agreed the basis."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-focus-groups",
        title: "Focus Groups",
        subtitle: "A focus group gathers a small group of stakeholders to explore attitudes, needs, and reactions to an idea.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.21",
        explanation: [
            "A focus group gathers a small group of stakeholders to explore attitudes, needs, and reactions to an idea. A facilitator guides the discussion with prepared questions, keeps the conversation on topic, and draws out quieter voices. The value lies in the interaction: participants build on each other's views, revealing needs and concerns that one to one interviews would miss.",
            "This technique matters when attitudes and reactions matter as much as facts. A new payment screen, a changed process, or a new product all land differently with different groups, and a focus group surfaces those differences early, while the design can still change cheaply."
        ],
        outputName: "Focus group record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Participants", guidance: "Who took part, and what perspective each brings."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Questions", guidance: "The prepared questions, and the follow ups used."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Findings", guidance: "The views expressed, recorded faithfully, including disagreements."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Implications", guidance: "What the findings mean for the change.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Prepare open questions that invite discussion, not yes or no answers, and keep the group small enough for everyone to speak. Facilitate actively: draw out quiet voices, note disagreements rather than smoothing them over, and record views as views, not as facts. Close by reading back the main findings, so participants can correct the record while it is fresh."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: focus group on a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Participants: six payment operations officers, chosen for varied experience. Questions: how do you answer a customer query today, what would the screen need to show, and what would worry you about relying on it? Findings: officers welcomed the status history but worried the screen would show a status as final before settlement was confirmed.",
            "The worry was recorded as a finding, not dismissed, and it became a requirement: the screen must distinguish accepted from settled. The record shows the questions, the findings, and the implication for the design."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
