    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-functional-decomposition",
        title: "Functional Decomposition",
        subtitle: "Functional decomposition breaks a complex function into smaller functions, level by level, until each piece is small enough to understand, specify, and test.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.22",
        explanation: [
            "Functional decomposition breaks a complex function into smaller functions, level by level, until each piece is small enough to understand, specify, and test. The result is a hierarchy: the top level states the whole function, and each level below breaks one function into its parts. The hierarchy shows structure, not sequence: it answers what the parts are, not the order they run in.",
            "This technique matters because complex functions cannot be specified in one paragraph. A payment hub, a reconciliation process, or a validation engine all need to be broken down before they can be understood. Decomposition makes the parts visible, shows what is missing, and lets different teams own different parts without losing the whole."
        ],
        outputName: "Functional decomposition",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Top level function", guidance: "The whole function, stated in one sentence."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Levels", guidance: "Each function broken into its parts, level by level."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Leaf functions", guidance: "The smallest functions, each small enough to specify and test.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "State the top level function in one sentence, then break it into parts that together cover the whole, with no gaps and no overlaps. Keep breaking down until each leaf function is small enough to specify and test on its own. Check the hierarchy with the owning teams: a part that belongs to no team is a gap, and a part claimed by two teams is a conflict to resolve."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: decomposition of payment processing",
        workedExample: [
            "Top level: process a payment from instruction to settlement. Level 1: receive the instruction, validate it, route it, settle it, confirm status, and handle exceptions. Level 2: validate breaks into check the account, check the amount, check the payee, and check sanctions.",
            "Leaf functions: check the account is small enough to specify: it takes an account identifier, confirms the account exists and is open, and returns the result with a reason when it fails. The decomposition was checked with the hub team, who confirmed no function was missing."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-glossary",
        title: "Glossary",
        subtitle: "A glossary defines the business terms used in a change, in plain language, with one agreed definition per term.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.23",
        explanation: [
            "A glossary defines the business terms used in a change, in plain language, with one agreed definition per term. It is the shared vocabulary that stops two teams using the same word for different things, or different words for the same thing. It covers the terms that cause confusion, not every word in the dictionary.",
            "This technique matters because terminology confusion is a quiet cause of defects. In payments, words such as status, settlement, and return each carry several meanings, and a requirement that uses one of them without a definition will be read differently by every team. A glossary agreed early is cheap insurance against that class of misunderstanding."
        ],
        outputName: "Glossary",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Term", guidance: "The term, as used by the business."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Definition", guidance: "One agreed definition, in plain language."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Owner", guidance: "Who owns the definition, and who agreed it.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Define the terms that cause confusion, starting from the words people actually argue about. Write one definition per term, in plain language, with no system terms, and agree it with the people who use the term daily. Keep the glossary short and current: a glossary no one maintains becomes a second source of confusion, not a cure for the first."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: glossary entries for payment status",
        workedExample: [
            "Status: the latest known position of a payment, as reported by the most recent status report. Settlement: the movement of value between accounts, confirmed when the settlement confirmation is received. Return: the movement of value back to the sender after settlement, reported with a return message.",
            "Each definition was agreed with operations and technology, and the owner of each definition is named. The entry for status was added after a workshop showed three different uses of the word in one meeting."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-interface-analysis",
        title: "Interface Analysis",
        subtitle: "Interface analysis examines the points where systems, teams, or processes meet and exchange information.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.24",
        explanation: [
            "Interface analysis examines the points where systems, teams, or processes meet and exchange information. For each interface, it defines what crosses the boundary, in which direction, in what form, and under what rules. It covers system interfaces, human interfaces, and process handovers, because a handover between teams fails in the same ways a system interface does.",
            "This technique matters because boundaries are where information is lost, changed, or duplicated. A payment that crosses from a channel to a hub to a scheme crosses several interfaces, and each one needs its data, its rules, and its failure behaviour defined. An interface left undefined will be defined by whoever builds it first, and the other side will discover the difference during testing."
        ],
        outputName: "Interface analysis",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Interface", guidance: "The two sides, and what crosses between them."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Data", guidance: "What data crosses, in what form, and what must not change in transit."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Rules", guidance: "The rules: timing, ordering, validation, and failure behaviour."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Owners", guidance: "Who owns each side, and who owns the interface itself.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "List every interface the change touches, including handovers between teams, and define each one from both sides: what the sender sends and what the receiver expects. State what must not change in transit, the failure behaviour when the interface fails, and the owner of each side and of the interface itself. Review each interface with both owning teams together, not separately."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: interface analysis for payment initiation",
        workedExample: [
            "Interface: the corporate banking channel and the payment hub. Data: the payment instruction, including reference, amount, currency, and beneficiary details, in the agreed form. What must not change: the reference and the amount must reach the hub exactly as sent.",
            "Rules: the channel sends the instruction once, the hub acknowledges receipt, and a failed acknowledgement is retried under the agreed rule. Failure behaviour: when the hub is unavailable, the channel queues the instruction and tells the user. Owners: the channel team owns the sender side, the hub team owns the receiver side, and the interface is owned jointly."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-api-requirements"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-interviews",
        title: "Interviews",
        subtitle: "Interviews elicit information by asking prepared questions and listening carefully to the answers.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.25",
        explanation: [
            "Interviews elicit information by asking prepared questions and listening carefully to the answers. They suit sensitive topics, detailed topics, and topics where one person's knowledge is the main source. An interview can be structured, with fixed questions, semi structured, with a guide and room to follow up, or unstructured, with a topic and an open conversation. The choice depends on what is being learned and from whom.",
            "This technique matters because it reaches knowledge that documents do not hold: how work really happens, why a workaround exists, and what worries the people doing the work. A well prepared interview, with open questions and careful follow ups, produces information that no workshop or survey can reach, because it follows the interviewee's knowledge wherever it leads."
        ],
        outputName: "Interview record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Interview facts", guidance: "Who, when, purpose, and the questions asked."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Findings", guidance: "What was learned, as facts and views, kept separate."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Follow ups", guidance: "The questions the interview raised, with owners and dates."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Confirmation", guidance: "The record confirmed by the interviewee, with corrections.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Prepare open questions about how work happens today, and plan follow ups for vague answers: ask for an example, a recent case, or the exact step. Listen more than you talk, record facts and views separately, and confirm the record with the interviewee afterwards. A record the interviewee has not confirmed is a draft, not evidence."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: interview about payment repairs",
        workedExample: [
            "Purpose: understand how repair cases are worked today. Opening question: walk me through the last repair you handled, from start to finish. Follow up: what did you do when the status was unclear? Finding: the officer checks status by phone, because the screen shows no status for that payment type. View: the officer believes a status summary would cut handling time.",
            "Follow ups: who owns repairs arriving after cut off, owned by the operations lead. The record was confirmed by the interviewee, with one correction: the phone check goes to the back office, not to the sending bank. The correction is recorded."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
