    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-lessons-learned",
        title: "Lessons Learned",
        subtitle: "Lessons learned capture what went well, what went badly, and what should change, from a completed piece of work, while the memory is fresh.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.27",
        explanation: [
            "Lessons learned capture what went well, what went badly, and what should change, from a completed piece of work, while the memory is fresh. The capture is a structured review, not a blame session: it asks what happened, why it happened, and what to do differently, and it records actions with owners. A lesson without an action is a story, not a lesson.",
            "This technique matters because organisations repeat mistakes that individuals remember. A lesson recorded in a report no one reads will not change the next piece of work. A lesson turned into a changed checklist, a changed approach, or a changed template will. The value lies in the action, not in the capture."
        ],
        outputName: "Lessons learned record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "What happened", guidance: "The facts, stated without blame."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Why it happened", guidance: "The causes, separated from the people involved."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Lessons", guidance: "What should be kept, and what should change."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Actions", guidance: "The changes, each with an owner and a date to check the effect.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Hold the review soon after the work ends, while the memory is fresh, and keep it focused on facts and causes, not on people. Ask what went well first, so the strengths are recorded as carefully as the problems. Turn every lesson into an action with an owner, and check the action's effect later, or the same lesson will be learned again at the next review."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: lessons from a payments release",
        workedExample: [
            "What happened: exception requirements changed eleven times after approval, and three defects traced back to exception paths that were never specified. Why: exception handling was explored late, after the happy path was specified, because the approach had no explicit exception step.",
            "Lesson: explore exception paths early, for every payment change. Action: add an exception review with a checklist of reject, return, timeout, and duplicate cases to the approach, owned by the lead analyst, with the effect judged after the next two releases."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-metrics-and-key-performance-indicators-kpis",
        title: "Metrics and Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)",
        subtitle: "Metrics and key performance indicators measure what matters, so performance can be judged on evidence.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.28",
        explanation: [
            "Metrics and key performance indicators measure what matters, so performance can be judged on evidence. A metric is a measure of something: time, count, rate, or value. A key performance indicator is a metric chosen because it signals whether a goal is being met. The choice of indicators is the technique: measuring everything measures nothing, because no one can act on a hundred numbers.",
            "This technique matters because it turns opinion into evidence that stakeholders can trust. A solution judged by activity looks busy whether or not it delivers value. A solution judged by a small set of indicators tied to business needs shows, plainly, whether the value arrived, and where it did not."
        ],
        outputName: "Metrics and KPI set",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Indicator", guidance: "The measure, its definition, and how it is calculated."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Baseline and target", guidance: "The position before the change, and the agreed target."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Collection", guidance: "How the measure is collected, how often, and by whom."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Use", guidance: "Who acts on the measure, and what action it triggers.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Choose a small set of indicators, each tied to a business need, and define each one so two people calculating it reach the same number. Record the baseline before the change, agree the target with the people who own the need, and name who acts on the measure and what action it triggers. Review the set regularly: an indicator no one acts on should be dropped, and a gap in the set should be filled."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: indicators for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Indicators: share of customer queries answered from the screen, time from query to answer, and count of status wording complaints. Each is defined, baselined from the month before release, and given a target agreed with operations and the sponsor.",
            "Collection: the query log supplies the first two weekly, and the feedback record supplies the third monthly. Use: a measure moving the wrong way for two consecutive weeks is raised at the weekly review, with an owner assigned to investigate."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-mind-mapping",
        title: "Mind Mapping",
        subtitle: "Mind mapping captures ideas in a visual, branching form, starting from a central topic and growing outward.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.29",
        explanation: [
            "Mind mapping captures ideas in a visual, branching form, starting from a central topic and growing outward. Each branch holds a related idea, and sub branches hold the detail. The non linear form suits exploration: it shows connections between ideas that a list hides, and it lets a group build a shared picture together, visibly, in the room.",
            "This technique matters when a topic is new, broad, or tangled. A mind map of a payments problem shows the branches at a glance: data, timing, handling, and systems, each with its own detail. It is a thinking tool and a capture tool in one, and its output feeds directly into more structured techniques such as process modelling or requirements writing."
        ],
        outputName: "Mind map",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Central topic", guidance: "The question or topic, stated in a few words."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Branches", guidance: "The main themes, each with its sub branches."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Connections", guidance: "The links between branches, where one idea affects another.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Start with the central topic in a few words, and grow the branches from the group's ideas, recording every idea visibly. Keep each branch label short: a phrase, not a sentence. When the map stops growing, read it back as a whole, mark the connections between branches, and turn the main branches into the structure for the next step, whether that is a workshop agenda or a requirements outline."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: mind map of payment failure causes",
        workedExample: [
            "Central topic: why payments fail after leaving the sending bank. Branches: data, timing, handling, and systems. The data branch holds missing fields and wrong formats. The timing branch holds cut off handling and late status reports. The handling branch holds queue order and ownership.",
            "Connections: the timing branch connects to the handling branch, because late status reports leave officers working without information. The map fed the workshop agenda: one session per branch, starting with timing, which the group judged the largest cause."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-non-functional-requirements-analysis",
        title: "Non-Functional Requirements Analysis",
        subtitle: "Non-functional requirements analysis defines how well a solution must perform, rather than what it must do.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.30",
        explanation: [
            "Non-functional requirements analysis defines how well a solution must perform, rather than what it must do. It covers qualities such as response time, availability, capacity, security, usability, and recoverability. These requirements are constraints on the functional requirements: they state the conditions under which the behaviour must hold, and they must be testable, with a number or an observable result.",
            "This technique matters because a solution that does the right thing slowly, unreliably, or insecurely is not a solution. Non-functional requirements left vague, such as fast or reliable, cannot be built to or tested, and the argument about what they meant happens after release, when it is most expensive. Written with numbers and observable results, they become buildable and checkable."
        ],
        outputName: "Non-functional requirements list",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Quality", guidance: "The quality: response time, availability, capacity, security, usability, or recoverability."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Requirement", guidance: "The requirement, with a number or an observable result."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Conditions", guidance: "The conditions under which the requirement must hold."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "How it is checked", guidance: "The test or evidence that proves the requirement is met.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write each requirement with a number or an observable result, and state the conditions under which it must hold: response time under normal load is a different requirement from response time at peak. Agree every number with the people who own the service, mark targets that are still to be agreed, and write the check beside the requirement, so the tester knows what evidence proves it."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: non-functional requirements for a payment service",
        workedExample: [
            "Response time: a payment status request must return within the agreed target under normal load, and within the agreed peak target at peak load. Availability: the service must be available during the agreed hours, including weekends, with planned maintenance excluded. Failure behaviour: when the service fails, it must recover without losing a payment instruction, and must never report a failed payment as sent.",
            "Each requirement carries a number to be agreed with the service owner, the conditions under which it holds, and the check: load testing for response time, availability monitoring for availability, and a failure test for recovery. Numbers marked as targets to be agreed are not presented as facts."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-non-functional"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-observation",
        title: "Observation",
        subtitle: "Observation studies work as it really happens, by watching it, rather than by asking about it or reading about it.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.31",
        explanation: [
            "Observation studies work as it really happens, by watching it, rather than by asking about it or reading about it. The analyst watches the work, records what happens, and asks questions only where watching cannot explain what is seen. It reveals the real process, including the workarounds, waits, and informal checks that no procedure describes and no interview recovers.",
            "This technique matters because people describe work as it should happen, and documents describe work as it was designed. Observation shows work as it is. In payments operations, watching the queue being worked for a day reveals more about exception handling than a week of interviews, because the workarounds are visible in the doing, not in the telling."
        ],
        outputName: "Observation record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "What was observed", guidance: "The work, the place, the time, and the duration."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "What happened", guidance: "The steps seen, in order, including waits and workarounds."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Questions raised", guidance: "What watching could not explain, for follow up."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Findings", guidance: "What the observation means for the change.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Agree the observation with the people being observed, explain the purpose, and stay unobtrusive: the aim is to see normal work, not a performance. Record steps in order, including waits, interruptions, and workarounds, and note the time each step takes. Ask questions only where watching cannot explain what is seen, and confirm the record with the people observed, so the findings rest on agreed facts."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: observation of the exception queue",
        workedExample: [
            "Observed: the exception queue being worked, over one working day, in the operations area. What happened: exceptions arrived in arrival order, officers checked status by phone for one payment type, and the same exception was opened by two officers on three occasions. Waits: officers waited for phone answers several times during the day.",
            "Questions raised: why the queue has no age ordering, and who owns an exception once it is opened. Findings: the queue order and the ownership gap are the priority for the change, because they cause the duplicate working seen during the day."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-organizational-modelling",
        title: "Organizational Modelling",
        subtitle: "Organizational modelling shows the structure of an organisation: its units, their responsibilities, and the relationships between them.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.32",
        explanation: [
            "Organizational modelling shows the structure of an organisation: its units, their responsibilities, and the relationships between them. It goes beyond the reporting chart to show who does what, who decides what, and where the handovers sit. It is used to see where a change lands, who owns each step, and where a gap in ownership will leave work undone.",
            "This technique matters because changes land on structures, and a change designed without the structure in view will assign work to no one, or to two groups at once. A model that shows units, responsibilities, and handovers lets the analyst place each new step with an owner, and lets reviewers see the gaps before the change goes live."
        ],
        outputName: "Organizational model",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Units", guidance: "The units involved, with their responsibilities."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Relationships", guidance: "How units relate: reporting, service, and handover relationships."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Handovers", guidance: "Each handover: what passes, from whom, to whom, and under what rule.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Model the units that the change touches, with their responsibilities stated as what each unit does, not as job titles. Show the relationships and the handovers, and check the model with each unit involved: a handover that one side does not recognise is a gap, not a detail. Use the model to place every new step with one owner, and to name the gaps where no owner exists."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: model for payment operations",
        workedExample: [
            "Units: payment operations, the payment hub team, and the customer service team. Responsibilities: operations owns exception handling, the hub team owns the payment engine, and customer service owns customer queries. Relationships: operations serves customer service with status information, and the hub team serves operations with payment data.",
            "Handovers: exceptions pass from the hub to operations with a status summary, and status information passes from operations to customer service on request. The model showed one gap: after cut off exceptions had no named owner, which became an action for the operations lead."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-prioritization",
        title: "Prioritization",
        subtitle: "Prioritization orders requirements, or any set of items, by agreed criteria, so the most important work is done first.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.33",
        explanation: [
            "Prioritization orders requirements, or any set of items, by agreed criteria, so the most important work is done first. It is a decision technique: it forces the trade offs into the open, by requiring the criteria, the order, and the reason for each position to be written down. A list that is not prioritized is a list in which everything is equally important, which is the same as nothing being important.",
            "This technique matters because resources are finite and requests are not. Without prioritization, the loudest request wins, the riskiest work waits, and the team finishes the quarter having built the easy items while the valuable ones stayed in the list. With prioritization agreed and recorded, the order can be explained, challenged, and revisited on evidence."
        ],
        outputName: "Prioritized list",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Criteria", guidance: "The criteria and their relative weight, agreed before ordering."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Order", guidance: "The items in order, with the reason for each position."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Deferred items", guidance: "What was deferred, what the deferral costs, and when it is revisited.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Agree the criteria and their weights before ordering, then order the items and write the reason for each position. Common criteria are value, risk, dependency, and cost. Record what was deferred, what the deferral costs, and when it is revisited, and revisit the order when conditions change. An order that never changes is either perfect or ignored."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: prioritized requirements for a payment hub change",
        workedExample: [
            "Criteria: risk first, then value, then dependency. Order: first, the duplicate detection requirement, because a duplicate moves real money. Second, the exception queue ordering requirement, because it cuts handling time daily. Third, the report improvement, deferred to the next release.",
            "Deferral cost: the operations team continues manual sorting for one more release, accepted by the operations lead. The order is revisited if the duplicate check slips, and the criteria are revisited at the next release planning session."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-process-analysis",
        title: "Process Analysis",
        subtitle: "Process analysis examines a process as it really runs, to find where value is added, where time is lost, and where risk sits.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.34",
        explanation: [
            "Process analysis examines a process as it really runs, to find where value is added, where time is lost, and where risk sits. It measures the process: cycle time, waits, handoffs, rework, and failure points. It separates the steps that add value from the steps that exist by habit, and it looks for the root causes of waste and risk, not just their symptoms.",
            "This technique matters because processes grow by accretion: a step added for a reason no one remembers, a check duplicated because no one trusted the first one, a wait inserted because a handover was never designed. Analysis makes the real process visible, with measures, so improvement is aimed at the steps that matter rather than at the steps that are easiest to change."
        ],
        outputName: "Process analysis",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Process as run", guidance: "The process as it really runs, with measures: time, waits, handoffs, and rework."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Value analysis", guidance: "Which steps add value, which are necessary but add no value, and which add neither."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Root causes", guidance: "The causes of waste and risk, with evidence."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Improvements", guidance: "The improvements, each with its expected effect and its owner.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Map the process as it runs, from evidence, and measure it: cycle time, waits, handoffs, and rework. Classify each step by whether it adds value, is necessary without adding value, or adds neither, and find the root causes of the waste and risk, not just their locations. Write each improvement with its expected effect and its owner, and measure the effect after the change."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: analysis of exception handling",
        workedExample: [
            "Process as run: exceptions wait in an unordered queue, officers check status by phone, and the same exception is often worked twice. Measures: time from arrival to first action, and the count of exceptions worked twice, taken from the exception log. Value analysis: the phone check adds no value, it compensates for a missing status summary.",
            "Root causes: the queue has no ordering or ownership, and one payment type lacks a status report. Improvements: queue ordering and ownership first, then the status summary. Expected effect: fewer duplicates and faster first action, measured after the change."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-process-modelling",
        title: "Process Modelling",
        subtitle: "Process modelling draws a process as a sequence of steps, decisions, and handovers, so the whole journey is visible on one page.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.35",
        explanation: [
            "Process modelling draws a process as a sequence of steps, decisions, and handovers, so the whole journey is visible on one page. It shows who does each step, what triggers it, what decisions branch the flow, and where the process ends. A model is a shared picture: it lets people who do different parts of the process see how their part connects to the whole.",
            "This technique matters because words alone hide gaps, overlaps, and conflicting ideas about the same process. Two people can describe the same payment journey in words and believe they agree, while their models would show different steps, different owners, and different decisions. A model agreed in a room is a test of shared understanding that words alone cannot provide."
        ],
        outputName: "Process model",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Trigger and outcome", guidance: "What starts the process, and what a finished process looks like."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Steps", guidance: "Each step, with its owner and its inputs and outputs."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Decisions", guidance: "Each decision point, with the branches and their conditions."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Handovers", guidance: "Where work passes from one owner to another.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Start with the trigger and the outcome, then draw the steps in order, naming the owner of each step. Show the decisions as branches with their conditions, and mark the handovers, because handovers are where work waits and information is lost. Model the process as it runs, including exceptions, and check the model with the people who do the work, step by step."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: model of a payment journey",
        workedExample: [
            "Trigger: a payment instruction is received. Outcome: the payment is settled and the status is confirmed. Steps: receive, validate, route, settle, and confirm, each with its owner. Decisions: validation passes or fails, with the fail branch going to exception handling. Handovers: from the channel to the hub, and from the hub to operations for exceptions.",
            "The model includes the exception branch and the return branch, not just the happy path. It was checked with operations and the hub team, who corrected two steps: the validation step splits into two checks, and the return branch starts after settlement, not before."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-functional-spec"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-prototyping",
        title: "Prototyping",
        subtitle: "Prototyping builds a simplified version of a solution, or part of it, to learn from before building the real thing.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.36",
        explanation: [
            "Prototyping builds a simplified version of a solution, or part of it, to learn from before building the real thing. A prototype can be a sketch, a clickable mock up, or a working slice: the form follows the question. Its purpose is learning, not delivery, so it is built quickly, shown early, and changed or discarded freely. A prototype that is too polished to change has missed its purpose.",
            "This technique matters because it turns abstract requirements into something stakeholders can see, touch, and criticise. A screen described in words will be imagined differently by every reader. The same screen as a prototype produces specific feedback in minutes: this field is missing, this order is wrong, this wording misleads. That feedback is cheapest before build."
        ],
        outputName: "Prototype and feedback record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Question", guidance: "What the prototype is meant to learn."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Prototype", guidance: "The simplified version, at the fidelity the question needs."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Feedback", guidance: "What stakeholders said, recorded faithfully."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Decisions", guidance: "What changed as a result, and what stayed the same.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "State the question the prototype must answer before building anything, and build only to the fidelity the question needs: a sketch for layout, a clickable mock up for flow, a working slice for behaviour. Show it early, to the people who will use the real thing, and record the feedback faithfully, including the criticisms. Close by recording what changed and what stayed the same, with reasons."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: prototype of a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Question: does the screen show the statuses operations needs, in the order operations needs them? Prototype: a clickable mock up with the status list, the history view, and the search. Fidelity: screens only, no live data. Feedback: operations asked for the status time beside the status, and for accepted and settled to be visually distinct.",
            "Decisions: the time was added beside the status, and accepted and settled were given distinct labels and colours. The search stayed as drawn. The feedback record shows the question, the feedback, and the decisions with reasons."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [
            "studio-functional-spec"
        ],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-technique-reviews",
        title: "Reviews",
        subtitle: "Reviews examine a work product, such as a requirements pack, a model, or a design, to find defects and improve it before it is used.",
        kind: .technique,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "10.37",
        explanation: [
            "Reviews examine a work product, such as a requirements pack, a model, or a design, to find defects and improve it before it is used. A review can be informal, a colleague reading a draft, or formal, a structured walkthrough with a checklist and a recorded outcome. The review checks the product against its purpose and its quality standard, and it records defects, corrections, and the decision to accept or rework.",
            "This technique matters because defects found in review cost far less than defects found in testing or after release. A requirement misunderstood in review is a sentence to rewrite. The same requirement misunderstood in build is a feature to rebuild. Reviews also spread understanding: a reviewer who reads the pack critically understands the change better than one who only attends the presentation."
        ],
        outputName: "Review record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Product reviewed", guidance: "What was reviewed, its version, and its date."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Reviewers", guidance: "Who reviewed, and what each reviewer checked."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Defects", guidance: "Each defect, with the correction needed."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Outcome", guidance: "Accept, accept with corrections, or rework, with the reason.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Give reviewers the product early enough to read it, with a clear brief: what to check, and what is out of scope for this review. Use a checklist for the quality standard: completeness, clarity, consistency, and testability. Record every defect with its correction, recheck the corrections, and record the outcome with its reason. A review with no recorded outcome did not happen."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: review of a requirements pack",
        workedExample: [
            "Product: the validation requirements pack, version 2. Reviewers: the operations lead checked the handling steps, and the technology lead checked the interface requirements. Defects: three, including one requirement that used a vague time word and one contradiction between two requirements.",
            "Outcome: accept with corrections. The corrections were made, rechecked by the reviewers, and recorded. The review record shows the product, the reviewers, the defects, the corrections, and the outcome."
        ],
        keyPoints: [],
        practiceScenarios: [],
