    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-trustworthiness",
        title: "Trustworthiness",
        subtitle: "Behavioural Characteristics: being the person others can rely on for the truth, kept promises, and discretion.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.2.3",
        explanation: [
            "Trustworthiness is the quality of being relied on for the truth, for kept promises, and for discretion. Stakeholders trust an analyst who tells the truth as found, does what was promised, and keeps confidences. Trust is earned slowly, through consistent behaviour, and lost quickly, through one broken promise or one careless disclosure. In payments work, where information is sensitive and decisions carry real money, trustworthiness is not a soft skill. It is a professional requirement.",
            "Trustworthiness is built by alignment: what the analyst says, what the analyst does, and what the analyst writes must agree. It is also built by discretion: confidential information stays confidential, and the analyst never trades it for influence or favour. When trust is damaged, it is repaired by honesty about what happened, by making amends, and by consistent behaviour over time. There is no shortcut, and the analyst who seeks one will usually damage trust further."
        ],
        outputName: "Trust record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Promises", guidance: "The promises made, and whether each was kept."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Truth", guidance: "Findings reported as found, including unwelcome findings."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Discretion", guidance: "Confidential material handled, and how it was protected."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Repair", guidance: "Any trust damaged, and what was done to repair it.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Keep promises small and explicit, so keeping them is visible, and record each promise with its date. Report findings as found, including the unwelcome ones, and never soften a finding to please its reader. Handle confidential material with visible care: limit access, store it properly, and never discuss it outside the agreed circle. If trust is damaged, acknowledge it plainly, make amends, and behave consistently afterwards; repair is proven over time, not declared in a meeting."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: trust record for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Promises: the analysis outputs were delivered on the dates promised, and one slip was reported the day it became clear. Truth: the report stated the coverage limit plainly, though the sponsor wished it otherwise. Discretion: the exception log, containing client data, was shared only with the analysis team and stored in the agreed location. Repair: no repair was needed, because no promise was broken and no confidence was breached.",
            "A stakeholder once asked the analyst to share another team's confidential figures. The analyst declined, explained why, and offered to ask the other team directly. The stakeholder was briefly annoyed, then reassured: an analyst who protects others' confidences will protect mine too. The refusal had built trust with both parties."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Tell the truth as found, keep promises, and protect confidences.",
            "Align what you say, what you do, and what you write.",
            "Repair damaged trust with honesty, amends, and consistent behaviour over time."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-organization-and-time-management",
        title: "Organization and Time Management",
        subtitle: "Behavioural Characteristics: organising your work and your time so commitments are met without crisis.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.2.4",
        explanation: [
            "Organization and time management is the ability to organise your work and your time so that commitments are met without crisis. Analysts juggle many threads: elicitation, drafting, reviews, decisions, and the unexpected. Managing them means prioritising by value and deadline, breaking large work into small steps, protecting time for deep work, and keeping information organised so it can be found again. Disorganisation is not a personal quirk. It is a risk to every commitment the analyst makes.",
            "Good time management starts with honesty about capacity. An analyst who accepts every request will fail some of them, and the failures will be the ones nobody warned about. Saying no, or not yet, to a request is a professional act when capacity is full, provided the analyst offers an alternative: a later date, a smaller scope, or another person. The calendar is a promise: what is booked is what will happen, and what is not booked will be squeezed out by what is."
        ],
        outputName: "Weekly work plan",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Commitments", guidance: "The commitments due this week, in priority order."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Time", guidance: "The time booked for each, including time for deep work."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Information", guidance: "Where each piece of work lives, so it can be found."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Capacity", guidance: "What capacity remains, and what was declined or deferred.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "List the week's commitments in priority order, by value and deadline, and book time for each, including protected time for drafting and thinking. Break large work into steps small enough to finish in one sitting, and keep every piece of information in its agreed location, named so others can find it. End the week by reviewing what was completed, what slipped, and what capacity remains, and decline or defer new requests openly when capacity is full, offering an alternative each time."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: weekly work plan for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Commitments: the coverage note, two interviews, and the wording review booking. Time: the note drafted in two protected morning sessions, interviews in the afternoons, and the booking made on Monday. Information: the note in the shared workspace, the interview records beside it, and the decision log updated daily. Capacity: one new request was deferred to the following week, with the requester told on the same day and offered a smaller interim answer.",
            "The protected drafting time mattered most. Without it, the note would have been written in fragments between meetings, and the quality would have shown it. The plan had treated thinking as work, and booked time for it like any other commitment."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Prioritise by value and deadline, and book time for deep work.",
            "Keep information organised and findable, in agreed locations.",
            "Be honest about capacity: decline or defer openly, offering an alternative."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-adaptability",
        title: "Adaptability",
        subtitle: "Behavioural Characteristics: adjusting well when facts, people, or plans change.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.2.5",
        explanation: [
            "Adaptability is the ability to adjust well when facts, people, or plans change. Change is the analyst's subject, and it applies to the analysis itself: scope shifts, stakeholders change their minds, new evidence overturns a conclusion, and plans are replanned. The adaptable analyst treats change as information, not as insult, and adjusts the approach, the outputs, and the expectations without losing the thread of the work.",
            "Adaptability needs both flexibility and steadiness. Flexibility means changing the plan, the method, or the conclusion when the evidence requires it. Steadiness means holding to the standards that must not change: honesty, evidence, and traceability. An analyst who changes everything with every shift has no anchor, and an analyst who changes nothing has no learning. The balance is struck by asking, at each change, what must adapt and what must hold."
        ],
        outputName: "Change response note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Change", guidance: "What changed, and what it affects."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Adapt", guidance: "What will adapt: plan, method, outputs, or expectations."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Hold", guidance: "What will hold: standards, decisions, and commitments that stand."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Communication", guidance: "Who was told, what they were told, and when.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Describe the change and its effects plainly, without blame, and separate what will adapt from what will hold. Adapt the plan, the method, or the conclusion as the evidence requires, and hold the standards that must not move: honesty, evidence, and traceability. Tell everyone affected promptly, stating what changed, what adapts, and what holds, and record the note so the next change builds on a clear history. Adaptability shown in writing becomes organisational learning; adaptability shown only in conversation is forgotten."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: change response note for a doubled scope",
        workedExample: [
            "Change: the scope doubled when a second payments channel was added in week five. Adapt: the analysis approach moved from light to structured, with added workshops and a deeper exception review. Hold: the standards for evidence and traceability, and the commitment to the original scope decision record. Communication: the sponsor and all four stakeholder groups were told within two days, with the revised plan and the reasons.",
            "The team had feared the scope change would derail the work. Because the response was written down, with adapts and holds separated, the team could see that only the approach changed, while the standards and the history stood. The change was absorbed in a week, and the work continued without losing its thread."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Treat change as information, not insult, and adjust without losing the thread.",
            "Separate what adapts from what holds: standards must not move with every shift.",
            "Tell everyone affected promptly, and record the response in writing."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-business-acumen",
        title: "Business Acumen",
        subtitle: "Business Knowledge: understanding how the business makes money, serves customers, and survives.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.3.1",
        explanation: [
            "Business acumen is understanding how the business makes money, serves customers, manages cost and risk, and survives in its market. It means seeing a change not only as a process or a system, but as a business decision: what it earns, what it costs, what it risks, and what it enables. An analyst with business acumen asks the questions a business owner would ask, and frames analysis in terms the business recognises.",
            "Business acumen grows from curiosity about the business beyond the analyst's own corner. It means learning how revenue is earned, where cost sits, what customers value, and what competitors offer. It also means understanding the numbers that matter: volumes, margins, and the cost of failure. An analyst who speaks only in process language will be heard as a technician. An analyst who speaks in business language will be heard as a partner."
        ],
        outputName: "Business sense note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Earning", guidance: "How the business earns its revenue in this area."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Cost", guidance: "Where the cost sits, and what the change does to it."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Customer value", guidance: "What customers value, and how the change affects it."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Risk", guidance: "The business risks the change carries, and how they are handled.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Describe how the business earns revenue in the affected area, and where cost sits, using the business's own terms and numbers where they are available. State what customers value and how the change affects it, and name the business risks the change carries, with their handling. Frame every recommendation in business terms first, and process terms second: the business case for a change is the reason it exists, and the process description is how it will be done. Keep the note short, and check it with someone who owns the numbers."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: business sense note for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Earning: the payments business earns fees per transaction and per account, and status queries cost officer time without earning revenue. Cost: manual status checks consume hours daily, and inconsistent answers risk customer loss. Customer value: corporate customers value accurate, timely status for cash decisions. Risk: the change risks overpromising status the data cannot support, handled by the plain no status message and the compliance wording review.",
            "Framing the change in these terms changed the sponsor conversation. The sponsor approved the change on the strength of the customer value and the cost of manual checking, not on the elegance of the screen design. The business sense note had spoken the sponsor's language."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "See every change as a business decision: earning, cost, customer value, and risk.",
            "Frame recommendations in business terms first, process terms second.",
            "Check the note with someone who owns the numbers."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-industry-knowledge",
        title: "Industry Knowledge",
        subtitle: "Business Knowledge: knowing the industry, its rules, its practices, and where it is heading.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.3.2",
        explanation: [
            "Industry knowledge is knowing the industry the analyst works in: its rules, its practices, its participants, and where it is heading. In payments, this means understanding schemes and rails, message standards, settlement cycles, regulatory expectations, and the direction of change, from instant payments to new data standards. Industry knowledge lets the analyst ask informed questions, recognise patterns, and spot when a proposal ignores how the industry actually works.",
            "Industry knowledge is never finished. Rules change, standards evolve, and new participants reshape the market. The analyst keeps current by reading industry material, attending briefings, and learning from every change. Industry knowledge also needs humility: knowing the industry is not the same as knowing this organisation's corner of it, and the analyst must check general knowledge against local facts before relying on it."
        ],
        outputName: "Industry knowledge note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Landscape", guidance: "The industry landscape for this change: participants, rules, and standards."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Practices", guidance: "The common practices, and how this organisation compares."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Direction", guidance: "Where the industry is heading, and what that means for this change."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Limits", guidance: "What is not yet known, and how it will be learned.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Sketch the industry landscape for the change: the participants, the rules, and the standards that bind it, and note the common practices and how this organisation compares. Describe where the industry is heading, and what that direction means for the change being analysed, separating fact from forecast. List the limits of your knowledge honestly, with a plan to close each gap, and check the note with a domain expert before relying on it. Industry knowledge stated without its limits is a liability."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: industry knowledge note for payment status reporting",
        workedExample: [
            "Landscape: domestic and cross border rails, scheme rules for status reporting, and the message standards the feed uses. Practices: most participants report status on a fixed cycle, and the missing report for one payment type is unusual. Direction: the industry is moving towards faster status and richer data, which means the screen change should be built to accept richer status when it arrives. Limits: the timing of the missing report is unknown, and the feed provider has been asked.",
            "The direction finding shaped a design choice: the screen was built to show the status and its time in a form that could carry richer data later, without redesign. The industry note had turned a general trend into a specific, useful decision."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Know the industry: rules, practices, participants, and direction of travel.",
            "Check general knowledge against local facts before relying on it.",
            "State the limits of your knowledge, with a plan to close each gap."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-organization-knowledge",
        title: "Organization Knowledge",
        subtitle: "Business Knowledge: knowing how this organization works, who decides what, and how things really get done.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.3.3",
        explanation: [
            "Organization knowledge is knowing how this organization works: its structure, its decision makers, its culture, its history, and how things really get done. It is the difference between the organisation chart and the organisation as lived: who must be consulted, who can block a decision, which teams work well together, and what has been tried before. An analyst with organization knowledge navigates the organisation efficiently and avoids the traps that catch newcomers.",
            "Organization knowledge is built by paying attention: listening in meetings, asking about history, learning the informal routes, and remembering who helped and who was harmed by past changes. It must be used with care. Knowledge of internal politics must serve the change, not the analyst's influence, and it must never be used to bypass a stakeholder who should be involved. The analyst's organisation knowledge is a trust, held for the work, not a weapon."
        ],
        outputName: "Organization knowledge note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Structure", guidance: "The structure: teams, roles, and reporting lines for this change."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Decisions", guidance: "Who decides what, and how decisions are really made."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "History", guidance: "What has been tried before, and what it taught."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Ways of working", guidance: "How things really get done: the informal routes and habits.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Map the structure as it affects this change, then add the decision picture: who decides what, and how decisions are really reached, including the consultations that happen before the formal meeting. Record the relevant history, what was tried before and what it taught, and describe the ways of working honestly. Use the note to plan engagement, and keep it private where it names sensitivities: organisation knowledge shared carelessly becomes gossip, and gossip destroys the trust it was built on."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: organization knowledge note for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Structure: payments operations, technology, and compliance, with the operations head as sponsor. Decisions: scope is decided by the sponsor after consulting the operations lead, and wording is decided by compliance alone. History: a previous status change failed because operations were consulted late, and the team still remembers it. Ways of working: the operations lead prefers a short conversation before any formal paper, and the compliance reviewer works best with material sent a week ahead.",
            "Planning engagement from this note changed the sequence: the operations lead was consulted in conversation before the formal scope paper, and compliance received the wording material a week early. Both decisions arrived on time, and the history of the failed change was not repeated."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Know the organisation as lived: structure, decisions, history, and ways of working.",
            "Use the knowledge to plan engagement, and keep sensitivities private.",
            "Never use internal knowledge to bypass a stakeholder who should be involved."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-solution-knowledge",
        title: "Solution Knowledge",
        subtitle: "Business Knowledge: knowing the solutions in use, their strengths, their limits, and how they fit together.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.3.4",
        explanation: [
            "Solution knowledge is knowing the solutions in use: the systems, products, and services that carry the business, their strengths, their limits, and how they fit together. In payments work, this means understanding the payment hub, the status feed, the operations screen, and the reconciliation tools, well enough to describe what each does, what it cannot do, and what changing one means for the others.",
            "Solution knowledge stops the analyst describing solutions that cannot be built, and helps the analyst recognise when an existing capability already serves a need. It is built by studying the solutions, talking to the people who build and support them, and learning from every change. The analyst need not be a technologist, but must know enough to ask the right questions, to understand the answers, and to translate between the business need and the technical reality."
        ],
        outputName: "Solution knowledge note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Solutions", guidance: "The solutions in use, and what each does."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Strengths and limits", guidance: "What each solution does well, and where it falls short."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Fit", guidance: "How the solutions fit together, and where they depend on each other."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Change effects", guidance: "What changing one solution means for the others.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "List the solutions in use, and describe each in one paragraph: what it does, who uses it, and what data it holds. For each, state its strengths and its limits honestly, with evidence from the people who build and support it. Sketch how the solutions fit together, marking the dependencies, and for any proposed change, write the effect on each solution, including the ones not directly changed. Check the note with an implementation expert before relying on it."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: solution knowledge note for payment processing",
        workedExample: [
            "Solutions: the payment hub, the status feed, the operations screen, and the reconciliation tool. Strengths and limits: the hub is reliable but slow to change, the feed is timely but covers only three payment types, the screen is familiar but shows no status, and the reconciliation tool reads the feed. Fit: the feed connects the hub to the screen and to reconciliation. Change effects: the screen change needs the feed, and the feed change must be sequenced around the reconciliation window.",
            "The fit finding prevented a clash: the feed change was scheduled outside the reconciliation window, avoiding a failure that would have affected month end reporting. Solution knowledge had turned a technical detail into a planning decision."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Know the solutions in use: what each does, its limits, and how they fit together.",
            "Check the note with an implementation expert before relying on it.",
            "For any change, write the effect on every solution, including untouched ones."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-methodology-knowledge",
        title: "Methodology Knowledge",
        subtitle: "Business Knowledge: knowing the methods available, and choosing the one that fits the change.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.3.5",
        explanation: [
            "Methodology knowledge is knowing the methods available for delivering change, and choosing the one that fits. Methods range from predictive approaches, where the work is defined in detail before build, to adaptive approaches, where the work is defined in small slices as it proceeds, with many hybrids between. Each method suits some changes and not others, and the analyst must know enough about each to advise, to adapt, and to work well within whichever method the organisation uses.",
            "Methodology knowledge is practical, not theoretical. It means knowing what each method asks of analysis: how much detail, when, in what form, and with what checks. It also means knowing how to adapt when a method is imposed: an analyst on a predictive programme and an analyst on an adaptive team both serve the same need, but the rhythm, the artefacts, and the conversations differ. The method serves the change, not the other way round, and the analyst says so when the method fights the work."
        ],
        outputName: "Method fit note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Methods", guidance: "The methods available, and what each asks of analysis."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Fit", guidance: "Which method fits this change, and why."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Rhythm", guidance: "The rhythm of analysis under the chosen method: detail, timing, and checks."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Adaptation", guidance: "How the method is adapted for this change, and what is protected.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Describe the methods available in plain terms, stating what each asks of analysis: the depth, the timing, the artefacts, and the checks. Judge fit against the change: its size, uncertainty, risk, and the stability of its needs, and record the reasoning. State the rhythm of analysis under the chosen method, and any adaptation, naming what is protected when the method is bent. A method followed without adaptation for an ill fitting change is a ritual, not a discipline."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: method fit note for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Methods: a predictive approach, with full definition before build, and an adaptive approach, with analysis one slice ahead of build. Fit: the adaptive approach fits, because the needs are partly unknown and the riskiest area, exception handling, must be explored early. Rhythm: analysis runs one cycle ahead, with short reviews at the end of each cycle, and the risky areas explored first. Adaptation: the control and reconciliation areas are explored upfront, even under the adaptive method, because their rules shape the design.",
            "The adaptation mattered: exploring controls upfront revealed a rule that changed the design of two slices. Had the team waited for those slices, the redesign would have cost a cycle. The method fit note had protected the change from its own method."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Know what each method asks of analysis: depth, timing, artefacts, and checks.",
            "Judge fit against size, uncertainty, risk, and need stability.",
            "Adapt the method to the change, and name what is protected."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-verbal-communication",
        title: "Verbal Communication",
        subtitle: "Communication Skills: speaking clearly, listening for understanding, and adapting to the listener.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.4.1",
        explanation: [
            "Verbal communication is speaking clearly, so the listener understands what was intended. It covers meetings, workshops, presentations, and the everyday conversations in which most analysis actually happens. Clear speaking means plain words, short sentences, and a structure the listener can follow: the point first, then the evidence, then what is needed. It also means adapting to the listener: a sponsor, an officer, and a developer need the same facts in different forms.",
            "Verbal communication is two way. The speaker watches for understanding, invites questions, and checks that the message landed as intended. The analyst also manages the difficult moments: disagreeing without hostility, delivering unwelcome findings without softening them, and keeping a meeting on its purpose when it drifts. What is said in a meeting becomes the record of the meeting, so the analyst confirms the key points afterwards, in writing, where they can be checked."
        ],
        outputName: "Verbal communication plan",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Audience", guidance: "Who will listen, and what each listener needs."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Message", guidance: "The point, the evidence, and what is needed, in that order."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Adaptation", guidance: "How the message adapts for each listener."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Confirmation", guidance: "How understanding will be checked, and the record kept.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Plan the message before the meeting: the point first, then the evidence, then what is needed, in plain words and short sentences. Adapt the form for each listener, keeping the facts identical: the sponsor hears the decision and its trade offs, the officer hears the effect on daily work, and the developer hears the rules and the edge cases. During the conversation, watch for understanding, invite questions, and summarise the key points at the end. Confirm the summary in writing the same day, so the spoken word becomes a checked record."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: verbal communication plan for a scope meeting",
        workedExample: [
            "Audience: the sponsor, the operations lead, and the feed developer, in one meeting. Message: the screen change will cover three payment types, because the fourth has no status report, and a decision on the separate investigation is needed. Adaptation: the sponsor heard the value and the cost, the operations lead heard the effect on the queue, and the developer heard the feed facts. Confirmation: the summary was shared the same day, and the operations lead corrected one point about queue volumes.",
            "The correction mattered: the queue volumes in the summary were from the previous quarter, and the current volumes were higher. The written confirmation had caught an error the spoken meeting had missed, and the plan was adjusted before anyone relied on the wrong figure."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Lead with the point, then the evidence, then what is needed.",
            "Adapt the form for each listener; keep the facts identical.",
            "Confirm spoken agreements in writing the same day."
        ],
