    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-non-verbal-communication",
        title: "Non-Verbal Communication",
        subtitle: "Communication Skills: reading and using body language, tone, and presence.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.4.2",
        explanation: [
            "Non-verbal communication is the sending and receiving of messages through body movement, posture, facial expressions, gestures, eye contact, and tone of voice. Much of what a stakeholder means is carried outside the words: hesitation before an answer, a glance at a colleague, a tone that contradicts the sentence. The analyst who reads these signals learns what the words leave out, and the analyst who manages their own signals communicates confidence, openness, and respect.",
            "Non-verbal signals must be read with care. A single gesture proves nothing, and signals vary between people and cultures. The analyst treats a signal as a question, not a conclusion: noticing hesitation, and asking what lies behind it, rather than assuming. The analyst also manages their own presence: arriving prepared, listening visibly, and matching tone to message. A reassuring sentence delivered with a dismissive tone will be believed as a dismissal."
        ],
        outputName: "Non-verbal awareness note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Signals", guidance: "The signals observed, described without interpretation."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Check", guidance: "The questions asked to test what the signals meant."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Meaning", guidance: "What the checks revealed, stated as findings."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Presence", guidance: "How the analyst's own presence supported the conversation.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Describe the signals observed without interpreting them: what was seen and heard, not what it supposedly meant. Test each signal with a question, asked with curiosity rather than accusation, and record what the check revealed. Manage your own presence deliberately: posture, eye contact, and tone that match the message, and visible listening that shows the speaker is heard. Never record a non-verbal judgement about a person as if it were a fact about the change; record the check and its finding instead."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: non-verbal awareness note for a status wording workshop",
        workedExample: [
            "Signals: during the workshop, two officers exchanged glances when the no status message was described, and one hesitated before agreeing. Check: the analyst asked what the message would mean for their daily work, and paused for the answer. Meaning: the officers worried customers would blame them for a missing status, and the message was rewritten to make clear the status had not arrived, not that the officer had failed. Presence: the analyst listened without interrupting, and summarised the concern back before responding.",
            "Had the signals been ignored, the message would have launched in a form officers resented, and adoption would have suffered. Reading the room, and checking the reading with a question, had turned a silent worry into a better design."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Read signals as questions, not conclusions, and test them with curiosity.",
            "Describe what was observed, not what it supposedly meant.",
            "Manage your own presence: tone and posture must match the message."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-written-communication",
        title: "Written Communication",
        subtitle: "Communication Skills: writing clearly, so the reader understands without asking.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.4.3",
        explanation: [
            "Written communication is writing clearly, so the reader understands what was intended without asking. Analysis lives in writing: requirements, notes, records, and messages that others build from, test from, and judge from. Clear writing means plain words, short sentences, one idea per paragraph, and a structure the reader can navigate. It also means writing for the reader, not for the writer: the reader's question comes first, and the writer's process comes last, if at all.",
            "Written communication carries a heavier burden than speech, because the writer is not present to correct misunderstanding. The analyst therefore writes to be checked: stating the point, the evidence, and the source, so the reader can verify rather than trust. Drafts are reviewed, errors are corrected visibly, and versions are marked, so the reader always knows which version is current. A document that cannot be checked will be doubted, however confidently it is written."
        ],
        outputName: "Written communication draft",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Reader", guidance: "Who will read it, and what question they need answered."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Point", guidance: "The point, stated first, in one paragraph."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Evidence", guidance: "The evidence, with sources, so the reader can verify."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Version", guidance: "The version, the date, and what changed since the last version.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Start from the reader's question, and answer it in the first paragraph, before any background. Use plain words and short sentences, one idea per paragraph, and headings that let the reader navigate. State the evidence with its sources, so the reader can verify rather than trust, and mark the version and the date, recording what changed. Have the draft reviewed by someone who will use it, and correct it from their feedback; a document reviewed only by its author has been checked by the person least able to see its gaps."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: written communication draft for a scope decision",
        workedExample: [
            "Reader: the sponsor, who must decide the scope. Point: the change will cover three payment types, because the fourth has no status report, and a separate investigation is recommended. Evidence: the feed log, the query log, and the interview record, each cited. Version: draft two, dated, with the change from draft one recorded: the cost figure was corrected from the finance review. The sponsor read the first paragraph, checked the evidence, and decided in the same meeting.",
            "The review caught a sentence that two readers had read differently. The sentence was rewritten, and the rewrite was checked with both readers. The document that circulated was the document everyone understood, because it had been tested on its readers before it was trusted."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Answer the reader's question in the first paragraph.",
            "Use plain words, short sentences, and one idea per paragraph.",
            "State evidence with sources, mark versions, and have the draft reviewed by a user."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-listening",
        title: "Listening",
        subtitle: "Communication Skills: hearing what is meant, not only what is said.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.4.4",
        explanation: [
            "Listening is hearing what is meant, not only what is said. It means giving full attention, following the speaker's reasoning, noticing what is left unsaid, and checking understanding by summarising back. Listening is the foundation of elicitation: the analyst who listens well learns what stakeholders know, want, and worry about, while the analyst who listens poorly gathers only what confirms existing views.",
            "Listening is active, not passive. It means asking follow up questions that open the topic further, pausing to let the speaker think, and resisting the urge to answer before the speaker has finished. It also means listening for the concern behind the statement: a stakeholder who argues about a detail may be worried about the change itself, and the analyst who hears only the detail will answer the wrong question. Listening well is a form of respect, and stakeholders notice when they have been truly heard."
        ],
        outputName: "Listening record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Speaker", guidance: "Who spoke, and what they were asked about."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Heard", guidance: "What was heard, in the speaker's words where possible."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Unsaid", guidance: "What was left unsaid, or hinted at, and how it was explored."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Summary", guidance: "The summary played back to the speaker, and their corrections.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Give full attention: close the laptop, face the speaker, and let silence do its work after a question. Follow the speaker's reasoning, ask follow up questions that open the topic, and summarise back what was heard, inviting correction. Note what was left unsaid, and explore it gently: the hesitation, the topic avoided, and the concern behind the detail. Record the summary and the corrections the same day, and confirm the record with the speaker, so listening becomes evidence rather than memory."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: listening record for a payment status interview",
        workedExample: [
            "Speaker: a senior officer, asked about the daily status work. Heard: the officer described the telephone checks, the waiting, and the customers who call twice. Unsaid: the officer hesitated when asked about the new screen, and the follow up revealed a worry that the screen would be used to measure individual speed. Summary: played back and corrected on one point, that the waiting time varied by payment type. The worry was recorded and addressed in the training plan, which stated plainly that the screen would not be used to measure individuals.",
            "Had the hesitation been missed, the worry would have spread quietly, and adoption would have suffered for reasons nobody named. Listening for the unsaid, and exploring it gently, had turned a hidden fear into a stated assurance."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Give full attention, follow the reasoning, and summarise back for correction.",
            "Listen for what is left unsaid, and explore it gently.",
            "Record the summary and the corrections the same day, and confirm with the speaker."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-facilitation",
        title: "Facilitation",
        subtitle: "Interaction Skills: guiding a group to a shared decision or understanding.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.5.1",
        explanation: [
            "Facilitation is guiding a group to a shared decision or a shared understanding, without imposing the facilitator's own view. The facilitator plans the session, keeps it on its purpose, draws out every voice, manages disagreement productively, and records the outcome visibly. Facilitation is the skill behind workshops, reviews, and any meeting where the outcome must be owned by the group, not by one loud voice.",
            "Good facilitation is invisible when it works: the group feels it reached the decision itself, because it did. The facilitator's tools are questions, structure, timekeeping, and the visible record. The facilitator protects quiet voices, challenges dominant ones with courtesy, and names disagreement early, while it is still about the work. A facilitated session that ends without a decision, an owner, and a record has been a conversation, not facilitation."
        ],
        outputName: "Facilitation plan and record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Purpose", guidance: "The decision or understanding needed, in one sentence."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Agenda", guidance: "The timed agenda, with the question for each part."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Voices", guidance: "How every voice will be heard, including the quiet ones."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Record", guidance: "The decisions, actions, and open questions, recorded visibly.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Plan the session around one clear purpose, with a timed agenda and a question for each part, and share the purpose and the material in advance. Open by stating the purpose and the ground rules, then facilitate actively: ask open questions, draw out quiet voices, park tangents visibly, and keep the record where everyone can see it. Close by reading back the decisions, the actions with owners, and the open questions, and share the record the same day. The facilitator's view stays out of the room; the group's view fills it."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: facilitation plan for an exception types workshop",
        workedExample: [
            "Purpose: agree the list of exception types the new hub must handle, and the owner of each. Agenda: review the current list, add missing types, agree owners, and record open questions, in ninety minutes. Voices: each officer named one missing type in turn, before any discussion, so the quietest voice spoke first. Record: eight types agreed, each with an owner, and two open questions, each with an owner and a date, shared the same day.",
            "One participant dominated the early discussion, and the facilitator intervened with courtesy, inviting the officers who had not spoken. The missing types came from those officers, and the list was complete because every voice had been heard. Facilitation had done its quiet work."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Plan around one purpose, with a timed agenda and a question for each part.",
            "Draw out every voice, protect quiet ones, and keep the record visible.",
            "Close by reading back decisions, actions with owners, and open questions."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-leadership-and-influencing",
        title: "Leadership and Influencing",
        subtitle: "Interaction Skills: guiding others towards a good outcome, without formal authority.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.5.2",
        explanation: [
            "Leadership and influencing is guiding others towards a good outcome, without relying on formal authority. Analysts rarely command anyone, yet they must move groups towards decisions, towards honesty about problems, and towards changes that unsettle people. Influence comes from credibility, evidence, and care: stakeholders follow an analyst who knows the facts, tells the truth, and visibly serves the shared goal rather than a personal agenda.",
            "Influencing is not manipulation. It means understanding what each stakeholder values, presenting the case in terms they recognise, and inviting them to judge the evidence for themselves. It also means leading by example: admitting mistakes, keeping promises, and doing the unglamorous work well. An analyst who influences honestly builds a following that survives disagreement, because stakeholders trust the analyst's motives even when they dispute the analyst's conclusions."
        ],
        outputName: "Influence plan",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Goal", guidance: "The outcome sought, and why it serves the shared goal."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "People", guidance: "The people whose support matters, and what each values."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Case", guidance: "The case, in terms each person recognises, with evidence."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Ask", guidance: "The specific support asked of each person, and when.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "State the outcome sought and why it serves the shared goal, then map the people whose support matters and what each values. Build the case in terms each person recognises, with evidence they can check, and present it as an invitation to judge, not as a demand. Ask for specific support, by name and by date, and follow up visibly. Lead by example throughout: keep promises, admit mistakes, and do the unglamorous work well, because influence follows credibility, and credibility follows behaviour."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: influence plan for a payment status scope decision",
        workedExample: [
            "Goal: agreement to narrow the screen scope to the payment types with a status report. People: the sponsor, who values customer outcomes, and the operations lead, who values queue stability. Case: for the sponsor, accurate answers for covered types and an honest limit for the rest; for the operations lead, a queue that does not promise what it cannot deliver. Ask: the sponsor to approve the narrower scope, and the operations lead to confirm the queue effect, both before the next cycle.",
            "Both agreed, because the case had been built in their terms and the evidence had been shared in advance. The analyst had not commanded, cajoled, or concealed. Influence, exercised honestly, had moved the group to a decision it owned."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Influence comes from credibility, evidence, and care for the shared goal.",
            "Present the case in terms each stakeholder recognises, as an invitation to judge.",
            "Lead by example: keep promises, admit mistakes, and do the unglamorous work well."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-teamwork",
        title: "Teamwork",
        subtitle: "Interaction Skills: working well with others, so the team achieves more than its members could alone.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.5.3",
        explanation: [
            "Teamwork is working well with others, so the team achieves more than its members could alone. Analysis is a team activity: the analyst works with stakeholders, developers, testers, support staff, and managers, each holding part of the picture. Teamwork means sharing information promptly, helping without being asked, accepting help graciously, and putting the team's outcome ahead of personal credit. A team that shares information freely will catch errors that a team of individuals will miss.",
            "Teamwork shows most clearly under pressure. When a deadline tightens or a problem appears, the team player shares the load, communicates early, and avoids blame. Blame is the enemy of teamwork: it hides errors, silences warnings, and turns colleagues into defendants. The analyst builds teamwork by being reliable, by crediting others, by raising problems with proposed solutions, and by treating every team member's knowledge as essential to the whole."
        ],
        outputName: "Teamwork note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Team", guidance: "The team, and what each member contributes."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Sharing", guidance: "What information is shared, how, and how often."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Help", guidance: "Help given and help received, and what it enabled."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Problems", guidance: "Problems raised, with proposed solutions, and how they were resolved.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Name the team and what each member contributes, so every contribution is visible. Agree what information is shared, how, and how often, and share it promptly, without waiting to be asked. Offer help before it is requested, accept help graciously, and credit the helper visibly. Raise problems early, with a proposed solution, and never with blame: describe what happened, what is needed, and what you propose, and let the team judge together."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: teamwork note for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Team: the analyst, two officers, the feed developer, and the tester. Sharing: a daily short update, the decision log, and the open questions list, all in the shared workspace. Help: the tester helped the analyst write testable requirements, and the analyst helped the officers prepare for the workshop. Problems: the feed coverage slip was raised the day it became clear, with a proposed replan, and the team agreed the replan in one meeting, without blame.",
            "The daily update caught an error early: the developer noticed the analyst's note assumed a feed cycle that had changed. The correction arrived before the note was relied on. Sharing information freely had turned a potential error into a routine fix."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Share information promptly, without waiting to be asked.",
            "Offer help before it is requested, accept it graciously, and credit it visibly.",
            "Raise problems early, with a proposed solution, and never with blame."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-negotiation-and-conflict-resolution",
        title: "Negotiation and Conflict Resolution",
        subtitle: "Interaction Skills: reaching agreement when interests differ, and resolving conflict fairly.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.5.4",
        explanation: [
            "Negotiation and conflict resolution is reaching agreement when interests differ, and resolving conflict fairly when it arises. Stakeholders disagree because they see different parts of the change, carry different risks, and value different outcomes. Disagreement is normal and useful: it surfaces trade offs that a false consensus would hide. The analyst's role is to bring the disagreement into the open, ground it in evidence, and help the parties find an agreement they can all support.",
            "Resolving conflict starts with listening: each party must feel heard before it will move. The analyst separates the people from the problem, focuses on interests rather than positions, and explores options before judging them. Agreement is recorded with its reasoning, so it survives the meeting. When agreement cannot be reached, the analyst escalates honestly, presenting the options and the trade offs to the person who must decide, without taking sides."
        ],
        outputName: "Negotiation and conflict record",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Parties", guidance: "The parties, and the interest each one carries."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Positions", guidance: "What each party asks for, and the interest behind it."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Options", guidance: "The options explored, with their trade offs."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Agreement", guidance: "The agreement reached, its reasoning, and who confirmed it.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Bring the disagreement into the open early, and listen to each party fully before responding, so every party feels heard. Separate the people from the problem: describe the interests behind each position, and explore options that serve several interests at once. Judge the options against agreed criteria, and record the agreement with its reasoning and the names of those who confirmed it. If no agreement is reached, escalate honestly, presenting the options and trade offs without taking sides."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: negotiation record for payment status wording",
        workedExample: [
            "Parties: operations, who want the fullest status shown, and compliance, who want nothing shown beyond the evidence. Positions: operations ask for all status values displayed, compliance ask for a restricted set. Interests: operations want fast answers, compliance want trustworthy messages. Options: show all values, show a restricted set, or show the status with its time plus a plain no status message. Agreement: the third option, confirmed by both parties, because it served both interests: fast answers that never overstate the evidence.",
            "The agreement held, because both parties had been heard and the reasoning had been recorded. When a new stakeholder later questioned the wording, the record answered the question without reopening the conflict. The negotiation had produced an agreement, and the record had preserved it."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Bring disagreement into the open early, and listen until each party feels heard.",
            "Focus on interests behind positions, and explore options before judging them.",
            "Record the agreement with its reasoning, and escalate honestly if none is reached."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-teaching",
        title: "Teaching",
        subtitle: "Interaction Skills: helping others learn, so knowledge spreads beyond the analyst.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.5.5",
        explanation: [
            "Teaching is helping others learn, so knowledge spreads beyond the analyst. Analysts teach constantly: explaining a process to a new team member, showing a stakeholder how to read a model, or training users on a new solution. Teaching well means understanding what the learner already knows, building from there in small steps, checking understanding as you go, and letting the learner practise. Knowledge kept by one person is a risk; knowledge shared is an asset.",
            "Good teaching starts from the learner, not from the subject. The teacher asks what the learner needs to do, not what the teacher wants to say, and chooses examples from the learner's own work. Teaching also needs patience: learners ask questions that seem obvious, make mistakes that seem avoidable, and need time to practise. The analyst who teaches well creates colleagues who can carry the work forward, which is the most durable contribution analysis can make."
        ],
        outputName: "Teaching session plan",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Learners", guidance: "Who will learn, and what they already know."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Outcome", guidance: "What learners will be able to do afterwards."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Steps", guidance: "The small steps, each with an example from the learners' work."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Check", guidance: "How understanding will be checked, and what practice follows.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Start from the learners: what they already know, and what they need to be able to do afterwards, stated as an outcome. Build the session in small steps, each with an example from the learners' own work, and check understanding at each step with a question or a short exercise. Let learners practise, correct mistakes kindly, and end with a summary the learners state in their own words. Follow up after the session, to answer the questions that only arise in real use."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: teaching session plan for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "Learners: the status team, who know the query work but not the new screen. Outcome: each officer can answer a status query from the screen, including the no status case. Steps: show the screen, walk through a query with a report, walk through a query without one, and let each officer try both cases. Check: each officer answers two sample queries, and the one page guide is left with the team. Follow up: the analyst visits the team after one week, to answer questions from real use.",
            "The follow up visit revealed a question nobody had asked in training: what to do when the feed is late. The guide was updated, and the answer was shared with the whole team. Teaching had not ended at the session; it had continued into real use, where the real questions live."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Start from the learner: what they know, and what they need to be able to do.",
            "Build in small steps, with examples from the learners' own work, and check at each step.",
            "Let learners practise, and follow up after the session for real use questions."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-office-productivity-tools-and-technology",
        title: "Office Productivity Tools and Technology",
        subtitle: "Tools and Technology: using everyday office tools well, so the work is clear, findable, and current.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.6.1",
        explanation: [
            "Office productivity tools and technology are the everyday tools of analysis: word processing, spreadsheets, presentation software, and office equipment. Mastery of these tools is not glamorous, but it decides whether analysis is clear, findable, and current. A well built spreadsheet shows its workings, a well written document can be navigated, and a well made presentation carries one message per slide. Poor tool use hides good analysis behind confusion.",
            "The discipline of office tools is the discipline of the craft: version control, clear naming, findable storage, and accessible formatting. A document nobody can find, a spreadsheet nobody can audit, or a presentation nobody can follow wastes the analysis behind it. The analyst learns the tools deeply enough to use them fluently, chooses the right tool for each job, and keeps the outputs organised so others can find, check, and reuse them."
        ],
        outputName: "Office tools setup note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Tools", guidance: "The tools used, and what each is used for."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Standards", guidance: "The standards: naming, versions, storage, and formatting."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Workings", guidance: "How workings are shown, so others can check and reuse them."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Access", guidance: "Who can find and use each output, and how.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Choose the right tool for each job: documents for narrative, spreadsheets for numbers and lists, presentations for decisions. Apply the standards consistently: clear names, marked versions, agreed storage, and formatting that aids navigation. Show workings in every spreadsheet, so a reader can follow the calculation, and keep outputs findable and accessible. Review the setup regularly, removing stale files and updating the standards, so the workspace stays trustworthy."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: office tools setup for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Tools: documents for requirements and records, a spreadsheet for the traceability list, and presentations for sponsor decisions. Standards: names that state the content and the date, versions marked, storage in the shared workspace, and headings that aid navigation. Workings: the traceability spreadsheet shows each link with its source, so any link can be checked. Access: the whole team can read, and the analyst owns the updates.",
            "A new team member found the current requirements in minutes, because the naming and storage standards had been followed. The same member had spent a day searching on a previous change, where no standards existed. The setup note had saved a day, and it cost an hour to write."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Choose the right tool for each job, and learn it deeply enough to use fluently.",
            "Apply standards for naming, versions, storage, and formatting, consistently.",
            "Show workings, so others can check and reuse the analysis."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-business-analysis-tools-and-technology",
        title: "Business Analysis Tools and Technology",
        subtitle: "Tools and Technology: using specialist analysis tools to model, manage, and trace the work.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.6.2",
        explanation: [
            "Business analysis tools and technology are the specialist tools of the craft: requirements management tools, modelling tools, and the systems that hold analysis information. These tools manage traceability, hold versions, link requirements to designs and tests, and make the analysis visible to the whole team. Used well, they turn a pile of documents into a connected, checkable body of work. Used poorly, they become a burden that analysts work around.",
            "The tool serves the analysis, not the other way round. The analyst chooses tools by fit with the work, learns them well enough to use them fluently, and keeps the information inside them current. A tool full of stale requirements is worse than no tool, because it misleads with authority. The analyst also knows when a simple tool serves better than a complex one: a small change may need only a document and a list, while a large programme needs the full machinery of traceability."
        ],
        outputName: "Analysis tools setup note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Tools", guidance: "The specialist tools used, and what each holds."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Structure", guidance: "How information is structured inside each tool."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Currency", guidance: "How the information is kept current, and who owns it."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Fit", guidance: "Why each tool fits this change, and what simpler tool was considered.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Choose each tool by fit with the change, and record why it fits and what simpler tool was considered. Structure the information inside the tool deliberately: the types, the links, and the fields, agreed before the tool fills up. Keep the information current, with a named owner for each part, and review it regularly, removing what is stale. A tool is only as trustworthy as its contents, and contents are only as trustworthy as their owner."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: analysis tools setup for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Tools: the requirements tool holds the requirements and their traces, and the modelling tool holds the process and data models. Structure: requirements carry an identifier, a kind, a source, and a link to the design and the test. Currency: the analyst owns the requirements, the designer owns the models, and both are reviewed weekly. Fit: the requirements tool fits because the change has many linked requirements; a document alone was considered and rejected, because the traces would have been unmanageable.",
            "A stakeholder asked which design served a particular requirement. The answer came from the tool in seconds, with the trace visible. The same question on an earlier change had taken a day of searching through documents. The tool had earned its place by answering the question the team actually asked."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Choose tools by fit with the change; the tool serves the analysis, not the reverse.",
            "Structure information deliberately, before the tool fills up.",
            "Keep contents current, with a named owner for each part."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-competency-communication-tools-and-technology",
        title: "Communication Tools and Technology",
        subtitle: "Tools and Technology: using communication tools well, so messages reach the right people, in the right form, at the right time.",
        kind: .competency,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "9.6.3",
        explanation: [
            "Communication tools and technology are the tools that carry messages between people: email, messaging, video conferencing, telephones, and presentation software. The analyst uses them daily, to share information, run sessions, and keep stakeholders engaged. The choice of tool shapes the message: a sensitive topic suits a conversation, a decision suits a written record, and a group discussion suits a facilitated video session with a visible record.",
            "Good use of communication tools means choosing the right channel for each message, writing for the channel, and keeping a record where a record is needed. It also means respecting attention: short messages for short matters, and no message at all when silence serves better. The analyst manages the risks of each tool: the email that reaches the wrong person, the message that is read out of context, and the video session where half the participants cannot hear. The tool is chosen for the message, and the message is written for the reader."
        ],
        outputName: "Communication tools plan",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Channels", guidance: "The channels used, and what each is used for."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Choice", guidance: "How the channel is chosen for each message."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Records", guidance: "Which messages need a written record, and where it lives."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Care", guidance: "The risks of each tool, and how they are managed.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Agree the channels with the team, stating what each is used for: email for records, messaging for quick coordination, video for discussion, and the telephone for sensitive topics. Choose the channel by the message: sensitive matters need conversation, decisions need a written record, and group discussion needs a facilitated session. Keep a record where a record is needed, in the agreed location, and manage the risks: check recipients before sending, write so the message survives being read out of context, and test the technology before the session starts."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: communication tools plan for a payment status change",
        workedExample: [
            "Channels: email for decisions and records, messaging for daily coordination, video for workshops, and the telephone for sensitive one to one conversations. Choice: the scope decision travelled by email with the decision record attached, the daily coordination ran in messaging, and the wording discussion ran as a video workshop with a visible record. Records: every decision record lives in the shared workspace, linked from the email that announced it. Care: recipients are checked before sending, and the workshop technology is tested the day before.",
            "A sensitive message about the coverage limit was delivered by telephone first, and by email afterwards, so the sponsor heard it from a person before reading it in a document. The choice of channel had shown respect, and the sponsor's response was calmer for it. The tool had served the message, and the message had served the relationship."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Agree channels with the team, stating what each is used for.",
            "Choose the channel by the message; decisions need a written record.",
            "Check recipients, write for context, and test technology before sessions."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 120,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 350
    ),
    ]
}

extension StudioLesson {
    /// All 5 perspectives, in chapter order.
    static let perspectives: [StudioLesson] = [
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-perspective-agile-perspective",
        title: "Agile Perspective",
        subtitle: "Small steps, close teamwork, honest priorities",
        kind: .perspective,
        level: .foundation,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "11.1",
        explanation: [
            "The agile perspective sees delivery as a series of small steps, each producing something usable and each teaching the team what to do next. Instead of defining everything up front, the team works in short cycles, shows working results often, and adjusts the plan based on what is learned. For a payments change, this might mean delivering the core payment flow first, then adding exception handling, then adding reporting, with each step tested with real users. The approach suits work where needs are not fully known at the start and where learning quickly matters more than predicting perfectly.",
            "Backlog Management is the discipline of keeping an ordered list of the work, with the most valuable and most urgent items at the top. The analyst helps the owner of the work keep the list honest. Items near the top are small, clear, and ready to build, while items further down stay larger and less detailed until their turn comes. Priorities change as the team learns, as clients ask for new things, or as risks appear, and the backlog changes with them. Managing the backlog well means saying plainly what will not be done yet, so the team can focus on what matters now without guilt or confusion.",
            "User Stories are a way of describing a piece of work from the point of view of the person who needs it. A story names the person, the need, and the benefit, in plain words, and it is small enough to build and test in one cycle. A payments story might describe an operations user who needs to see why a payment failed, so that the case can be fixed without calling another team. Stories work best as a starting point for conversation, not as a contract, and the analyst helps the team talk through the story, agree what done means, and add the detail and the edge cases as understanding grows. Just enough documentation supports this way of working, as the team records what is needed to build, test, and support the change, and no more, keeping the record close to the work itself.",
            "Close collaboration is what makes the agile perspective work. The analyst sits with the delivery team, answers questions as they arise, and tests understanding daily rather than at the end. Changing priorities are handled openly, with the effect of each change stated plainly, so the owner can choose knowingly. This closeness demands trust, clear communication, and a willingness to revise earlier thinking when new evidence appears. The BA in an agile setting is not a scribe who hands over a document and steps back, but a partner in the daily work of shaping, testing, and learning."
        ],
        outputName: "How the work changes under this perspective",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Short cycles", guidance: "Work in short cycles, show usable results often, and adjust from what is learned."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Ordered backlog", guidance: "Keep the backlog ordered, with the top items small, clear, and ready to build."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "User stories", guidance: "Write user stories from the user's point of view, as a starting point for conversation."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Just enough documentation", guidance: "Record just enough documentation to build, test, and support the change."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Close collaboration", guidance: "Stay close to the delivery team, and handle changing priorities openly.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "In an agile setting, the analyst's work follows the rhythm of short cycles. Requirements are refined just in time: the top of the backlog is small and clear, later items stay light, and detail is added as the work approaches. The analyst stays close to the delivery team, answers questions quickly, and treats changing priorities as normal, not as failure.",
            "Write user stories from the user's point of view, with acceptance criteria that make each story testable, and record just enough documentation to build, test, and support the change. The test of the documentation is whether the team can build from it and support from it, not whether it is long. Review what was delivered at the end of each cycle, and let what was learned change the next cycle's plan."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: agile analysis for a payment status screen",
        workedExample: [
            "The team worked in short cycles. The first cycle delivered the status display for one payment type, with acceptance criteria agreed with operations. The second cycle added the status history, and the third added the remaining payment types. Each cycle ended with a review with operations, and what was learned changed the next cycle.",
            "The backlog stayed ordered by value: the status display first, because it answered the most common query, then the history, then the remaining types. Documentation was just enough: stories, criteria, and a short decision record. When priorities changed mid cycle, the change was handled openly, with the trade off recorded."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Work in short cycles, show usable results often, and adjust from what is learned.",
            "Keep the backlog ordered, with the top items small, clear, and ready to build.",
            "Write User Stories from the user's point of view, as a starting point for conversation.",
            "Record just enough documentation to build, test, and support the change.",
            "Stay close to the delivery team, and handle changing priorities openly."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-TC-10", title: "The dashboard that learned as it grew", takeaway: "Deliver in small steps and reorder the backlog as learning arrives.", story: [
            "A team planned a payments dashboard as one large release after six months.\nThree months in, the users asked for a different set of views.\nThe plan had no room to change course.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The analyst proposed splitting the dashboard into small usable parts\n→ The first part showed the daily queue, and users tested it within weeks\n→ User feedback reshaped the second part before it was built\n→ Priorities were reordered openly at each cycle boundary\n→ The dashboard reached the users in stages, with each stage used and tested",
            "The team said:\n\"We learned more from the first small release than from three months of planning.\"",
            "So the team delivered the dashboard in stages, each stage shaped by real use, and the final product matched what the users actually needed. The earlier plan was not wasted. It became the backlog that the team reordered as learning arrived.",
            "Why?",
            "Large up front plans assume that needs stay still while the work happens. They rarely do. Small cycles turn that risk into learning, because each release tests an assumption with real users. Backlog Management makes the learning useful, by keeping the ordered list honest and by stating plainly what will wait. The analyst's role was to keep the conversation about value and priority open, so the team could change direction without blame or confusion. That is the agile perspective in practice.",
            "Small releases turn guessing into learning.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Split large work into small parts that users can test early\n→ Reorder the backlog openly as learning arrives\n→ Treat changing priorities as information, not as failure\n→ Keep the team and the users talking throughout the work",
            "Have you ever seen a plan improve because users tested a small part early?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-TC-11", title: "A story that listed the problem, not the fix", takeaway: "Write stories around the user's next step, with checks anyone can test.", story: [
            "A user story said that operations staff needed to see failed payments.\nThe team built a list, but the staff still could not fix the cases.\nThe story had named the need but not the benefit or the checks.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The analyst sat with the staff and watched them work a failed case\n→ The staff needed the failure reason and the next action, not just a list\n→ The analyst rewrote the story around the reason, the action, and the benefit\n→ The team agreed simple checks for the common reasons and the unknown case\n→ The rebuilt view let staff fix most cases without calling another team",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"The first version showed us the problem, the second helped us solve it.\"",
            "So the story was rewritten, the checks were agreed, and the view was rebuilt in the next cycle. The staff tested it with real cases and confirmed it served their work. The team kept the lesson for future stories, asking always what the user must be able to do next.",
            "Why?",
            "A story that names only a feature leaves the team guessing about its purpose. A story that names the person, the need, and the benefit gives the team a test. Can the user do the next step. The analyst closed the gap by watching the work, rewriting the story with the user, and agreeing checks that anyone could test. Stories are conversations, not contracts, and the conversation must include the person whose work will change. Just enough detail, agreed together, beats a long specification written at a distance.",
            "A story is good when the user can do the next step.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Name the person, the need, and the benefit in every story\n→ Agree simple checks that show what done means\n→ Watch the user work before rewriting a story\n→ Keep the story small enough to build and test in one cycle",
            "What would you do if a story described a feature but not the job it serves?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-perspective-agile-perspective-q1", question: "You are the BA supporting an agile team. How do you help manage the backlog so the team always knows what matters most?", answer: [
            "I would explain that I keep the backlog as one ordered list, owned by the person accountable for value, with my help on clarity and readiness. Items at the top are small and clear, with agreed acceptance checks, while items lower down stay rough until they approach the top. I review the list regularly, removing items that are no longer needed and splitting items that are too large. I ask the owner to confirm the order after each cycle, based on value, risk, and learning, and I state plainly what is being deferred. I avoid letting the list become a wish list with no order, because an unordered list is not a plan. I also keep the reasoning visible, so the team understands why an item sits where it does. When a new request arrives, I help the owner place it honestly, showing what it displaces. Good Backlog Management, in my view, is a conversation about priorities, held often and recorded simply, so the team always knows what matters most and why."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-perspective-agile-perspective-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you wrote User Stories for a payments feature. How did you make them clear and testable?", answer: [
            "I would explain that I write the story from the user's point of view, naming the person, the need, and the benefit, in plain words. Then I work with the team to agree what done means, using simple checks that anyone can test. For a payments story about seeing why a payment failed, the checks might cover the common failure reasons, the missing data case, and what the user sees when no reason is known. I keep the story small enough to build and test in one cycle, and I split it if the team says it is too large. I treat the story as a promise of a conversation, not as a finished specification. I stay available while the team builds, answering questions and refining detail as understanding grows. I also add the edge cases the team discovers, so they are recorded and tested. After the cycle, I check the result with the user and record what was learned. A good story, in my view, is clear, testable, and honest about what is still unknown, and it leaves room for the team to shape the detail together."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-perspective-agile-perspective-q3", question: "A stakeholder asks to change priorities in the middle of a sprint. How do you handle it as the BA?", answer: [
            "I would explain that I welcome the change as learning, and I make its effect visible so the owner can choose knowingly. First I would understand the new priority and why it matters now. Then I would look at the work in progress and the items waiting, and set out the options. We can swap the new item in and defer another, split the new item so the urgent part ships first, or hold the change until the next cycle. I would state the cost of each option in plain terms, including work already done that would be paused. I would recommend the option I think serves the goal best, with reasons, and I would let the owner decide. Once the decision is made, I would update the backlog, tell the team clearly, and record the reason for the change. I would avoid blaming anyone for the shift, because changing priorities are a normal part of agile work. The discipline is not to prevent change. It is to handle change openly, so the team is never surprised and the plan always reflects the current truth."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-perspective-agile-perspective-q4", question: "How do you decide what counts as just enough documentation on an agile payments project?", answer: [
            "I would explain that just enough means the record supports building, testing, and supporting the change, and nothing beyond that is added for its own sake. I would keep the essential artefacts close to the work. The story, the acceptance checks, the key decisions and their reasons, and any diagrams the team actually uses. I would avoid long documents that repeat what the team already knows, and I would retire notes that no longer reflect the truth. For a regulated payments change, I would check what evidence the control functions need, and I would record that evidence as part of the work, not as an afterthought. I would ask the team and the reviewers what they actually read and use, and shape the record around that. I would also keep the record current during the cycle, not in a rush at the end. Documentation, in my view, is a service to the future reader, including the future team. Just enough is the smallest record that lets a new person understand the decision, build the change safely, and support it after release. Anything less is a risk, and anything more is waste."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-perspective-business-intelligence-perspective",
        title: "Business Intelligence Perspective",
        subtitle: "The business intelligence perspective applies business analysis to decisions that rest on data: reporting, analytics, and the platforms that carry them.",
        kind: .perspective,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "11.2",
        explanation: [
            "The business intelligence perspective applies business analysis to decisions that rest on data: reporting, analytics, and the platforms that carry them. The analyst's job is to define what decision each report or measure must support, what data it needs, and how good that data must be. A report that no decision uses is not a business intelligence asset, it is a cost.",
            "This perspective matters because data work fails differently from other work. A report can be technically correct and still mislead, when its definitions differ from the definitions the business uses, or when its data is incomplete in ways no one stated. The analyst prevents that failure by defining the decision, the data, and the quality rules together, before the report is built.",
            "The work emphasises data definitions, data quality, and the meaning of measures. It asks, for every report: who decides what from this, what data proves it, and what happens when the data is wrong? Those questions, answered early, are what separate a trusted report from a pretty one."
        ],
        outputName: "How the work changes under this perspective",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Decision first", guidance: "Name the decision each report or measure supports, before defining the data."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Data definitions", guidance: "Define every field and measure, so two readers reach the same number."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Data quality", guidance: "State the quality rules, and what happens when data fails them."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Trust", guidance: "Make the limits of the data visible, so the report is trusted for what it is.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Start from the decision, not from the data. For each report, write the decision it supports, the question it answers, and the action it triggers. Then define the data: every field, every measure, and the quality rules, checked against real data. State the limits beside the results, so the reader knows what the report cannot show.",
            "Work with the data owners from the start, and test the report against known cases before it is trusted. A report that survives a check against real cases earns trust quickly. A report that is trusted without a check loses it at the first wrong number, and trust, once lost, is slow to return."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: business intelligence analysis for a reconciliation report",
        workedExample: [
            "Decision: the operations lead decides each morning which breaks to work first. Question: which breaks are oldest, and how old is each? Data: the break list, with the age of each break, defined as the time since the break was first recorded. Quality rule: a break with no recorded time is shown as unknown, never as zero.",
            "The report was tested against the previous week's break list, and one definition was corrected: age counted from first recording, not from the last update. The limits are shown on the report: breaks from one payment type are excluded until their data is available, and the exclusion is stated, not hidden."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Name the decision each report or measure supports, before defining the data.",
            "Define every field and measure, so two readers reach the same number.",
            "State the quality rules, and what happens when data fails them.",
            "Make the limits of the data visible, so the report is trusted for what it is."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-perspective-business-intelligence-perspective-q1", question: "You are the BA for a new payments dashboard. How do you make sure each measure on the dashboard supports a real decision?", answer: []),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-perspective-business-intelligence-perspective-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you found that a report's definitions differed from the definitions the business used. What did you do?", answer: [])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-perspective-information-technology-perspective",
        title: "Information Technology Perspective",
        subtitle: "The information technology perspective applies business analysis to changes driven by technology: software delivery, system integration, and infrastructure.",
        kind: .perspective,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "11.3",
        explanation: [
            "The information technology perspective applies business analysis to changes driven by technology: software delivery, system integration, and infrastructure. The analyst bridges the business need and the technical solution, translating each side's language into the other's, and keeping the focus on the outcome the technology must deliver.",
            "This perspective matters because technology change is where business needs are most easily lost in technical detail. A system can be delivered on time, to specification, and still fail the business, when the specification described the system rather than the need. The analyst keeps the need visible throughout, and checks every technical decision against it.",
            "The work emphasises interfaces, data, non-functional requirements, and the constraints that technology imposes. It asks, for every technical choice: what does this mean for the business need, for the user, and for the teams that must support the change after release?"
        ],
        outputName: "How the work changes under this perspective",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Need first", guidance: "State the business need before the technical solution, and keep it visible."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Interfaces", guidance: "Define every interface: what crosses, in what form, and under what rules."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Non-functional requirements", guidance: "State how well the solution must perform, with numbers and checks."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Constraints", guidance: "Name the technical constraints, and what they mean for the business.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Write the business need in business language, and keep it attached to every technical requirement, so the need is never lost in the detail. Define interfaces from both sides, state non-functional requirements with numbers and checks, and name the technical constraints plainly, with their business meaning. Review technical decisions with the business owners, in business language, before they are locked in.",
            "Stay close to the delivery team, answer questions quickly, and treat technical constraints as information, not as obstacles. When a constraint changes what the business asked for, record the change, its reason, and its effect on the need, and get the business owner's agreement before proceeding."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: IT perspective on a payment hub interface",
        workedExample: [
            "Need: the corporate banking channel must send payment instructions to the hub, and the hub must confirm receipt. Interface: the instruction, including reference, amount, currency, and beneficiary details, in the agreed form, with the reference and amount unchanged in transit. Non-functional: the hub must acknowledge receipt within the agreed time, and must queue instructions when unavailable.",
            "Constraint: the hub cannot accept one field in the form the channel sends it, so the field is transformed at the interface, under a rule agreed with both teams. The transformation, its reason, and its effect were recorded, and the business owner agreed the change before build."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "State the business need before the technical solution, and keep it visible.",
            "Define every interface: what crosses, in what form, and under what rules.",
            "State how well the solution must perform, with numbers and checks.",
            "Name the technical constraints, and what they mean for the business."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-perspective-information-technology-perspective-q1", question: "You are the BA on a system integration change. How do you keep the business need visible when the technical detail grows?", answer: []),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-perspective-information-technology-perspective-q2", question: "Tell me about a time a technical constraint changed what the business asked for. How did you handle it?", answer: [])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-perspective-business-architecture-perspective",
        title: "Business Architecture Perspective",
        subtitle: "The business architecture perspective applies business analysis at the level of the whole enterprise: capabilities, value streams, and the alignment between strategy and delivery.",
        kind: .perspective,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "11.4",
        explanation: [
            "The business architecture perspective applies business analysis at the level of the whole enterprise: capabilities, value streams, and the alignment between strategy and delivery. The analyst works across boundaries, mapping what the organisation can do, how value flows through it, and where a change lands. The view is wide and the horizon is long.",
            "This perspective matters because enterprise change fails when it is designed one part at a time. A change that improves one capability can break another, and a change that serves one value stream can starve another. The enterprise view shows the connections, so the change is designed for the whole, not for the part that shouted loudest.",
            "The work emphasises capability maps, value streams, and the alignment of change with strategy. It asks, for every change: which capabilities does it touch, which value streams does it serve, and how does it move the organisation toward its strategic goals?"
        ],
        outputName: "How the work changes under this perspective",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Capability map", guidance: "Map the capabilities the change touches, and assess their strength."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Value streams", guidance: "Show how value flows, and where the change improves or interrupts the flow."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Alignment", guidance: "State how the change serves the strategic goals, with evidence."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Roadmap", guidance: "Sequence the change so value arrives early and dependencies are respected.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Map the capabilities and value streams before designing the change, and use the map to place every part of the change with an owner. State the alignment with strategy plainly, with evidence, and sequence the change so that value arrives early and the riskiest dependencies are retired early. Review the map as the change proceeds, because the enterprise moves while the change is under way.",
            "Work across boundaries, and bring the owners of each affected capability into the design early. A capability owner who learns about a change late will resist it, however good it is. A capability owner who helped design it will defend it, however hard it is."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: business architecture view of a payments change",
        workedExample: [
            "Capability map: validate a payment, route a payment, confirm status, handle exceptions, and reconcile accounts. The change touches exception handling and status confirmation. Value stream: from customer instruction to confirmed settlement, with exception handling as the branch that decides whether a failed payment recovers.",
            "Alignment: the change serves the strategic goal of faster, more reliable cross border payments, evidenced by the exception log and handling times. Roadmap: queue ordering and ownership first, then the status summary, because the first phase needs no new data and delivers value immediately."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Map the capabilities the change touches, and assess their strength.",
            "Show how value flows, and where the change improves or interrupts the flow.",
            "State how the change serves the strategic goals, with evidence.",
            "Sequence the change so value arrives early and dependencies are respected."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-perspective-business-architecture-perspective-q1", question: "You are the BA on an enterprise wide change. How do you show where the change lands across capabilities and value streams?", answer: []),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-perspective-business-architecture-perspective-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you had to align a change with strategy when different parts of the business wanted different things. What did you do?", answer: [])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-perspective-business-process-management-perspective",
        title: "Business Process Management Perspective",
        subtitle: "The business process management perspective applies business analysis to processes themselves: discovering, modelling, improving, and governing how work flows.",
        kind: .perspective,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: "11.5",
        explanation: [
            "The business process management perspective applies business analysis to processes themselves: discovering, modelling, improving, and governing how work flows. The analyst treats the process as the unit of change, measuring it, finding its waste and risk, and redesigning it with the people who do the work. The process, not the system, is the product.",
            "This perspective matters because most business problems are process problems wearing system clothes. A slow payment journey, a high error rate, or a queue that never clears are usually caused by the process: its steps, its handovers, its waits, and its rules. Fixing the system without fixing the process automates the problem, it does not solve it.",
            "The work emphasises process discovery, modelling, analysis, and governance. It asks, for every process: who owns it, how does it perform, where does it waste time or add risk, and how is it kept healthy after the change?"
        ],
        outputName: "How the work changes under this perspective",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Discovery", guidance: "Discover the process as it runs, from evidence, including exceptions."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Modelling", guidance: "Model the process with steps, decisions, owners, and handovers."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Analysis", guidance: "Measure the process and find the waste, risk, and root causes."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Governance", guidance: "Name the process owner, and how the process is monitored and improved.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Discover the process as it runs, from observation and real cases, and model it with the people who do the work, including the exception paths. Measure it: cycle time, waits, handoffs, and rework, and find the root causes of waste and risk. Redesign with the process owner, name the governance: who owns the process, how it is monitored, and how it improves after the change.",
            "Keep the process healthy after the change by monitoring the measures and reviewing the process regularly. A process that is redesigned and then ignored will drift back to its old shape, because the old shape is what people remember. Governance is what keeps the new shape."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: BPM view of exception handling",
        workedExample: [
            "Discovery: observation of the queue over five working days, and interviews with three officers. Model: the process as it runs, with the unordered queue, the phone checks, and the duplicate working. Analysis: the queue order and the ownership gap cause the duplicate working, and the missing status summary causes the phone checks.",
            "Redesign: an ordered queue, one owner per exception, and a status summary. Governance: the operations lead owns the process, the measures are reviewed weekly, and the process is reviewed after each release. The new shape is kept by the weekly review, not by hope."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Discover the process as it runs, from evidence, including exceptions.",
            "Model the process with steps, decisions, owners, and handovers.",
            "Measure the process and find the waste, risk, and root causes.",
            "Name the process owner, and how the process is monitored and improved."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-perspective-business-process-management-perspective-q1", question: "You are the BA improving a payments process. How do you discover the process as it really runs, not as it is documented?", answer: []),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-perspective-business-process-management-perspective-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you redesigned a process. How did you make sure the new process stayed in place after the change?", answer: [])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    ]
}

extension StudioLesson {
    /// Orientation lessons: choosing and combining techniques.
    static let orientations: [StudioLesson] = [
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-orientation-choosing-the-right-technique",
        title: "Choosing the Right Technique",
        subtitle: "Pick the technique that fits the question",
        kind: .orientation,
        level: .practitioner,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: nil,
        explanation: [
            "A technique is only useful when it answers the question in front of you. A good analyst starts from the problem, not from a favourite tool. If the question is why payments keep failing at one step, a walkthrough of the process will help more than a long survey. If the question is which option costs least over time, a comparison of options will help more than a workshop. The starting point is always the decision that needs to be made and the people who need to trust the answer.",
            "Context shapes the choice as much as the question does. The time available, the people who can take part, the quality of the data, and the appetite for change all matter. A short deadline may call for a focused interview rather than a large group session. A sensitive topic, such as a change to how exceptions are handled, may need one to one conversations before anything is discussed in a group. A new payments product with many unknowns may need small experiments and prototypes rather than a single detailed specification written up front.",
            "Analysts rarely use one technique alone. They combine techniques so that each one checks the others. An interview can surface a claim, observation of the live work can test it, and a review of the data can confirm how often it happens. In a payments example, staff may say that manual checks cause delays. Watching the queue, timing a sample of cases, and checking the volumes together give a fuller picture than any one source alone, and combining methods in this way builds confidence and catches blind spots early.",
            "Two examples show the range of choice. Decision Analysis helps when several options compete and the trade offs are not obvious, such as choosing between fixing an old reconciliation process and replacing it. It sets out the options, the criteria that matter, and the consequences of each choice in a structured way. Estimation helps when the team must judge size, effort, or duration before the work is fully known, such as sizing a change to add a new payment type. Good estimation states its assumptions openly, so that others can challenge them and the estimate can be revised as knowledge grows."
        ],
        outputName: "Technique choice note",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Decision", guidance: "The decision that must be made, and who must trust the answer."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Question", guidance: "The question the technique must answer."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Options", guidance: "The techniques considered, and what each would give."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Choice", guidance: "The technique chosen, and why it fits the question, the people, and the time.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Start from the decision that must be made, not from a favourite technique. Name the question the technique must answer, and the people who must trust the answer, then consider the techniques that answer that question. Match the choice to the context: the time available, the people who can take part, the quality of the data, and the sensitivity of the topic.",
            "Combine techniques so that each one checks the others: a survey before a workshop, a document review before interviews, a prototype after a model. Record the choice and its reason, so the next analyst can see why this technique, for this question, at this time, and can choose differently when the context differs."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: choosing a technique for a failing payment step",
        workedExample: [
            "Decision: why payments keep failing at one step, and what to change. Question: what happens at that step, in practice? Options: a survey of the operations team, interviews with the officers who work the step, or observation of the step being worked. Choice: observation first, because the question is about what happens, not about what people think happens.",
            "The observation showed a workaround that no interview had mentioned: officers re-entered a field by hand, and the re-entry introduced errors. Interviews followed, to learn why the workaround existed. The combination answered the question, where either technique alone would have missed half of it."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Start from the decision that must be made, not from a favourite technique.",
            "Match the technique to the time, people, data, and sensitivity of the situation.",
            "Combine techniques so that each one checks the others.",
            "Use Decision Analysis when options compete and trade offs need to be made visible.",
            "Use Estimation with open assumptions, and revise the estimate as knowledge grows."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-TC-01", title: "The wrong tool for a failing payment step", takeaway: "Match the technique to the question, and test findings with a second source.", story: [
            "A bank noticed that a small share of cross border payments stalled at the same check.\nThe analyst was asked to find out why and to propose a fix.\nA long questionnaire was sent to three teams.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The questionnaire asked broad questions about the whole payment journey\n→ Replies were short and described different steps in different words\n→ No reply pointed clearly to the stalling check\n→ The analyst then watched the queue with the operations team for one morning\n→ The watch showed the check waiting on a missing reference that arrived late",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"We answered your survey, but nobody asked us to show the actual cases.\"",
            "So the analyst dropped the survey results, worked through a sample of stalled cases with the team, and traced each one to the late reference. The fix was a change to the order in which information was requested, not a new system, and the stall rate fell in the following weeks.",
            "Why?",
            "A questionnaire is a weak tool when the problem is specific and the language varies between teams. It collects opinions at a distance and hides the detail that matters. Watching the work and tracing real cases answered the actual question, which was what happens at one step, not what people think of the whole journey. The lesson is to match the technique to the question. Broad questions suit broad tools, and narrow problems need close, direct evidence.",
            "The right technique is the one that answers the question you actually have.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ State the decision or question before choosing any technique\n→ Use direct observation and real cases for narrow, specific problems\n→ Treat a survey as a starting point, never as proof on its own\n→ Combine sources so that one finding checks another",
            "Have you ever picked a familiar technique first and found it answered the wrong question?"
        ]),
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-TC-02", title: "Two estimates and one honest conversation", takeaway: "Surface the assumptions behind an estimate before debating the number.", story: [
            "A delivery team had to size a change to add a new account to account payment option.\nTwo senior staff gave very different estimates for the same work.\nThe sponsor wanted one number for the plan.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The first estimate assumed the existing checks could be reused unchanged\n→ The second estimate assumed new checks and extra testing throughout\n→ Neither estimate listed its assumptions in writing\n→ The analyst asked each person to state what was included and excluded\n→ The gap turned out to be one testing assumption, not the build itself",
            "The plan said:\n\"We will agree one estimate before the planning meeting.\"",
            "So the analyst produced a single estimate with the assumptions written beside it, plus a range that showed the effect of the testing choice. The sponsor chose the fuller testing option with open eyes, the plan used the higher figure, and the team was not blamed later for a gap that had been visible from the start.",
            "Why?",
            "Estimates disagree most often because the assumptions disagree, not because the work itself is mysterious. When assumptions stay hidden, people argue about numbers and nobody learns anything. Writing the assumptions down turns the argument into a decision. The sponsor can then choose between options knowingly, and the estimate can be updated when an assumption is settled. Honest estimation is a communication skill as much as a counting skill.",
            "An estimate without its assumptions is a guess wearing a number.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Ask every estimator to list what is included and excluded\n→ Show a range when a key assumption is still open\n→ Let the sponsor choose between assumptions, not between people\n→ Revisit the estimate when an assumption is confirmed or removed",
            "What would you do if two trusted colleagues gave you estimates weeks apart?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-orientation-choosing-the-right-technique-q1", question: "You are the BA on an agile team starting a new cycle, and the team cannot agree whether to run a workshop, hold interviews, or review documents to understand a payments exception process before refinement. How do you choose?", answer: [
            "I would start by asking what decision the refinement session has to support and who has to trust the answer, keeping the choice small enough to fit the cycle. A workshop suits a shared decision where people need to hear each other and agree together. Interviews suit sensitive topics or cases where people hold different pieces of the story. Document review suits checking what the rules say today, but it rarely explains what people actually do when a case is unusual. I would also weigh time, access to the right people, and how much is already known. In a payments exception process, I would usually combine methods. I might start with a short review of the queue data and the current guidance, then interview two or three people who handle the hardest cases, then test what I learned by watching the work for a short period. I would explain my choice to the team in plain terms, naming what each method can and cannot show. If the first method left a gap, I would switch rather than push on. The test I use is simple. Will this evidence let the decision maker act with confidence. If not, I change the approach early."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-orientation-choosing-the-right-technique-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you used Decision Analysis or a structured comparison to help a stakeholder choose between options. What did you do?", answer: [
            "I would describe a case where two options looked equally attractive until the trade offs were laid out side by side. First I made sure the options were real and comparable, with the same scope in each one. Then I agreed the criteria with the stakeholder before scoring anything, because scoring first and agreeing criteria later invites argument. The criteria covered cost, delivery time, risk to live payments, effect on the operations team, and how hard each option would be to change later. I gathered evidence for each criterion rather than relying on opinion, and I marked clearly where evidence was thin. Where a criterion depended on judgement, I said so and showed the reasoning. Then I presented the comparison as a picture of trade offs, not as a single winner. I explained what each option gave up and what it protected. The stakeholder could then make a choice that matched their priorities, and the reasoning was recorded so the decision could be revisited if facts changed. What mattered most was that the decision felt owned, not imposed, because the stakeholder had shaped the criteria from the start."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-orientation-choosing-the-right-technique-q3", question: "A stakeholder says your estimate for a backlog item doubled after refinement. How do you explain the change in an agile setting?", answer: [
            "I would welcome the question, because a changing estimate usually means learning happened, and hiding that learning helps nobody. I would explain that the first figure was based on limited knowledge and stated assumptions, and that refinement tested those assumptions. Perhaps the item touched a shared payment validation step, or needed extra testing for exception cases that were not visible at first. I would show the original assumptions beside the new ones, so the stakeholder can see exactly what changed and why. I would avoid blaming the team or defending the old number. Instead I would talk about the choice now open to the stakeholder. We can accept the larger item, split it so the most valuable part ships first, or simplify the scope to hold the size down. I would recommend the option I think serves the goal best, with reasons. I would also note what we learned for future estimation, such as allowing more time for items that touch shared checks. In an agile setting, an estimate is a planning aid that improves as understanding grows. The honest conversation about the change is part of the value, not a failure of the method."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    StudioLesson(
        id: "lesson-orientation-techniques-for-the-wider-picture",
        title: "Techniques for the Wider Picture",
        subtitle: "See the wider picture before you decide",
        kind: .orientation,
        level: .advanced,
        knowledgeArea: nil,
        babokReference: nil,
        explanation: [
            "The Balanced Scorecard is a way of looking at performance from more than one angle at once, so that success in one area does not hide trouble in another. Instead of judging a change by cost alone, the analyst considers the client view, the internal process view, the learning and growth view, and the financial view together. In a payments example, a change might cut handling cost while making life harder for clients, and a single measure would call that a success. A balanced view asks how clients experience the change, how the process performs, what the team learns, and what the change costs, so the decision reflects the whole picture rather than one convenient number.",
            "Organizational Modelling is the practice of drawing how an organisation is put together, including its teams, roles, and reporting lines, and how work flows between them. It helps a BA see where a change will land, who must be involved, and where handoffs create risk. In a payments setting, a model might show the relationship between the front office, the operations team, the technology team, and the control functions, and the points where a payment passes from one to another. The value is not the diagram itself. It is the conversation the diagram starts, which surfaces gaps, overlaps, and assumptions about who owns each step before the change begins.",
            "Process Analysis is the careful study of how work is actually done, step by step, from start to finish, including the waits, the checks, and the exceptions. The analyst walks the process, talks to the people who run it, and tests the picture against real cases, so the analysis describes reality rather than the official version. In payments, this might mean tracing a payment from initiation through validation, clearing, and reconciliation, noting where manual work, rework, or delay appears. The result is a clear view of where value is added, where risk is managed, and where effort is wasted, which gives the business a sound basis for choosing what to change.",
            "Vendor Assessment is the structured comparison of outside providers who might supply a product or service, judged against the needs of the organisation rather than against a sales presentation. The analyst agrees the criteria first, gathers evidence for each one, and compares the options on the same basis. For a payments service, criteria might include fit with the current process, ease of support, data handling, resilience, cost over time, and the effort needed to move away later. The aim is a decision the organisation can explain and defend, with the trade offs recorded, so that the choice serves the long term interest and not only the most polished demonstration."
        ],
        outputName: "Wider picture assessment",
        outputStructure: [
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Angles", guidance: "The angles judged: financial, customer, process, and learning."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Organisation", guidance: "Where the change lands, and who owns each step."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Process", guidance: "The process as it runs, including waits and exceptions."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Vendors", guidance: "Vendors assessed against agreed criteria, on the same basis."),
            StudioOutputSection(title: "Trade offs", guidance: "The trade offs recorded, with the reasoning behind them.")
        ],
        howToWrite: [
            "Judge the change from several angles at once, so a gain in one angle is not bought by a hidden loss in another. Model the organisation to see where the change lands and who owns each step, analyse the process as it really runs, and assess vendors against agreed criteria on the same basis. Record the trade offs and the reasoning, so every wider decision can be explained.",
            "Use these techniques together, not one at a time. The scorecard shows the angles, the organisation model shows the landing places, the process analysis shows the reality, and the vendor assessment shows the options. Together they give a picture that no single technique can give alone."
        ],
        workedExampleTitle: "Worked example: wider picture for a payment hub decision",
        workedExample: [
            "Angles: the scorecard showed cost falling and complaints rising, which made the trade off visible. Organisation: the model showed the change landing on three teams, with one handover lacking an owner. Process: the analysis showed the real process, including waits that the documented process hid.",
            "Vendors: three vendors were assessed against the same criteria, with the same test cases, and the assessment record showed the evidence for each score. Trade offs: the recommended vendor cost more to run but fitted better, and the fit gap of the cheaper vendor would have needed custom work. The reasoning was recorded, and the decision was explained to every affected team."
        ],
        keyPoints: [
            "Judge performance from several angles, not from cost alone.",
            "Model the organisation to see where a change lands and who owns each step.",
            "Analyse the process as it really runs, including waits and exceptions.",
            "Assess vendors against agreed criteria and evidence, on the same basis.",
            "Record trade offs and reasoning so every wider decision can be explained."
        ],
        practiceScenarios: [
            StudioPracticeScenario(id: "BA-TC-12", title: "Three demonstrations and one fair test", takeaway: "Compare vendors on agreed criteria and the same real cases.", story: [
            "A bank considered replacing the tool used to track payment investigations.\nThree providers gave polished demonstrations in the same week.\nEach demonstration showed a different strength and hid a different weakness.",
            "Here is what happened:",
            "→ The analyst agreed the assessment criteria with users and support staff\n→ Each provider was asked to show the same three investigation cases\n→ The analyst scored the options on the same basis, with evidence noted\n→ One provider shone on the demonstration but struggled on the hard case\n→ The team chose the provider that handled all three cases steadily",
            "The stakeholder said:\n\"Seeing the same cases side by side made the choice obvious.\"",
            "So the team signed with the steady provider, the users received the tool they had tested, and the support team knew what to expect. The written assessment helped the sponsor explain the decision to others, and the trade offs were understood before the contract was signed.",
            "Why?",
            "Demonstrations are designed to impress, and each one highlights a different strength. A fair assessment removes that advantage by judging all options against the same criteria and the same real cases. The analyst protected the decision by agreeing the criteria first, gathering evidence, and recording the trade offs. The choice was not the most polished option. It was the option that served the daily work best. That is the wider picture in action. Look past the presentation, test the reality, and choose for the long term.",
            "Judge every option on the same real cases, not on the best show.",
            "BA Takeaway",
            "→ Agree assessment criteria with users and support staff first\n→ Test all options against the same real cases\n→ Score on the same basis and mark thin evidence openly\n→ Record the trade offs so the decision can be explained later",
            "Have you ever seen a polished demonstration win over a steadier option?"
        ])
        ],
        practiceQuestions: [
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-orientation-techniques-for-the-wider-picture-q1", question: "You are the BA and a payments change cut costs but complaints rose. How would a balanced view help you judge it?", answer: [
            "I would explain that I look at the change from several angles before calling it a success. I would ask how clients experience the change, whether the process runs more smoothly, what the team learns, and what the change costs over time. A payments change might reduce handling time but increase client calls, and a single cost measure would hide that harm. I would agree a small set of measures with the sponsor, one or two for each angle, and I would gather the baseline before the change goes live. After the change, I would compare the new figures with the baseline and report all angles together, including any that moved the wrong way. I would avoid choosing measures that flatter the project. The honest picture, including the uncomfortable parts, is what lets the business improve. I would present the results plainly, explain what each measure shows, and recommend the next action. A balanced view, in my view, protects the organisation from celebrating a narrow win that creates a wider loss."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-orientation-techniques-for-the-wider-picture-q2", question: "Tell me about a time you analysed a process end to end. How did you find where the real waste and risk sat?", answer: [
            "I would explain that I start by mapping the current process with the people who run it, step by step, including the waits and the exceptions. I would walk through real cases, not only the happy path, because the exceptions often hold the cost and the risk. I would ask at each step who owns it, what information arrives, what leaves, and what happens when something is missing. I would look for handoffs, rework, and checks that add delay without adding safety. Then I would test the picture with volumes and timings, so the pain points are sized, not just described. I would present the findings as a clear picture of the current state, with the evidence beside each claim, and I would separate facts from opinions. I would involve the team in shaping the future picture, so the change is owned by the people who must live with it. I would recommend the change that removes the most waste with the least risk, and I would state what will be measured after the change. Good Process Analysis, in my view, earns trust by describing reality accurately before proposing anything new."
        ]),
            StudioPracticeQuestion(id: "lesson-orientation-techniques-for-the-wider-picture-q3", question: "You are the BA leading a Vendor Assessment for a new payments service. How do you keep the comparison fair?", answer: [
            "I would explain that I agree the criteria before meeting any provider, so the comparison is fair and the decision can be defended. I would involve the people who will use and support the service, because their needs matter more than a feature list. The criteria would cover fit with the current process, ease of support, data handling, resilience, cost over time, and the effort needed to move away later. I would gather evidence for each criterion, asking for demonstrations against our own cases rather than polished examples. I would score each option on the same basis, mark where evidence was thin, and record the trade offs plainly. I would avoid letting a single impressive demonstration outweigh a weak fit elsewhere. Then I would present the comparison as a picture of trade offs, recommend the option I think serves the long term interest, and explain the reasons. I would record the decision and the evidence, so the choice can be revisited if facts change. A structured Vendor Assessment protects the organisation from buying a promise and discovering the gap after the contract is signed."
        ])
        ],
        linkedExerciseIDs: [],
        suggestedMinimumWords: 150,
        suggestedMaximumWords: 400
    ),
    ]
}

extension StudioLesson {
    /// The lessons for one level, grouped once: the concatenation
    /// and filter run a single time, on first access, instead of
    /// on every call. The grouping preserves the display order
    /// exactly: key concepts, tasks, techniques, competency
    /// groups followed by their individual competencies,
    /// perspectives, then orientation lessons.
    private static let lessonsByLevel: [StudioLevel: [StudioLesson]] = {
        let combined = keyConcepts + tasks + techniques + competencies + individualCompetencies + perspectives + orientations
        var map: [StudioLevel: [StudioLesson]] = [:]
        for level in StudioLevel.allCases {
            map[level] = combined.filter { $0.level == level }
        }
        return map
    }()

    /// All lessons, in learning order: Level 1, then Level 2,
    /// then Level 3. Within a level: concepts, tasks,
    /// techniques, competencies, perspectives, then orientation
    /// lessons. Evaluated once: the old computed property
    /// re-concatenated and re-filtered all 156 lessons on every
    /// access, including once per row for the gating checks.
    static let all: [StudioLesson] = StudioLevel.allCases.flatMap { lessonsByLevel[$0] ?? [] }

    /// The lessons for one level, in display order: key concepts
    /// first, then tasks, techniques, competency groups followed
    /// by their individual competencies, perspectives, then
    /// orientation lessons.
    static func lessons(for level: StudioLevel) -> [StudioLesson] {
        lessonsByLevel[level] ?? []
    }

    /// The task lessons for one knowledge area, in order.
    static func tasks(for area: StudioKnowledgeArea) -> [StudioLesson] {
        tasks.filter { $0.knowledgeArea == area }
    }
}

// MARK: - Career guides

/// One numbered step in a career guide section, adapted from the
/// staged career guides.
struct StudioGuideStep: Hashable {
    let title: String
    let action: String
    let timeframe: String
}

/// One section of a career guide: paragraphed teaching, numbered
/// steps, and key points.
struct StudioGuideSection: Hashable {
    let heading: String
    let paragraphs: [String]
    let steps: [StudioGuideStep]
    let keyPoints: [String]
}

/// One certification entry in a career guide. Names identify the
/// certifications only; the Studio shows the career guide
/// no-endorsement note beside them.
struct StudioCertification: Hashable {
    let name: String
    let body: String
    let note: String
}

/// One career guide, adapted from the staged career guides:
/// paragraphed teaching sections, skills, tools, certifications,
/// portfolio artifacts, and common mistakes. Certification names
/// are used to identify the certifications only.
struct StudioCareerGuide: Identifiable, Hashable {
    let id: String
    let title: String
    let level: StudioLevel
    let description: [String]
    let audience: String
    let timeframe: String
    let sections: [StudioGuideSection]
    let skills: [String]
    let tools: [String]
    let certifications: [StudioCertification]
    let portfolio: [String]
    let mistakes: [String]
}

extension StudioCareerGuide {
    /// All 5 career guides, in level order.
    static let all: [StudioCareerGuide] = [
    StudioCareerGuide(
        id: "how-to-become-a-business-analyst",
        title: "How to Become a Business Analyst",
        level: .foundation,
        description: [
            "This guide explains what a business analyst does, which skills to build, and how to gather proof of your work, step by step from first principles to your first role. It focuses on practical habits, clear documents, and honest preparation rather than theory alone."
        ],
        audience: "This guide is for complete beginners and career switchers from operations, support, testing, development, finance, customer service, and similar roles who want a practical route into business analysis.",
        timeframe: "Most beginners need 6 to 12 months of steady part-time study and practice to be ready to apply for a first BA role, depending on prior experience and the time available each week.",
        sections: [
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "What a business analyst actually does", paragraphs: [
            "A business analyst helps a business understand a problem, agree what should change, and describe that change clearly enough for other people to build, test, and support it. The work sits between the people who need the change and the people who deliver it. A BA listens, asks questions, checks facts, and turns messy conversations into clear, agreed records that a team can act on.",
            "What a BA delivers depends on the project, but it usually includes a clear statement of the problem, an analysis of how work happens today, options for how it could happen in future, and written requirements that describe what is needed. A BA may also produce process flows, data definitions, acceptance criteria, and notes from workshops and decisions. The value is not the document itself. The value is shared understanding, so everyone works from the same agreed picture.",
            "The role is different from project management and testing, although the three roles work closely together. A project manager plans the schedule, budget, risks, and coordination of delivery. A tester checks that what was built matches what was agreed and finds defects before release. A BA focuses on the need itself: what problem to solve, for whom, and what good looks like when the change is done.",
            "BAs work in many industries, including banking, insurance, healthcare, retail, government, and technology. In banking and payments, for example, a BA might work on how customer payments are processed, how exceptions are handled when a payment fails, or how operations teams reconcile accounts at the end of the day. The core craft stays the same across industries: understand the need, involve the right people, and describe the change clearly."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Define the role in your own words", action: "Write a short paragraph that explains what a business analyst does, in plain language, without copying a job listing. Include who a BA works with, what a BA produces, and what good looks like when the work is done well. Read it aloud and rewrite any sentence that sounds vague or copied, until a friend outside the field could understand it.", timeframe: "Weeks 1-2"), StudioGuideStep(title: "List what a BA delivers", action: "Make a list of typical BA deliverables, such as a problem statement, process flows, requirements, acceptance criteria, and workshop notes. For each item, write one sentence on what it contains and one sentence on who uses it. Keep the list for later steps, and add to it as you learn new artifact types.", timeframe: "Weeks 1-2"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Compare BA, PM, and tester roles", action: "Draw a simple three-column comparison of the BA, project manager, and tester roles, covering focus, main outputs, and how each role measures success. Add one example of a task where the roles overlap and note who leads and who supports in that case. Use the comparison to explain the difference to someone else in under two minutes.", timeframe: "Weeks 1-2"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Research where BAs work", action: "Read a range of BA job listings across different industries, including banking and payments, and note the common duties, skills, and domain knowledge they ask for. Record the patterns you see, and mark which patterns match your background and which are new to you. Use what you find to shape your learning plan, not to judge whether you belong in the role yet.", timeframe: "Weeks 2-3")], keyPoints: [
            "A BA turns messy needs into clear, agreed records a team can act on.",
            "Deliverables serve shared understanding, not paperwork for its own sake.",
            "BA, project management, and testing have different focuses and work together.",
            "The core craft transfers across industries, including banking and payments."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Start from where you are", paragraphs: [
            "Starting well means starting honestly. Before choosing courses or certifications, take stock of what you already do well and where you are less confident. Look at real tasks from your current or past work, not at job titles. A clear picture of your starting point stops you from studying topics you already know and helps you spend limited time on the gaps that matter most.",
            "Almost every previous role brings transferable skills. Operations work builds process knowledge and attention to detail. Support and customer service build listening skills, patience, and the habit of explaining things clearly. Testing builds precision and a habit of checking edge cases. Development builds comfort with systems and technical language. Finance builds care with numbers, controls, and reconciliation. Name these skills in plain terms, with examples of work you actually did, so you can use them later in your CV and interviews.",
            "Gaps are normal, and closing them in the right order matters more than closing all of them at once. Most beginners need to strengthen requirements writing, stakeholder handling, and modelling before they worry about advanced topics. Pick a small number of gaps, work on them in short focused sessions, and practise with realistic examples rather than reading alone. Progress comes from repeated practice with feedback, not from collecting notes.",
            "A learning routine only works if it fits around work and life. Short regular sessions beat occasional long sessions that leave you tired and behind. Set a fixed number of sessions per week, protect that time, and give each session one clear outcome, such as drafting one process flow or rewriting one requirement. Review your plan every few weeks, keep what helps, and change what does not."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Audit your current skills honestly", action: "List the skills you use in your current or most recent role, with a concrete example for each skill. Rate each skill honestly as strong, developing, or new, based on real work you have done rather than topics you have read about. Keep the audit private and blunt, because an honest starting point makes the rest of the plan realistic.", timeframe: "Weeks 2-3"), StudioGuideStep(title: "List transferable skills from past roles", action: "For each previous role, write down two or three skills that transfer directly to business analysis, such as interviewing, process knowledge, or careful documentation. Add one work example for each skill that you could describe in an interview. Save this list, because it becomes the evidence base for your CV later in this guide.", timeframe: "Weeks 3-4"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Pick your three biggest gaps", action: "Compare your audit with the patterns from job listings and choose the three gaps that would hold you back most in a first BA role. Write each gap as a skill to build, not as a vague worry, and note how you will practise it. Leave other gaps for later, so your effort stays focused on a small number of clear priorities.", timeframe: "Weeks 3-4"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Build a weekly learning routine", action: "Choose a fixed number of study sessions per week that you can sustain alongside work, and block that time in your calendar. Give each session one outcome, such as completing one exercise or drafting one document, and end each session by noting what you learned and what to do next. Review the routine after three weeks and adjust the timing or workload if it is not working.", timeframe: "Weeks 4-5")], keyPoints: [
            "An honest audit of current skills makes the learning plan realistic.",
            "Previous roles bring transferable skills worth naming with examples.",
            "Close a small number of gaps first, in order of importance.",
            "Short regular sessions beat occasional long sessions."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Learn the core craft", paragraphs: [
            "The core craft of business analysis is learning what people really need, not just what they first ask for. Elicitation means drawing out needs through interviews, observation, document review, and workshops, then checking what you heard with the people who said it. Good elicitation is patient and curious. It asks open questions, listens without jumping to a solution, and follows up when answers conflict or leave gaps.",
            "Stakeholder analysis helps you work out who is affected by a change, who decides, who provides knowledge, and who will live with the result. A change that helps one team can create extra work for another, and a BA needs to surface those effects early. Map stakeholders by their interest and influence, note what each person needs from the change, and plan how and when to involve each group. Keeping stakeholders involved through the work prevents late surprises.",
            "Requirements writing and modelling turn what you learn into records a team can build and test. Requirements should be clear, testable, and free of unnecessary jargon, with each requirement covering one need. Models help people see the same picture: process flows show steps and decisions, use cases show how a user interacts with a system to reach a goal, and user stories describe a need from a user point of view with the value it brings. Choose the model that helps the conversation, and keep it simple enough for non-specialists to follow.",
            "Acceptance criteria and traceability connect the need to the finished work. Acceptance criteria state the conditions that must be true for a requirement to count as done, in terms a tester can check. Traceability links each requirement back to the business need it serves and forward to the design, build, and test work that covers it. Together they help a team spot missing work, manage change, and show why each piece of work exists."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Practise elicitation interviews", action: "Practise interviewing with a friend or colleague playing the role of a stakeholder with a vague request. Ask open questions, summarise what you heard, and check your summary with the stakeholder before moving on. After each practice interview, write up the needs you captured and note which questions worked best and which missed useful detail.", timeframe: "Weeks 5-7"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Map stakeholders for a sample project", action: "Choose a realistic sample change, such as improving how a team handles failed payments, and list everyone affected by it. Group stakeholders by interest and influence, note what each group needs, and plan how you would involve each group during the work. Review the map with someone else and add any stakeholder or need you missed.", timeframe: "Weeks 6-8"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Write requirements and process flows", action: "For your sample change, draw the current process flow and a future process flow, showing steps, decisions, and handoffs between teams. Write a short set of requirements in clear, testable language, with one need per requirement, using a diagramming tool such as Lucidchart and a spreadsheet such as Excel to keep the work tidy. Ask a reviewer to read the flows and requirements without your help, and revise anything they find unclear.", timeframe: "Weeks 7-10"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Write acceptance criteria and trace links", action: "For each requirement in your sample set, write acceptance criteria that state clearly when the requirement counts as done. Build a simple traceability list that links each requirement to the business need it serves and to the test checks that would cover it. Check the list for requirements with no need behind them and needs with no requirement covering them, and fix both kinds of gap.", timeframe: "Weeks 8-10")], keyPoints: [
            "Elicitation checks what people really need, not only what they first ask for.",
            "Stakeholder analysis surfaces effects on every affected group early.",
            "Clear requirements and simple models help everyone see the same picture.",
            "Acceptance criteria and traceability link needs to finished work."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Learn how teams deliver", paragraphs: [
            "Teams deliver change in different ways, and a BA needs to adapt to the approach in use. Agile delivery breaks work into small pieces, delivers in short cycles, and welcomes change as learning grows. Scrum is one common Agile framework, with fixed-length sprints, a prioritised backlog, and regular events for planning, review, and reflection. In Agile teams, a BA often refines backlog items, clarifies needs during the sprint, and works closely with the team every day.",
            "Waterfall delivery plans the whole change in sequence, with each phase finishing before the next begins. It suits work where needs are stable, well understood, and tightly controlled, such as some regulated or large-scale changes. Many organisations use a hybrid approach, with upfront planning for scope and governance combined with iterative delivery for build and test. A BA should understand each approach well enough to adjust documents, meetings, and level of detail to fit.",
            "Working well with developers and testers is a daily part of the role. Developers need requirements that are clear about behaviour, data, rules, and edge cases, and they will ask questions that expose gaps. Testers need acceptance criteria they can turn into checks, and they often spot risks the BA missed. Answer questions promptly, record agreed decisions, and treat challenge as useful: a hard question during analysis costs far less than a defect found after release.",
            "Workshops and facilitation bring groups together to agree a need, a process, or a decision in the time available. A good facilitator prepares a clear purpose and agenda, keeps discussion on topic, draws out quieter voices, and records outcomes and actions before people leave. Facilitation is a skill that improves with practice and honest reflection. Start with small sessions, ask for feedback on how the session ran, and build up to larger or more difficult groups over time."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Learn Agile and Scrum basics", action: "Study the basics of Agile delivery and Scrum, including sprints, the product backlog, sprint planning, reviews, and retrospectives. Practise writing user stories with acceptance criteria and ordering a small backlog by value and risk, using a backlog tool such as Jira to record the work. Explain the Scrum events in your own words to someone else, and note any part you cannot yet explain clearly.", timeframe: "Weeks 9-12"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Compare Waterfall and hybrid delivery", action: "Write a short comparison of Waterfall, Agile, and hybrid delivery, covering when each fits, how requirements are handled, and how change is managed. For each approach, note what a BA produces and when, and what happens when a requirement changes late in the work. Use a real or realistic example to test your comparison, and revise it where the example does not fit.", timeframe: "Weeks 10-12"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Practise working with delivery teams", action: "Ask a developer or tester you know to review your sample requirements and raise every question or gap they can find. Answer each question in writing, record any decision you agree, and update the requirements so the agreed answer is visible in the document. Reflect on which questions surprised you, and use them to improve how you write the next set of requirements.", timeframe: "Weeks 11-14"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Run a practice workshop", action: "Plan and run a short practice workshop with two or three people, with a clear purpose, a timed agenda, and a written outcome to agree by the end. Facilitate the discussion, keep notes visible, draw out quieter participants, and close by confirming decisions and actions. Ask participants for feedback on how the session ran, and write down one facilitation habit to keep and one to improve.", timeframe: "Weeks 12-14")], keyPoints: [
            "Agile, Waterfall, and hybrid approaches need different BA habits.",
            "Scrum basics include sprints, a backlog, and regular review events.",
            "Developers and testers expose gaps that strengthen requirements.",
            "Good facilitation needs preparation, inclusion, and recorded outcomes."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Build proof you can do the work", paragraphs: [
            "Employers need proof that you can do the work, not just a list of topics you studied. A portfolio of practice artifacts shows how you think, how you write, and how you handle a realistic problem from start to finish. Build it from practice projects based on everyday processes you understand, such as handling a customer request, processing a refund, or reconciling a daily report. Keep each artifact tidy, realistic, and honest about what is practice work and what came from a real job.",
            "Your current job, if you have one, is often the best source of real practice. Volunteer for analysis tasks that others avoid, such as documenting a process, writing up meeting outcomes, clarifying a confusing request, or mapping how a handoff works between teams. Ask your manager for small, bounded tasks first, deliver them well, and build trust for larger pieces of work. Real tasks done carefully teach stakeholder handling in a way that study alone cannot.",
            "One full end-to-end example is worth more than several fragments. Pick a single realistic change and take it through every stage: the problem statement, stakeholder map, current and future process flows, requirements, acceptance criteria, and a short summary of decisions and open questions. Show your working, including what you assumed, what you checked, and what changed after feedback. A complete example lets a reviewer follow your reasoning without guessing.",
            "Feedback from a practising BA will improve your work faster than working alone. Ask a BA you know, or someone in a professional community, to review one artifact and tell you what is unclear, missing, or unrealistic. Listen for patterns in the feedback rather than defending each point, then revise the artifact and note what you changed and why. Keep the revised version in your portfolio, so your learning is visible in the work itself."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Start a practice project portfolio", action: "Create a tidy portfolio folder with a short index page that lists each artifact, the problem it addresses, and what it shows about your work. Add your best artifacts from earlier steps, cleaned up and consistently formatted, and record them in a tracking board such as Trello so you can see what is finished and what still needs work. Keep the portfolio honest by labelling practice work as practice work.", timeframe: "Weeks 13-16"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Volunteer for analysis tasks at work", action: "Ask your manager for a small, bounded analysis task, such as documenting a process, clarifying a request, or writing up the outcome of a meeting. Deliver the task on time, check your work with the people involved, and ask for feedback on clarity and usefulness. Record the task, what you produced, and what you learned, so you can add it to your portfolio and CV with permission.", timeframe: "Weeks 14-20"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Document one end-to-end example", action: "Choose one realistic change and document it fully, from problem statement and stakeholder map through process flows, requirements, acceptance criteria, and decisions. Show your working throughout, including assumptions, checks you made, and changes you made after feedback. Read the full example as a stranger would, and fix any place where the reasoning jumps or the evidence is missing.", timeframe: "Weeks 16-22"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Get feedback from a practising BA", action: "Ask a practising BA to review your end-to-end example and tell you what is unclear, missing, or unrealistic. Listen for repeated themes in the feedback, revise the example to address them, and note what you changed and why. Thank the reviewer, keep the revised version in your portfolio, and apply the same lessons to your other artifacts.", timeframe: "Weeks 18-24")], keyPoints: [
            "A portfolio proves what you can do, beyond a list of topics studied.",
            "Small real tasks at work build stakeholder skills that study cannot.",
            "One complete end-to-end example beats several fragments.",
            "Feedback from a practising BA improves work faster than working alone."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Certification, CV, and the first role", paragraphs: [
            "Certification can support your move into business analysis, but it does not replace evidence of work. An entry-level certification shows commitment and a shared vocabulary, and it can help a CV pass an initial screen. Hiring decisions still rest on what you can show and explain: your portfolio, your examples, and how you handle interview questions. Treat certification as one part of the plan, alongside practice, feedback, and real tasks, not as a guarantee of a role.",
            "Choose a first certification that fits your experience level and the time you can give to preparation. Entry-level options suit beginners with little or no BA experience, while intermediate and advanced options expect years of BA work and suit later career stages. Check the eligibility rules and syllabus for any certification you consider, and prepare by studying the topics and practising application, not by memorising answers. If a certification needs experience you do not yet have, treat it as a later goal and focus first on building that experience.",
            "A BA CV should lead with evidence. For each role, describe the analysis work you did, the problem you helped solve, and the outcome, using plain language and concrete detail. Include your portfolio artifacts, practice projects, and any volunteering or internal analysis tasks, labelled honestly. Tailor the CV to each role by matching your strongest relevant examples to the needs described in the listing, and keep it clear, short, and free of claims you cannot support in an interview.",
            "Interview preparation means turning your experience into clear situational examples. For each example, practise explaining the situation, what you did, and what happened as a result, with enough detail to show judgement without overloading the listener. Prepare examples for common themes: handling a difficult stakeholder, clarifying a vague request, managing a change to requirements, and resolving conflicting needs. In your first 90 days, listen more than you speak, learn the domain and the people, deliver small pieces of work well, and ask for feedback early so you can adjust quickly."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Choose a first certification", action: "Compare entry-level certification options against your experience, budget, and available study time, and check the eligibility rules before committing. Choose one first certification that fits your current level, and treat intermediate and advanced certifications as later goals once you have BA experience. Build a study plan that covers the syllabus through reading and applied practice, not memorisation alone.", timeframe: "Weeks 20-26"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Write a CV built on evidence", action: "Draft a CV that leads with analysis evidence, describing the work you did, the problem you helped solve, and the outcome for each role or project. Include your portfolio artifacts and practice projects, labelled honestly, and tailor the strongest examples to each role you apply for. Ask a reviewer to check the CV for vague claims, and rewrite any claim you could not support with an example in an interview.", timeframe: "Weeks 22-28"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Prepare situational interview examples", action: "Prepare five or six situational examples covering difficult stakeholders, vague requests, changed requirements, conflicting needs, and a workshop or facilitation task. For each example, practise a clear account of the situation, what you did, and what happened, with enough detail to show judgement. Rehearse the examples aloud, time them, and trim any part that does not help the listener follow your reasoning.", timeframe: "Weeks 24-30"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Plan your first 90 days", action: "Write a first 90 days plan that starts with listening, learning the domain, meeting stakeholders, and understanding how work flows today. Set small, deliverable goals for each month, such as owning one analysis task well, building one trusted relationship in each key team, and asking for feedback at regular points. Keep the plan flexible, because your first weeks will teach you what the role really needs.", timeframe: "Months 6-12")], keyPoints: [
            "Certification supports evidence of work, and does not replace it.",
            "Choose a first certification that fits your current experience level.",
            "A BA CV should lead with evidence, tailored to each role.",
            "In the first 90 days, listen, learn, deliver small wins, and seek feedback."
        ])
        ],
        skills: [
            "Elicitation and interviewing",
