        ],
        mistakes: [
            "Collecting courses and notes without practising the work leaves no proof that you can do analysis.",
            "Studying advanced topics before requirements writing, stakeholder handling, and modelling wastes limited study time.",
            "Writing vague requirements that no tester could check creates rework and confusion for the whole team.",
            "Skipping stakeholder analysis leads to late surprises from groups who were not involved early enough.",
            "Copying example documents without understanding them produces a portfolio you cannot explain in an interview.",
            "Treating certification as a guarantee of a role ignores the portfolio and interview evidence that hiring decisions rest on.",
            "Applying with a generic CV that lists duties instead of analysis evidence hides the strengths a reviewer needs to see."
        ]
    ),
    StudioCareerGuide(
        id: "how-to-become-a-payments-and-banking-ba",
        title: "How to Become a Payments and Banking BA",
        level: .practitioner,
        description: [
            "This guide shows working analysts and operations staff how to build payments and banking knowledge step by step, from payment rails and messages to controls and a portfolio that proves the specialism. Each section combines plain-language study text with practical steps you can complete alongside work."
        ],
        audience: "This guide is for working business analysts who want to specialise in payments and banking, and for payments operations, reconciliation, or support staff who want to move into a business analyst role.",
        timeframe: "A realistic range is 6 to 12 months of steady study alongside work, depending on prior experience, familiarity with banking, and the time available each week.",
        sections: [
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "The banking and payments landscape", paragraphs: [
            "Banks and payment providers work together to move money for people and businesses. A bank typically holds accounts, offers payment services, and connects to clearing and settlement systems that move funds between banks. A payment provider may offer a narrower service, such as helping a business accept payments or send payouts, and it often relies on a bank for accounts and settlement. As a business analyst, it helps to start with this simple picture: customers and businesses give instructions, banks and providers process them, and shared systems settle the movement of funds.",
            "Retail banking serves individuals and small businesses, with high numbers of relatively small payments such as salary credits, bill payments, and card purchases. Corporate banking serves larger businesses, which need bulk payments, payroll runs, supplier payments, collections, and detailed reporting for reconciliation. The same bank may serve both groups, but the products, channels, volumes, and reporting needs differ. A corporate client, for example, may send a single file containing many payment instructions, while an individual customer usually makes one payment at a time through an app or a branch.",
            "Every payment follows a life cycle, even when the steps happen quickly. It starts with an instruction that states who pays, who receives, how much, in which currency, and when value should be given. The instruction is checked for completeness, funds, limits, and controls, then processed and sent through the relevant clearing or messaging route. Settlement moves the funds between accounts, confirmation goes back to the parties involved, and reconciliation checks that records match at each stage. When any step fails or needs review, the payment becomes an exception that the operations team must investigate and resolve.",
            "A business analyst adds value by making this life cycle clear, complete, and testable. That work includes mapping current flows, writing requirements for new payment products or changes, spotting gaps between business needs and system behaviour, and helping stakeholders agree on rules for cut-offs, fees, limits, and exceptions. The analyst also supports testing by defining realistic scenarios, including failures and edge cases, and helps operations teams prepare for the change. In a bank, that clarity matters because a small gap in a requirement can affect many payments, many customers, and many downstream records."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Map the banking landscape", action: "Draw a one-page map showing customers, the bank, payment providers, and clearing and settlement systems, with arrows for instructions and funds. Label each party in plain words and add one sentence for what each party does. Keep the map simple enough to explain to a colleague in two minutes.", timeframe: "Weeks 1-2"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Compare retail and corporate needs", action: "Make a two-column comparison of retail and corporate banking needs, covering payment types, channels, volumes, and reporting. Add one example payment for each side and note where the needs differ. Use the comparison to practise explaining why one design rarely fits both groups.", timeframe: "Weeks 2-3"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Trace one payment end to end", action: "Choose one simple payment, such as a domestic transfer between accounts at different banks, and write out each step from instruction to reconciliation. Note who acts at each step, what is checked, and what happens if the step fails. Ask a colleague in operations to review the trace and correct any missing steps.", timeframe: "Weeks 3-4"), StudioGuideStep(title: "List where a BA adds value", action: "Write a short list of business analyst contributions for a payment change, such as flow mapping, requirements, gap analysis, scenario definition, and readiness support. For each contribution, add one example of what good looks like. Keep the list to use when you explain your role to stakeholders.", timeframe: "Weeks 4-5")], keyPoints: [
            "Banks hold accounts and connect to shared systems; providers often rely on banks for settlement.",
            "Retail and corporate customers need different payment products, channels, and reporting.",
            "Every payment moves from instruction through checks, clearing, settlement, and reconciliation.",
            "A BA adds value by making flows, rules, and exceptions clear and testable."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Payments rails and clearing", paragraphs: [
            "A payment rail is the route a payment travels to reach its destination, including the systems, rules, and timetables that govern the journey. Domestic clearing brings together payment instructions from different banks, checks and sorts them, and prepares them for settlement. Some systems process payments in batches at set times, while others process payments one by one in near real time. As an analyst, you need to know which rail a payment uses, because the rail decides timing, data needs, limits, and what happens when something goes wrong.",
            "Real-time payments settle quickly and are available for much of the day and week in many domestic systems. That speed changes requirements: checks must happen before release, balances and limits must be current, and exception handling must be fast because there is little time to fix a payment after it has gone. Batch payments, by contrast, are grouped and processed at agreed times, which allows more time for validation and repair but means the recipient waits longer. A good requirements pack states which model applies and what the customer and the operations team should expect at each stage.",
            "Cross-border payments often travel through correspondent banking, where banks hold accounts with each other to settle payments in currencies and places where they have no direct presence. A payment may pass through one or more intermediary banks, each checking the instruction and passing it on with the details needed for the next step. Fees, currency conversion, and time zones can affect the amount received and the date value is given. The analyst must capture these steps, the parties involved, and the information that must travel with the payment so it is not delayed or returned.",
            "Cut-off times and value dating control when a payment counts as made. A cut-off time is the latest time an instruction can be accepted for processing on a given day; an instruction received after the cut-off is normally processed on the next working day. Value dating states the date on which funds are treated as available to the recipient or debited from the sender, which may differ from the date the instruction was sent. Requirements must state cut-offs, working day rules, and value dating clearly, because customers, operations teams, and reconciliation all depend on the same dates."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Learn domestic clearing basics", action: "Write a plain-language summary of how domestic clearing works, covering instruction collection, sorting, checks, and preparation for settlement. Include batch and single-payment processing and note when each is used. Test your summary by explaining it without notes to a colleague.", timeframe: "Weeks 5-6"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Compare real-time and batch payments", action: "Build a comparison table for real-time and batch payments covering speed, availability, checks, limits, and exception handling. Add one requirement difference for each row, such as what must be validated before release. Use the table when you review requirements for a new payment product.", timeframe: "Weeks 6-7"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Trace a cross-border payment", action: "Draw the journey of a cross-border payment through the sending bank, any intermediary banks, and the receiving bank. Label the accounts used for settlement, the checks at each step, and the details that must travel with the payment. Note where fees, currency conversion, and time zones affect the outcome.", timeframe: "Weeks 7-8"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Explain cut-offs and value dating", action: "Write three worked examples showing how cut-off times and value dates affect a payment sent in the morning, late in the day, and before a non-working day. State the processing date and value date for each example in plain words. Share the examples with the operations team and correct them from feedback.", timeframe: "Weeks 8-9")], keyPoints: [
            "The payment rail decides timing, data, limits, and exception handling.",
            "Real-time payments need checks before release; batch payments allow more repair time.",
            "Cross-border payments may pass through intermediary banks that hold settlement accounts.",
            "Cut-off times and value dates must be stated clearly in every payment requirement."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Messaging: SWIFT and ISO 20022", paragraphs: [
            "Payment messages carry the structured details that systems need to process a payment without manual retyping. A message states the sender and receiver, the amount and currency, account details, dates, charges, and any extra information the recipient needs for reconciliation. If a detail is missing, unclear, or in the wrong field, the payment can be delayed, repaired by hand, or returned. The analyst must understand what each message is for, what it must contain, and how systems use each field.",
            "The MT family is the older generation of structured financial messages, written in a compact format with coded fields. The MX family is the newer generation based on ISO 20022 and written in XML, which carries richer and more clearly labelled data. In plain terms, MT messages say the same kinds of things as MX messages, but MX messages organise the data more clearly and allow more detail, such as structured addresses and richer remittance information. Learning programmes offered by standards bodies can help here, but focus first on reading real message structures and understanding what each part means.",
            "Two messages are worth learning early. The pain.001 message is a customer-to-bank instruction that starts a credit transfer, carrying the payment details the bank needs to act. The pacs.008 message is a bank-to-bank instruction for a customer credit transfer, carrying the details from one bank to the next so the payment can be processed and settled. When you read either message, start with the parties, amount, currency, dates, and accounts, then look at charges, purpose, and remittance details. A statement message such as camt.053 then reports account activity back to the customer, which supports reconciliation.",
            "Mapping fields between old and new formats is core payments analysis work. A mapping shows which field in the old message feeds which field in the new message, what transformation is needed, and what to do when there is no direct match. For example, a customer credit transfer sent as an MT103 maps in the new world to a pacs.008, and the analyst must confirm that every needed detail survives the move. The migration from MT to MX is therefore not only a technical change: it needs business decisions about data quality, structured addresses, character sets, and how to handle information that has no exact equivalent. Requirements must cover mandatory fields, optional fields, validation rules, and fallback handling, and testing must prove that mapped messages still produce the right payment outcome."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Learn what a message carries", action: "List the details a payment message must carry, grouped as parties, amount and currency, accounts, dates, charges, and remittance information. For each group, note what goes wrong if the detail is missing or wrong. Keep the list as a reference when you read any payment message.", timeframe: "Weeks 9-10"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Read a pain.001 and a pacs.008", action: "Obtain sample structures for a pain.001 and a pacs.008 from training material or a test environment and read them field by field. Identify the parties, amount, currency, dates, accounts, and remittance details in each message. Write a plain-language summary of what each message does and who sends it to whom.", timeframe: "Weeks 10-11"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Practise mapping old to new fields", action: "Build a field mapping in Excel with columns for the source field, the target field, the transformation rule, and the handling when no match exists. Map a small set of fields from an MT message to its MX equivalent and add validation notes for each row. Ask a technical colleague to review the mapping for gaps and unclear rules.", timeframe: "Weeks 11-13"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Summarise the MT to MX migration", action: "Write a two-page summary of the MT to MX migration in plain language, covering why the change is happening, what data improves, and what decisions the business must make. Include data quality, structured addresses, and fallback handling for unmatched information. Use the summary to explain the migration to a non-technical stakeholder.", timeframe: "Weeks 12-14")], keyPoints: [
            "Messages carry structured payment details; missing or wrong fields cause delays and returns.",
            "MT is the older compact format; MX is the ISO 20022 format with richer, clearer data.",
            "pain.001 starts a customer credit transfer; pacs.008 carries it between banks.",
            "Mapping must cover transformations, validation, and handling for unmatched fields."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Cards, settlement, and liquidity", paragraphs: [
            "A card payment has three main stages: authorisation, clearing, and settlement. Authorisation checks, at the time of purchase, whether the card is valid, whether funds or credit are available, and whether fraud checks pass. Clearing sends the transaction details from the accepting side to the issuing side so the amounts can be checked and agreed. Settlement then moves funds between accounts to pay for the cleared transactions, and the cardholder sees the transaction on a statement.",
            "Settlement accounts and funding keep this movement orderly. Banks and providers hold settlement accounts where cleared amounts are collected and paid, and they must fund those accounts so payments can settle on time. If funding is short, settlement can be delayed, which affects merchants, customers, and downstream reconciliation. Requirements for card and payment changes must state settlement timing, funding responsibilities, and what happens when funds are not available as expected.",
            "Liquidity is the ability to meet payment obligations when they fall due, and intraday positions show how that ability changes during the day. As payments go out and come in, balances move, and the operations and treasury teams watch positions to avoid shortfalls. A payment that settles early in the day uses funds differently from one that settles late, and large or unexpected payments can change the position quickly. The analyst does not need to manage liquidity directly, but must understand timing, cut-offs, and funding needs well enough to write requirements that do not create avoidable strain.",
            "Reconciliation checks that records match across systems, accounts, and external reports, and exception handling deals with the breaks. Common breaks include missing transactions, amount differences, duplicate records, and timing differences where one side has recorded an item and the other has not yet done so. Good exception handling defines how breaks are found, who investigates them, what evidence is needed, and how long resolution should take. The analyst helps by mapping reconciliation points, defining break categories, and writing clear rules for investigation and correction."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Draw a card payment flow", action: "Draw a flow showing authorisation, clearing, and settlement for one card purchase, with the cardholder, merchant, and banks shown as separate parties. Label the checks at authorisation and the records created at each stage. Walk through the flow with a colleague and fix any steps that are out of order.", timeframe: "Weeks 14-15"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Explain settlement and funding", action: "Write a short explanation of settlement accounts and funding, covering where cleared amounts collect, who funds the account, and when settlement happens. Add one example of what happens if funding is short. Keep the language simple enough for a stakeholder who has not worked in operations.", timeframe: "Weeks 15-16"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Describe intraday liquidity simply", action: "Write a one-page note explaining intraday liquidity in plain terms, using a simple timeline of payments in and out during a day. Show how timing and large payments change the position and why cut-offs matter. Ask someone in operations or treasury to check the note for accuracy.", timeframe: "Weeks 16-17"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Work through reconciliation breaks", action: "Collect five example break types, such as a missing item, an amount difference, a duplicate, a timing difference, and a fee difference, and write the investigation steps for each. State the evidence needed, the owner, and the correction action for every type. Build the examples into a checklist in Excel for use during testing.", timeframe: "Weeks 17-19")], keyPoints: [
            "Card payments move through authorisation, clearing, and settlement.",
            "Settlement accounts must be funded on time or settlement is delayed.",
            "Intraday positions change as payments arrive and leave during the day.",
            "Reconciliation finds breaks; exception handling defines owners, evidence, and fixes."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Compliance, risk, and controls", paragraphs: [
            "Anti-money-laundering rules require banks to understand their customers, monitor activity for unusual patterns, and report concerns through proper channels. Sanctions screening checks payment parties and details against lists of restricted persons, organisations, and places, and payments that match must be held for review. These checks happen alongside payment processing, so requirements must state when screening occurs, what happens to a held payment, and who reviews it. The analyst must treat these controls as part of the payment flow, not as an extra step added at the end.",
            "Fraud controls protect customers and the bank from unauthorised or deceptive payments. Controls include authentication checks, transaction limits, velocity checks that spot unusual frequency, and monitoring rules that flag patterns for review. Each control needs clear rules: what triggers it, what action follows, how the customer is told, and how a genuine payment is released without undue delay. Requirements must balance protection with customer experience, and must state how controls are tested without exposing real customer data.",
            "Audit trails and evidence prove what happened, when it happened, and who acted. A good audit trail records the instruction, checks performed, decisions made, changes to payment details, and confirmations sent, in a form that can be retrieved later. Evidence also includes approved requirements, test results, and records of stakeholder agreement, which show that a change was properly understood and checked. The analyst helps by defining what must be logged, how long records are kept under internal policy, and how reviewers can find the evidence they need.",
            "Regulation shapes requirements because payment products must follow the rules that apply in each place where they operate. A new feature may need extra customer checks, clearer disclosures, stronger controls, or different handling for certain payment types. Working well with compliance and risk teams means involving them early, asking what rules apply, and writing requirements that show how each rule is met. Early involvement avoids late rework, when a missing control or a misunderstood rule forces a design to change after build has started."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Learn screening concepts simply", action: "Write plain-language definitions for customer checks, transaction monitoring, sanctions screening, and payment holds. For each term, add one example of how it affects a payment in flight. Review the definitions with a compliance colleague and correct any loose wording.", timeframe: "Weeks 19-20"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Map fraud controls to payment steps", action: "Take one payment flow and mark where each fraud control applies, such as authentication, limits, velocity checks, and monitoring rules. For each control, state the trigger, the action, and how a genuine payment is released. Note what must be tested for each control without using real customer data.", timeframe: "Weeks 20-21"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Define audit evidence for a change", action: "For a sample payment change, list the audit evidence required, including logs, decisions, approvals, and test results. State what each item must show, who can retrieve it, and how it will be stored under internal policy. Share the list with a risk colleague and add any missing evidence types.", timeframe: "Weeks 21-22"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Practise working with control teams", action: "Draft a short set of questions to ask compliance and risk teams at the start of a payment change, covering applicable rules, required controls, and evidence needs. Practise the conversation with a colleague playing the reviewer role and note where your questions were unclear. Refine the question set into a reusable checklist for future changes.", timeframe: "Weeks 22-24")], keyPoints: [
            "Screening and monitoring are part of the payment flow, not an afterthought.",
            "Fraud controls need clear triggers, actions, and release paths for genuine payments.",
            "Audit trails must show what happened, when, and who acted.",
            "Involve compliance and risk teams early to avoid late rework."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Build the specialism", paragraphs: [
            "A payments portfolio proves the specialism better than a list of courses. It should show real analytical work, even when the examples are anonymised or built as practice exercises: a message mapping, a payment flow diagram, an exception case write-up, and a requirements pack for a payment change. Each item should be clean, clearly labelled, and explained in plain language, so a reviewer can see your thinking without needing a guided tour. Certificates help, but evidence of domain work matters more than certificates when a hiring manager judges whether you can do payments analysis.",
            "Learning from operations teams and real cases builds judgement that theory alone cannot give. Operations staff see where payments fail, which exceptions recur, and which requirements were unclear in practice, and their stories teach the details that matter. Ask to sit with the operations team, review anonymised exception cases, and trace how each case was investigated and resolved. Keep notes on patterns you see, such as repeated data errors or unclear cut-off rules, and turn the patterns into better requirements and better test scenarios.",
            "Credibility in payments comes from precision with terms, checking facts, and admitting what you do not yet know. Use each term the same way every time, confirm field meanings and timetables before writing them into requirements, and ask when a stakeholder uses a term loosely. Small errors, such as mixing up clearing and settlement or misstating a value date rule, damage trust quickly because payment work depends on exact detail. Build the habit of verifying every payment fact against a reliable source and recording where you confirmed it.",
            "Landing the first payments business analyst role usually starts close to where you already are: a payments team in your current organisation, a project that needs payments knowledge, or a role that values operations experience. Use the portfolio to show what you can do, talk about real cases and flows in interviews, and be clear about the rails, messages, and controls you understand. Once in role, keep growing by taking on harder mappings, larger changes, and closer work with compliance, operations, and technical teams, and by keeping your portfolio current as your experience deepens."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Build a message mapping sample", action: "Create a polished mapping sample in Excel covering a small set of fields from an MT message to its MX equivalent, with transformation rules and validation notes. Add a one-page cover note explaining the purpose, scope, and decisions in the mapping. Treat the sample as a portfolio piece and check every field meaning before finalising it.", timeframe: "Months 6-7"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Write up three exception cases", action: "Write up three anonymised exception cases, each covering what went wrong, how it was found, how it was investigated, and how it was fixed. Remove all real customer and account details and use generic parties throughout. Add a lessons section to each case showing what requirement or check would prevent a repeat.", timeframe: "Months 6-8"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Build credibility habits", action: "Start a personal glossary of payments terms with your own plain-language definitions and one example for each term. Review the glossary weekly, correct loose definitions, and check new terms against a reliable source before using them in requirements. Share the glossary with a peer and invite corrections.", timeframe: "Months 7-9"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Plan the move into a payments role", action: "Write a short plan naming the payments teams, projects, or roles you will target, the portfolio items you will show, and the gaps you will close first. For each gap, set a practical next step with a date, such as completing a flow diagram or shadowing the operations team. Review the plan monthly and update it as you complete items and learn more.", timeframe: "Months 8-10")], keyPoints: [
            "A portfolio of mappings, flows, and case write-ups proves payments ability.",
            "Operations experience and real exception cases build practical judgement.",
            "Precision with terms and checked facts build credibility fast.",
            "Target roles close to your current experience and grow from there."
        ])
        ],
        skills: [
            "Payment life cycle knowledge",
            "Payment message reading",
            "Field mapping and data analysis",
            "Process modelling and flow diagrams",
            "Requirements writing for payment changes",
            "Reconciliation and exception analysis",
            "Risk and control thinking",
            "Stakeholder interviewing and workshops",
            "Testing payment scenarios and edge cases",
            "Clear writing with precise payment terms"
        ],
        tools: [
            "Excel",
            "SQL",
            "Jira",
            "Confluence",
            "Visio",
            "Lucidchart",
            "Postman",
            "Power BI",
            "Tableau",
            "Miro"
        ],
        certifications: [StudioCertification(name: "CCBA", body: "IIBA", note: "Suits analysts with some business analysis experience who want a recognised foundation before specialising further in payments."), StudioCertification(name: "CBAP", body: "IIBA", note: "Suits experienced analysts who want an advanced credential after building several years of business analysis experience."), StudioCertification(name: "PMI-PBA", body: "PMI", note: "Suits analysts who work closely with project teams and want to evidence business analysis within a project delivery setting.")],
        portfolio: [
            "Payment life cycle flow diagram",
            "Message field mapping sample",
            "Card payment flow diagram",
            "Exception handling case write-up",
            "Reconciliation break analysis example",
            "Requirements pack for a payment change",
            "Controls and audit evidence checklist"
        ],
        mistakes: [
            "Learning message names without understanding what each field means in a real payment.",
            "Using payments terms loosely and mixing up clearing, settlement, and authorisation.",
            "Skipping operations experience and trying to learn payments from theory alone.",
            "Treating compliance checks as an afterthought instead of part of the requirements from the start.",
            "Writing requirements without cut-off times, value dates, and exception paths.",
            "Collecting certificates without building portfolio evidence that shows payments work.",
            "Assuming one payment type explains all payment types, instead of comparing rails and flows."
        ]
    ),
    StudioCareerGuide(
        id: "how-to-become-a-product-owner",
        title: "How to Become a Product Owner",
        level: .practitioner,
        description: [
            "This guide explains what a Product Owner owns, how to build backlog and stakeholder skills, and how to move into the role with proof of work. It covers the move from business analysis, value based ordering, and the first steps in the role."
        ],
        audience: "This guide is for business analysts moving into the Product Owner role, for testers, developers, and support and operations staff aiming at the role, and for new Product Owners finding their feet.",
        timeframe: "Most learners need three to six months of steady part time study and practice to be ready to apply for Product Owner roles, depending on prior experience and time available.",
        sections: [
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "What a Product Owner actually owns", paragraphs: [
            "A Product Owner is accountable for the product backlog and for the value the product delivers. That accountability means the Product Owner decides what gets built next, what waits, and what does not get built at all. The backlog is not a wish list. It is an ordered list that reflects judgement about value, risk, learning, and the needs of the people who use the product.",
            "Owning decisions is different from writing tickets. Writing a clear ticket matters, but the core of the role is deciding why an item matters, how it fits the wider goal, and whether it is worth doing now. A Product Owner who only records other people's requests has not taken ownership. Ownership shows when the Product Owner can explain each ordering choice in plain language and can change the order when new evidence appears.",
            "The role works alongside a Scrum Master and a development team, and each role has a different focus. The Scrum Master helps the team work well and remove obstacles. The development team decides how to build the work. The Product Owner decides what the work should achieve and in which order it should be tackled. In payments and banking work, this separation matters because the Product Owner often carries the conversation with operations, risk, and compliance colleagues while the team focuses on building a safe and workable change.",
            "Good looks ordinary from the outside. The backlog is ready for the next sprint without being over specified. Stakeholders know what is coming and why. The team gets quick answers and honest feedback. The Product Owner spends time with users, reads feedback, checks what happened after a release, and adjusts the plan. The work is steady rather than dramatic, and the value comes from many small, well judged decisions."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Write down what the Product Owner decides", action: "Write a one page list of the decisions a Product Owner owns, including backlog order, acceptance of work, and release scope. For each decision, write one sentence on what good judgement looks like. Keep the list where you can revisit it as you learn, and update it when your understanding changes.", timeframe: "Weeks 1-2"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Map the backlog to value, not to requests", action: "Take five real or fictional requests and sort them by the value they would deliver, not by who asked first. Write one sentence of reasoning for each position in the order. Share the order with a colleague and ask them to challenge your reasoning.", timeframe: "Weeks 1-2"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Observe how the role works with the team", action: "Observe or read about how a Product Owner, a Scrum Master, and a development team work together during a sprint. Note who decides what, and where the boundaries sit. Write down two questions you would ask to clarify the boundaries in your own workplace.", timeframe: "Weeks 2-3"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Describe a good Product Owner day in writing", action: "Write a short description of a good Product Owner day, hour by hour, including time with users, time with the team, and time reviewing feedback. Compare it with how you spend your day now. Identify one habit to start and one habit to reduce.", timeframe: "Weeks 2-3")], keyPoints: [
            "The Product Owner is accountable for the backlog and for value.",
            "Owning decisions matters more than writing tickets.",
            "The Product Owner, Scrum Master, and development team have different focuses.",
            "Good looks like a ready backlog, quick answers, and learning after release."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "The BA to PO path", paragraphs: [
            "Business analysts bring a strong starting point to this role. Elicitation, modelling, stakeholder work, and careful thinking about requirements all transfer directly. An analyst who can listen well, ask precise questions, and explain a complex process in simple terms already has much of the craft a Product Owner needs. In banking and payments, knowledge of how money moves, where controls sit, and what operations teams need day to day is also valuable, because it helps the Product Owner judge what matters and what is risky.",
            "What changes is who makes the final call. As an analyst, you often present options and recommend a course of action while someone else decides. As a Product Owner, you own the decision and the ordering that follows from it. You also own saying no, or not yet, when a request does not serve the product goal. That shift can feel uncomfortable at first, especially when the request comes from a senior stakeholder or a loud voice in the room.",
            "Some analyst habits need to be unlearned, or at least loosened. Trying to specify every detail before work starts slows the team down and leaves little room for learning. Waiting for perfect agreement before deciding also slows delivery. A Product Owner needs enough clarity to start, a way to learn quickly, and the willingness to revisit a decision when the evidence changes. Detail still matters, but it is added where it reduces risk, not everywhere by default.",
            "You do not need the title before you practise ownership. Volunteer to own the ordering of a small backlog, a set of defects, or a list of small improvements. Make the order visible, explain your reasons, and invite challenge. Ask for feedback on your decisions after a release or a sprint. People moving from testing, development, support, or operations can do the same by owning one area, learning how users experience it, and showing that they can decide, explain, and learn from the result."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "List the BA skills that transfer directly", action: "List your analysis skills and mark which ones transfer directly, such as elicitation, process modelling, and stakeholder management. Add an example of each skill from your own work. Use the list to write a short paragraph on why your background fits the Product Owner role.", timeframe: "Weeks 3-4"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Practise making a decision and recording it", action: "Choose a real decision you can make this week, such as the order of a small list of improvements. Make the decision, record your reasons in writing, and tell the people affected. After a week, review whether the decision held up and note what you learned.", timeframe: "Weeks 3-4"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Spot habits that keep you in analysis mode", action: "Review a recent piece of analysis and highlight where you added detail that did not change the decision. Rewrite the same material with only the detail needed to start safely. Ask a colleague whether the shorter version is clear enough to act on.", timeframe: "Weeks 4-5"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Take ownership of one small area end to end", action: "Volunteer to own one small area end to end, such as a set of defects, a help page, or a list of small fixes. Keep the order visible, explain changes, and report on what happened after each change. Treat questions and pushback as practice for stakeholder conversations.", timeframe: "Weeks 4-6")], keyPoints: [
            "Elicitation, modelling, and stakeholder work transfer directly.",
            "The change is owning decisions, ordering, and saying no.",
            "Loosen the habit of specifying everything before starting.",
            "Practise ownership on a small area before holding the title."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Backlog craft", paragraphs: [
            "Backlog craft is the daily work of turning needs into items a team can build and test. A good item states who it is for, what they need to do, and why it matters. It is small enough to finish within a sprint and clear enough that the team can discuss it without guessing. The story is a starting point for conversation, not a contract that removes the need to talk.",
            "Acceptance criteria describe how you will know the item works. Write them as checks that can be observed, using concrete behaviour rather than vague words. For a payments example, a criterion might describe what the user sees after a payment is submitted, what happens when a required field is missing, and what record is kept for later checking. Criteria like these help the team, help testers, and give the Product Owner a fair basis for accepting the work.",
            "Large items need splitting before they can be built well. Split by user need, by a single workflow step, or by a simple version first with extra cases later. Avoid splitting by technical layer alone, because that often leaves nothing usable until every layer is done. Each split item should still deliver something real, even if it is a narrow slice of the whole.",
            "Ordering is a judgement about value and risk, not a queue in arrival order. Ask what reduces the largest risk, what teaches the most, what unblocks other work, and what users need soonest. Refinement keeps the backlog ready by discussing items before the sprint, asking questions early, and removing items that no longer matter. Keep near term items clear and detailed, and keep later items light, so effort goes where it helps most."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Rewrite vague requests as clear user stories", action: "Rewrite three vague requests as user stories that state who the user is, what they need, and why it matters. Remove jargon and keep each story to two or three sentences. Ask someone unfamiliar with the work to read them and tell you what is still unclear.", timeframe: "Weeks 5-6"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Add acceptance criteria that can be checked", action: "Add three to five acceptance criteria to each story you rewrote, written as checks that can be observed. Include a normal case, an error case, and an edge case where one exists. Check that each criterion could be tested without further explanation from you.", timeframe: "Weeks 5-7"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Split one large item into smaller usable items", action: "Take one large item and split it into three or four smaller items, each delivering something usable on its own. Use a backlog tool such as Jira to record the split and link the smaller items to the original need. Review the split with a colleague and remove any item that adds no value alone.", timeframe: "Weeks 6-7"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Order a practice backlog and explain the order", action: "Order a practice backlog of ten items by value and risk, and write a short reason for the top three positions. Move one item up and one item down, and write down what would need to change for that new order to be right. Keep the exercise to one page so the reasoning stays sharp.", timeframe: "Weeks 6-8")], keyPoints: [
            "A good item states who it is for, what they need, and why.",
            "Acceptance criteria describe observable checks.",
            "Split large items into small, usable slices.",
            "Order by value and risk, and refine just enough, just in time."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Working with the team and stakeholders", paragraphs: [
            "Sprint events give the Product Owner regular points to listen, decide, and learn. In sprint planning, the Product Owner explains the goal and the most important items, and answers questions so the team can plan with confidence. In the daily standup, the Product Owner listens for blocks and questions, and offers help or decisions without taking over the meeting. In the sprint review, the Product Owner shows what was built, gathers feedback, and discusses what should change. In the retrospective, the Product Owner listens to how the team worked and agrees improvements together.",
            "Fast feedback is one of the most valuable things a Product Owner gives the team. When a question waits for days, work stalls or the team guesses. When feedback arrives quickly and clearly, the team can adjust while the work is still fresh. If you cannot answer at once, say when you will answer and keep to that time. A short, honest answer is better than a long silence.",
            "Stakeholders will bring competing demands, and many of them will be reasonable on their own. The Product Owner manages this by keeping one visible order, explaining the reasons behind it, and showing what a change would displace. Invite stakeholders to discuss the trade off rather than arguing item by item. When people understand the goal and the limits on time, they are more willing to accept that their item must wait.",
            "Trust with the delivery team grows from consistency. Say what you mean, keep decisions stable unless new evidence appears, and protect the team from constant changes of direction during a sprint. Credit the team for the work, share user feedback directly, and admit it plainly when a decision did not work out. Teams work best with a Product Owner who is present, prepared, and willing to learn alongside them."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Learn the purpose of each sprint event", action: "For each sprint event, write one paragraph on its purpose and what the Product Owner contributes. Include sprint planning, the daily standup, the sprint review, and the retrospective. Use this as a checklist before your next sprint, and note where you added value in each event.", timeframe: "Weeks 7-9"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Practise giving feedback within one working day", action: "For one sprint, answer every team question within one working day, or reply within that time with a clear time for a full answer. Keep a simple log of questions, answers, and waiting time. At the end of the sprint, review the log and identify questions you could have answered earlier.", timeframe: "Weeks 7-9"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Handle competing requests with a shared order", action: "When two stakeholders ask for conflicting work, put both requests in one visible order and explain the trade off in plain language. Show what would be delayed if the order changed. Record the agreed order and the reasons, and share it with both stakeholders.", timeframe: "Weeks 8-10"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Build trust by closing the loop with the team", action: "After each sprint, share user feedback with the team promptly and name what the team did well. Follow up on any promise you made during the sprint, even if the answer is that the work must wait. Ask the team for one thing you could do differently to help them next sprint.", timeframe: "Weeks 8-10")], keyPoints: [
            "Use each sprint event for its own purpose.",
            "Give fast, clear feedback, or say when it will come.",
            "Manage competing demands with one visible order.",
            "Build trust through consistency and follow through."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Value, outcomes, and saying no", paragraphs: [
            "Outputs are what you ship. Outcomes are what changes for users or for the business as a result. A new screen, a new report, or a faster process is an output. Fewer failed payments, less manual rework for operations, or faster answers for customers are outcomes. A Product Owner should define the outcome before the work starts, so the team knows what success looks like beyond finishing the build.",
            "Judging value does not need complex methods. Ask who benefits, how much it matters to them, what happens if the work is delayed, and what risk it removes. Look for simple evidence, such as user complaints, support contacts, manual workarounds, failed transactions, or time spent on a task. Use that evidence to compare items, and be honest when the evidence is thin. A decision based on stated assumptions is better than a decision based on the loudest request.",
            "Prioritisation is always a trade off. Choosing one item means delaying another, and the Product Owner should make that cost visible. Consider value, urgency, risk, effort, and learning together, rather than relying on a single score. Sometimes the right choice is a small item that removes a large risk. Sometimes it is a larger item that unlocks several later items. Explain the reasoning in plain language so others can challenge it fairly.",
            "Saying no is part of protecting value. Decline with a reason tied to the product goal, offer an alternative where one exists, and say what would need to change for the answer to change. After a release, check whether the intended outcome happened, share what was learned, and adjust the backlog. Learning from releases, including releases that missed the mark, is how judgement improves over time."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Rewrite output goals as outcome goals", action: "Take three output goals, such as shipping a new screen, and rewrite each one as an outcome goal that describes what changes for users. Add a simple way to observe whether the outcome happened. Discuss the rewritten goals with a colleague and refine any that are still vague.", timeframe: "Weeks 9-11"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Judge value with simple questions and evidence", action: "Choose five backlog items and judge each one with four questions: who benefits, how much it matters, what happens if it waits, and what risk it removes. Use simple evidence such as support contacts, manual workarounds, or user comments. Rank the items and write one sentence on the evidence behind each rank.", timeframe: "Weeks 9-11"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Make a trade-off decision and explain it plainly", action: "Pick a real trade off, such as a new feature against fixing a painful defect, and decide which comes first. Write a short explanation covering value, risk, effort, and learning. Share the explanation with a colleague and revise it until a non specialist can follow the reasoning.", timeframe: "Weeks 10-12"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Decline a request with reasons and an alternative", action: "Practise declining a request by writing a reply that gives the reason, ties it to the product goal, and offers an alternative or a later date. State what would need to change for the answer to change. Read the reply aloud and remove any wording that sounds dismissive or unclear.", timeframe: "Weeks 10-12")], keyPoints: [
            "Define outcomes, not just outputs, before work starts.",
            "Judge value with simple questions and real evidence.",
            "Make trade offs visible when you choose an order.",
            "Decline with reasons and alternatives, then learn from releases."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Certification, proof, and landing the role", paragraphs: [
            "Two well known entry certifications are CSPO and PSPO. CSPO is earned by completing a training course with an approved trainer, and it suits learners who want guided learning with discussion and examples. PSPO is earned by passing an assessment, and it suits learners who prefer self study and are comfortable preparing on their own. Check the current format, cost, and renewal requirements from the official source before you commit, because these details change over time.",
            "Certification supports your move into the role, but it does not replace evidence of work. Hiring managers look for proof that you can order a backlog, make decisions, work with a team, and learn from outcomes. A certificate on its own does not show those things. Use certification to build knowledge and shared language, and use a portfolio to show how you apply that knowledge in practice. For analysts, a BA certification such as CBAP can also support the path by evidencing analysis depth, but the same rule applies: evidence of ownership matters more than any single credential.",
            "Build a small portfolio that a hiring manager can read quickly. Include an ordered backlog example with acceptance criteria, a simple roadmap, and a short release story that explains a decision, what happened, and what you learned. Use fictional or anonymised examples if your real work cannot be shared, and make clear what you decided and why. Keep each artifact short, clean, and focused on judgement rather than volume.",
            "In interviews, prepare stories about real decisions, including one that did not work out and what you changed as a result. Practise explaining ordering choices, trade offs, and how you handled a difficult stakeholder request. Once in role, use the first 90 days to listen, learn the product and its users, build trust with the team, and make the backlog visible. Avoid large changes in the first weeks. Earn credibility through steady decisions, clear communication, and follow through on what you promise."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Compare CSPO and PSPO before choosing one", action: "Compare CSPO and PSPO on learning style, format, cost, and time needed, using the official sources for current details. Choose the option that fits how you learn best and the time you have available. Set a study plan with weekly goals and a target date, and record why you chose that option.", timeframe: "Weeks 11-13"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Build a small portfolio with three artifacts", action: "Build three portfolio artifacts: an ordered backlog example, a simple roadmap, and a short release story. Use fictional or anonymised examples where real work cannot be shared. Keep each artifact to one or two pages and make your decisions and reasoning easy to find.", timeframe: "Weeks 12-14"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Prepare interview stories about real decisions", action: "Prepare five interview stories covering an ordering decision, a trade off, a difficult stakeholder request, a release that taught you something, and a time you changed your mind. Practise telling each story in under three minutes, covering the situation, your decision, and the result. Ask a colleague to listen and note where your reasoning is unclear.", timeframe: "Weeks 13-16"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Plan your first 90 days as a Product Owner", action: "In your first 90 days, listen first, learn the product, meet users and stakeholders, and make the backlog visible. Set up regular feedback loops with the team and agree how questions will be answered. Avoid large changes in the first weeks, and earn trust through steady decisions and follow through.", timeframe: "First 90 days in role")], keyPoints: [
            "Choose CSPO or PSPO based on how you learn best.",
            "Certification supports, but does not replace, evidence of work.",
            "Show judgement through a small, focused portfolio.",
            "Use the first 90 days to listen, learn, and earn trust."
        ])
        ],
        skills: [
