        mistakes: [
            "The Product Owner writes tickets all day while avoiding the ordering and value decisions the role exists to make.",
            "The Product Owner orders the backlog by who shouted loudest instead of by value and risk.",
            "The Product Owner over specifies items months ahead instead of refining near term work and leaving later items light.",
            "The Product Owner leaves team questions unanswered for days, so the team guesses or stalls.",
            "The Product Owner says yes to every stakeholder request, so the backlog grows and nothing has a clear priority.",
            "The Product Owner measures success by output shipped instead of checking whether the intended outcome happened.",
            "The Product Owner relies on a certificate alone instead of building a portfolio that shows real decisions and learning.",
            "The Product Owner changes direction constantly during a sprint, so the team loses trust and momentum."
        ]
    ),
    StudioCareerGuide(
        id: "how-to-become-a-product-manager",
        title: "How to Become a Product Manager",
        level: .practitioner,
        description: [
            "This guide explains what product managers do, the skills the role needs, and practical steps to move into product management from business analysis, Product Owner work, or another role."
        ],
        audience: "This guide is for business analysts, Product Owners, and people in engineering, design, marketing, operations, or customer support who want to move into product management.",
        timeframe: "A realistic preparation period is around six to eighteen months of steady learning and practice, depending on prior experience and the time available each week.",
        sections: [
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "What product management is", paragraphs: [
            "A product manager is accountable for deciding what a product should do, who it is for, and what success looks like. The role brings together user needs, business goals, technical possibilities, and design choices into a clear direction that a team can follow. Product managers usually do not build the product themselves. They work through engineers, designers, data specialists, and other colleagues, and they stay accountable for the outcome even when they do not control every part of the work.",
            "Business analysis, Product Owner work, and product management overlap, and the boundaries vary between organisations. A business analyst often focuses on understanding a business problem, working with stakeholders, and defining what a change must achieve. A Product Owner often focuses on ordering and clarifying work for one delivery team over the short term. A product manager holds a wider view across the whole product life cycle, from early discovery through launch, measurement, and later improvement or retirement, and is accountable for whether the product achieves its intended outcomes.",
            "Accountability for outcomes means more than delivering a list of features. It means checking whether users can complete their tasks, whether the product solves the problem it set out to solve, and whether the business result follows. When evidence shows a different direction is needed, the product manager is expected to raise it, explain the trade-off, and help the team change course. This responsibility continues after launch, when real use, feedback, and measures show what is working and what needs attention.",
            "Product management roles differ a great deal in practice. Some roles focus on products used by the public, others on products used by other businesses, and others on internal tools used by employees. Some roles are close to technology and data, while others are close to customers, operations, or commercial decisions. In banking and payments, for example, a customer facing payment product and an internal operations tool serve different users and carry different risks and constraints, but both need the same core work: understand the problem, choose a direction, align the people involved, and learn from results."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Describe the role in your own words", action: "Write a short paragraph that explains what a product manager does, in plain language, without copying a job description. Include who the users are, what decisions the role owns, and how success would be recognised. Compare your version with two or three real role descriptions and note where your understanding needs to grow.", timeframe: "Weeks 1-2"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Map PM, BA, and Product Owner work", action: "Draw a simple table with three columns, one for product management, one for business analysis, and one for Product Owner work. For a product or change you know well, list the tasks, decisions, and time horizons that belong in each column. Mark the overlaps honestly, and note which tasks you already do and which are new to you.", timeframe: "Weeks 1-2"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Trace one product through its life cycle", action: "Choose a product you use regularly and trace its journey from first idea to current use, including launch, changes, and any features that were removed. For each stage, note what problem was being solved, who benefited, and what evidence might have guided the decision. Write a short summary of what the product manager would have been accountable for at each stage.", timeframe: "Weeks 2-3"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Compare PM roles across product types", action: "Find role descriptions for three different kinds of product roles, such as a public facing product, a business product, and an internal tool. Compare their users, decisions, skills, and measures of success, and note what is shared and what differs. Use the comparison to decide which type of role fits your background and interests best for a first move.", timeframe: "Weeks 2-4")], keyPoints: [
            "Product managers are accountable for outcomes, not only for delivering features.",
            "The role works through others, so clarity, influence, and trust matter as much as analysis.",
            "Business analysis and Product Owner work overlap with product management, but the product manager holds the wider life cycle view.",
            "Titles and scope vary, so read each role description for its users, decisions, and measures."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Strategy, discovery, and customers", paragraphs: [
            "Strong product work starts with a clear understanding of users and their problems. A user may be a customer, an employee, or another team that depends on the product to get work done. Understanding means learning about their goals, their context, the obstacles they face, and the workarounds they already use. Without that grounding, it is easy to build something that looks complete but does not help in real use.",
            "Discovery is the regular habit of gathering that understanding, not a single exercise at the start of a project. Useful habits include short interviews, watching how people carry out tasks, reviewing feedback and requests, and looking at how the product is actually used. Good discovery listens for evidence and surprises, rather than seeking confirmation of an existing idea. Small, frequent checks usually teach more than occasional large studies, because learning stays close to decisions.",
            "Market and competitor awareness adds context to what users say. It helps to know what alternatives users already have, including manual methods, spreadsheets, phone calls, and other products, and what each alternative does well or poorly. Awareness of wider trends, constraints, and risks also matters, including rules and controls that shape what is possible in regulated areas such as banking and payments. The aim is not to copy others, but to understand the choices users face and where a product can offer something genuinely better.",
            "Over time, discovery should form a product point of view: a clear statement of who the product serves, which problem matters most, why it matters now, and what a good result would look like. A point of view helps a team choose between competing ideas and explain those choices to others. It should be firm enough to guide decisions, and open enough to change when new evidence appears. Writing it down, testing it with users and colleagues, and revising it is part of the work, not an extra task."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Interview three users about a problem", action: "Arrange short interviews with three people who face the same problem, and prepare open questions about their goals, tasks, and frustrations. Ask about recent examples and what they did next, and listen without pitching a solution. Write up what you heard, including concrete examples, patterns across the three conversations, and questions that remain open.", timeframe: "Weeks 3-5"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Observe how work really happens", action: "Spend time watching how users carry out a task in their normal setting, with their permission, and note the steps, tools, interruptions, and workarounds you see. Pay attention to what people do, not only what they say they do, and ask short clarifying questions where helpful. Summarise the gaps between the intended process and the real process, and what those gaps suggest about the underlying problem.", timeframe: "Weeks 3-5"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Review feedback and support themes", action: "Gather recent feedback, requests, complaints, and support themes for a product or service you know, and group them by underlying problem rather than by requested feature. Count how often each theme appears in your sample, and note which themes affect the most important tasks. Write a one page summary that separates frequent frustrations, rare but serious problems, and requests that may not point to a real need.", timeframe: "Weeks 4-6"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Write a one-page product point of view", action: "On one page, state who the product serves, the main problem it should solve, why that problem matters now, and what success would look like for users and for the business. Support each claim with a piece of evidence from your interviews, observation, or feedback review, and mark any claim that is still an assumption. Share the page with a colleague for challenge, and revise it based on the questions they raise.", timeframe: "Weeks 5-6")], keyPoints: [
            "Start with the user problem, not with a feature idea.",
            "Discovery is a regular habit of interviews, observation, and feedback review.",
            "Alternatives include manual workarounds, not only other products.",
            "A written point of view turns scattered findings into a direction the team can test."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Roadmaps and prioritisation", paragraphs: [
            "A roadmap communicates direction. It shows the goals a product is working towards, the broad themes of work that support those goals, and the rough sequence in which they will be explored. A good roadmap helps people outside the team understand why work is planned in a certain order, and helps the team stay focused when new requests arrive. It is a communication and alignment tool first, and a schedule second.",
            "Prioritisation is the work of choosing what to do first when time and capacity are limited. Every choice to pursue one item is also a choice to delay or decline another, so trade-offs should be made openly. Simple frameworks can help structure the discussion, such as comparing expected value with effort, weighing urgency against importance, or considering risk and what the team needs to learn next. Frameworks support judgement, they do not replace it, and the reasons behind a ranking matter more than the score itself.",
            "Saying no is a normal and necessary part of the role. A clear no explains what is being deferred or declined, why, and what evidence or trade-off supports the decision. It also leaves room for change by stating what new information would lead to a different answer. Handled with respect and reasons, a no protects focus and builds trust, while an unclear yes that cannot be delivered damages both.",
            "A roadmap stays honest only if it changes when facts change. New user evidence, technical findings, business shifts, or results from launched work can all justify a revised order or a changed goal. When that happens, update the roadmap visibly, explain what changed and why, and note what is affected. A roadmap that never changes is rarely a sign of good planning, it is often a sign that learning is being ignored."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Draft a goal-led roadmap for one product", action: "Choose a product or service you know and draft a roadmap covering the next two or three periods of work, organised by goals and themes rather than by features. For each theme, write the goal it supports, the problem it addresses, and how progress would be recognised. Keep dates rough and labelled as intentions, and add a short note on what is deliberately not included yet.", timeframe: "Weeks 6-8"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Prioritise ten items with clear reasons", action: "List ten possible items of work for the same product, including new ideas, improvements, and fixes, and rank them in order. For each item, write one or two sentences on expected value, effort, risk, and what would be delayed by choosing it. Ask a colleague to challenge your order, and revise the ranking where their challenge is supported by evidence.", timeframe: "Weeks 6-8"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Practise saying no with reasons", action: "Write three short responses to realistic requests you cannot accept now, each giving the reason, the trade-off involved, and what would need to change for a different answer. Practise delivering one of them aloud in a calm, respectful tone, and ask for feedback on clarity and tone. Keep the written versions as templates you can adapt, so future replies stay consistent and based on evidence.", timeframe: "Weeks 7-9"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Update a roadmap after new evidence", action: "Take the roadmap you drafted earlier and introduce one realistic piece of new evidence, such as user feedback, a technical constraint, or a changed business priority. Update the goals, themes, or sequence to reflect the new evidence, and write a short explanation of what changed, why, and what is affected. Review the updated roadmap for honesty, and remove any item that no longer earns its place.", timeframe: "Weeks 8-10")], keyPoints: [
            "A roadmap communicates goals, themes, and sequence, not a fixed list of features and dates.",
            "Prioritisation is an open trade-off, and the reasons matter more than any score.",
            "A clear no, with reasons and a route back, protects focus and trust.",
            "Revisit the roadmap when evidence changes, and explain each revision."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Delivery partnership", paragraphs: [
            "Delivery works best when product, engineering, design, and data colleagues act as partners on a shared problem. Each discipline brings knowledge the others do not have, from technical constraints and design possibilities to patterns in data and user behaviour. Early and regular involvement helps the team spot risks sooner, shape better solutions, and avoid late surprises. Partnership shows in how decisions are made together, how disagreements are handled, and how credit is shared.",
            "Clear problem statements help a partnership work well. A good statement describes who is affected, what problem they face, what evidence supports it, what outcome would count as success, and any constraints that must be respected. It leaves room for the team to explore solutions, rather than prescribing a detailed answer in advance. When the problem is clear, engineers and designers can propose approaches the product manager might not have considered, and the discussion stays focused on the outcome.",
            "Unblocking delivery is a core part of the role during build. Blockers often come from unclear decisions, missing inputs, dependencies on other teams, or changing expectations. The product manager helps by making timely decisions, securing the information or agreement the team needs, resolving dependencies early, and communicating changes promptly. Protecting the team from unnecessary churn, while staying available for genuine questions, keeps momentum without hiding real problems.",
            "Scope pressure is common during build, as new requests, edge cases, and ideas appear once work is visible. Each addition should be assessed against the original outcome, the added effort and risk, and the effect on other work. Sometimes the right answer is to include a small change, sometimes to defer it, and sometimes to revisit the scope as a whole. Being open about the trade-off, and agreeing the revised scope with the team and stakeholders, is better than quietly absorbing extra work until quality or timing suffers."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Write problem statements, not solutions", action: "Rewrite three solution focused requests as problem statements, each covering who is affected, the problem, the evidence, the desired outcome, and the constraints. Remove any wording that prescribes a detailed solution, and leave the approach open for the team to explore. Share one statement with a colleague from another discipline and revise it based on what they find unclear.", timeframe: "Weeks 9-11"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Join design and engineering discussions early", action: "Ask to join early design and engineering discussions for a piece of work, even if your role is mainly to listen and ask questions at first. Note the constraints, options, and risks raised, and how they shape the possible solutions. After the discussion, write a short summary of what you learned and how it affects the problem statement or the plan.", timeframe: "Weeks 9-12"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Keep a decision and blocker log", action: "Start a simple log for a current piece of work, recording each decision, who made it, the reasons, and the date, plus each blocker, its owner, and its next step. Review the log twice a week, chase overdue items, and update entries when decisions change. At the end of the work, review the log for patterns, such as repeated blockers or decisions that were revisited too often.", timeframe: "Weeks 10-12"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Handle a late scope request calmly", action: "Take a realistic late request and work through it in writing: restate the original outcome, assess the added effort and risk, and note the effect on other work and timing. Draft a response that offers clear options, such as include, defer, or swap with another item, with the trade-off for each option stated plainly. Discuss the options with the team and the requester, and record the agreed scope and reasons.", timeframe: "Weeks 11-13")], keyPoints: [
            "Involve engineering, design, and data early, as partners on the problem.",
            "State the problem, evidence, outcome, and constraints, and leave solution space open.",
            "Unblock delivery with timely decisions, clear inputs, and early handling of dependencies.",
            "Assess scope changes against the outcome, and agree revised scope openly."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "Metrics, experimentation, and commercial sense", paragraphs: [
            "Measures help a product manager judge whether a product is achieving its intended result. The most useful measures connect directly to a user outcome and a business outcome, such as whether users complete a key task successfully and whether that completion supports the wider goal. A small set of clear measures is usually more helpful than a long list, because the team can focus on moving the measures that matter. Each measure needs a clear definition, so everyone interprets it in the same way.",
            "Reading data well requires care. Before drawing a conclusion, check how a measure is defined, which users and time period it covers, and whether the sample is large enough to support the reading. Look at segments separately where useful, since an overall average can hide very different experiences for different groups. Combine numbers with qualitative evidence, such as interviews and feedback, and ask what else could explain a change before attributing it to a single cause.",
            "Simple experiments turn assumptions into learning. An experiment starts with a clear statement of what is expected to happen, a small change or test that can reveal whether the expectation holds, and a measure that will show the result. The learning matters whether the result confirms the expectation or not, because both outcomes guide the next decision. Short learning loops, where the team tests, reviews, and decides quickly, reduce the risk of committing heavily to an untested idea.",
            "Commercial sense means understanding how a product creates value and what it costs to run. This includes how the product earns money or saves money, what users value enough to pay for or keep using, and the ongoing costs of building, supporting, and operating it. In payments and banking, reliability, trust, and the cost and risk of each transaction or case also shape what good looks like, and a commercially sound decision must weigh those factors alongside user benefit. Knowing when to stop or change direction is part of the same judgement: when evidence stays weak, costs outweigh benefits, or a better use of effort appears, a clear decision to stop, pause, or pivot protects resources for work with stronger prospects."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Choose three measures for one product", action: "For a product you know, choose three measures that together show user success, business value, and product health, and write a clear definition for each one. Note how each measure would be collected, how often it would be reviewed, and what change would count as meaningful progress. Share the set with a colleague and revise any measure that is vague, easy to game, or disconnected from the intended outcome.", timeframe: "Weeks 12-14"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Analyse a metric change with care", action: "Take a real or realistic change in a measure and investigate it before concluding what caused it, checking the definition, time period, segments, and any known events or changes. Write down at least two other explanations for the change besides the most obvious one, and note what extra evidence would help distinguish between them. Summarise your conclusion with its limits stated plainly, including what remains uncertain.", timeframe: "Weeks 13-15"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Design and run a small experiment", action: "Choose one assumption about users or a product change, and design a small test with a clear expectation, a defined audience or sample, and one main measure. Run the test at a scale that limits risk while still producing useful learning, and record the result without overstating what it proves. Write a short learning note stating what you learned, what you will do next, and what you will not do as a result.", timeframe: "Weeks 14-17"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Review the commercial picture simply", action: "For a product you know, write a simple one page review of how it creates value, what it costs to build and run, and what users value most about it. Include the main risks and dependencies that could affect value or cost, and note where your understanding is incomplete. Use the review to identify one commercial question to explore further with a finance, operations, or commercial colleague.", timeframe: "Weeks 15-18")], keyPoints: [
            "Choose a small set of measures linked to user and business outcomes, with clear definitions.",
            "Check definitions, segments, and context before drawing conclusions from data.",
            "Run small experiments with a clear expectation, and treat learning as the result.",
            "Weigh value, cost, and risk together, and be willing to stop or change direction on the evidence."
        ]),
            StudioGuideSection(heading: "The path in", paragraphs: [
            "There is no single required route into product management. Business analysts often bring stakeholder, analysis, and change skills that transfer well, and Product Owners bring backlog, delivery, and team working experience. People from engineering bring technical understanding, designers bring user insight, and colleagues in marketing, operations, and customer support bring direct knowledge of customers and how work flows in practice. Many first moves happen inside the same organisation, through taking on product work alongside a current role, volunteering for discovery or roadmap tasks, or moving into a junior or associate product role.",
            "A portfolio makes that experience visible. Useful pieces include a case study that shows a problem, the evidence gathered, the decisions made, and the outcome or learning, a teardown of an existing product with thoughtful improvement ideas, and a roadmap example with goals and trade-offs explained. The portfolio should show thinking and judgement, not only polished outputs, and it should be honest about what was real work, what was practice, and what remains uncertain. Generic product management courses and certificates can help structure learning, but evidence of real work and decisions matters more than certificates alone when showing readiness for the role.",
            "Interview preparation should cover product sense, execution, and communication. Product sense questions explore how you understand users, structure an ambiguous problem, and form a point of view, often using an unfamiliar product or scenario. Execution questions explore prioritisation, metrics, trade-offs, and how you would handle delivery challenges. Prepare by practising aloud, structuring answers clearly, asking clarifying questions before answering, and drawing on real examples from your portfolio and past work.",
            "The first 90 days in a product role are mainly about learning, trust, and steady progress. Spend time understanding users, the team, the product, the systems and constraints, and how decisions are currently made. Build relationships by listening, delivering small useful wins, and following through on commitments, rather than proposing sweeping changes before the context is clear. Keep notes on decisions, open questions, and lessons learned, set learning goals with your manager, and seek feedback early and often."
        ], steps: [StudioGuideStep(title: "Choose your route into product work", action: "Review your current skills and experience against the skills in this guide, and identify the two or three gaps that matter most for the kind of product role you want. Choose a realistic route, such as product work inside your current organisation, a junior product role, or a gradual shift through Product Owner work, and note the first step for that route. Write a short plan with dates for closing one gap, and share it with a trusted colleague or mentor for feedback.", timeframe: "Weeks 18-20"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Build a three-piece PM portfolio", action: "Create three portfolio pieces: a case study, a product teardown, and a roadmap example, each showing the problem, evidence, decisions, trade-offs, and outcome or learning. Keep each piece concise, honest about what was real work and what was practice, and free of confidential information from any employer. Ask two people to review the set for clarity and credibility, and revise it based on their feedback before sharing it further.", timeframe: "Weeks 18-22"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Prepare for product sense interviews", action: "Practise product sense and execution questions aloud, using a clear structure: clarify the problem, consider users and evidence, weigh options and trade-offs, and state a recommendation with reasons. Use products you know well and unfamiliar scenarios, and time your answers so they stay focused. After each practice answer, note what was strong, what was unclear, and one improvement to make next time.", timeframe: "Weeks 20-24"), StudioGuideStep(title: "Plan your first 90 days", action: "Draft a first 90 days plan in three parts: learning users, team, product, and constraints in the first month, building relationships and delivering small wins in the second, and shaping clearer direction with the team in the third. For each part, list the meetings, questions, and outputs that would help, and the habits you will keep, such as a decision log and regular feedback. Keep the plan flexible, so it can adapt to what you actually learn once in the role.", timeframe: "First 90 days in role")], keyPoints: [
            "Routes into product management vary, and skills from analysis, delivery, and customer facing roles all transfer.",
            "A portfolio should show thinking, evidence, and trade-offs, with honest notes on what was real and what was practice.",
            "Interview practice should cover product sense, execution, and clear communication, using real examples.",
            "The first 90 days are for learning, trust, and small wins, not sweeping change."
        ])
        ],
        skills: [
            "Product strategy and roadmapping",
            "Customer discovery and user research",
            "Prioritisation and trade-off decisions",
            "Writing clear problem statements",
            "Working with engineering and design teams",
            "Data analysis and metric interpretation",
            "Experiment design and learning from results",
            "Stakeholder communication and alignment",
            "Commercial awareness and pricing basics",
            "Storytelling with evidence",
            "Facilitation and decision making",
            "Product sense and market awareness"
        ],
        tools: [
            "Jira",
            "Confluence",
            "Trello",
